<body marginheight="0" topmargin="0" leftmargin="0" style="margin: 0; padding:0; background:white !important;">
<main id="maincontent" tabindex="-1">
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 100</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Cover crops; Organic agriculture; Rice; Soil properties</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Exploration of Climate Smart Organic Farming in Nepal: Impact of Cover Crops on Rice Growth, Yield and Soil Health in Organic Management System</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Achyut Gaire</u>, Ram Kumar Shrestha, Kishor Dahal, Dilip Nandwani</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Rampur Campus, Tribhuvan University, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">achyut.gaire@rc.tu.edu.np</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">A two-year field experiment was conducted at the Organic Agriculture Farm in Chitwan, Nepal, during the 2023 and 2024 to compare the effects of different cover crops on soil properties and rice productivity in an organic farming system. Four replications of six cover crops as a treatment: Control, Soybean, Sesame, Cowpea, Sesbania, and Mungbean grown in field plots were arranged in randomized complete plot design (RCBD). Sesame plots recorded significantly lower soil temperatures across intervals, and cover crop biomass varied significantly, with mungbean producing the highest biomass. After cover crop incorporation, 25-day-old rice seedlings (cv. Ramdhan) were transplanted, and growth and yield attributes were recorded. Significant differences were observed in plant height of rice across different growth stages, with mungbean consistently producing the tallest plants. In both years, significantly higher effective tiller density of rice was observed in sesame (278 m⁻²) and mugbean (275 m⁻²) plot. Grain yield was significantly higher under mung bean cultivated plot (5.66 MT ha⁻¹). Residual soil analysis following rice cultivation indicated that all cover crop treatments contributed to enhanced soil health between 2023 and 2024. The greatest increases in soil pH were observed under soybean and sesame, while organic matter and nitrogen levels were most enhanced by sesame and cowpea. Mungbean led to the highest improvement in available phosphorus, and both mungbean and sesbania significantly increased available potassium. Thus, cover crops appeared to differ not only in their effects on the soil properties, but also has potential to influence the performance of organic rice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 101</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Vulture, NAIDs, Restriction, Coordination, Policy</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Securing Nepal’s Vulture Future: From Policy Success to Action</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sadiksha Basnet</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Bird Conservation Nepal (BCN), Nepal; <span class="paper_email">sadiksha@birdlifenepal.org</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Nepal’s vulture conservation journey demonstrates how science-based advocacy, progressive policy, and sustained field action can reverse a severe biodiversity crisis. Home to all nine Asian vulture species, Nepal experienced population declines of over 90% in the 1990s and early 2000s, largely driven by the veterinary drug diclofenac. In response, Nepal restricted veterinary diclofenac in 2006, promoted meloxicam, developed successive Vulture Conservation Action Plans, and established Vulture Safe Zones, including Asia’s first formally declared VSZ in Gandaki–Lumbini in 2021. These measures have contributed to population recovery and stronger provincial engagement.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">As of now, Nepal has made remarkable progress in vulture conservation through strong policies, scientific advocacy, Vulture Safe Zones and sustained community engagement. Recently, in April 2026 Nepal also restricted acceclofenac, nimesulide and ketoprofen.  A ploicy advocacy from BCN for over decades has resulted in this outcome. </p>

<p class="paper_abstract">However, these gains remain vulnerable due to weak inter-agency coordination, continued access to vulture-toxic veterinary drugs, open-border risks, and gaps between policy and implementation. Addressing these challenges requires stronger coordination among wildlife, veterinary, health and drug-regulation authorities; effective regulation and enforcement of harmful drugs; cross-border cooperation; and consistent implementation and monitoring of conservation measures. Sustaining Nepal’s vulture recovery will depend on turning established policy commitments into coordinated, timely and accountable actions.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Nonetheless, the emerging threats and policy gaps still remain prominent. Weak inter-agency coordination, delayed administrative decisions, and the continued availability of vulture-toxic drugs could undermine conservation gains. Urgent regulatory action, stronger coordination, effective implementation, and sustained monitoring are essential to secure Nepal’s vulture recovery and maintain its leadership in vulture conservation.</p>


</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 102</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate governance, policy accumulation, policy mixes, industrial parks, sustainability transitions</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Rising Sustainability Ambitions, Evolving Policy Mixes? China’s Industrial Parks in Transition</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yuxi Li</u><sup>1</sup>, Jan Fransen<sup>2</sup>, Martin de Jong<sup>3</sup>, Michael Faure<sup>4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Erasmus Graduate School of Law, Erasmus University Rotterdam, Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Institute for Housing and Urban Development Studies, Erasmus University Rotterdam, Netherlands; 3:&nbsp;Erasmus Graduate School of Law, Erasmus University Rotterdam, Netherlands; 4:&nbsp;Erasmus Graduate School of Law, Erasmus University Rotterdam, Netherlands; <span class="paper_email">yuxi@law.eur.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainability ambitions have become increasingly prominent in industrial-development policy, connecting environmental protection and resource efficiency with industrial decarbonization. Yet successive ambitions are pursued through policies that interact with existing goals, instruments and implementation arrangements. Understanding this interaction matters because the introduction of new policies does not, by itself, explain how they work alongside earlier ones or what their combined implementation requires.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">China’s industrial parks provide a longitudinal case, with national initiatives spanning eco-industrial development, circular transformation, low-carbon development, green manufacturing and zero-carbon development. This study asks whether China’s national industrial-park sustainability policies accumulated between 2006 and September 2026 and, if so, how this accumulation is reflected in their policy mixes. It also considers when accumulation may support or complicate implementation, and for whom.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study employs longitudinal document analysis of five national initiatives, drawing on formal policy documents and associated standards. Policy mixes are examined through three components: policy goals, policy instruments and formal implementation arrangements. The analysis first reconstructs changes within each initiative, then traces continuity, additions and revisions over time, and finally compares recurring characteristics and explicit connections across initiatives. Particular attention is paid to changes in policy instruments and how their use is specified, alongside the implementation arrangements that define the conditions, procedures and responsibilities for their application.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By examining these arrangements together, the study seeks to explain how successive sustainability ambitions reshape both the means available for implementation and the tasks expected of parks and public authorities. Its contribution is to clarify when policy accumulation may facilitate further improvement and when it may create difficulties for particular actors. This perspective can inform industrial decarbonization and sustainable development by and beyond 2030 by connecting policy ambition with participation opportunities, implementation capacity and coordination.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 103</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Agroecology, PRISMA systematic review, Soil restoration, Post-2030 SDGs, Andean-Amazonian ecosystems</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Agroecological Practices for Soil Restoration in Ecuador and Colombia: Systematized Lessons and Directions for Post-2030 Sustainability Agendas</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Daniela Salome Gonzalez</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Universidad de Cuenca; <span class="paper_email">pinosdaniela26@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Soil degradation caused by intensive agricultural and livestock activities threatens ecosystem stability and food security across the Andean and Amazonian regions. As the deadline for the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) approaches, particularly SDG 15 (Life on Land) and SDG 13 (Climate Action), evaluating the real-world effectiveness of nature-based solutions is crucial for designing post-2030 sustainability strategies. Using the PRISMA systematic review protocol, this study analyzed 45 peer-reviewed scientific articles (Colombia: 76%, Ecuador: 24%) to assess how agroecological practices influence biological, chemical, and physical soil quality indicators across major land-use systems, focusing on silvopastoral and agroforestry models. The synthesis demonstrates that silvopastoral systems (predominant in 57% of Ecuadorian and 46% of Colombian reviewed studies) and agroforestry systems (29% and 38%, respectively) consistently enhance organic carbon sequestration, improve water infiltration, reduce soil compaction, and foster edaphic biodiversity. Tree root dynamics and organic matter incorporation via leaf litter effectively act as natural subsoilers, accelerating soil recovery in tropical and Andean ecosystems. However, adoption remains constrained by high initial establishment costs and technical management requirements. A decade of SDG implementation shows that technical agroecological solutions are highly effective for ecological restoration, but their scalability depends on targeted economic incentives, land-tenure security, and regional policy integration. Moving beyond 2030, sustainable land management policies must transition from passive conservation to active support for integrated socio-ecological systems.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 104</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Indonesia, Komnas HAM, human rights, SDGs, NHRI</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Strengthening the Role of National Human Rights Institutions in Advancing the Sustainable Development Goals: The Experience of Komnas HAM Indonesia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mimin Hartono</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Indonesian National Human Rights Commission, Indonesia (Komnas HAM); <span class="paper_email">mimin.dwi.hartono@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) provide a global framework for achieving inclusive and sustainable development, while human rights provide the normative foundation, accountability mechanisms and principles necessary to ensure that development leaves no one behind. Recognizing the close relationship between the SDGs and human rights, the National Commission on Human Rights of the Republic of Indonesia (Komnas HAM) conducted research to examine and strengthen the position and role of Komnas HAM in supporting the achievement of the SDGs in Indonesia.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The research starts from the premise that human rights are not merely an outcome of development but also a framework for designing, implementing and monitoring development policies. Although Komnas HAM has often been associated primarily with SDG 16 on peace, justice and strong institutions, its constitutional and statutory mandate extends across virtually all SDGs, including poverty, health, education, gender equality, decent work, inequality, environmental protection and access to basic services. This reflects the recognition that the achievement of all SDGs has significant human rights implications. </p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The research examines the institutional mandate, existing practices and potential contributions of Komnas HAM through a human rights-based approach (HRBA) to development. It identifies opportunities for Komnas HAM to contribute through human rights monitoring, research, policy advice, public participation, data development, advocacy and accountability mechanisms. Particular attention is given to the principles of participation, equality and non-discrimination, accountability, transparency, and the principle of leaving no one behind.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The research argues that National Human Rights Institutions (NHRIs) can play a strategic bridging role between human rights obligations and development commitments. By integrating human rights standards and data into SDG planning, implementation and monitoring, Komnas HAM can help identify groups that are marginalized or excluded from development, assess structural inequalities and policy gaps, and strengthen government accountability for SDG commitments.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 105</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">water, food, climate change, Adaptation, environment</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Traditional water management knowledge for sustainable climate change adaptation for dry zone of Sri Lanka</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Ranjana Piyadasa</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Colombo, Sri Lanka; <span class='paper_email'>ranjana@et.cmb.ac.lk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">In Sri Lanka, dry-zone regions is traditionally harvested and managed through man-made, interconnected cascade tank ecosystems for paddy cultivation. Ancient cascade ecosystems across the country are now under unprecedented pressure, posing serious threats to their sustainability and long-term development prospects. All cascade-based irrigation systems were traditionally operated and managed by local communities, with responsibility vested in the village leadership and community-based organizations. This study was conducted in the Horivila cascade system in the dry zone of Sri Lanka, to identify the present status of community-based management of cascade water systems. The findings reveal that several key ecological components and natural ecosystem types are still being maintained by the community, contributing to the sustainability of the system. Community organizations are adapting to increasingly precarious climatic conditions through the application of indigenous knowledge, diversified cropping systems, the use of drought-tolerant paddy varieties, changes in food consumption patterns, and reliance on locally derived agro-inputs. Many indigenous and traditional farming practices demonstrate significant potential for wider application in enhancing climate change adaptation. With increasing unpredictability in rainfall seasonality and variability in the study area, community organizations actively promote drought-tolerant crop varieties to ensure food security during drought periods. A defining feature of Sri Lankan community-based Ecosystem-based Adaptation to climate change is that, beyond environmental and adaptive benefits, it actively pursues social co-benefits for local communities. These include enhanced household incomes, economic diversification, expanded livelihood opportunities, improved access to education, strengthened gender equality, and inclusive benefits for women, youth, and other vulnerable populations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 106</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Rainfall variability, climate change adaptation, climate resilience, geospatial analysis, sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate-informed resilience: Integrating rainfall variability, geospatial analysis, and data-driven evidence for sustainable development in Nepal</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Rajan khadka</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Shaheed Smriti Multiple Campus,TU, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">rajankhadka455@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Nepal&#39;s monsoon-dependent socioeconomic systems are becoming more vulnerable to hydroclimatic fluctuations, and the country&#39;s complicated topography causes noticeable spatial variations in precipitation, which have an impact on infrastructure, agriculture, water, and local adaptability. This study assesses how observable hydroclimatic signals might guide climate-resilient development and describes the spatiotemporal variability of rainfall throughout Chitwan District, a strategically significant agricultural region in the central Himalayan foothills, between 2014 and 2024.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Daily rainfall observations from 10 meteorological stations operated by the Department of Hydrology and Meteorology were examined at annual, seasonal, and monthly scales. Temporal characteristics and monotonic trends were assessed using the Mann–Kendall test and Sen’s slope estimator, while geographic information system-based inverse distance weighting interpolation was used to resolve spatial patterns in rainfall distribution. Results demonstrate a strongly seasonal precipitation regime, with the monsoon contributing approximately 78–83% of annual rainfall. July consistently recorded the highest rainfall, whereas November represented the driest month. Annual rainfall exhibited a declining tendency, with an estimated Sen’s slope of approximately −0.82 mm/year, while spatial interpolation revealed substantial heterogeneity across the district. These findings indicate that climate-sensitive planning based on district-wide averages alone may obscure meaningful local hydroclimatic differences. Integrating station-based observations, robust non-parametric trend analysis, and spatial rainfall characterisation therefore provides an evidence base for geographically differentiated adaptation, particularly for water-resource management, agricultural planning, infrastructure development, and disaster-risk reduction. The study demonstrates how routinely collected hydroclimatic information can be translated into locally actionable resilience intelligence, supporting risk-sensitive development and contributing to climate adaptation objectives under the Paris Agreement, the Sendai Framework, the Sustainable Development Goals, and the post-2030 sustainability agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 107</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Renewable Energy; MSMEs; Green Transformation; Energy Transition; Sustainable Development; Inclusive Growth; Green Finance; Nepal</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Renewable Energy for MSME Transformation in Nepal: Turning Energy Challenges into Opportunities for Sustainable and Inclusive Growth</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Guna Raj Dhakal Bilas</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Federation of Nepal Cottage and Small Industries (FNCSI), Nepal; <span class="paper_email">grd.recon@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective:</strong> Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) are the backbone of employment, local economic development, and livelihoods in Nepal. However, many MSMEs are facing challenges arising from high energy costs, unreliable and unequal access to energy, dependence on imported fossil fuels, limited access to clean technologies, and inadequate green finance. This paper examines how renewable energy can transform these challenges into opportunities for sustainable, competitive, and inclusive MSME growth.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods:</strong> The study adopts a practice and policy oriented approach, drawing on the experience of the Federation of Nepal Cottage and Small Industries (FNCSI), stakeholder consultations, policy reviews, and evidence from Nepal’s renewable energy and MSME sectors. It examines opportunities for solar energy, bioenergy, micro hydropower, energy efficiency, battery energy storage, and decentralized renewable energy systems across productive sectors, including agriculture, manufacturing, tourism, and services.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Key Findings:</strong> The analysis indicates that renewable energy can reduce exposure of MSME to imported fossil fuel price volatility, diversify the energy mix, improve energy reliability, increase productivity and competitiveness, create green jobs, and stimulate local value chains. Distributed renewable energy can further support enterprises in rural and underserved areas by enabling the productive use of locally available resources. However, significant barriers remain, including limited access to affordable finance, clean technologies and technical skills, unstable policies, inadequate market linkages, and weak mechanisms for scaling successful solutions.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Nepal needs an integrated <strong>Green MSME Transformation Framework</strong> that links renewable energy deployment with energy efficiency, green finance, technology transfer, skills development, circular economy approaches, digitalization, and market access. Strong public private partnerships and locally adapted financing mechanisms are essential to accelerate and scale this transformation.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Relevance for Sustainable Development by 2030 and Beyond: Accelerating the renewable energy transformation of MSMEs can simultaneously contribute to climate action, affordable and clean energy, decent work, inclusive economic growth, and reduced inequalities. </strong></p>


</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 109</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Anthropocene, Clean air, Health, Human rights, SDGs</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Clean air in the Anthropocene</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jos Lelieveld</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Max Planck Institute for Chemistry; <span class="paper_email">jos.lelieveld@mpic.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Massive anthropogenic emissions in the Anthropocene have transformed atmospheric composition to such an extent that biogeochemical cycles, air quality, and climate have changed globally and, in some regions, profoundly. Air pollution is estimated to cause 7–8 million premature deaths annually, corresponding to 120–200 million years of life lost every year. Worldwide, air pollution is the leading environmental risk factor for human health, and achieving the World Health Organization’s air quality guidelines could substantially reduce morbidity and premature mortality.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Clean air should be recognized as a human right and is fundamental to achieving many of the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals, including good health and well-being, climate action, sustainable cities and communities, affordable and clean energy, and the protection of life on land and below water. Yet clean air itself is not explicitly represented among the Sustainable Development Goals. This is remarkable because, like clean water, clean air is a fundamental prerequisite for human health and well-being, while exposure to ambient air pollution is largely involuntary and affects nearly the entire global population. Moreover, the burden disproportionately affects vulnerable populations and low- and middle-income countries.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Reducing air pollution simultaneously benefits human health, climate and ecosystems, making clean-air policies among the most effective means of advancing multiple Sustainable Development Goals. Given its universal importance and the need for measurable targets and accountability, clean air should be adopted as a Sustainable Development Goal in its own right.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 110</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">ransitional justice; Equity; Community resilience; Sustainable transformation; The Gambia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Justice as a Foundation for Resilience: Transitional Justice, Equity, and Sustainable Transformation in The Gambia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jimmy Nzally</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Antwerp, Belgium; <span class='paper_email'>jimmy.nzallyh@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective. This paper examines how unresolved transitional justice undermines community resilience in a small post-conflict state. It argues that historical accountability is a structural precondition for sustainable transformation and resilience, rather than a separate human-rights process.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods. The study draws on documentary analysis of The Gambia’s Truth, Reconciliation and Reparations Commission (TRRC), focusing on the implementation of its recommendations. It is complemented by a forthcoming programme of 20–25 semi-structured interviews with victims of political violence during Yahya Jammeh’s 22-year rule (1994–2016), using purposive nonprobability sampling. The study builds on the author’s doctoral research, which included 75 semi-structured interviews in The Gambia, examining how victims’ interpretations of past violence shape perceptions of justice, state legitimacy, and institutional trust.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results. TRRC reporting indicates that transitional justice remains substantially unresolved nearly a decade after Jammeh’s departure. By 2025, only a minority of recommended measures had been fully implemented. A Special Tribunal established through an ECOWAS framework in December 2024 remained unfunded as of April 2026, while victims continue to express concern that they may die without seeing justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions. Resilience-building that treats accountability as a separate and time-bound process risks overlooking its continuing influence on institutional legitimacy and social trust. Justice and accountability should therefore be integrated into resilience and development strategies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Relevance beyond 2030. A justice- and equity-centred approach to resilience, consistent with Leave No One Behind, requires unresolved historical accountability to be recognised as a structural component of sustainable transformation in post-conflict societies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 111</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Indigenous knowledge; Griot tradition; Community resilience; Social equity; The Gambia; Decolonisation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Indigenous Peoples and local communities; Community resilience; Social equity</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jimmy Nzally</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Antwerp, Belgium; <span class='paper_email'>jimmy.nzallyh@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective. This contribution examines the Gambian griot (jali) oral tradition as an indigenous knowledge system with direct relevance to community resilience. It argues that the continued marginalisation of griot knowledge from formal heritage, education, and development planning constitutes an equity gap in resilience-building.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods. The analysis draws on a review of existing scholarship on the Senegambian griot tradition, colonial administrative and heritage documentation, and a forthcoming programme of community-based interviews with griot lineages and elders in the Albreda–Juffureh–Barra corridor. Access is grounded in the author’s decade of engagement in Gambian public life and current teaching of decolonial and oral studies at the University of The Gambia.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results. Existing scholarship reveals a substantial documentation and recognition gap. Griot tradition represents a structured system of historical, genealogical, ethical, and social knowledge that predates colonial rule. Yet colonial administrative practices and subsequent heritage-tourism development have largely privileged written and externally produced accounts, positioning griot knowledge as supplementary cultural material rather than recognising it as an authoritative knowledge system. This marginalisation limits its visibility within contemporary resilience and development frameworks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions. Resilience approaches that treat indigenous knowledge primarily as cultural heritage overlook functioning systems of knowledge transmission, social cohesion, community governance, and intergenerational learning. Recognising griot knowledge as a legitimate institutional resource can therefore strengthen locally grounded approaches to resilience and sustainable transformation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Relevance to sustainable development by and beyond 2030. Consistent with the principle of Leave No One Behind, a post-2030 development agenda should recognise Indigenous Peoples and local communities as knowledge holders and resilience actors. Integrating indigenous knowledge into development and resilience planning can advance equity, decolonisation, and more locally legitimate pathways to sustainable transformation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 112</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">environmental peacebuilding; India–Pakistan relations; Indus Waters Treaty; smog diplomacy; conflict transformation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Rescaling and Reconfiguring Environmental Crisis Deadlocks: Pathways Toward Security–Sustainability Transformations in India–Pakistan Relations</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sukthawee Suwannachairop</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Helsinki University, Finland; <span class="paper_email">sukthawee.suwannachairop@helsinki.fi</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The India–Pakistan conflict remains one of the world’s most enduring interstate rivalries, unfolding in a region that is simultaneously among the most climate-vulnerable. Drawing on Johan Galtung’s concepts of negative peace, positive peace, and transcendence, the study examines how climate change can either reinforce adversarial security practices or catalyse novel forms of cross-border cooperation. Using qualitative comparative case analysis, the article compares three climate-security episodes: the 2025 Indus Waters Treaty crisis, the emergence of “smog diplomacy” between Indian and Pakistani Punjabs, and proposals for Indo-Gangetic airshed governance. The findings demonstrate that climate-related crises within</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">the same rivalry can produce divergent pathways. While water insecurity was securitised and deepened geopolitical confrontation, transboundary air pollution created practical, technocratic collaboration based on shared environmental risks. The Indus crisis remained embeded in national-level zero-sum politics whereas smog diplomacy emerged through subnational actors who reframed the issue around public welfare. Airshed governance extends this logic by shifting cooperation to a broader regional, multi-country scale that can dilute bilateral tensions. These cases demonstrate that climate risks can either entrench conflict or create pathways</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">toward more cooperative regional governance, depending on how they are framed, scaled, and institutionally managed.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 113</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">competitive cooperation; climate action; decarbonisation; climate incentives; climate cooperation; game theory;</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From decolonisation to decarbonisation: self-interest and material incentives as drivers of global competitive cooperation for a fairer climate future</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sukthawee Suwannachairop</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Helsinki University, Finland; <span class="paper_email">sukthawee.suwannachairop@helsinki.fi</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Mainstream frameworks for climate and economic governance remain shaped by the colonial foundations of the global economy, yet these legacies are seldom placed at the centre of debates over how decarbonisation might be achieved. This article situates global climate politics within a postcolonial political economy of structural dependency, showing how colonial extraction, unequal exchange, external debt, and technological hierarchy continue to constrain the fiscal and policy space of states across the Global South. Empirical analysis of UNFCCC and COP processes demonstrates that climate ambition is routinely diluted by democratic short-termism and an anarchic international order, yielding lowest-common-denominator agreements that privilege consensus over ambitious decarbonisation target. Calls for climate justice and rights-based approaches supply vital normative legitimacy but rarely achieve systemic transformation when decoupled from the material structures of the world economy. By contrast, the Montreal Protocol’s phase-out of ozone-depleting substances shows that robust, redistributive cooperation becomes feasible when environmental goals align with technology transfer, productivity gains, and predictable, targeted financing. Building on this analysis, the article advances the concept of ‘competitive cooperation’: a reframing of climate governance in which durable decarbonisation emerges when climate ambition is made economically rational and technologically enabling for structurally diverse states, generating a “race to the top” in green investment. Just as empires unravelled primarily for strategic, economic, and security reasons rather than through a sudden embrace of human-rights norms, climate action is most likely to advance when institutional designs realign material incentives in ways that are equitable across diverse geopolitical spaces.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 114</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Historical, Urban, mortality, Air Pollution, Inequality</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Reconstructing Urban Air Pollution and its Mortality Burdens: Historical Health Inequalities in Maastricht 1864-1950.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Catherine Simpson</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Utrecht University, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">c.e.simpson@uu.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Ambient air pollution remains a leading global driver of cardiometabolic, cancer, and respiratory mortality, yet most exposure-response evidence comes from twentieth- and twenty-first-century monitoring data, limiting our understanding of health effects under the extreme, largely unregulated exposure levels of early industrialisation. This study addresses that gap by reconstructing ambient air pollution in Maastricht, the Netherlands, across a critical 86-year window of industrial growth and early governance response (1864–1950), and linking it to individual-level cause-of-death records to estimate historical premature mortality attributable to pollution exposure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using newly developed historical ambient pollution estimates alongside spatial mortality data, this study tests whether industrial pollution produced observable, spatially patterned health inequalities across the city. This topic is of direct relevance to current environmental justice research, where it remains contested whether pollution control narrows or simply displaces exposure disparities. Contrary to expectations, citywide analysis reveals no significant spatial variation in respiratory mortality. This study proposes three explanations with implications beyond this case: (1) domestic rather than industrial combustion may have driven particulate exposure; (2) exposure may have been sufficiently uniform across the city to flatten spatial health inequality even as pollution burden remained severe, suggesting that spatial homogeneity in exposure does not equate to equitable outcomes; and (3) socioeconomic mediators (healthcare access, education, social infrastructure) may buffer pollution&#39;s mortality effects independently of exposure level, a mechanism difficult to isolate in contemporary, already-regulated settings.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By spanning nearly a century of shifting fuel use, industrial practice, and governance, this study also offers a long-run temporal baseline for research into the cumulative health impacts of urban air pollution, and provides a historical anchor point for studies examining how energy transitions reshape exposure and mortality patterns over time.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 115</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Marginal abatement costs; Industrial decarbonization; Carbon markets; Firm-level heterogeneity; Partial frontier estimation; India</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Firm-Level Marginal Abatement Cost Heterogeneity in Indian Manufacturing: A Convex Quantile Approach</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Cheshta Grover</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">shiv nadar institution of eminence, India; <span class="paper_email">cg739@snu.edu.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">India faces the challenge of decarbonizing its energy-intensive manufacturing sector while sustaining industrial growth. Exploiting differences in energy-use adjustment costs across firms and inputs could substantially lower mitigation costs. Using a panel of 504 firms across seven energy-intensive and trade-exposed industries over 2012-2018, this paper estimates input-specific marginal abatement costs (MACs) for coal, purchased electricity, diesel-generated electricity, and furnace oil. We employ a convex quantile regression directional distance function (CQR-DDF), which estimates local partial frontiers rather than imposing a common frontier and allows MACs to vary across firms with different production and energy-use characteristics. Results reveal a highly asymmetric cost structure: most firms have zero or near-zero MACs, indicating substantial low-cost adjustment opportunities, while significant costs are concentrated among a smaller subset. Coal is the least costly input to reduce, with a median MAC of approximately Rs. 55/tCO₂, whereas purchased and diesel-generated electricity involve substantially higher costs. Furnace oil exhibits particularly high costs for some firms, highlighting technological rigidity in selected combustion-based processes. A stylized simulation of India’s Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS) shows that cost dispersion, rather than average costs, drives market outcomes. Trading reallocates mitigation toward lower-cost firms and reduces compliance costs by 39-58% under stringent targets, equivalent to approximately Rs. 214 billion, although gains decline as low-cost opportunities are exhausted. These findings show that effective CCTS design requires recognizing firm- and energy-input-specific cost heterogeneity in benchmark design and targeting complementary support toward high-cost technologies and energy transitions.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 116</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">experience curves, macroeconomic learning, directed technical change, artificial intelligence, climate policy</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Invisible Plateau: Experience Curves, Artificial Intelligence, and the Terminal Cost of the Green Transition</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>James Rice</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Essex, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">james.k.rice@essex.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Integrated assessment models, national decarbonisation plans and the ratchet</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">mechanism of the Paris Agreement all rest on a projection of where clean</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">energy costs eventually stop falling --- the technological plateau that follows</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">an exponential growth cycle. This paper asks whether that plateau can be seen</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">in the data at all, and what artificial intelligence does to it. We first</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">establish a theoretical result: when a technology&#39;s cost is the sum of</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">components with heterogeneous learning rates, its measured learning elasticity</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">must decline monotonically, at a rate exactly equal to the share-weighted</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">variance of component learning rates, converging to the slowest component&#39;s</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">rate. Saturation is generic, not exceptional. In the data, however, the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">opposite happens. Using seven energy technologies over 1975--2024, we find that</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">measured learning elasticities are rising: for onshore wind the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">fifteen-year rolling elasticity climbs from 0.09 to 0.81. We show this is an</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">identification artefact rather than an acceleration of technical progress. The</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">experience-curve slope is arithmetically the ratio of the cost-decline trend to</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">the deployment-growth trend; the correlation between the estimated elasticity</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and that ratio is 0.999, and deployment growth has decelerated significantly in</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">every technology we examine. We draw out what an unidentified plateau implies for the treatment of endogenous technical change in climate-economy models and for the design of international environmental agreements.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 117</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate-development nexus, just transition, resilient infrastructure, climate justice, equitable adaptation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Navigating the Climate-Development Nexus: Frameworks for Equitable Infrastructure and Just Energy Transitions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ganesh Dahal</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kathmandu University, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">ganesh_mtvet2023@kusoed.edu.np</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Abstract</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The interplay between climate action and socioeconomic development presents a critical challenge for achieving long-term sustainability beyond 2030 which we have just faced due to Bhotekoshi floods in Nepal on 26th August, 2026. I also mention that across the Himalayas, more than 200 potential “glacial time bombs” may be hidden in river basins and mountain valleys like the Bhote Koshi and Trishuli. We do not yet know when or where a massive ice–rock collapse or GLOF could occur, potentially sending destructive flows from above 5,000 metres downstream at extreme speeds and causing devastation like the recent Rasuwa disaster. So, we must urgently collaborate on comprehensive Himalayan hazard research, monitoring and early-warning systems before the next disaster strikes. While rapid decarbonization and climate adaptation are essential to prevent irreversible environmental degradation, top-down policy interventions often risk exacerbating social inequalities and disproportionately burdening vulnerable populations. This study examines how integrated, community-centered civil engineering and development frameworks can bridge the gap between climate resilience and social equity. By evaluating case studies of infrastructure development and energy transition initiatives across diverse regions, this paper highlights key mechanisms for balancing structural adaptation with equitable development. The analysis demonstrates that climate interventions are most effective when aligned with local development priorities, transparent decision-making, and targeted resource allocation for marginalized communities. Furthermore, we outline actionable policy and engineering guidelines designed to ensure that sustainable infrastructure investments strengthen institutional capacity while fostering social inclusion.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Ultimately, my paper will contribute evidence-based strategies to the post-2030 sustainability agenda, demonstrating that navigating the climate change–development nexus requires moving beyond simple mitigation metrics toward systemic, just, and equitable transformations across human and built environments.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 118</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate agnotology, climate misinformation, manufactured doubt, climate governance, sustainable transformation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Manufactured Doubt and Climate Governance Failure</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>James Rice</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Essex, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">james.k.rice@essex.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate misinformation is often treated as a communication failure or a stock of mistaken beliefs. This paper instead theorises climate agnotology—the deliberate, socially organised production of ignorance—as a supplied political-economic good that obstructs climate action and weakens the institutional foundations of sustainable transformation. It develops a three-component model of manufactured doubt. First, ideologically predisposed citizens and elites provide a receptive population whose uncertainty can be mobilised. Second, algorithmically curated and ideologically homogeneous media environments confine misinformation, recirculating rather than correcting it. Third, fossil-fuel interests, partisan entrepreneurs, think tanks, and platform-native amplifiers sustain the system through strategic investment. Together, these conditions transform diffuse scepticism into coherent and durable public doubt.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The model predicts a threshold effect. Below a critical level of organised investment, scepticism remains fragmented and largely self-cancelling; above it, mobilisation and communicative confinement reinforce one another, making doubt production self-sustaining even as its marginal cost falls. The paper constructs and stress-tests this argument through comparative-static reasoning, specifying its actors, mechanisms, scope conditions, rival explanations, and falsifiable propositions. It selectively draws on threshold models of collective behaviour, alongside information theory’s distinction between signal and noise, while grounding the analysis in political economy, democratic theory, and research on organised climate denial.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This framework links epistemic disruption to both governance and finance. It predicts that sustained agnotological coherence increases policy polarisation, lengthens legislative timelines, weakens international climate commitments, raises the transaction costs of coordination, and distorts price discovery. By explaining how organised interests convert information asymmetries into durable obstacles to mitigation, the paper identifies misinformation as a structural barrier to resilient and equitable climate transformation. It therefore advances agnotology from historical description toward a predictive theory of when climate-governance systems become vulnerable to manufactured doubt and where interventions may most effectively disrupt its amplification before hardening into persistent governance and market failure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 119</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Agricultural Engineering; climate-smart agriculture; precision agriculture; resources conservation technologies; water</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate Change, Water &amp; Agriculture Sector in Nepal: Management and Adaptation Strategies in the Framework of Agricultural Engineering</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jeet Chand</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Government of Nepal, Ministry of Agriculture, Forests &amp; Environment, Sighdurbarm Kathmandu; <span class='paper_email'>jeetchand06@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The focus of this study is to document major impacts of climate change in water and agriculture sector, and key adaptation management strategies applied at farm level from agricultural engineering perspectives focusing food security. Climate change is causing Nepal to experience a number of negative effects, including unpredictable rainfall patterns, a significant and rapid temperature rise, an increase in the frequency of extreme events, and rapidly melting glaciers with an average retreat of more than 30 meters per year. Agriculture, water, forest, health and biodiversity are the hardest-hit sectors from changed climatic pattern in Nepal. Nepal&#39;s temperature is rising at a rate of roughly 0.06°C per year, which is faster than the global average. Glaciers in Nepal melted 65% faster in recent years compared to prior decades. Nepal is the 10th most affected country from 2000 to 2021 on the long-term Global Climate Risk Index. More than 80% of property loss due to disasters is attributable to climate hazards, particularly water-related events such as floods, landslides &amp; GLOF. Water availability, quality, and stream flow have significantly changed across the nation, mostly as a result of changes in weather patterns. It is predicted that 14.5% national GDP in Nepal will be lost by 2100 due to climate change related issues if proper adaptation strategies would not be applied. Since agriculture is the backbone of national economy, its reliance on rainfall, water and irrigation makes it particularly vulnerable to climate change. Climate-smart agriculture, resources conservation technologies, farm mechanization integrated with automation, application of precision agriculture, and enabled climate-friendly policy are key adaptation strategies from agricultural engineering perspectives to manage adverse effects of climate change in Nepal. “Nothing Goes Right if Agriculture Goes Wrong”- and it is recommended that “agriculture can’t go right if engineering goes wrong because modern agriculture is engineered agriculture”.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 120</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">water, energy, agrifood systems, war</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Relief to Resilience: Sustainable Water and Energy Support in Conflict-Affected Lebanon</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Marwan Issa</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Oxfam, Lebanon (Lebanese Republic); <span class='paper_email'>missa2@oxfam.org.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">How can sustainability be advanced amid intersecting crises, including war, unprecedented drought, and a prolonged socioeconomic crisis? This session sheds light on the research and implementation work undertaken by Oxfam in Lebanon over the past three years, during which the country witnessed an exponential deterioration in security conditions. This period was marked by recurrent Israeli attacks on water, energy, and other critical infrastructure, alongside the widespread burning and degradation of approximately 56,000 hectares of agricultural land.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The session addresses two key issues. First, it examines how humanitarian interventions can respond to urgent needs while building the foundations for sustainable water and energy systems. It explores how Oxfam’s provision of clean solar energy to water institutions in communities affected by attacks on water and energy networks simultaneously supported immediate service continuity and longer-term resilience. These interventions demonstrated their efficiency both during escalations, when conventional services were severely disrupted, and during periods of relative de-escalation, when they continued to strengthen access, institutional capacity, and preparedness.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Second, the session discusses Oxfam’s research, policy, and advocacy efforts to document the environmental and sustainability-related impacts of war and connect resilience and sustainability to international humanitarian law and policymaking processes. It is grounded in the philosophical conviction that multilateral efforts toward sustainable development cannot be separated from protecting international legal standards and advancing just security arrangements.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The session contributes directly to discussions surrounding SDGs 6, 7, 13, and 16 by connecting access to water and clean energy with climate resilience, accountable institutions, peace, and justice. Lebanon’s experience, and that of the wider region, offer an important lesson for the post-2030 agenda: sustainability frameworks must account for conflict, infrastructure destruction, and institutional fragility while supporting solutions capable of functioning across both crisis and recovery contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 121</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable Pathways, Recycled Fiber-Reinforced Concrete; LCA</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sustainable Pathways for Recycled Fibre-Reinforced Concrete: A Life Cycle Assessment Perspective</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Muhammad Shafique</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Brunel University of London, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">muhammad.shafique@brunel.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The construction industry faces increasing pressure to reduce resource consumption, greenhouse gas emissions, and environmental impacts while maintaining the performance and functionality of infrastructure. In this context, the integration of recycled materials into cement-based composites offers an opportunity to support more circular and sustainable construction practices. Fibre-reinforced concrete composites are increasingly promoted as a circular alternative to virgin-material-based construction, providing a pathway for diverting waste from disposal and reducing dependence on virgin resources. However, the environmental performance of recycled fibre-reinforced concrete depends not only on the material itself but also on the processes used to recover and prepare the fibres.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A cradle-to-gate life cycle assessment (LCA) was conducted to evaluate the environmental impacts of virgin and recycled fibres. Compared with the conventional steel-fibre benchmark, mechanically recycled fibres reduced climate impacts by 28 to 61%, with bio-based fibres demonstrating the greatest environmental benefits. The study provides insights into circular material strategies and supports the development of more environmentally responsible fibre-reinforced concrete for future construction applications.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Importantly, circularity is not environmentally beneficial by default: the recovery pathway, rather than the recycled label itself, determines whether a material genuinely closes resource loops or merely shifts environmental burdens. Low-intensity mechanical recycling therefore warrants greater consideration in procurement criteria, material standards, and design guidance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 122</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate Policy, Public Health Governance, Societal Engagement, Africa CDC, Sustainable Development Goals</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Strengthening Climate-health Governance and Societal Engagement for Resilient Sustainable Development in Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>James Mawanda</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Net Zero Hub Ltd, Uganda; <span class="paper_email">mawandajames@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The intersecting crises of climate change and public health pose a profound threat to global progress toward the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Extreme weather events continue to devastate global health, with projections indicating that climate-induced health shocks could drive over 44 million people into extreme poverty by 2030, according to World Bank studies. In Africa, the situation is particularly critical: national implementation remains fragmented, and only 35% of countries explicitly prioritize health within their climate policies, according to the African Institute for Development Policy. In response, the Africa CDC Strategic Framework calls on African Union member states to build climate-resilient health systems, strengthen disease surveillance, and secure dedicated implementation budgets through 2029. Utilizing a secondary research methodology, this desktop study analyzes climate-health governance across the continent to provide actionable integration frameworks that foster active societal engagement for resilient, sustainable development. The study will contribute to evidence-based policy harmonization through practical methodologies to embed environmental and epidemiological risk data directly into municipal and local development planning. The study will strengthen institutional capacity building through evaluation of historical barriers, such as financing gaps, weak data infrastructure, and political silos, to strengthen health system governance. The study will also enhance community-led engagement through actionable strategies to catalyze localized dialogue, integrating indigenous knowledge and traditional governance into climate adaptation. The investigation will promote climate finance alignment through integrated roadmaps demonstrating how health-resilient policies and green infrastructure simultaneously advance SDG 3 (Good Health), SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities), and SDG 13 (Climate Action).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 123</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Nickel mining, Community empowerment; Post-mining resilience; Market decoupling; Sustainable livelihoods; Creating Shared Value (CSV); Extractive-Agrarian Decoupling Framework (EADF)</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Monopsonistic Traps to Decoupled Value Chains: An Extractive-Agrarian Framework for Post-Mining Resilience in Indonesian Nickel Frontiers</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Miftah Faridl Widhagdha</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Sebelas Maret University, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">miftahwidhagdha@staff.uns.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">As the global energy transition accelerates demand for nickel, mining corporations in Indonesia deploy agricultural Community Empowerment Programs (PPM) to secure their Social License to Operate (SLO) and mitigate the local resource curse. This study critically compares two divergent agricultural PPM models operating in Sulawesi’s nickel frontiers: PT Vale Indonesia Tbk’s integrated pineapple farming in Wasuponda (Luwu Timur) and PT Hengjaya Mineralindo’s short-cycle vegetable program in Makarti (Morowali). Employing a qualitative comparative case study design based on Most Similar Systems Design (MSSD) and grounded in critical realism, this research utilizes the Sustainable Livelihoods Framework (SLF) and Creating Shared Value (CSV) concepts. Empirical data were collected through in-depth semi-structured interviews, focus group discussions (FGDs), field observations, and documentary analysis. Findings reveal a fundamental structural divergence driven by corporate operational maturity (brownfield vs. greenfield) and commodity time horizons. PT Hengjaya’s short-cycle horticulture model delivers immediate financial liquidity, rapid cash flow, and gender inclusion via Women Farmer Groups (KWT), yet locks farmers into a monopsonistic dependency on mine catering supply chains. Conversely, PT Vale’s integrated agro-industrial model transforms pineapples into high-value secondary processed products managed by Village-Owned Enterprises (BUMDes), successfully achieving market decoupling from mining operations and establishing post-mining economic resilience despite facing local institutional capacity bottlenecks. This paper conceptualizes the Extractive-Agrarian Decoupling Framework (EADF), a novel four-quadrant typology explaining how corporate operational maturity and value-added commodity processing determine rural autonomous resilience versus monopsonistic dependency. It recommends that mining regulators integrate &quot;Market Decoupling Indices&quot; into official community development evaluations to prevent the cliff effect upon mine closure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 124</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change, human security, resource conflict, equity and justice.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Understanding Climate Change and Climate Action for Human Security</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Dela Khoirul Ainia</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Chulalongkorn University, Thailand; <span class="paper_email">dela.khoirul.a@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change not only impacts the environment surrounding humans but also affects security, which can threaten the economy and influence access to resources, social stability, human mobility, and interregional relations. The increase in natural disasters caused by climate change-such as droughts, floods, rising sea levels, and clean water scarcity-has the potential to spark competition over limited resources. Climate change can also drive population displacement-both internally and across national borders-raising critical questions about responsibility, protection, and the distribution of burdens among communities and nations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper aims to understand the relationship between climate change, climate action, and human security from the perspective of justice and equity. This paper also analyses mitigation and adaptation strategies that can be implemented to ensure human security. The central argument of this article emphasizes that human security in the face of climate change cannot be understood solely from the perspective of national security or control; rather, it requires a human security-oriented approach that places the rights, vulnerabilities, and capacities of communities at the centre of climate change response efforts. To address climate change, there must be transnational cooperation, equitable resource management, and a fair distribution of responsibilities so that the burden does not fall solely on poor communities or nations. Through this approach, it is hoped that climate actions can be developed that are not only ecologically effective but also promote justice and strengthen security in the face of increasingly complex climate change challenges.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 125</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital Twins, SEEA Energy Accounting, Green Climate Finance, AI for Sustainability, Climate-Resilient Development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digital Twins for Climate-Resilient Development: Integrating SEEA Energy Accounting with AI to Unlock Green Climate Finance in Developing Economies</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jasleen Kaur</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Indian Institute of Forest Management Bhopal, India; <span class="paper_email">jkaurdsf27@iifmbhopal.edu.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals have underscored the critical need for integrated data systems to track progress on climate action (SDG 13), clean energy (SDG 7), and sustainable finance (SDG 17). However, developing economies face persistent barriers in mobilizing green climate finance due to fragmented environmental and economic data, limited institutional capacity, and the absence of transparent, real-time decision-support tools. This research addresses these gaps by proposing a Digital Twin framework that integrates the System of Environmental-Economic Accounting (SEEA) for Energy with Artificial Intelligence (AI) to enable evidence-based climate finance decisions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on empirical insights from a pilot project in Bhutan—where the SEEA-Energy framework was operationalized with Green Climate Fund support—this study demonstrates how AI-powered Digital Twins can model climate-resilient development pathways. The framework integrates natural capital accounting, energy flow data, and climate vulnerability indices to simulate green investment scenarios, track environmental-economic trade-offs, and enhance policy coherence. The pilot engaged over 25 stakeholders across eight ministries, achieved 95% satisfaction, and directly influenced Bhutan&#39;s 5th National Development Plan.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methodologically, the research employs a hybrid approach combining AI-based predictive modeling, geospatial analysis, and stakeholder-validated scenario planning. Key outputs include dynamic ESG dashboards, climate risk heatmaps, and automated reporting aligned with TNFD and CSRD. The Digital Twin architecture enables real-time updates, transparent audit trails, and participatory co-design—addressing critical gaps in trust, data autonomy, and governance identified in post-2030 agenda discussions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Findings reveal that AI-integrated SEEA frameworks unlock green finance by improving investor confidence, reducing transaction costs, and aligning national priorities with global climate commitments. The study concludes by proposing a scalable Digital Twin model for other developing economies, offering a replicable pathway for the twin green and digital transition. This research contributes to the broader discourse on reimagining sustainability governance and monitoring beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 126</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Johads, Indigenous Knowledge Systems &amp; Practices, North Delhi, Geo-cultural Mapping</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Reimagining Water Governance in the Age of Climate Change: Positioning Johads as Indigenous Pathways to Urban Water Resilience in North Delhi</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Puneet Kumar</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Delhi, India; <span class="paper_email">pkumar@polscience.du.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that the water crisis in the current context of global climate change cannot be addressed only through technological and infrastructural interventions, and that the revival of traditional water bodies and the knowledge systems associated with them is critical for building climate-resilient cities in India. There is a growing importance of blue infrastructure in responding to climate change, particularly amid rising heat, flooding, water scarcity, groundwater depletion, and shifting rainfall patterns. The paper also examines the mounting recognition of Indigenous Knowledge Systems and Practices in global climate governance. It argues that India’s climate politics have nevertheless continued to privilege modern knowledge systems and economic interests in water governance, while ignoring traditional water bodies such as Johads (traditional earthen reservoirs) in Delhi, which were economically regenerative and ecologically sustainable.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">While applying the political economy approach to the case study of Johads in North Delhi district, the paper draws on focus group discussions with local communities and in-depth interviews with water experts. The paper argues that the Johads are socio-ecological structures that contribute to physical, psychological, social, cultural, and ecological well-being. Their restoration can support groundwater recharge, flood mitigation, reduction of urban heat, biodiversity, and community well-being. It signifies an epistemological standpoint that respects the indigenous connection between humans and water, grounded in values of peaceful coexistence. Finally, the paper proposes three steps for protecting Johads: identification, demarcation, and notification, while introducing a tool called geo-cultural mapping, based on a participatory approach that integrates GIS data with local knowledge and cultural practices to document and reviatize johads.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 127</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital inequality; Digital justice; SDG 4; Higher education; South Asia; Digital inclusion</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Digital Access to Digital Justice: Rethinking SDG 4 through the Experience of South Asian Higher Education</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Abirlal Mukherjee</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of the People, Unites States; <span class="paper_email">abirlal55@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The digital transformation of higher education has become increasingly central to the achievement of Sustainable Development Goal 4 (SDG 4), particularly its commitment to inclusive and equitable quality education. Yet the experience of South Asian higher education institutions suggests that expanding digital access does not necessarily produce equitable educational participation. This paper critically examines what the SDG era has revealed about the relationship between digitalisation, educational inclusion, and structural inequality in higher education.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on the literature on digital inequality, higher education and sustainable development, the paper develops a multidimensional framework for evaluating digital educational equity beyond the conventional measurement of connectivity and device ownership. It distinguishes between infrastructural access, affordability, digital capability, quality of participation, institutional support, and the ability to translate digital access into meaningful educational outcomes. Particular attention is given to socioeconomic, geographical, gendered and institutional inequalities that shape students’ experiences of online and technology-mediated learning across South Asia.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that the SDG approach has often treated digital technology as an enabling infrastructure without sufficiently addressing the unequal social conditions under which that infrastructure is accessed and used. Consequently, measures of technological penetration may obscure persistent inequalities in educational opportunity. The experience of South Asian higher education offers an important lesson for the post-2030 sustainability agenda: digital inclusion should be understood not merely as connectivity but as a question of digital justice.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The paper proposes that future global development frameworks integrate measurable indicators of meaningful digital participation, institutional capacity, affordability, accessibility and educational outcomes. Such a shift would reposition digital equity from a peripheral concern within SDG implementation to a foundational condition of sustainable and inclusive development beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 128</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital inequality; Higher education; Social mobility; Student well-being; Sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Generative AI and Educational Inequality in the Global South: Who Gains from the AI Revolution?</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Abirlal Mukherjee</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of the People, Unites States; <span class="paper_email">abirlal55@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Generative artificial intelligence has introduced a new phase of digital transformation in higher education. Students can now access tools capable of generating text, translating information, explaining complex concepts and supporting research. However, these capabilities may not be equally distributed or equally usable. This paper examines the implications of generative AI for educational inequality in the Global South.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Focusing on South Asian higher education, the paper develops a conceptual framework for analysing differential access to generative AI across socioeconomic, institutional and linguistic dimensions. It considers how differences in connectivity, devices, AI literacy, language capabilities and institutional policies may influence students’ ability to derive educational value from generative technologies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that generative AI creates a paradox. It has the potential to democratise access to forms of intellectual assistance previously available mainly to students with substantial educational resources, while simultaneously creating new advantages for students who possess stronger digital capabilities and access to more sophisticated technologies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper therefore proposes that AI inclusion must move beyond universal availability towards equitable capability. Institutions should provide students with opportunities to develop critical AI literacy, understand algorithmic limitations and use AI responsibly within educational contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper contributes to discussions of AI as an enabler of sustainable development by examining the distributional consequences of technological innovation. It argues that the sustainability of the AI transformation will depend substantially on whether educational systems can prevent new technological capabilities from becoming mechanisms of social reproduction.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper concludes by proposing an equity-oriented model for integrating generative AI into higher education in the Global South.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 129</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Artificial intelligence; AI divide; Digital inequality; Higher education; Equity and justice</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Digital Divide to AI Divide: Emerging Inequalities in the Intelligent University</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Abirlal Mukherjee</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of the People, Unites States; <span class='paper_email'>abirlal55@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The rapid emergence of generative artificial intelligence is transforming the technological landscape of higher education. While AI tools promise personalised learning, automated feedback, research assistance and expanded access to knowledge, their benefits depend upon unequal technological and human capabilities. This paper asks whether the digital divide is evolving into a more complex “AI divide”.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using South Asian higher education as a critical context, the paper conceptualises the AI divide as a multidimensional inequality encompassing access to AI tools, digital literacy, AI literacy, institutional infrastructure, quality of outputs and the capacity to use AI strategically. These inequalities may exist between institutions as well as among students within the same institution.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that the AI divide differs from earlier forms of digital inequality because access to technology alone is insufficient. Meaningful participation increasingly depends upon the ability to evaluate, interpret and critically use AI-generated information. Consequently, inequalities in educational benefit may persist even when basic access to AI tools becomes widespread.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper proposes an AI equity framework integrating access, capability, critical literacy, institutional governance and educational outcomes. It suggests that universities should evaluate AI adoption through an explicit sustainability and equity lens.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution lies in extending digital inequality research into the emerging AI era. Rather than asking simply whether AI is accessible, the paper asks who can use AI effectively, who can critically evaluate it, and who gains the greatest educational advantage from its integration.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper concludes that addressing the AI divide will be essential if digitalisation is to function as an enabler rather than a multiplier of educational inequality beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 130</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Responsible Innovation; GM Crops; Agricultural Innovation Governance; Regenerative Agriculture; Climate Resilience; Sustainable Agriculture; Stakeholder Governance; India</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">asugetme@gmail.com</p>
<p class="paper_author"> ASTHA JAISWAL</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">INDIAN INSTITUTE OF SCIENCE, India; <span class='paper_email'>asthak1@iisc.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">India’s agricultural sector faces growing pressures from climate change, resource constraints, environmental degradation and the need to ensure long-term food security. At the same time, agricultural innovation remains highly contested, particularly in relation to genetically modified (GM) crops. The Indian experience demonstrates that debates around agricultural biotechnology can become polarised between GM and non-GM positions, potentially overlooking the wider range of technological, ecological and knowledge-based pathways available for sustainable agricultural transitions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on ongoing research on the “Governance of Genetically Modified Crops in India: Mapping Stakeholders and Embedding Responsible Innovation,” this paper examines what lessons India offers for governing contested agricultural innovation in ways that support sustainable development, health equity and societal well-being. The study adopts a qualitative case-study approach, drawing on policy documents, scientific literature, regulatory materials and stakeholder perspectives relating to agricultural innovation governance in India. The analysis is informed by the principles of Responsible Innovation—anticipation, inclusion, reflexivity and responsiveness—and the Purpose–Process–Outcomes framework.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that India’s experience offers three important lessons. First, agricultural innovation governance should be problem-oriented rather than technology-centred, beginning with the social, ecological and agricultural problems that innovation is intended to address. Second, sustainable transitions require consideration of a diversified innovation portfolio, including GM crops alongside crop diversification, millets and locally adapted crops, agroecological practices, conventional and molecular breeding, gene editing, and technologies improving soil, water and resource efficiency. Third, decisions about agricultural innovation should incorporate the perspectives and interests of farmers, consumers, scientists, regulators and other affected stakeholders, while considering social, environmental and economic consequences.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper proposes Responsible Innovation as a governance approach for moving beyond polarised technology debates towards inclusive, anticipatory and context-sensitive agricultural policy. India’s experience offers broader lessons for aligning agricultural innovation with climate resilience, natural-resource conservation, health, livelihoods and sustainable well-being in the post-2030 sustainability agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 131</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">*Keywords:*  India Climate Scenarios, Green Growth, Environmental Law, Carbon Credit Trading Scheme [CCTS], EU CBAM, PARIVESH Portal, Green Credit Programme, EPR, NGT, Precautionary Principle, Polluter Pays, Absolute Liability, Digital Public Infrastructu</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">“INDIAN CLIMATIC SCENARIOS ALIGNED TO GREEN GROWTH AND ENVIRONMENTAL LAW AMID GLOBAL HEADWIND</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">AMIT GUPTA</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Applied Economics and Statistics)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">RITAM GUPTA</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (IIM KOLKATA)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">ABSTRACT - 250 Words.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The relationship between digitisation and productivity in India is moderated not by access to technology but by contextual factors — export orientation, workforce composition and market structure. Export-oriented firms facing EU CBAM and CSRD adopt AI for carbon accounting due to competitive pressure, while firms with higher skilled workforce translate digital tools into gains; low-skill firms see only costs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Digitisation pressure varies across industries. Sectors most at risk of digital lag are construction, agriculture and low-margin traditional manufacturing, marked by informality and low data maturity. International evidence from Germany&#39;s Mittelstand-Digital and Denmark&#39;s MADE shows generic subsidies fail; effective interventions are sector-specific data cooperatives and applied-tech intermediaries providing competence development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Banks must evolve beyond credit to advisory and ecosystem-building. Our questionnaire finds banks offering carbon accounting APIs, subsidised IoT and cluster advisory can de-risk adoption for MSMEs, but Indian commercial banks have low readiness — lacking tech talent and advisory trust. Viability requires RBI and SIDBI to enable fee-based advisory decoupled from lending, risk-sharing guarantees for green-tech loans, and incentives for bank-incubated digital hubs as in DBS Singapore.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The digital-ESG intersection risks green-washing. Genuine environmental co-benefits from AI occur only when investments are tied to measured resource KPIs with third-party MRV and absolute reductions — e.g., AI forecasting cutting renewable curtailment or digital twins for ZLD in textiles. Efficiency narratives without baseline disclosure are green-washing.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Thus, green growth requires a triad: Law provides trust via CCTS, Green Credit Rules and NGT enforcement; DPI provides scale via PARIVESH portal, National Carbon Registry and open environmental data; AI provides intelligence for predictive governance — transforming environmental law from compliance burden to competitive advantage for a resilient, just and equitable transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 132</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Post 2030 Agenda, Development Finance, SDG Implementation, Domestic Public Finance, Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Goals to Financing: Lessons from Mozambique for the Post 2030 Sustainable Development Architecture</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mariam Umarji</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Independent Individual Expert, Mozambique; <span class="paper_email">umarjimariam@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals established an ambitious global framework for sustainable development, but their implementation has exposed a persistent gap between global commitments and the fiscal, financing and institutional conditions required for delivery. This paper uses Mozambique as a country case to examine what this implementation gap implies for the design of the sustainable development architecture beyond 2030.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Using a policy and institutional case study approach, the paper synthesizes evidence from national development planning, public finance, debt, official development assistance, climate finance and sector financing processes. It examines how international commitments are translated through domestic budgets, fiscal institutions, external financing arrangements and implementation systems, and where these relationships constrain delivery.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The Mozambique case highlights four recurrent weaknesses. First, financing remains insufficiently integrated into global goal setting. Second, domestic and external financing systems remain fragmented, weakening alignment with national priorities. Third, global commitments often insufficiently reflect fiscal space, debt pressures and institutional implementation capacity. Fourth, accountability for financing and delivery is dispersed across multiple actors and instruments. These challenges are becoming more acute as countries such as Mozambique confront changing development cooperation, climate related financing demands and competing public expenditure priorities.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that the post 2030 framework should embed financing and implementation architecture within the design of global development goals rather than treat financing as a parallel enabling agenda. This requires stronger links between global commitments, national planning and budgeting, debt sustainability, development cooperation, climate finance and institutional delivery. Mozambique illustrates a wider lesson: sustainable development goals are credible only when accompanied by realistic, nationally grounded pathways for financing and implementation. The post 2030 architecture should therefore be assessed not only by the ambition of its objectives, but also by whether countries possess the fiscal, institutional and financing conditions required to achieve them.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 133</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Just Energy Transition Climate Finance Development Finance Climate Justice South Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Climate Finance Commitments: Financing a Just and Equitable Transition in South Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mariam Umarji</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Independent Individual Expert, Mozambique; <span class="paper_email">umarjimariam@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">South Africa has become one of the most prominent test cases for aligning climate finance with development objectives through its Just Energy Transition. Yet the scale of announced international support has also highlighted a critical question: whether headline climate finance commitments translate into financing that is sufficiently accessible, additional, concessional, transparent and aligned with national development priorities to support a genuinely just transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper examines the climate development nexus through the financing architecture of South Africa’s energy transition. Using a policy oriented case study approach, it analyzes public information on international climate and development finance, financing instruments, sector allocations, implementation arrangements and the evolving development cooperation landscape. Particular attention is given to the distinction between pledges, commitments, allocations and disbursements, and to how the terms and structure of finance affect implementation capacity and distributional outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The analysis identifies several tensions. Large aggregate financing commitments can mask differences in concessionality, additionality and accessibility. Fragmented financing channels can weaken alignment with national institutions and priorities. Financing for infrastructure and decarbonization may also advance faster than support for employment, local economic development, social protection and other dimensions of justice. At the same time, fiscal pressures and changing official development assistance increase the importance of how climate finance interacts with broader development finance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that effective climate development finance should be assessed not only by volumes mobilized, but by its quality, allocation, institutional alignment and contribution to equitable development outcomes. South Africa demonstrates that a just transition requires a financing architecture capable of connecting decarbonization with jobs, livelihoods, public investment and local development. These lessons are relevant beyond South Africa as countries seek financing models that reconcile climate ambition with fiscal realities, development priorities and social justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 134</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Drought, Early Warning, Farmers, Agromet Advisories, Digital Literacy, Just Transformation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Advisories for Whom? Digital Early Warning Systems and the Reproduction of Vulnerability in Indian Agriculture</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>vandana choudhary</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">NIDM, India; <span class="paper_email">vandanac990@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">As climate crises intensify, digital early warning systems have become a central node in the climate change–development nexus, reshaping disaster risk communication and, with it, expectations of resilient and equitable adaptation. India has introduced digital initiatives like SACHET for rapid-onset hazards (cyclones, floods, heat waves, lightning), yet slow-onset drought remains peripheral to these otherwise effective initiatives, posing structural challenges that existing frameworks for drought are ill-equipped to address. Drought early warnings for farmers are generated through monitoring and forecasting, disseminated as layered agro-meteorological advisories — historically via print and extension services, and increasingly via digital modes in recent years. However, fragmented institutions and the lack of a unified framework trap these advisories in complex digital dissemination channels, undermining last-mile reach and, with it, the promise of a just transformation in climate risk communication. This study examines advisory dissemination among progressive and non-progressive farmers in Rajasthan this El Niño year through telephonic surveys, revealing distinct inequalities: progressive farmers face technology-centered rather than user-focused services, while non-progressive farmers confront deeper issues of accessibility, comprehensibility, and trust. Thus, despite rising Information and Technology adoption, simply disseminating advisories digitally without accounting for existing inequalities in access and understanding between progressive and marginalized groups fails to ensure equitable reach, and risks reproducing the very vulnerabilities that resilient development is meant to reduce. The study identifies key dissemination challenges and argues that navigating the climate change–development nexus toward resilient, just, and equitable transformation requires inclusive institutional architecture, not technology alone.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Keywords: Drought, Early Warning, Farmers, Agromet Advisories, Digital Literacy, Just Transformation</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 135</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Heatwave, Health, urban, Slums, Resilience</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate Extremes and Urban Slums: Heatwave Impacts, Health Vulnerabilities, and Community Adaptation in Jaipur</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>vandana choudhary</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">NIDM, India; <span class="paper_email">vandanac990@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">According to the Medical Health and Family Welfare Department of Rajasthan, a total of 6,069 heat stroke cases were reported in 2024, reflecting the severe health implications of extreme temperatures in the state. This raises critical questions of who is being impacted, why certain groups are more vulnerable, and how they cope with such extreme conditions. Slum populations, already burdened by livelihood insecurity and inequality, are disproportionately exposed to climate impacts and extreme events like heatwaves. This study was undertaken during the summer months of 2024 with two objectives: first, to assess the impacts of heatwaves and the associated vulnerabilities in the slums of Jaipur city; and second, to explore the adaptive strategies and the key challenges and propose pathways for policy interventions. Personal interviews and focus group discussions were conducted with residents of five slum settlements in Jaipur, alongside interactions with institutional stakeholders. The adaptive capacity is categorised in generic and specific strategies, supported by a SWOT-based assessment of policy and field realities. Heatwaves severely affect community health, water resources, and livelihoods, with disproportionate impacts on vulnerable groups such as pregnant women, children, the elderly, and women, often leading to unreported deaths. The findings underscore the persistent vulnerability of marginalized populations, challenging the current policy emphasis on resilience-building without adequately addressing underlying vulnerabilities. The study argues that focusing narrowly on specific adaptive capacities is insufficient for sustainable resilience. Instead, the state must engage more deeply with marginalized communities to design inclusive strategies that genuinely strengthen resilience by first acknowledging and addressing their structural vulnerabilities. By identifying who is most affected, how, and why, such studies can inform long-term transformative solutions that address both climate change vulnerabilities and entrenched social inequalities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 136</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Farmers, risk perception, drought, temperature rise, adaptation action, societal resilience, community, household survey</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate Extremes and Farmers: Impacts of Drought and Rising Temperatures, Adaptation and Mitigation Measures</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>vandana choudhary</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">NIDM, India; <span class="paper_email">vandanac990@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">As climate extremes intensify, building societal resilience depends on grounding adaptation action in how communities themselves perceive and respond to risk. This paper explores the concept of &#39;Risk and Vulnerability&#39; by investigating farmers&#39; perceptions of the risks associated with drought and heat waves, and their corresponding adaptation strategies, in the dry-land region of India. The research concentrates on an area of grassroots investigation, meticulously chosen through a vulnerability assessment carried out using the IPCC framework, with specific emphasis on the state of Rajasthan. Through a primary survey of 150 rural households within the agricultural community, the study elucidates the perceptions of the agriculture community in Sikar district regarding the occurrence of drought and heat waves in the region. It further explores the impact of these events on their livelihoods and examines the adaptation actions and mitigation strategies employed by the community in response. Analysis of secondary data reveals an insignificant decrease and increase in drought and heat wave days, respectively, for the region. The household survey indicated that farmers perceive highly variable rainfall and increasing heat intensity due to climate change, giving rise to locally understood conditions such as Akal, Trikal, and Bhujkal. Farmers in the region have responded to these events primarily through adjustments in agricultural practices, a shift toward non-farm activities, migration, and fodder storage — adaptation actions that vary from household to household based on social and economic status. Moreover, government initiatives to help these farmers cope with such adverse situations remain limited. The findings underscore the importance of capturing micro-level grassroots perceptions, which offer valuable insight into locally evolved adaptation strategies and the institutional gaps that constrain them. Understanding these perceptions is essential to identifying effective, locally grounded actions for climate change adaptation and to building a more resilient society in the face of increasingly extreme climate disasters.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 137</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">biofuels, sustainability, circularity, decision making, framework</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A Comprehensive Assessment Framework for Biofuels Integrating Sustainability and Circularity Dimensions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Ana Arias Calvo, Evina Katsou</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Imperial College London, United Kingdom; <span class='paper_email'>a.ariascalvo@imperial.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Transitioning to low-carbon energy systems requires biofuels that are demonstrably sustainable and circular, yet existing assessment frameworks lack an integrated approach that simultaneously addresses circularity alongside the three conventional sustainability dimensions, environmental, social, and economic, and none provides a structured classification of requirements across minimum and advanced performance tiers. This study develops a comprehensive, evidence-based decision-support framework for biofuel sustainability and circularity assessment, organized across four hierarchical levels: pillars, areas of concern, criteria, and requirements with associated indicators. The framework was developed considering a systematic review and analysis of voluntary certification schemes recognized under the EU Renewable Energy Directive, alongside EU directives, international regulations, technical standards, and globally recognized institutional reports, to identify sustainability and circularity requirements and indicators. In a second stage, requirements were classified into minimum and advanced performance tiers through the application of a structured five-question decision matrix, ensuring a transparent and accurate classification procedure. The resulting framework encompasses 4 pillars, 23 areas of concern, 101 criteria, 139 requirements, and associated quantitative and qualitative indicators. A coverage analysis reveals that circularity and economic sustainability remain critically underrepresented across existing certification schemes and regulatory instruments, confirming the need for the integrated approach proposed. The framework provides a structured, transparent basis for assessing biofuel performance and supports the design of practical tools toward best practices to make informed, evidence-based choices when selecting sustainable biofuels.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 138</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Geoinformation; Sustainable Development Goals; Governance; Environmental monitoring; UAVs</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Geoinformation Technologies and UAVs as Cross-Domain Enablers of the SDGs: Evidence, Gaps, and Governance Implications</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ana Arias Calvo</u>, Evina Katsou</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Imperial College London, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">a.ariascalvo@imperial.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The achievement of the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) demands integrated, multi-scale approaches capable of capturing the complexity of socio-environmental systems. This contribution examines how geoinformation technologies, including Geographic Information Systems (GIS), remote sensing, and Earth observation, alongside Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), function as cross-domain enablers of sustainable environmental management and SDG implementation across natural, agricultural, coastal/aquatic, urban, and Arctic environments. Drawing on a systematic cross-domain analysis of 84 reviewed applications across 13 SDGs, the study reveals a concentrated but uneven distribution of sustainability contributions. SDG 13 (Climate Action) emerges as the most cross-domain SDG (n=18), spanning agriculture, coastal/aquatic/Arctic, and urban contexts. SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities) ranks second (n=16), driven by urban and coastal applications in flood risk mapping, air quality, and disaster risk reduction. SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) demonstrates the highest domain specificity (n=14), concentrated in precision agriculture and food security applications. SDG 12 (n=10), SDG 15 (n=7), and SDG 14 (n=6) further reflect the resource-efficiency and ecological dimensions of these technologies. Critically, SDG 17 (Partnerships for the Goals) records the lowest representation (n=1), signalling a significant governance gap in data-sharing and international cooperation frameworks. Despite their multi-SDG contributions, persistent structural barriers constrain broader operationalisation. EU drone regulations restrict deployment in sensitive and transboundary environments, while data standardisation deficits limit cross-institutional comparability. Furthermore, high computational demands and limited open data infrastructure in low- and middle-income countries risk deepening geospatial inequalities. Addressing these challenges requires coordinated investment in open data ecosystems, inclusive governance frameworks, and capacity-building initiatives, imperatives directly relevant to equitable SDG implementation and the post-2030 sustainability agenda.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 139</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">EV charging infrastructure, the Netherlands, transportation emissions, electricity-system integration.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Where, when, and how should EV charging infrastructure be deployed in the Netherlands to reduce transportation emissions and optimize electricity-system integration?</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ayat-Allah Bouramdane ayatallahbouramdane</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Independent researcher, Morocco; <span class="paper_email">ayatallahbouramdane95@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The rapid growth of electric vehicles (EVs) in the Netherlands creates an opportunity to reduce transportation emissions while also challenging the electricity system. This study investigates how the Dutch electricity system can support EV charging infrastructure and where charging stations should be deployed to maximize environmental and energy-system benefits.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">First, the study examines the Netherlands’ transportation emissions, focusing on road, maritime, and aviation sectors, and assesses the existing EV charging network, including station location and utilization. Second, it evaluates whether the electricity system can accommodate additional EV charging demand by analyzing the generation mix, merit-order curve, and temporal electricity dispatch. Particular attention is given to identifying electricity sources supplying EV charging and determining whether optimized charging times can increase the use of low-carbon electricity and reduce grid pressure.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Third, the study develops a spatial assessment of EV charging potential across the Netherlands. Regions are classified into low-, medium-, and high-potential zones based on technical, economic, environmental, transportation, and social criteria, including EV adoption, traffic, population density, existing charging infrastructure, grid capacity, electricity prices, renewable-energy availability, and proximity to highways and urban centers.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The results aim to identify where and when EV charging infrastructure should be expanded to reduce transportation emissions, support cleaner electricity use, and minimize grid stress. The study provides a basis for policymakers, electricity-system planners, and infrastructure developers to prioritize charging investments and support sustainable electric mobility.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 140</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable leadership, Connectedness to nature, Green shared vision Green ambidexterity</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Navigating Sustainable Performance through the Lens of Green Shared Vision</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Raheel yasin Raheel yasin</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">International University of Rabat, Morocco; <span class="paper_email">Raheelyasin@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Grounded in the green resource base theory, this study delves the impact of sustainable leadership on sustainable performance. In addition to that, in this study we measure mediating role of green shared vision along with that we also measure the moderating effect of connectedness to nature and moderating role of green ambidexterity. Data were collected from the Vietnam hospitality sector using a three-wave research design and hypotheses were tested using Lavaan package of R software. The results confirmed existence of positive correlation between sustainable leadership and green shared vision. Green shared vision mediates the nexus between sustainable leadership and sustainable performance. Finally results confirm the moderating role of connectedness to nature and green ambidexterity. We also examine the machine learning application, specifically the random forest algorithm by using Random Forest package of R Software, to augment objectivity and reliability in performance evaluation. Results confirm that sustainable leadership is most important for economic performance, whereas green shared vision is most important element for environmental performance and sustainable leadership is most important for social performance. This study extends the green resource base theory by elucidating mechanisms through sustainable leadership and becomes a source of sustainable performance in the hospitality context.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 141</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">stunting, women&#39;s role, gender equality, family empowerment</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">WOMEN&#39;S ROLES AND FAMILY GENDER DYNAMICS IN REDUCING STUNTING AT YOGYAKARTA CITY: A SCOPING REVIEW</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Redhita Rizky</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Ida Nurhayati</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Rita Rena Pudyastuti</u><sup>2</sup>, Andika Saputra<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Health Polytechnic of the Ministry of Health, Yogyakarta, Indonesia, Indonesia; 2:&nbsp;National Research and Innovation Agency (BRIN), Jakarta Pusat, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">redhita.putri@poltekkesjogja.ac.id</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">ida.nurhayati@poltekkesjogja.ac.id</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">ritapudyastuti1968@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This study aims to map scientific evidence on women&#39;s roles and family gender dynamics in reducing childhood stunting in Yogyakarta City, and to identify research gaps and family empowerment strategies based on pro-equity gender roles.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A scoping review was conducted following the Arksey and O&#39;Malley framework and PRISMA-Scoping Reviews guidelines. Literature covering 2016 to 2026 was searched across national and international databases, including Google Scholar, Scopus, PubMed, ScienceDirect, and Garuda, using combinations of keywords related to stunting, women&#39;s role, gender equality, and family empowerment. From 150 records initially identified, 120 remained after duplicate removal and screening, and 10 studies met the inclusion criteria for thematic analysis.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Five key aspects of family-based stunting prevention were identified: nutritional fulfilment, parenting practices, utilization of health services, environmental sanitation, and household decision-making. Women, particularly mothers, held strategic roles in nutrition provision and childcare, while paternal involvement as an active partner within a gender-equitable support system remains underexplored. A family model built on pro-equity gender roles has the potential to strengthen collaboration between women and men, consistent with the decline in Yogyakarta City&#39;s stunting prevalence to 9.13 percent by October 2025, below the government&#39;s 10 percent target.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Optimizing women&#39;s roles alongside greater paternal involvement, guided by pro-equity gender principles, offers a promising family-level pathway to reduce stunting and its long-term impact on children&#39;s growth and cognitive development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Relevance to sustainable development by and beyond 2030: These findings support progress toward gender equality (SDG 5), good health and well-being (SDG 3), and zero hunger and malnutrition reduction (SDG 2), demonstrating that gender-responsive family empowerment is essential to achieving equitable and sustainable health outcomes by and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 142</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital taxation; artificial intelligence; big data; digital economy; fiscal capacity; sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">AI, Big Data and Digital Taxation: Indonesia’s Emerging Framework for Sustainable Fiscal Capacity</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Benito Rio Avianto</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Coordinating Ministry for Economic Affairs Indonesia, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">benitorio14@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The rapid digitalization of economic activity is reshaping how value is created, delivered, and consumed. Digital platforms and data-driven business models allow economic value to be generated beyond boundaries. This creates challenges for conventional tax systems, which were designed around identifiable businesses, locations, and transactions. The availability of Big Data and artificial intelligence (AI) provides governments with new opportunities to understand economic activity, improve tax administration and fiscal governance. This study explores how Indonesia can use these capabilities to develop a digital tax framework, strengthen fiscal capacity, and support sustainable development by 2030 and beyond.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study adopts a prospective policy-analysis approach, drawing on the digital economy, Indonesia’s tax architecture, international tax reforms, and sustainable development objectives. It develops a Digital Fiscal Capture Framework connecting digital value creation with domestic resource mobilization, digital tax instruments, data-driven tax administration, international tax cooperation, institutional capacity. The study examines how Big Data and AI-enabled analytical capabilities can support taxpayer identification, risk-based compliance, monitoring digitally generated activity, and responsive fiscal decision-making. The framework is assessed against revenue potential, feasibility, efficiency, investment incentives, equity, and sustainability.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The analysis identifies a growing gap between the expansion of digital economic activity and the capacity of fiscal arrangements to capture digitally generated value. It also highlights an opportunity: digital data and AI can strengthen governments’ ability to identify economic activity, analyze compliance risks, and improve fiscal administration. It proposes a phased pathway to 2030 combining digital tax instruments, data-driven compliance, AI-enabled analytical capacity, institutional strengthening, and international cooperation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Digital taxation should not be viewed as a new revenue instrument, but as part a transformation of fiscal governance. Indonesia’s emerging approach is presented as a policy-learning case that may offer lessons for developing economies seeking to strengthen domestic resource mobilization while balancing revenue, innovation, investment, equity, and sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 143</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Native philanthropy; Local NGOs; Sustainable financing; Local development; Post-2030 development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Financing Local Development Beyond 2030: Native Philanthropy and Sustainable Financing Pathways for Local NGOs in Bangladesh</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Md Shumon zihady</u>, Mokbul Morshed Ahmad</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Asian Institute of Technology, Thailand, Thailand; <span class="paper_email">shumon.zihady90@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">As the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) approach 2030, an important question concerns not only what development priorities should follow, but how locally driven development can be sustainably financed. Local non-governmental organizations (NGOs) play an important role in translating development priorities into community-level action. However, most of them remain dependent on external development finance. Bangladesh provides an important context for examining whether locally embedded resources, particularly native philanthropy, can complement external funding and contribute to a more resilient financing architecture for sustainable local development.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This study investigates the dynamics between native philanthropy and local NGOs in Bangladesh, examining the possibilities and constraints involved in transforming locally embedded traditions and practices of giving into sustainable financing pathways for local NGO. Empirically, the study focuses on Sylhet, Bangladesh, and draws on in-depth interviews, key-informant interviews, focus group discussions, observation, and document analysis involving local NGOs, philanthropists, community and religious actors, civil society representatives, and relevant institutional stakeholders. Guided by critical realism, the research combines inductive analysis with retroduction to identify the underlying institutional, relational, and structural factors that shape the relationship between native philanthropy and local NGOs.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The study examines the financing challenges of local NGOs and the motivations and practices of native philanthropy. It also explores the gaps between philanthropic preferences and local development priorities and how these two areas can be better connected.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">By bringing the financing of local development into the post-2030 sustainability debate, the study contributes to broader scholarship on SDG localization and locally led development. It suggests that lessons from the SDG era should extend beyond identifying development goals toward the unexplored potentials of local knowledge and resources. In this pursuit, the Bangladesh case shifts attention from what sustainable development should achieve to how locally driven development can be institutionally and financially sustained beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 144</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">HPV vaccine, social media, health communication, vaccine communication</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">HPV Vaccine Communication on Social Media: A Systematic Review of Platforms, Messages and Outcomes</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Apirapee Tancharoenwong</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Srinakharinwirot University, Thailand; <span class="paper_email">apirapee@g.swu.ac.th</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">In 2023, Thailand reintroduced its nationwide Human Papillomavirus (HPV) vaccination programme for girls and young women, generating greater public discussion about HPV vaccination and cervical cancer prevention. Social media has become an important environment for health communication, providing opportunities for vaccine promotion while also facilitating misinformation, safety concerns, and negative narratives.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Based on this background, this systematic literature review examines global research on HPV vaccine communication through social media, focusing on the platforms studied, message and content characteristics, and reported communication and behavioural outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Following PRISMA guidelines, Scopus was searched for peer-reviewed journal articles published between 2016 and 2026 using terms related to HPV vaccination and social media. The search identified 66 articles. Following title, abstract, and full-text screening against predefined eligibility criteria, 41 articles were included in the final review.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings show that research has examined HPV vaccine communication across platforms including Twitter/X, Facebook, YouTube, Instagram, and TikTok. Social media performs a dual role: it supports health-information dissemination, vaccine promotion, and communication interventions, while also enabling the circulation of misinformation, vaccine-safety concerns, personal narratives, and mistrust of health authorities. Reported outcomes include knowledge, perceptions, trust, vaccine hesitancy, acceptance, vaccination intention, and uptake.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The review demonstrates that effective HPV vaccine communication requires platform-sensitive strategies that account for information sources, message characteristics, and audience responses. By strengthening vaccine confidence and equitable access to reliable health information, such strategies can support cervical cancer prevention and progress towards SDG 3 on health and well-being.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 145</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Green View Index, Housing Prices, Spatial Econometrics, XGBoost, Urban Greenery, Hedonic Pricing</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Valuing visible urban greenery for sustainable housing and urban transformation in Bengaluru</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Sayan Roy<sup>1</sup>, Arijit Dey<sup>2</sup>, Parthasarathy Ramachandran<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Indian Institute of Science, Bengaluru, India; 2:&nbsp;Mohamed Bin Zayed University of Artificial Intelligence (MBZUAI), Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates; <span class='paper_email'>sayanmath015@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Urban transformation requires better measures of environmental quality that reflect how residents experience their neighborhoods. This study examines whether visible street-level greenery is associated with apartment prices in Bengaluru, India, and evaluates its relevance for sustainable housing and urban planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The study combines 4,663 apartment transactions from January to August 2024 with Google Street View imagery, remotely sensed environmental indicators, and neighborhood accessibility measures. Visible greenery is measured using the Green View Index (GVI) derived through a Grounding DINO and Segment Anything Model (SAM) computer vision pipeline. Hedonic pricing models are estimated using Ordinary Least Squares, Spatial Error, and Spatial Lag models, while Extreme Gradient Boosting and SHapley Additive exPlanations assess nonlinear relationships and variable importance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Visible greenery is positively and significantly associated with apartment prices across the econometric specifications after controlling for housing characteristics, accessibility, and environmental conditions. In the preferred Spatial Error Model, a 0.1 increase in the GVI corresponds to an approximately 2.7% increase in apartment prices. Satellite-derived vegetation and land surface temperature also show significant relationships with housing values. Machine-learning results further demonstrate that visible greenery contributes to housing-price prediction alongside structural and neighborhood characteristics.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Pedestrian-scale greenery represents an economically valued dimension of urban environmental quality. Integrating street-level and satellite-based measures can strengthen the evidence base for evaluating urban green infrastructure and sustainable housing.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Relevance to sustainable development by and beyond 2030: The findings demonstrate how digital environmental measurement and housing-market evidence can support sustainable urban transformation. Because environmental amenities are capitalized into housing values, urban greening strategies should consider both environmental benefits and equitable distribution to avoid reinforcing spatial inequalities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 146</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Solar–biogas hybrid mini-grid; Renewable energy; Off-grid electrification; Climate change mitigation; Energy access; Coastal Bangladesh; Techno-economic feasibility; Sustainable development.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">&quot;A Solar–Biogas Hybrid Mini-Grid for Off-Grid Coastal Bangladesh: Technical, Economic, and Socio-Environmental Feasibility — A Case Study of Galachipa Upazila&quot;</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>HASIB MAHMUD</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Barishal, Bangladesh, People&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh; <span class="paper_email">hmahmud21.glm@bu.ac.bd</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Bangladesh sits in South Asia and is home to about 161 million people across roughly 29 million families, spread over 147,570 square kilometers. Around 15 million people live in rural areas, many of them far inland and out of reach of the power grid. Extending the grid to these areas is expensive and slow. (Bangladesh Bureau of Statistics [BBS], 2023)</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">At the top of the atmosphere, Earth receives about 175 petawatts of solar irradiance. Roughly 30% of it bounces back to space, and the rest is absorbed by land, clouds, and water. In Bangladesh, solar insolation ranges from 3.8 to 6.4 kWh per square meter per day, peaking in March and April and dropping to its lowest in December and January. That range makes solar power a realistic option for the country. (NASA POWER, 2024; Das et al., 2020).Bangladesh is largely an agriculture-based, temperate country with good conditions for poultry farming and dairy cattle, which means a steady supply of cow dung and poultry droppings, the starting material for biogas. Poultry farming has long been a self-sustaining part of rural economies in many developing countries, and Bangladesh is no exception: the sector has grown at an average rate of 20 percent a year over the last two decades, supporting rural livelihoods and giving families a path to higher income. (Rahman et al., 2018; Saha et al., 2025)</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 147</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Coastal area, disaster risk, risk analysis, risk reduction, Galachipa Upazila</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Prioritizing Climate-Induced Hazards in a Vulnerable Coastal Delta: Evidence from Galachipa Upazila, Southern Bangladesh</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>HASIB MAHMUD</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Barishal, Bangladesh, People&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh; <span class="paper_email">hmahmud21.glm@bu.ac.bd</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The coastal area of Bangladesh is highly vulnerable to climate change and climate-induced natural hazards. The coastal community is suffering from different forms of natural hazards such as floods, tropical cyclones, tornadoes, tidal surges, droughts, and river erosion almost annually. The main aim of the study was to identify, analyze, and prioritize the dominating climate-induced hazards of the study area. The research was carried out in Galachipa Upazila of Patuakhali District of the southern coastal region of Bangladesh. Data were collected through direct observations, key informant interviews, focus group discussions, and literature review.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The research establishes the hazards&#39; ranking according to their level of impact and severity as perceived. Salinity is ranked number one, reflecting high risk. Close behind are Flood, Storm Surge, and Storm winds, which are ranked two, three, and four, respectively. River bank erosion and high tides are ranked moderate at five and six, respectively, in light of their possibilities to inflict damage, but to a lesser degree. Irregular rainfall, Pest attack and cyclones are ranked middle level of severity and positioned at seven, eight, and nine, respectively. At the rear is the Hail storm at ten.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The research recommends that the same disaster risks be taken into account in planning and decision-making for any development project for disaster risk reduction. In addition, the research can be used as a guideline for prioritizing mitigation and preparedness measures and resource allocation to address ranked higher hazards of greater consequence to the community or environment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 148</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Traditional Security, Human Security, Health Security, Sustainable Development Goal 3, India</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Traditional Security to Human Security: A Case Study of SDG-3 in India</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jay Koche</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Central University of Punjab, India; <span class="paper_email">jaykoche23@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">India’s vision of good health goes beyond the absence of disease; it encompasses physical, mental, and social well-being for all. In India, the paradigmatic shift from traditional security to human security is most clearly reflected in the recognition of health as a strategic domain, where pandemics, healthcare inequities, and climate-sensitive diseases pose greater threats to human survival than conventional military dangers. Yet the literature on these two areas remains disconnected: human security scholarship treats health as one of seven core dimensions but rarely translates this into indicator-level analysis, while India’s SDG-3 policy literature tracks health outcomes in detail without framing them as security concerns. We aimed to critically assess India’s progress toward SDG-3 targets between 2015 and 2025 through a human security framework, and to identify the structural gaps in healthcare financing, workforce availability, and inter-state disparities most likely to prevent India from meeting its 2030 health security commitments. SDG 3 addresses key health issues, including reproductive, maternal, and child health, as well as communicable and non-communicable diseases. SDG 3 helps identify priority areas and targets, and design evidence-based policies and interventions to effectively achieve the other SDGs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 149</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change adaptation, Seaports, Airports, Transport resilience, Critical infrastructure</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate change adaptation for seaports and airports</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mark Ching-Pong Poo</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Liverpool Hope University, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">pooc@hope.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Seaports and airports are critical nodes in global transport and supply chain networks, yet their fixed locations, complex infrastructure systems, and dependence on interconnected transport networks make them increasingly vulnerable to climate-related hazards. Rising temperatures, sea-level rise, extreme precipitation, flooding, storms, and other climate extremes can disrupt infrastructure, operations, accessibility, and the wider economic systems that depend on these transport hubs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation synthesises insights from previous research on climate risk, resilience, and adaptation in major transport infrastructure, with a particular focus on seaports and airports. It examines the similarities and differences in climate exposure, operational vulnerability, and adaptation requirements across the two sectors. Particular attention is given to how climate hazards interact with infrastructure characteristics, operational dependencies, and existing resilience capacity, highlighting the need to move beyond hazard-specific assessments towards integrated and system-level approaches.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation reviews emerging approaches for assessing climate risks and supporting adaptation decision-making, including expert-based assessment, multi-criteria decision analysis, probabilistic risk modelling, and scenario-based evaluation. It also discusses a range of adaptation strategies, from physical infrastructure protection and drainage enhancement to operational preparedness, monitoring, redundancy, and long-term planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings highlight that effective climate adaptation requires not only stronger physical infrastructure but also adaptive management, cross-sector coordination, and consideration of interconnected transport and supply chain networks. By comparing seaports and airports, the presentation identifies transferable lessons between the two sectors and proposes directions for developing more integrated, evidence-based, and forward-looking climate resilience strategies for critical transport infrastructure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 150</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">nexus approaches, sustainable resource management, food system transformation, water resilience, multi-level governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Governing the food–water–energy nexus: Community resilience in Bali and lessons for European water policy</p>
<p class="paper_author"> I Gusti Ngurah Krisna Dana</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Warmadewa, Indonesia; <span class='paper_email'>ngurahkrisnadana@warmadewa.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Water scarcity increasingly exposes the limits of sectoral approaches to sustainability because pressures on water availability simultaneously affect food production, energy use, livelihoods, and institutional resilience. This study examines how community-based water governance shapes resilience within the food–water–energy nexus in Bali, Indonesia, and considers its relevance to emerging European water-resilience policy. It addresses a gap in nexus scholarship by shifting attention from technical resource optimisation toward the institutional processes through which competing demands are negotiated and coordinated. The research employs a qualitative case-study design based on semi-structured interviews with community water managers and users, field observations, local institutional documents, and policy analysis of the European Water Resilience Strategy and the Water Framework Directive. The findings show that nexus resilience depends not only on resource availability, but also on institutional capacity to allocate water, distribute costs and benefits, adapt rules, coordinate infrastructure, and maintain legitimacy under environmental and socioeconomic pressure. Community-based arrangements can strengthen resilience through collective management and locally legitimate allocation, but their effectiveness weakens when scarcity, infrastructure dependence, and external resource competition exceed local institutional and financial capacity. These findings resonate with the European Union’s shift toward restoring the water cycle, improving water efficiency, strengthening governance, and integrating water resilience with wider economic and security objectives. The study argues that European water policy can benefit from greater attention to locally embedded governance, while the Bali case illustrates how broader policy frameworks can support community institutions without displacing them. By and beyond 2030, resilient food–water–energy systems will require multi-level governance that combines resource efficiency, infrastructure investment, equitable allocation, institutional adaptability, and meaningful local participation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 151</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food security; smallholder farmers; resilience; agricultural transformation; Benin.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Agroecological Transition and Food Security in Benin: Lessons for Sustainable Agricultural Transformation beyond 2030</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>MAANZOU BOUKARI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Laboratory for Research on Innovation for Agricultural Development (LRIDA), Benin; <span class="paper_email">bmaanzou@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Agroecological transition offers a pathway for improving food security, strengthening farming-system resilience, and advancing sustainable agricultural transformation. However, adoption remains limited among smallholder farmers due to constraints related to technical knowledge, appropriate inputs, markets, finance, and institutional support. Understanding these dynamics is essential for informing agricultural policies and shaping sustainable transformation beyond 2030.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines the relationship between agroecological transition and food security among smallholder farmers in the Donga Department of Benin, focusing on Djougou, Ouaké, and Copargo. A mixed-methods approach combining household surveys, semi-structured interviews, and field observations was used to compare conventional, transitional, and agroecological farming systems. Descriptive statistics, chi-square tests, multivariate analysis, and qualitative content analysis were used to assess differences in agricultural practices and food-security outcomes.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Preliminary results indicate that <strong>18.0%</strong> of farmers are engaged in agroecological farming, <strong>36.5%</strong> are in transition, and <strong>45.5%</strong> remain predominantly conventional. Crop diversification is reported by <strong>64.8%</strong> of agroecological farmers, compared with <strong>48.6%</strong> of transitional and <strong>34.7%</strong> of conventional farmers. Organic fertilizers and locally available soil-fertility resources are used by <strong>69.4%</strong>, <strong>46.8%</strong>, and <strong>27.9%</strong> of farmers, respectively. Relatively favourable food-security conditions are reported by <strong>71.2%</strong> of agroecological, <strong>59.6%</strong> of transitional, and <strong>48.3%</strong> of conventional farmers. Key constraints include limited technical support (<strong>56.2%</strong>), insufficient agroecological inputs (<strong>48.7%</strong>), and limited remunerative markets (<strong>44.5%</strong>).</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The findings suggest that agroecological transition can contribute to more diversified, resilient, and food-secure farming systems, while highlighting the importance of institutional, technical, and market conditions for accelerating sustainable agricultural transformation beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 152</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food safety, well-being, pesticide exposure, sustainable food systems, Benin.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Agroecology, Food Safety and Well-being: Rethinking Sustainable Food Systems for Healthier Communities in Benin</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>MAANZOU BOUKARI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Laboratory for Research on Innovation for Agricultural Development (LRIDA), Benin; <span class="paper_email">bmaanzou@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Agroecological transition is increasingly recognized as a pathway towards more sustainable and healthier food systems. Beyond environmental benefits, agroecological practices can contribute to reducing exposure to synthetic pesticides, improving food safety, supporting dietary diversity, and strengthening household well-being. However, the links between agricultural production practices, food safety, nutrition, and well-being remain insufficiently documented in Benin, particularly within rapidly changing urban and coastal food systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines the contribution of agroecological practices to food safety and household well-being in Benin, focusing on Houéyogbé, Cotonou, and Grand-Popo. A mixed-methods approach combining household surveys, semi-structured interviews, and field observations was used to assess production practices, pesticide use, food-safety practices, dietary diversity, and perceived well-being. Descriptive statistics, chi-square tests, and qualitative content analysis were used to compare agroecological and conventional production systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The results indicate that 18.0% of surveyed producers were engaged in agroecological production, while the majority relied on conventional or mixed practices. Among agroecological producers, 67.4% reported avoiding or significantly reducing synthetic pesticide use, compared with 32.6% who continued to use them. Furthermore, 61.8% of agroecological producers reported respecting recommended pre-harvest intervals, compared with 38.5% of conventional producers. On the consumer side, 72.3% perceived agroecologically produced food as safer, while 58.7% reported paying greater attention to the origin and production practices of food. Household dietary diversity was reported by 64.2% of households associated with agroecological production, compared with 45.8% among conventional production systems. In addition, 69.5% of respondents associated agroecological practices with improved household well-being.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings suggest that agroecological transition can contribute simultaneously to safer food, reduced pesticide exposure, improved dietary diversity, and enhanced well-being. Strengthening farmer training, consumer awareness, local markets, and institutional support could therefore help position agroecology as an important pathway for healthier and more sustainable food systems in Benin.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 153</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food system resilience, digitalization, agricultural innovation, digital inclusion, West Africa.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digital Agriculture and Food System Resilience: Opportunities and Challenges for Smallholder Farmers in West Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>MAANZOU BOUKARI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Laboratory for Research on Innovation for Agricultural Development (LRIDA), Benin; <span class="paper_email">bmaanzou@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Digital agriculture is increasingly recognized as a pathway for strengthening the resilience, productivity, and inclusiveness of food systems in West Africa. Mobile phones, digital advisory services, weather information platforms, digital marketplaces, and mobile financial services can improve farmers’ access to information, markets, inputs, and financial resources. However, unequal access to digital technologies, limited connectivity, and low digital literacy remain major constraints.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines the opportunities and challenges associated with digital agriculture among smallholder farmers in Benin, Nigeria, and Togo. A mixed-methods approach combining household surveys, semi-structured interviews, and a review of digital agricultural services was used to assess farmers’ use of digital technologies for agricultural information, climate information, market access, financial services, and farm management. Descriptive statistics and comparative analysis were used to identify patterns of digital adoption and key constraints.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The results indicate that 22.0% of smallholder farmers use digital technologies for agricultural purposes. Among these users, 61.5% access agricultural information, 52.8% use digital tools for market information, 47.6% access weather and climate information, and 35.4% use digital financial services for agricultural transactions. Furthermore, 58.7% report that digital tools have improved their farm-level decision-making. However, 51.9% identify limited digital literacy, 46.3% poor internet or mobile network connectivity, and 39.7% the cost of smartphones, data, or digital services as major barriers.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings highlight the potential of digital agriculture to strengthen food system resilience. However, inclusive digital transformation requires improved rural connectivity, digital literacy, affordable services, farmer-centred innovation, and supportive institutional frameworks across West Africa.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 154</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Eco-Innovation Adoption, Drivers, Barriers, Sustainable Business Outcomes, Manufacturing Firms, Pakistan</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Drivers, barriers, and outcomes of eco-innovation: a qualitative investigation of manufacturing industries in Pakistan</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sadia Shaukat</u>, Zainab Rehman, Haroon Rasheed</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Sargodha, Pakistan; <span class="paper_email">sadia.shaukat@uos.edu.pk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The purpose of this study is to identify key drivers and barriers influencing the adoption of eco-innovation and to explore its contribution to sustainable business outcomes across manufacturing sector of Pakistan by adopting qualitative research approach. Data was collected through semi-structured interviews with key informants from 15 of manufacturing companies actively engaged in eco-innovation practices.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The main insights of this study are three fold. First, companies seem to be primarily influenced by external factors in implementing eco-innovation. Among the internal drivers, the most emphasis has been placed on cost saving and cost reduction, cleaner technology, organizational capacities and cultural legacy, while the most important external drivers are customer green demand, competitive pressure, and compliance with international standards. Secondly, barriers to eco-innovation are also derived from external and internal pressures. Cost (High upfront and capital cost), lack of managerial commitment and limited resource availability prevail within internal barriers, while legislation, inadequate regulatory and government support, weak absorptive capacity and market uncertainty seems to be the most commonly experienced external barriers. finally, the outcomes of eco-innovation that firms most often experience are improved resource efficiency, reduced operational costs, and adoption of greener production technologies are coherent with reported outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusion- The study concludes that successful implementation of eco-innovation in Pakistan’s manufacturing sector depends on addressing both external institutional and market conditions as well as internal firm-level constraints. The result underscores the need for more institutional and regulatory support, improved organizational capabilities, investment in technical skills, and increased managerial commitment to eco-innovation, all of which have practical implications for policymakers, practitioners, industry leaders and manufacturing firms. Overall, the study adds to the growing literature on eco-innovation by providing empirical insights into eco-innovation drivers, barriers and sustainable business outcomes within the framework of developing economy.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 155</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Social Capital, Alternative Adaptive Mechanisms, Development For Profit, Collective Resilience, Social Contracts, Alternative Governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Integrated Social Capital and Functional Community Contracts: Introducing Growth Cooperatives as Societal Foundations for Collective Well-Being and Resilience in Climate-Vulnerable Bangladesh</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Md Magfi Reza Siddique</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Daahuk- Research &amp; Development organisation., Bangladesh, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class="paper_email">siddique1237@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This research examines how climate-vulnerable communities can build locally governed institutions for collective well-being, social harmony, and resilient growth without depending primarily on donations, debt-based microfinance, or externally prescribed solutions. It investigates whether purpose-oriented community contracts can convert existing trust, knowledge, relationships, aspirations, and reciprocal responsibilities into durable cooperative institutions, and whether such institutions can respond differently to distinct social, cultural, and ecological contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study adopts a comparative, practice-based and participatory research design across two contrasting landscapes of Bangladesh: Tanguar Haor, a distinctive wetland ecosystem that supports fishing, boating, tourism, and seasonal agriculture, and the lower Teesta River basin in Lalmonirhat, characterised by river dynamics, char settlements, and seasonal cropland. Through community consultations, participatory observation, institutional mapping, and case-based comparison, the research examines community aspirations, cultural and livelihood patterns, existing relationships, locally negotiated contracts, cooperative purposes, and decision-making practices. This design enables comparison of how a common institutional approach is adapted to different landscapes and community realities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Emerging Field Findings and Innovation:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Field evidence indicates an emerging innovation: Growth Cooperatives, structured as non-credit and purpose-oriented community institutions, can transform informal social relationships into accountable systems of collective action. Instead of distributing external resources, the model begins with community-defined purposes and contracts that establish responsibilities, mutual commitments, resource mobilisation, leadership, and collective benefit. Early experience across the two landscapes suggests that this approach can strengthen local organisation while supporting economic initiative, mutual support, dignity, participation, and social peace and harmony.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Implications:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The research proposes Growth Cooperatives as an alternative institutional pathway for climate-vulnerable communities—one that treats communities as producers of their own social and economic infrastructure rather than passive recipients of assistance. It offers a transferable, landscape-sensitive model for strengthening collective well-being and resilience through locally generated cooperation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 156</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Economic growth; Information access; Spatial disparities; Factor mobility; China</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Scaling Information Access: Evidence from a Nationwide Digital Policy in China</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Shilong Zhao<sup>1</sup>, <u>Dominik Naeher</u><sup>2</sup>, Juan Liu<sup>3</sup>, Junqiao Ma<sup>4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Georgia, USA; 2:&nbsp;University of Goettingen, Germany; 3:&nbsp;Renmin University, China; 4:&nbsp;Huazhong Agricultural University, China; <span class="paper_email">dnaeher@uni-goettingen.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Access to information, particularly in digital form, is increasingly recognized as a driver of economic development, yet the evidence comes mostly from small-scale studies whose scalability to national policy remains unknown. We address this gap by exploiting the staggered rollout of China’s Information Access to Villages and Households (IAVH) program across counties. Using a panel of 17,172 county-year observations for 2009–2020 and a difference-in-differences design, we estimate the effect of IAVH on rural economic activity, proxied by rural nighttime light intensity isolated from satellite imagery. IAVH adoption is associated with a 1.5% increase in rural nighttime light intensity, and the estimate is robust across specifications. The effect is larger in counties with (a) deeper information barriers, reflected in poorer transport accessibility and greater administrative and cultural fragmentation, and (b) more uneven spatial endowments, a pattern suggesting that IAVH narrows rather than widens spatial disparities. Individual-level panel evidence is consistent with IAVH lowering the transaction costs that fragment rural land, labor, and capital markets, with the association ending at each market’s non-informational boundary. Because IAVH was implemented after broadband coverage was largely in place, the findings indicate that the marginal benefit of a large-scale digital policy depends not only on how much connectivity it adds but also on whether it converts existing connectivity into use.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 157</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.3 AI and big data for decision-making and sustainable governance as a supporting decision tool</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital Information Design, E-Commerce, Information processing, Welfare effects</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Recommend or Simplify? Digital Information Design with Cross-Category Spillovers</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Dominik Naeher</u><sup>1</sup>, Sergey Turlo<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Goettingen, Germany; 2:&nbsp;Goethe University Frankfurt, Germany; <span class="paper_email">dnaeher@uni-goettingen.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Digital markets are increasingly shaped by AI-based technologies that curate and prioritize information. Firms use these tools to facilitate product search and steer consumer attention, yet their effects remain poorly understood when consumers choose across multiple product categories. We develop a theoretical framework of a multi-category seller facing buyers with heterogeneous information-processing capacities, contrasting two instruments: simplification, which reduces learning costs, and recommendation, which increases the share of fully informed buyers. Category-specific information provision generates cross-category attention and pricing spillovers and can induce non-monotonic attention responses. The two instruments have sharply different effects on prices and market coverage. Simplification typically raises willingness to pay and prices within a pricing regime, reducing buyer surplus while increasing total welfare; at discrete thresholds, however, it can expand market coverage, lower prices, and raise buyer surplus. Recommendation instead leaves prices unchanged within regimes and typically benefits buyers, but can contract market coverage at discrete thresholds, causing prices to jump and both buyer surplus and total welfare to fall. Our results offer new insights for the design and regulation of information systems and highlight potential risks of AI-driven information provision in multi-category markets.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 158</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Psychologically Sustainable Agency; Within-Person Dynamics; Pro-Environmental Behaviour; Environmental Engagement; Behavioural Recovery</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Engagement: Within-Person Dynamics of Environmental Action, Recovery, and Psychologically Sustainable Agency</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Deepu Barik</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Nalanda University, Rajgir, India; <span class="paper_email">deepu.202612008@nalandauniv.edu.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Environmental engagement is often studied as a relatively stable characteristic, with research asking why some individuals are more environmentally concerned or behaviourally active than others. This between-person perspective, however, leaves an important temporal question unresolved: <strong>how does environmental engagement change within the same person, and what enables it to persist, decline, or return after disengagement?</strong> I approach pro-environmental behaviour as a dynamic trajectory rather than a fixed individual attribute.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This research examines whether within-person changes in psychological agency and adaptive capacity predict subsequent environmental engagement, while accounting for prior behaviour and stable differences between individuals. I further propose a conditional model in which perceived climate threat is processed through emotional and coping responses that shape agency, willingness to act, perceived feasibility, and subsequent engagement. The focus therefore shifts from whether people are environmentally engaged to the <strong>conditions under which engagement is maintained, interrupted, and recovered</strong>.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Repeated National Climate Action Survey data will provide the primary longitudinal basis for examining these trajectories. Where measures permit, I will distinguish psychologically sustainable persistence from persistence accompanied by substantial emotional or psychological costs. Cross-national robustness will be assessed through the International Climate Psychology Collaboration, while the Bath dataset will provide an India-focused and broader Global-South perspective.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">I develop <strong>Psychologically Sustainable Agency (PSA)</strong> as a framework describing the capacity to remain responsive to environmental threats while preserving efficacy, emotional functioning, and the psychological resources required to continue or recover constructive action.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">By shifting the analytical unit from <strong>the engaged individual to the changing trajectory of engagement</strong>, this research seeks to explain not only why environmental action lasts, but also why it falters  and how it can be renewed.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 159</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">digital impression management; institutional theory; symbolic management; sustainability; analyst coverage; ownership balance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">When Symbolic Responses Become Consequential: Digital Impression Management and Corporate Sustainability</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Kesen Zhang</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Nantong University, China; <span class="paper_email">zhksen@163.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Organizations face pressure to demonstrate digital transformation, yet their digital representations may outpace substantive action. We examine whether this divergence—digital impression management (DIM)—affects corporate sustainability. Drawing on institutional theory, we theorize that symbolic digital responses translate into sustainability outcomes through external evaluation, resource access, and organizational action. We further distinguish two governance conditions: external supervision (analyst coverage breadth) and internal governance (ownership balance). Using a longitudinal sample of Chinese listed firms, we find that DIM is associated with lower energy consumption, mediated through external evaluation, resource access, and organizational action. The resource-access pathway accounts for the largest share of the total effect. Broader analyst coverage weakens, whereas greater ownership balance strengthens, the DIM–sustainability relationship. By shifting attention from whether symbolic responses are decoupled from substantive practices to how such responses become consequential, this study extends institutional research on decoupling and symbolic management. More broadly, the findings suggest that the consequences of digital transformation arise not only from what firms substantively do, but also from how their digital actions are represented, evaluated, and translated into organizational outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 160</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.2 Environmental health and emerging pollutants, including regulatory and governance responses, and policy translation of exposure science</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Peatlands, Integrated Agriculture-Aquaculture System, Income</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">INTEGRATED AGRICULTURE-AQUACULTURE SYSTEM AS AN ALTERNATIVE TECHNOLOGY TO IMPROVING SMALL-SCALE FOOD PRODUCTION IN LEYTE SAB-A BASIN PEATLAND, EASTERN VISAYAS, PHILIPPINES</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Brandon Briggs Silvano</u><sup>1</sup>, Quenstein Lauzon<sup>2</sup>, Mary Ann Madeja<sup>2</sup>, Chrizhane Domingo<sup>2</sup>, Shereen Merro<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Asian Institute of Technology, Thailand; 2:&nbsp;Visayas State University Tolosa, Philippines; <span class="paper_email">st127067@ait.asia</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Leyte Sab-a Basin Peatland, the second-largest peatland in the Philippines and the largest water catchment basin in Eastern Visayas, represents a critical ecosystem of ecological and socio-economic importance. Encompassing approximately 3,088 ha, of which about 1,740 ha (44%) have been reclaimed for agriculture, the basin supports predominantly low-income households engaged in agriculture and small-scale aquaculture as sources of food and supplemental livelihood. However, low production among fishpond operators in the municipalities of Alangalang, Santa Fe, and San Miguel, Leyte, is constrained by limited financial resources, remoteness from urban centers, inadequate knowledge of resource utilization, limited awareness of nature-based production technologies, and increasing climate-related pressures. This study introduces the Integrated Agriculture–Aquaculture System (IAAS), a resource-efficient production model that integrates aquaculture with crop or livestock production to maximize the productive use of available land and water resources while maintaining peatland integrity. The system utilizes locally available and peat-adapted plant and animal commodities, providing opportunities to enhance household food production, diversify income sources, and improve resilience to climate-related production risks. In this context, IAAS contributes particularly to SDG 2 (Zero Hunger) and SDG 13 (Climate Action) by strengthening local food production and promoting climate-responsive livelihood strategies while supporting responsible resource utilization. The technology introduction, initiated in November 2024, followed a systematic process involving site assessment, targeted capacity building, validation of site suitability, and phased implementation, complemented by scientific interventions to improve productivity and system sustainability. To date, 15 participating fishpond operators have adopted IAAS configurations, including rice–fish, vegetable–fish, root crop–fish, fruit–fish, and livestock–fish integrations. Preliminary outcomes indicate improved household food security and diversified income opportunities. The study aims to establish an evidence-based, locally adaptable, and environmentally responsible production model for sustainable livelihood development within the Leyte Sab-a Basin Peatland.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 161</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Indigenous knowledge, Equity, Gender, Local governance, Community resilience</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Whose knowledge is recognized and sustained? Indigenous Knowledge, Women Artisan and Systematic Recognition in Local Sustainability Governance in Nepal.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Nistha Thapa Shrestha</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kathmandu University; <span class='paper_email'>nistha_msd2022@kusoed.edu.np</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Indigenous Knowledge systems are increasingly recognized as important for sustainable and resilient communities, and yet the institutional processes to recognise, support and sustain them remain uneven. This paper examines how local government recognition of Indigenous Knowledge and the contribution of women who sustain it in the Prajapati community of the historic town of Madhyapur Thimi, Nepal, as a case study. It asks whose knowledge and labour are recognized within the local governance, how women’s contribution as a knowledge holder of indigenous pottery are perceived and recognized. Additionally, what these patterns of recognition reveal about equity and community resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on a qualitative interpretive case study, the research analyses municipal annual planning, policy and budget documents, alongside interviews with municipal representatives, focus groups with community members and women potters. Reflective thematic analysis is used to examine how indigenous pottery is framed within local government priorities and how the contribution of women artisans are represented.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings identify a gap between symbolic representation of pottery as part of Madhyapur Thimi’s heritage and its institutional recognition requiring systematic and sustained support. Similarly,while pottery remains closely interconnected to communities&#39; identities, intergenerational knowledge, and livelihood, the labor through which women contribute are frequently embedded within households and support the production process rather than explicit recognition in local governance. This creates an important equity question: while cultural practices may be celebrated as heritage but the knowledge holders and gendered labour who are sustaining them remain insufficiently visible in development plans.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that community resilience cannot be understood merely as the preservation of cultural practices. It depends on whether the governance system recognizes the knowledge, labor, and agency of the communities sustaining them. It contributes to the debate on sustainable transformation, through understanding, whose knowledge is visible, and whose contribution counts in local sustainability processes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 162</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Fish Protein Concentrate, Hedonic scale</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sensory Characteristics of Cookies Formulated with Fish Protein Concentrate Powder Produced from Nile Tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus Linnaeus, 1758)</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Brandon Briggs Silvano<sup>1</sup>, Justine Cerena<sup>2</sup>, Rolaine Cabillo<sup>2</sup>, Johndie Eyana<sup>2</sup>, Daniel Solano<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Asian Institute of Technology, Philippines; 2:&nbsp;Visayas State University Tolosa; <span class='paper_email'>st127067@ait.asia</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">In Leyte, Eastern Visayas, Philippines, a significant portion of small and large-scale fish pens and fishponds culture Nile Tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus Linnaeus, 1758). However, due to unsustainable aquaculture practices which include poor knowledge on tilapia culture breeding practices resulting to unintentional inbreeding, concerns have arisen regarding the poor quality of Nile Tilapia produced in terms of size and weight. To utilize this affected species, this study was conducted to evaluate the sensory characteristics of the traditional cookies supplemented with fish protein concentrate (FPC) extracted from Nile Tilapia. A total of four treatment samples with varying FPC levels were evaluated in terms of color, aroma, taste, texture, and overall acceptability using the 9-point Hedonic Scale. Treatment 2, the cookie formulation containing 10% FPC, received the highest acceptability score of 6.96, corresponding to a &quot;like moderately&quot; rating. Acceptability scores across all treatments ranged from 6.45 to 6.94, falling within the &quot;like slightly&quot; to &quot;like moderately&quot; categories in the Hedonic scale. A two-way ANOVA revealed significant interactions for sensory attributes. Consumer preference testing further demonstrated that the majority of evaluators favored FPC-enriched cookies over commercially available options, indicating potential market opportunity. Therefore, this study demonstrated the potential for developing fishery products from aquaculture waste, such as Nile Tilapia produced from unintentional inbreeding practices, thereby enhancing the value of the resource that would otherwise be discarded or used for low-value purposes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 163</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Carbon emission disclosure, Firm profitability, ESG reporting, Emerging markets</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Does Carbon Emission Disclosure Influence the Profitability of Listed Firms in Thailand?</p>
<p class="paper_author"> CHANON THONGTAI, MARISA PHAKPHUM</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Faculty of Economics, Thailand; <span class='paper_email'>chanon.th@chula.ac.th</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Stock Exchange of Thailand (SET) has been encouraging companies to publicly disclose sustainability information with GRI standards including greenhouse gas emissions since year 2021 under voluntary period, with a compulsory phase planned in 2030. Certain are under impression that doing so can damage their profitability due to time and costs of collecting data, reporting, and validating. This study examines whether the disclosure of carbon emission information affects the profitability of companies listed in SET by collecting ESG statistics, including scope-1 and scope-2 emissions data from sustainability reports as well as the profitability data from their official financial reports. Studied companies are in the energy, property and construction, and industrial sectors. Study period is during 2021-2025 subject to availability of data and sources. Panel regression models with firm fixed effects were employed to analyze the influence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The regression results indicate that carbon emission disclosure has negligible effect on profitability of the studied firms among Thai listed companies. This implies that the negative influence on profitability in terms of costs of sustainability reporting is not scientifically observed in practice. It can also be regarded that market participants may consider incorporate ESG disclosure practice and include in their investment decisions, but some firms might not fully incorporate unless the benefits of disclosure balance outbalance the costs. As Thailand mostly provide primary products as a downstream within the global supply chain, it is essential for Thai firms to align their practices with international sustainability and disclosure standards. Such alignment not only enhances their competitiveness and credibility in global trade but also ensures that businesses should create value not only for their shareholders but for all stakeholders—employees, suppliers, customers, partners, the local community.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 164</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Agency; double literacy; ProSocial AI; Hybrid intelligence</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond the SDGs: Hybrid Development Goals for an Age of Natural and Artificial Intelligence.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Cornelia Walther</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Sunway University, Malaysia; <span class="paper_email">corneliaw@sunway.edu.my</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective. The Sustainable Development Goals were designed for a world in which human institutions remained the principal agents of development. That premise is changing. Artificial intelligence increasingly influences how knowledge is produced, decisions are made, resources are allocated and behaviour is shaped. As the international community prepares for the post-2030 agenda, this paper proposes Hybrid Development Goals (HDGs) as a framework for development in societies increasingly shaped by interaction between natural and artificial intelligence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods. The paper combines systems thinking, sustainability governance, human-AI interaction and emerging evidence on cognitive offloading and agency decay. It examines where the SDG architecture becomes insufficient when AI is treated mainly as an instrument for accelerating existing targets. The proposed HDG framework is operationalised through Double Literacy, which combines technological literacy with critical reasoning, judgement and responsible action, and ProSocial AI, which evaluates technological choices across Purpose, People, Prosperity and Planet.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results. The analysis identifies human agency as a missing dimension of contemporary sustainability governance. AI can expand analytical capacity, access to knowledge and coordination while also shifting cognitive effort, judgement and decision-making away from individuals and institutions. Development metrics must therefore assess not only outcomes, but also the quality of human-AI relationships: who retains meaningful agency, whose knowledge shapes decisions, how technological systems affect future generations, and whether they strengthen social and planetary resilience. This reframes agency preservation as an enabling condition for sustainable development in increasingly hybrid societies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions. The post-2030 agenda requires goals designed for a hybrid age. Hybrid Development Goals would extend sustainable development beyond what societies achieve to include how human and artificial intelligence interact in achieving it. Their purpose is to ensure that technological capability expands alongside human agency, social legitimacy and planetary responsibility.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 165</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Home-based beauty service, Informal Economy, LGBTQIA+, Intersectionality Theory, Rational Choice Theory</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">“Navigating the Intersection of Gender Identity in the Informal Economy: Exploring the Case of Gay Men Providing Home-based Beauty Services</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sunshine Enaguas</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Asian Institute of Technology, Davao del Norte State College; <span class="paper_email">enaguassunshine@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This study explored the experiences of gay men who provide home-based hair,</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">makeup, and nail services in the informal economy in Davao City. Using a</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">qualitative case study design, data were gathered through in-depth interviews</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">with seven gay men engaged in home-based beauty services for at least five years.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Guided by an integrated framework of Intersectionality Theory and Rational</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Choice Theory, the study examined the factors influencing their entry into the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">field, challenges and risks encountered, and how economic, social, and cultural</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">factors intersect with their social identities. It also explored the rational coping</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">strategies they employed to navigate the precarious nature of informal work. The</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">thematic analysis revealed that gay men&#39;s engagement in the home-based beauty</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">work is shape by both economic practicality and social benefits, such as passion</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and identity. They encountered multiple challenges, including economic</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">insecurity, toxic competition, and the emotional labor of managing difficult clients.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">They are expose to economic, emotional, and physical risk while working in the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">field. These challenges and risks intersect with poverty, limited education, gender</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">identity and discrimination, and family responsibilities that lead to participants</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">normalizing their vulnerabilities, while weighing the benefits and costs of</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">informal work. To cope with the challenges and risks, they maintain</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">professionalism, regulate their emotions, develop their skills, build referrals, and</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">expand their social media advertising skill. This study contributes to the sociology</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">of labor in the informal economy, and fills a gap in the literature by focusing on</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">the marginalization, vulnerabilities, and agency produced by the intersection of</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">LGBTQIA+ social identities and informal labor conditions. Ultimately, it challenges the inclusivity of current global frameworks, offering a vital critique for the post-2030 sustainability agenda regarding &quot;decent work&quot; and social equity for marginalized informal workers.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 166</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Social Inequality, Multidimensional Exclusion, Community Sustainability, Marginalized Communities</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Social Inequalities and Multidimensional Exclusion Undermine the Sustainability of Local Communities in Developing and Underdeveloped Countries</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Mushfiqur Rahman Bayzid</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Dhaka, Bangladesh, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class='paper_email'>mushfiqurrhamanbayzid@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The sustainability of local communities refers to their ability to survive, adapt, and develop while ensuring sustainable social, political, and economic conditions for present and future generations. Despite global commitments to equality and justice, many communities in developing and underdeveloped countries continue to experience persistent social inequalities and multidimensional forms of exclusion. These challenges are particularly evident across South Asia and Africa where they undermine both community sustainability and national development. This study asks: how do social inequalities and multidimensional forms of exclusion undermine the sustainability of marginalized local communities in Bangladesh? Marginalized communities live in remote rural areas, riverine regions, coastal zones, hill tracts, and urban slums. They have limited access to education, healthcare, public services, and economic opportunities and depend largely on low-income traditional occupations including fishing, sanitation work, tea garden labor, jhum cultivation, and begging. Persistent poverty forces many children into labor, restricting their education and future opportunities. Consequently, these communities remain excluded from political participation, social decision-making, and public policy. Poor health awareness, inadequate healthcare, and malnutrition further reinforce their vulnerability, while weak economic structures and insufficient policy support leave their basic needs unmet. This study employs a qualitative research design based on semi-structured interviews with members of the River Gypsy (Bede) community, tea garden workers, coastal communities, Hill Tracts communities, and urban slum dwellers. The research also incorporates a qualitative review of reports published in Prothom Alo and The Daily Star. The findings demonstrate that social inequality and economic, social, and political exclusion are the principal barriers to the sustainability of local communities. The study contributes to the sociology of local communities by providing empirical evidence from Bangladesh and argues that targeted, inclusive, and context sensitive public policies are essential for overcoming structural barriers and promoting sustainable local community development in developing and underdeveloped countries.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 167</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Vehicle-to-grid, techno-economic analysis, passenger vehicle</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Commercialization of vehicle-to-grid technology across passenger vehicles requires localized policies</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Xin Sun</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Groningen, Netherlands, The; <span class='paper_email'>xin.sun@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Vehicle-to-grid (V2G) technology could transform electric vehicles into distributed energy storage resources, but its commercial viability remains highly heterogeneous across regions and poorly understood. Here we develop a bottom-up techno-economic framework to evaluate V2G commercialization across 337 Chinese cities, incorporating city-level differences in electric passenger vehicle markets, driving behavior, climate, electricity tariffs, and ancillary service compensation. The levelized cost of storage estimated ranges from 1.2–6.1 CNY/kWh for frequency regulation and 1.1–8.1 CNY/kWh for peak shaving and valley filling, while benefit-cost ratios vary from 9–22% and 15–136%, respectively, meaning V2G in most cities remain unprofitable. Achieving profitability would require frequency regulation subsidies to increase by 4.7–22.3 times and the time-of-use price spreads to increase by 1.0–45.1 times. Our analysis shows that V2G commercialization is most favorable in southeastern China for large battery electric vehicles, highlighting the importance of localized rather than uniform national policies for accelerating V2G deployment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 168</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.1 Modeling Earth system interactions, including climate impacts on development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Snowfall, extreme, climate change, Greenland</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Shifting Northern Hemisphere snowfall extremes and their physical drivers</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Richard Bintanja</u><sup>1</sup>, Nick Romein<sup>2</sup>, Eveline van der Linden<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;RUG, Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;TU Delft, Netherlands; 3:&nbsp;KNMI, Netherlands; <span class="paper_email">r.bintanja@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Northern Hemisphere extreme snowfall changes are studied using the Large ENsemble TIme Slice (LENTIS) model. Extreme snowfall frequency and amount are projected to increase more strongly than light snowfall in several regions. The sign and magnitude of change depend on the regions present-day seasonal mean climatological temperature. While light and extreme snowfall are projected to decline significantly, particularly in maritime and mid-latitude regions near the melting point, increases are expected in high-latitude maritime, select high-altitude and continental areas. Compared to light snowfall, extreme snowfall only begins to decline at seasonal mean temperatures roughly 7 °C higher. Consequently, increases in light snowfall are limited to the coldest regions, whereas increases in extremes already occur in relatively warmer, yet still freezing regions. In sufficiently cold regions, warming enhances extreme event frequency (up to 278%) and amount (up to 271%) more strongly than light snowfall (up to 101% and 152%). Regions near the melting point are thermodynamically controlled due to climate warming, while colder regions are likely influenced by both thermodynamic warming and dynamical circulation changes, evident for Greenland. Extreme Greenland snowfall is found to be associated with a sea level pressure anomaly dipole between Greenland and Northern Europe, promoting warm moist Atlantic air advection. Using the Greenland Oscillation Index (GOI) – which measures the strength of the dipole – it is found that increases in extreme snowfall events are linked to a higher frequency of favorable circulation patterns with above-median GOI, particularly over Eastern, Central and Northern Greenland.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 169</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">municipal heat planning, urban transformation, socio-spatial analysis, citizen participation, GIS</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Socio-Technical Characterization of Urban Quarters for Municipal Heat Planning: A Spatial Cluster Analysis of Energy and Socio-Demographic Milieus in Bremen, Germany</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Timmy Schwarz</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Hochschule Bremen, Germany; <span class='paper_email'>timmy.schwarz@hs-bremen.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Municipal heat planning in Germany identifies suitable neighbourhoods for centralized or decentralized heat supply mainly on energetic and urban-structural grounds. The socio-cultural composition of residents and their potential to engage with this transformation are rarely considered systematically. Citizen participation is nevertheless a key precondition for implementation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This contribution presents a GIS-based framework that links energy-related and socio-demographic data. It characterizes urban quarters along two dimensions: technical suitability for heat supply options (individual, district, or low-temperature network heating) and societal activation potential. The analysis uses a 100m x 100m grid for the city of Bremen. It combines milieu-based social-geospatial data (Sinus-Milieus) with seven indicators from the 2022 census. These indicators cover home-ownership rate, building age, vacancy rate, and household structure. A spatial cluster analysis identifies spatially coherent quarter types. These types are then intersected with previously derived suitability areas for heat supply options.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The resulting two-dimensional quarter typology is explorative and hypothesis-generating. It offers municipalities and energy utilities an empirical basis for prioritizing quarters and tailoring citizen-activation strategies. This links technical infrastructure planning with the social dimension of urban transformation. The approach is transferable to other cities with comparable data availability. It contributes to Theme 2.4 by showing how socio-spatial analysis can inform urban transformation measures beyond purely energetic planning criteria.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 170</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Solar PV, Battery Energy Storage, Distributed Energy, MSMEs, Renewable Energy Investment, Nepal, Green Enterprise, Nepal China Cooperation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Investment Opportunities in Solar PV, Battery Storage and Distributed Energy in Nepal</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Guna Raj Dhakal Bilas</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Federation of Nepal Cottage and Small Industries (FNCSI); <span class="paper_email">gunarajdhakal@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Nepal is entering a new phase of renewable energy development in which hydropower, traditionally the backbone of the national electricity system, can be complemented by solar photovoltaic (PV), battery energy storage systems (BESS), distributed renewable energy, and smart energy management. This paper examines Nepal’s emerging renewable energy market and highlights opportunities for investment, technology transfer, enterprise development, and international partnership. Particular attention is given to utility scale and rooftop solar PV, commercial and industrial solar plus storage, agricultural energy applications, mini-grids and microgrids, electric mobility, and digital energy solutions. Battery storage is identified as an important market enabler for renewable energy integration, system flexibility, commercial and industrial backup, peak management, resilient mini grids, and EV charging. The paper further argues that Nepal’s energy transition should move beyond conventional energy access toward <strong>productive energy use</strong>, where clean electricity directly supports enterprises, employment, income generation, and local economic development. Micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) are positioned as critical actors in this transformation, particularly in agriculture, manufacturing, tourism, services, and green industries. Accordingly, renewable energy investment should not focus solely on large infrastructure projects but should also enable thousands of productive enterprises across Nepal. Potential investment and cooperation models include foreign direct investment, joint ventures, public private partnerships, EPC plus-finance, Energy-as-a-Service, local assembly and manufacturing, and technology licensing and transfer. Nepal–China cooperation offers particular potential by combining China’s technology, manufacturing capacity, investment, and supply chains with Nepal’s renewable resources, growing energy demand, local enterprises, and emerging market opportunities. The proposed pathway is therefore <strong>Technology → Partnership → Investment → Enterprise → Prosperity</strong>, transforming Nepal’s renewable energy potential into reliable energy, competitive enterprises, green jobs, and sustainable economic growth.</p>


</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 171</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">child wasting; clean water; electricity access; utilities; child health; Indonesia; dynamic panel data; system GMM</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Effect of Utility Services to Child Health in Indonesia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Shahnaz Natasya Arina</u><sup>1</sup>, Tajul Ariffin Masron<sup>2</sup>, <u>Kusumo Bambang Nugroho</u><sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Indonesia, Indonesia; 2:&nbsp;Universiti Sains Malaysia, Malaysia; 3:&nbsp;KONEKSI, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">naz.natasya@gmail.com</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">kusumobn@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Child wasting remains a significant public health concern in Indonesia, while existing research has largely focused on stunting and child mortality rather than wasting. This study examines the relationship between access to basic utility infrastructure—particularly clean water and electricity—and the prevalence of wasting among children in Indonesia. Using balanced panel data covering 33 provinces over the period 2012–2022, the study employs a dynamic panel-data approach using the Generalized Method of Moments (GMM). Child wasting is used as the dependent variable, while utility indicators include the volume and value of clean water distributed, household access to improved drinking-water sources, electricity distributed, and household access to electricity from the State Electricity Company (PLN). The models also control for provincial economic conditions, protein consumption, consumer prices, government health expenditure, and carbon dioxide emissions. The results from the two-step system GMM indicate significant negative relationships between child wasting and several utility indicators. A 1% increase in the volume of clean water distributed is associated with a 5% reduction in the wasting rate, while a 1% increase in the value of clean water distributed is associated with a 4.8% reduction. A 1% increase in the proportion of households with access to improved drinking-water sources is associated with a 41.4% reduction in child wasting. For electricity, the volume of electricity distributed is not statistically significant, but a 1% increase in the proportion of households with access to electricity from PLN is associated with a 26.5% reduction in wasting. These findings highlight the importance of addressing child wasting through an integrated approach that combines nutrition interventions with improvements in essential infrastructure. Strengthening access to safe drinking water and household electricity should therefore form part of broader multisectoral strategies to improve child health and reduce wasting in Indonesia.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 172</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">production and consumption, value chains, business models, sustainable resource management, food system transformation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digital Regional: Innovative Tools to Increase Local Food Choices in Online Grocery Retail</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Svea Spieker, Lisa Herrmann, Christian Herzig</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Justus-Liebig-Universität, Germany; <span class='paper_email'>svea.spieker@fb09.uni-giessen.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">OBJECTIVE: The import-dependent food system is environmentally and socially vulnerable to opaque supply chains, geopolitical uncertainties and climate change, highlighting the need for organic-regional value chains. Addressing convenience and knowledge barriers, the project „Bio-Regional-Development” (BiRD) examines two digital measures at the online store Ökokiste Boßhammersch Hof (food-box supplier): a recipe tool highlighting (less-in-demand) regional foods with one-click cart additions and gamified regional bonuses with points and discount challenges.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">METHODS: In a mixed-methods design (recipe tool: May/June 2025; regional bonuses: May–July 2026), think-aloud tests captured cognitive processes, evaluated via content-structuring qualitative analysis after Kuckartz and Rädiker. A full census of A/B-test sales data assessed cart-level economic success descriptively. Additionally, customer surveys based on the extended Theory of Planned Behavior before (February/March 2025) and after implementation (November 2026) evaluate intention-behavior gap changes via structural equation modeling addressing test-situation and generalizability limitations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">KEY RESULTS: The recipe tool significantly increased intervention-group sales and shows that full-recipe purchases localize vegetable consumption, though absolute sales remain low. For regional foods with low demand (e.g. less-known varieties), no effect emerged, presumably due to limited recipe coverage. Usability tests revealed an inspiration effect driving post-viewing purchases - observed overall, but particularly pronounced for non-regional products.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">CONCLUSIONS: Digital recipe tools can effectively nudge consumers toward local food when purposefully designed.Practically, prominent placement, more recipes with regional niche products and convenience optimizations are recommended. How regional bonuses support this effect and whether both tools jointly reduce the intention-behavior gap is currently evaluated; final results will be completed in Q1/2027.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">RELEVANCE TO SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT BY AND BEYOND 2030: Digital platforms for organic-regional food systems strengthen local circular economy and resilience against crisis. This directly supports the 2030 Agenda (SDGs 2, 12, 13), providing practical insights into how digital innovations advance the socio-ecological transformation of food systems beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 173</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">low-carbon urban transformation, street-scape visual features, travel-related carbon emissions, urban parks</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Street-scape visual features, sustainable mobility, and low-carbon urban transformation: Evidence from urban park visits in Shenzhen, China</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Wenting Zhang, Yiming Cheng, Li Hua</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Huazhong agricultural university, China, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class='paper_email'>wentingzhang@mail.hzau.edu.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Promoting sustainable mobility has become an important component of low-carbon urban transformation. Existing studies have mainly examined the environmental characteristics of trip origins and destinations, while paying less attention to the built environment along travel routes. Taking Shenzhen, China, as a case study, this research investigates how street-scape visual features influence residents’ low-carbon travel to urban parks and evaluates the carbon-reduction potential of different urban transformation measures.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A questionnaire survey conducted among urban park visitors between March and May 2023 yielded 3,970 valid responses. Approximately 137,000 street-view images were analyzed to quantify visual features along respondents’ travel routes, including the green view index, sky openness index, sidewalk proportion, and wall coverage. These indicators were combined with visitors’ sociodemographic characteristics, park attributes, and other built-environment variables in a generalized ordered logistic regression model.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The results show that street greenery and sky openness are key factors associated with low-carbon travel (Std = −2.886, p &lt; 0.001; Std = −2.249, p = 0.004). In 2023, the 176 urban parks included in the study received approximately 492 million visits, generating an estimated 41,300 tonnes of travel-related carbon emissions. Higher emissions were concentrated in coastal districts such as Nanshan and Futian, while emission intensity generally decreased from west to east.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Three transformation scenarios were evaluated: increasing the public transport modal share to 65%, increasing the proportion of new-energy vehicles to 40%, and increasing the green view index to 0.18. These scenarios could reduce emissions by 27%, 17%, and 4%, respectively. Although street-environment enhancement produces a smaller direct reduction, it remains a practical complementary measure for promoting sustainable mobility in high-density cities. The findings provide empirical support for integrating transportation, vehicle-energy, and street-environment measures into low-carbon urban planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 174</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Nutrition, health, NFHS, SDG, Viksit Bharat</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Nutritional Commitments of India: Challenges for Sustainable Development Goals and ‘Viksit Bharat Aspirations’</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Rabiul Ansary</u>, Amit kumar Sahoo, KR Palta Singh</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Ravenshaw University, India; <span class="paper_email">rabiulansary.ansary786@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">India carries one of the heaviest burdens of malnutrition in the world, with a double-edge sword situation. Child and adult undernutrition remain widespread even as adult overweight and obesity are on a steady rise , producing a double burden. Because this gets transmitted across the life course and between generations. This study assesses how far India and its major states have progressed toward the child nutrition targets under Sustainable Development Goal 2.2 and towards the ‘halt the rise’ benchmark for both 2030 and the Viksit Bharat 2047 horizon. We calculated prevalence rates, actual progress, and required time using NFHS-5 &amp; 6 data. The results are sobering. Except for stunting, all other indicators experienced either very slow progress or deterioration. Most states are off target and may fail to achieve SDG 2.2 in 2030. Only two states have already met the children underweight target, and fifteen are unlikely to reach it even by 2047. Stunting is improving faster, wasting is effectively stalled, and maternal thinness is receding slowly. Adult obesity meanwhile rises unchecked in the absence of any binding reduction target. Better-performing states either recorded a little improvement or deterioration in child and maternal undernutrition. Closing these gaps by 2030 for SDG realisation and before 2047 for Viksit Bharat aspirations, requires effective intervention aimed simultaneously at both tails of the nutritional distribution.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 175</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Household air pollution, Southeast Asia, Trend analysis, Forecasting, Sustainable Development Goals</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Are Southeast Asian Countries on Track to Reduce Household Air Pollution? A Regional Trend, Inequality, and Forecasting Analysis (1990–2030)</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Agus Fitriangga</u>, Alex Alex, Faisal Kholid Fahdi</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Universitas Tanjungpura, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">afitriangga@medical.untan.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Background: Household air pollution (HAP) remains a major environmental health risk in low- and middle-income countries, yet the extent to which progress has accelerated in the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) era is unclear. This study assessed long-term trends, HAP exposure projections by 2030, and shifts in trajectories before and after 2015 in Southeast Asia.<br>Methods: An ecological longitudinal study was conducted using the Global Burden of Disease database for 1990-2023 in 11 Southeast Asian countries. Log-linear regression analysis was performed to obtain the annual percentage change (APC), autoregressive integrated moving average models were used to forecast HAP exposure up to 2030, and segmented regression was performed to assess changes before and after 2015. Regional estimates were population-weighted, and sensitivity, uncertainty, and panel mixed-effects analyses were done for robustness.<br>Results: Exposure to HAP decreased in all countries, but to a different degree. Viet Nam and Thailand had the largest decreases, with projected 2030 exposures of 17.3% and 5.2%, against 96.4% in Lao PDR and 67.5% in Myanmar. Regionally, HAP exposure decreased from approximately 70% in 1990 to 40% in 2023 (p&lt;0.001) and was projected to fall to 25-30% by 2030. However, there were several countries that remained off the desired trajectory. No significant regional shift in trend after 2015 was found, and large disparities between countries persisted.<br>Conclusion: Exposure to HAP has decreased significantly in Southeast Asia, but it is uneven and insufficient in some countries. The lack of a significant change after 2015 suggests that the SDG era has not yet brought about a significant regional change in trajectory. More targeted and equitable interventions are needed to accelerate progress toward 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 176</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">artificial intelligence, sustainable development, spillover effects, twin transition, urban governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Artificial intelligence for urban sustainability synergies and spillovers across cities</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Runchuan Feng</u>, Prajal Prandhan</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, The Netherlands; <span class="paper_email">leofrc@163.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Artificial intelligence is increasingly used to support climate adaptation, food security, energy transition, public health, and sustainable urban planning. Yet most urban AI applications remain city-centred: they evaluate how digital tools improve sustainability within one city, while paying less attention to how these interventions reshape sustainability outcomes elsewhere. This paper proposes an AI-assisted framework for understanding cross-city sustainability synergies and spillover effects in the context of the twin green and digital transition. The central argument is that AI can help cities move beyond isolated sustainability actions by detecting whether sustainable development progress in one urban system generate co-benefits or hidden burdens for other cities. The proposed framework integrates machine learning, natural language processing, and scenario modelling. It can be applied to identify how policies in climate governance, energy efficiency, food logistics, and health services influence other cities through resource and service demand, technology diffusion and migration, supply chains and policy learning. For example, AI-based traffic management may reduce local emissions while shifting logistics pressure to neighbouring cities; conversely, shared urban climate platforms may create positive spillovers by transferring knowledge, improving early warning systems, and coordinating regional adaptation. By linking local AI applications with inter-city sustainability interactions, this study updates artificial intelligence as more than a technical tool for optimizing individual cities. It becomes an auxiliary technology for identifying synergies, anticipating and decreasing unintended negative spillovers, and designing cooperative urban sustainability pathways. The framework contributes to post-2030 sustainable development by supporting cities that are not only smarter and greener internally, but also more responsible in their effects on other urban systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 177</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">sustainable development; SDGs; post-2030; urban resilience; traditional water systems; hiti; ponds; indigenous knowledge; socio-ecological systems; Bhaktapur; Nepal</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Hiti to Resilience: Lessons from Bhaktapur’s Traditional Urban Water System for Integrated Sustainable Transformation Beyond 2030</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Rabin Rachalica</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Bhaktapur Tours Research Centre, Nepal; <span class='paper_email'>rachalica@hotmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">As the 2030 deadline for the Sustainable Development Goals approaches, sustainable urban transformation requires approaches that connect environmental systems with social institutions, cultural knowledge and everyday urban life. This paper examines Bhaktapur, Nepal, where traditional stone spouts (hiti), ponds, recharge areas, settlements, public spaces and community practices historically formed an interconnected urban water system. Rather than treating these elements solely as heritage assets, the study examines how their relationships can inform urban resilience and sustainable development beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Bhaktapur is examined as a qualitative case study using walking-based field observation, heritage and infrastructure documentation, community interaction, tourism observation, and analysis of relationships among hiti, ponds, neighbourhoods, public spaces and cultural institutions. The study conceptualises the traditional water system as socio-ecological infrastructure linking water management, settlement organisation, community stewardship, cultural practices, public space and local economic activity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings indicate that Bhaktapur’s traditional water system extends beyond water provision. Its spatial organisation connects water infrastructure with neighbourhood life, public interaction, cultural practices and locally embedded knowledge. The hiti–pond network demonstrates how ecological functions and social institutions can operate as interconnected components of urban infrastructure. At the same time, its contemporary condition highlights challenges associated with urban transformation, changing environmental conditions and the continuity of traditional knowledge.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that the post-2030 lesson is not to reproduce historical systems unchanged, but to recover their underlying principles of integration, community stewardship, local knowledge and multifunctional public infrastructure. Bhaktapur provides a locally grounded case for linking water resilience, cultural heritage, inclusive public space and community-based urban transformation. The study proposes greater recognition of locally evolved socio-ecological systems and community knowledge in post-2030 sustainability frameworks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 178</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Menstrual hygiene management; Flood exposure; Disaster risk reduction; India; NFHS-5; SDG 3</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Flood Exposure and Menstrual Health among Young Women in India: Implications for Climate-Resilient Health Systems</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jenica Barnwal</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Dilwar Hussain</u><sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Health Systems Studies, Tata Institute of Social Sciences, India; 2:&nbsp;Jawaharlal Nehru University; <span class="paper_email">zenicaa282@gmail.com</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">dilwargeo@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Floods have the potential to disrupt water, sanitation, and hygiene (WASH) services, which are essential components of safe menstrual hygiene management (MHM). However, menstrual health remains insufficiently discussed in disaster preparedness and often receives limited attention in climate-resilient health planning. Our study assessed the association between residence in flood-prone districts and adequate MHM among young women in India.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The data were collected from two sources: the National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5, 2019–21) and the Central Water Commission (CWC). NFHS-5 data</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">were linked with district-level flood classifications from the CWC. A total of 216,897 women aged 15–24 years were included. Adequate MHM was defined as hygienic absorbent use, improved access to water and sanitation, and the availability of a handwashing facility. Descriptive statistics, chi-square tests, and multivariable logistic regression were used to examine the association between flood-prone residence and adequate MHM.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Overall, 35.1% of young women had adequate MHM. Prevalence was lower in flood-prone districts than in non-flood-prone districts (21.8% vs 36.2%). Women living in flood-prone districts had lower crude odds of adequate MHM (COR: 0.49; 95% CI: 0.47–0.52), and the association remained after adjustment for individual, household, and contextual characteristics (AOR: 0.83; 95% CI: 0.79–0.91).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The lower prevalence of adequate MHM in flood-prone districts brings menstrual hygiene into the climate–health nexus as a dimension that remains unheeded in disaster preparedness. Ensuring access to menstrual materials, safe water, sanitation, and handwashing facilities during floods connects reproductive health with the broader objectives of SDGs 3, 5 and 6. Future research should examine whether the duration and severity of flooding modify the relationship between flood exposure and MHM.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 179</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Son preference, anaemia, gender, health penalty, gender discrimination, SDG</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Can Preference for Sons Lead to Health Penalty for Daughters? Evidence from Anaemia Among Young Girls in India</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Monika Narayan</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Jawaharlal Nehru University, India; <span class="paper_email">monikanrn@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This paper attempts to find out the causal impact of maternal son preference on childhood iron deficiency anaemia among female children in India. It uses microdata from the fifth round of National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5, 2019–21). We use a propensity score matching (PSM) framework and restrict our analytical sample to daughters aged 6–59 months. Treatment is defined as a mother&#39;s stated preference for sons (desiring more sons than daughters), compared against a control group of mothers with no stated son preference. Moreover, to isolate behavioural intra-household discrimination from intergenerational physiological transmission, we explicitly balance across mother’s anaemia status alongside demographic covariates, household wealth, and maternal land ownership as a proxy for empowerment. Our findings reveal a statistically significant &quot;Daughter Penalty.&quot; In baseline specifications, daughters of mothers expressing son preference are 3.9 percentage points more likely to be anaemic relative to structurally identical counterparts. Controlling for maternal anaemia through matching marginally reduces this effect to 3.1 percentage points,</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">confirming a distinct behavioural channel beyond biological transmission of anaemia from mothers to daughters. Disaggregated age-group analyses demonstrate that this health penalty depends heavily on developmental stage: the penalty is statistically indistinguishable from zero during early infancy (6–23 months) where breastfeeding acts as a protective nutritional buffer but rises to 4.6 percentage points in the post-breastfeeding window (24–59 months).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This divergence indicates that intra-household gender discrimination manifests most acutely when young girls transition from breastfeeding to family-allocated solid diets where structural inequality persists. By demonstrating how entrenched gender bias actively exacerbates child malnutrition, this study highlights that achieving the Sustainable Development Goals, specifically SDG 2.2 (Ending All Forms of Malnutrition), SDG 3.2 (Ending Preventable Child Mortality), and SDG 5.1 (Ending All Forms of Discrimination Against Women and Girls) requires gender-transformative public health strategies that target intra-household allocation alongside clinical nutrition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 180</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">institutional and governance reforms, future sustainability priorities, sustainability beyond the Sustainable Development Goals, institutional mission, Catholic universities</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Institutional mission as a missing link in sustainability beyond 2030: lessons from catholic universities</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Maria Zitto Soria</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Francisco de Vitoria University, Spain; <span class="paper_email">maria.zitto@ufv.es</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This study examines whether environmental sustainability can be integrated into the core identity and mission of Catholic universities and considers how mission-based institutional cultures may contribute to sustainability priorities beyond 2030. It asks whether sustainability can move from peripheral initiatives or strategic commitments to a structural principle guiding university education, research, management, and social engagement. </p>



<p class="paper_abstract">Methodology: The study combines documentary and conceptual analysis. It reviews the social and ecclesial context of environmental sustainability, analyzes institutional websites, scientific literature, and official documents from sixteen Catholic universities across several continents, and examines Francisco de Vitoria University in Spain as an in-depth case. The analysis applies the Expanded Reason framework through four dimensions—anthropological, epistemological, ethical, and meaning—to assess the foundations and institutional implications of environmental commitment. </p>



<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: The comparative review identifies recurring forms of engagement, including references to Laudato Si’ and integral ecology, dedicated institutional structures, curricular integration, research, campus policies, and social action, but also differing levels of institutionalization. The case analysis shows progress in curriculum, sustainability research, and community engagement, alongside gaps in cross-curricular integration, interdisciplinary synthesis, and the visible coherence of campus practices. The framework indicates that sustainability can be grounded in institutional mission rather than treated as an external or temporary agenda. </p>



<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: For the post-2030 sustainability agenda, the findings suggest that institutional mission, culture, and value frameworks can function as drivers of long-term sustainability integration. Embedding sustainability in university identity can strengthen continuity across leadership cycles and connect governance, education, research, and operations. The study therefore identifies mission-based institutional culture as a potential missing link in sustainability agendas and as a contribution to institutional reform and future sustainability priorities. </p>


</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 181</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG localization, monitoring progress, implementation gaps, Voluntary National and Local Reviews, municipal social media</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Localizing the Sustainable Development Goals without naming them: lessons from 4,875 municipal Instagram posts in Türkiye</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Maher Jarada</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Department of Business Administration, Institute of Graduate Education, Bilecik Şeyh Edebali University, Bilecik, Türkiye; <span class="paper_email">maher123jarada@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Local governments are central to localizing the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), yet little is known about how the goals appear in their everyday public communication. This study examines what municipal social media communication reveals about SDG localization as 2030 approaches.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: All 4,875 Instagram posts published by three municipalities in Türkiye's Bursa-Eskişehir-Bilecik region (two metropolitan, one provincial center) between April 2024 and April 2026 were analyzed. In a mixed-methods design, each post was coded in MAXQDA against the three pillars of the triple bottom line and all 17 goals, with pilot coding and an independent second coder, followed by thematic analysis.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Social sustainability dominated, appearing in 91% of posts, while economic (39%) and environmental (24%) content remained secondary. SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities) appeared in 68% of posts, whereas clean energy (SDG 7) and life below water (SDG 14) were almost absent (14 and 28 codings). Crucially, posts did not frame their content in SDG terms: alignment was implicit, embedded in routine service and event announcements rather than presented as a shared agenda. Communication was event-driven rather than strategic, and the environmental gap was widest in the smaller municipality, whose environmental content per post was about half the metropolitan level.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Municipalities are localizing the goals in practice without naming them. This silent localization leaves local contributions largely unrecognized as part of the global agenda and keeps energy and water transitions at the margins of public communication. For sustainable development by and beyond 2030, the study draws two lessons: future agendas should support explicit, balanced, goal-linked communication at the local level, and municipal social media can serve as a low-cost, publicly available data source for Voluntary Local Reviews, using simple indicators of dimensional balance and goal coverage.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 182</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Green Infrastructure, Climate Adaptation, Nature-Based Solutions, Urban Resilience, Environmental Economics</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Green Infrastructure as Climate Adaptation: A Cost-Benefit Analysis of Nature-Based Solutions in Vulnerable Cities</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mirhuseyn Taghiyev</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Baku State University, Azerbaijan; <span class="paper_email">mirhuseyn020@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">As climate change intensifies urban vulnerabilities—manifesting in severe heat island effects, flash flooding, and extreme stormwater runoff - municipalities worldwide face urgent demands to adapt infrastructure. Nature-Based Solutions (NbS), including urban wetlands, green roofs, permeable pavements, and biosphere corridors, have emerged as sustainable alternatives to traditional "grey" concrete infrastructure. This article provides a cost-benefit analysis (CBA) evaluating the economic, social, and environmental viability of green infrastructure in climate-vulnerable cities. While grey infrastructure offers single-purpose flood or heat control, it suffers from rapid capital depreciation and zero ecosystem co-benefits. In contrast, green infrastructure requires careful spatial integration and specialized ongoing upkeep, but delivers multi-functional value over its life cycle. On the cost side, this paper evaluates upfront capital expenditures, land opportunity costs, and maintenance budgets. On the benefit side, it quantifies direct financial savings - such as avoided flood damage repair, reduced stormwater management expenses, and lowered cooling energy demands - alongside broader co-benefits, including carbon sequestration, biodiversity conservation, and public health improvements. Through a comparative synthesis of municipal case studies, including Medellín’s Green Corridors and Copenhagen’s Cloudburst Management Plan, this study demonstrates that green infrastructure yields higher long-term net returns when co-benefits are factored into economic models. However, widespread adoption remains constrained by siloed municipal budgets, financing gaps, and short-term political horizons. The article concludes with key policy recommendations for urban planners and climate finance decision-makers to embed nature-based solutions into municipal capital budgeting frameworks.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 183</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Ecosystem disservices, ecosystem services, human well-being, systematic review, adverse effects</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Global Trends in Ecosystem Disservices Research: A Systematic Literature Review</p>
<p class="paper_author"> DAYA RAJ SUBEDI</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Mid-West University, Nepal; <span class='paper_email'>daya.subedi@mu.edu.np</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The ecosystem services and disservices framework is a key approach for understanding complex human-nature interactions and the contributions of ecosystems to human well-being. Although ecosystem services research has expanded substantially across regions and disciplines, ecosystem disservices research remains an emerging field of inquiry, with limited synthesis of its geographic and thematic scope. This study, therefore, systematically examines the spatial extent, research patterns, and thematic evolution of global ecosystem disservices literature. Following the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines, a systematic search using relevant keywords was conducted in the Web of Science (WoS) and Scopus databases. A total of 13, 585 records were retrieved from the searches, of which 518 documents were retained following full-text screening for data analysis. Data were manually extracted and analyzed thematically. The findings indicate that agriculture and urban ecosystem management dominate ecosystem disservices research, with studies mostly concentrated in the Global North. Most studies adopt mixed-method approaches and focus on local and provincial scales. Health and economic impacts associated with ecosystem disservices are prominent themes in the literature. The systematic review further shows that ecosystem disservices are frequently examined alongside ecosystem services, highlighting the interconnected nature of beneficial and adverse effects of ecosystems on human well-being. At the same time, diverse terminology has been used to describe adverse effects of ecosystems on people, reinforcing conceptual ambiguity within the ecosystem disservices literature. Because ecosystem disservices can impede sustainable development, incorporating ecosystem disservice targets and indicators into post-2030 sustainability framework may be essential for effective environmental governance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 184</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food-land nexus, human-altered soil erosion modelling, human activities, socio-ecological coupled, MRIO</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Solutions for mitigating soil loss from a human-nature coupled perspective</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Keke Li, Zhen Wang</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Laboratory of Climate Change Mitigation and Carbon Neutrality, Henan University, Zhengzhou 450046, China; <span class='paper_email'>kekeli@henu.edu.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Soil erosion threatens agricultural productivity, ecosystem functioning, and sustainable development. Although erosion occurs locally, its drivers and consequences extend across regions through hydrological processes and socioeconomic connections. Understanding these interactions requires a coupled human–land systems perspective that connects biophysical dynamics with production, consumption, and governance. This study proposes an integrated framework linking the attribution of soil erosion, the transmission of its impacts, and collaborative governance across spatial scales. First, the framework combines soil erosion modelling with spatial analyses of environmental and socioeconomic drivers to distinguish the contributions of climatic variability, land-use change, agricultural practices, and conservation interventions, while examining their interactions. Second, it links hydrological connectivity with interregional supply-chain relationships to investigate two distinct transmission pathways: the downstream movement of sediment and associated environmental impacts, and the displacement of erosion pressures and propagation of erosion-related production risks through trade. This distinction enables an integrated assessment of where erosion originates, which regions drive erosion-intensive production through consumption, and where its consequences are experienced. Third, scenario analysis explores how coordinated soil and water conservation, adjustments in agricultural production, and interregional cooperation could reduce erosion while safeguarding food supply and avoiding the transfer of environmental burdens. Particular attention is given to trade-offs among conservation effectiveness, economic costs, and the distribution of responsibilities and benefits. The proposed framework provides a basis for connecting place-based conservation with shared responsibility across watersheds and supply chains, thereby supporting more equitable and coordinated responses to soil erosion.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 185</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">agricultural system, life cycle assessment, uncertainty, food market, land use change</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Parametric consequential life cycle assessment and global sensitivity analysis for sustainable food production systems</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Weier Liu<sup>1</sup>, Hui Guo<sup>1</sup>, Nanlin Liao<sup>1</sup>, Lorie Hamelin<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Xianghu Laboratory, Hangzhou, China; 2:&nbsp;Toulouse Biotechnology Institute (TBI), Universit ́e de Toulouse, CNRS, INRAE, INSA Toulouse, France; <span class='paper_email'>liuweier@xhlab.ac.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainable agricultural management interventions not only alter inputs and outputs at farm level, but induce land use-related impacts and market driving feedback. Life cycle assessment (LCA) that omits these consequences or treat uncertain inventories deterministically may therefore misidentify environmental impacts and, therefore, mislead mitigation priorities. Here, we introduce a parametric, consequential LCA (cLCA) and global sensitivity analysis (GSA) workflow that emphasizes system expansion, uncertainty propagation and sensitivity for decision support. We model market-mediated product displacement and indirect land-use change (iLUC), while parameterized inventories preserve technical dependencies and propagate foreground and background uncertainties. We use GSA to identify key parameters governing result variances, and develop sensitivity-informed scenarios that convert influential, controllable parameters into robust mitigation thresholds and target-achievement probabilities. We demonstrate this methodological framework using vegetable soybean production and pond-based whiteleg shrimp farming systems in China. In the soybean case, our estimated benefits of improved management depended strongly on which foods the additional yield displaced. Substitution of plant-protein foods or meat produced substantially larger and more robust benefits than displacement of conventional soybean or vegetables, largely through avoided food production and iLUC. In the shrimp case, we combined one-at-a-time perturbation analysis of key control parameters and Monte Carlo simulations to show that lowering the feed conversion ratio to an threshold of approximately 1.30 established robust climate benefits and delivered consistent multi-impact improvements. Meanwhile solar substitution and electricity-efficiency scenarios produced favorable mean responses without a robust climate threshold over the tested ranges. Together, these cases show that the integration of parametric cLCA and GSA provides more nuanced LCA results that are explicit about substitution, LUC-impacts, uncertainty, trade-offs and the performance required for environmental improvement. Such information is critical for managers and practitioners in the decision-making processes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 186</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Adaptive governance, transboundary water governance, climate adaptation, ESG, Southern Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Towards a climate-adaptive governance framework for interconnected transboundary water infrastructure: the Lesotho Highlands Water Project and Integrated Vaal River System</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Moliehi Thakholi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Stellenbosch University, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">23376260@sun.ac.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change is exacerbating hydrological variability in Southern Africa; however, the governance of interconnected water infrastructure frequently lacks operational mechanisms that integrate climate-risk assessment, institutional accountability, and investment decision-making. This doctoral research endeavors to develop an evidence-based adaptive governance framework for climate adaptation planning in transboundary water infrastructure and to investigate whether environmental, social, and governance (ESG) principles can serve as a complementary tool within this framework.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A comparative qualitative case study will examine the Lesotho Highlands Water Project and the Integrated Vaal River System, which are interconnected through water transfers into South Africa yet governed under distinct institutional frameworks: a bilateral treaty and national water governance. Document analysis of treaties, policies, strategies, and environmental and social management records will be complemented by semi-structured interviews with officials, operators, regulators, and practitioners. Hybrid deductive-inductive thematic analysis and cross-case comparison will identify implementation gaps, and a preliminary framework will be refined through expert review.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">As the study is currently at the proposal stage, empirical findings are not yet available. The study is expected to generate comparative evidence on how climate risk and hydrological variability are incorporated into planning and governance; examine the potential complementarities, contributions and limitations of ESG in relation to established approaches such as adaptive governance and integrated water resources management; and contribute to the development of a context-specific governance framework for climate-adaptive interconnected transboundary water infrastructure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The research is anticipated to provide policymakers and infrastructure operators with practical pathways for climate-resilient decision-making within shared water systems. Its significance extends beyond 2030, addressing Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) 6 and 13 by examining how governance can transform adaptation principles into operational, accountable, and equitable practices across interconnected transboundary systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 187</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate extremes; power outages; energy system resilience; cascading risk</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Energy Storage as Shared Resilience Infrastructure under Climate-Driven Power Outages</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Pengfei Yuan</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Hamburg, Germany; <span class="paper_email">pengfei.yuan1@outlook.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Growing climate extremes are increasing power-outage risks worldwide, positioning energy storage as a critical adaptation strategy for enhancing power system resilience. Yet storage is often valued within the boundaries of the power system—through the flexibility and reliability it provides—while its contribution to the wider economy across locations and deployment scales in mitigating climate-driven supply chain disruptions remains poorly understood. Here we develop an integrated climate–energy–economic framework that links machine-learning projections of weather-induced outages at 4,353 primary substations under UKCP18 climate scenarios with an adaptive multi-regional input–output model spanning 32 regions and 61 sectors, using Great Britain as a case study. We find that future climate conditions intensify outage risk, while supply-chain propagation creates a marked spatial mismatch between physical outage exposure and economic losses. Storage in weather-exposed, supply-chain-critical regions can generate substantial economy-wide gains, averaging €129 million yr⁻¹. Accounting for cross-regional supply-chain spillovers increases the estimated resilience value of the same storage investment by 2–8-fold relative to a local-only valuation. These findings identify a previously missing value stream for storage in energy-system planning and provide a quantitative foundation for beneficiary-based cost allocation, regional coordination, and climate-risk-aware deployment of decentralized energy resources.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 188</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable Development Goals, voluntary national reviews, SDG prioritization, policy narratives, sentiment analysis, global sustainability governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Uneven attention and shifting narratives across the Sustainable Development Goals in national reviews</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ting Guan</u>, Xuan Zhou, Shaolin Wu, Prajal Pradhan, Ziwei Gou</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Beijing Normal University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class="paper_email">guanting@bnu.edu.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Voluntary National Reviews (VNRs) are a central means through which countries communicate progress on the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), yet less is known about how the goals are prioritized and portrayed within these reports. We map SDG mentions and the sentiment of associated text in VNRs submitted between 2016 and 2025, examining variation across goals, income levels and regions, as well as changes between countries' first and most recent reviews. SDG 17 is the most frequently mentioned goal, followed by SDGs 1 and 8, while attention to individual goals varies substantially across national contexts. Higher national income is associated with more frequent mentions of sustainable consumption (SDG 12) and clean energy (SDG 7), and with a greater share of positive language about several well-being goals. Across repeated reviews, mentions of poverty (SDG 1) decline and its associated language becomes less positive; climate action (SDG 13) shows the opposite pattern on both measures. Considering attention and sentiment together reveals distinct reporting profiles, including frequently mentioned goals described in less positive terms. These findings show that VNRs do more than record coverage of the SDGs: they reveal how countries select and frame development priorities. Interpreting these patterns alongside outcome indicators could help distinguish changes in reporting from changes in sustainable development performance.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 189</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Circular economy, Energy technologies, Critical raw materials, Life cycle assessment, Resource recovery</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Circular economy of clean energy technologies</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Xin Sun</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Fanran Meng</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Sheffield)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Yanqiu Tao</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Nanjing University of Aeronautics and Astronautics)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The transition to low carbon energy systems requires the deployment of solar photovoltaics, wind turbines, batteries, electrolysers, and other technologies at unprecedented scale. Alongside their climate benefits, these technologies create demand for materials, infrastructure, and manufacturing capacity, while generating waste streams. Circular economy strategies offer opportunities to reduce primary resource demand, retain material and functional value, and strengthen supply security. Realizing these opportunities requires understanding when, where, and under what conditions circular approaches deliver environmental and societal benefits.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This panel examines the circular economy of energy technologies across their life cycles, from material sourcing and product design to operation, reuse, remanufacturing, and recycling. It brings together perspectives from industrial ecology, engineering, economics, and governance to explore how circular strategies can support a sustainable and resilient energy transition. Particular attention will be given to interactions between technology deployment, material availability, product lifetimes, and the timing and quality of secondary resource supplies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The discussion will address three connected questions. How can circular design, lifetime extension, and improved utilization reduce material requirements while maintaining energy system performance? What technical, economic, and institutional barriers constrain collection, reuse, and recycling, and how can they be overcome? How should policies and business models account for tradeoffs among resource efficiency, emissions reduction, affordability, and equitable access to materials and technologies?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on material flow analysis, life cycle assessment, technoeconomic assessment, and system modeling, the panel will examine approaches for evaluating circular interventions across technologies, regions, and time horizons. It will also consider data gaps, uncertainty, international trade, and the distribution of costs and benefits along global supply chains. By connecting analytical evidence with practical implementation, the panel aims to identify research priorities and policy directions that move circularity beyond recycling targets toward measurable reductions in resource use and environmental burdens, while supporting energy security and social equity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">End-of-life treatment management for wind turbines</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Fanran Meng</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Sheffield</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Global installed capacity of wind power reached 1,136 GW in 2024 (representing 8.1% of total electricity generation), and continued installation of new capacity is needed to provide renewable energy. Effective end-of-life (EOL) management strategies are, therefore, needed to recover materials from wind turbines. This work assesses current and emerging EOL practices, comparing the environmental and economic trade-offs across mechanical, thermal and chemical recycling technologies. Wind turbines are built from a range of materials, including structural metals, concrete, composites and magnetic materials, which have distinct recovery pathways and barriers. Structural metal recycling is well developed and used commercially, but concrete recycling is more nascent and not competitive. Near-term scalable composite recycling technologies - including mechanical recycling and cement kiln co-processing - cannot recover the structural strengths of the original composite, but more advanced technologies - such as thermal fibre recovery and solvolysis - face energy and economic barriers to wide-scale deployment. Magnet recycling can either preserve material in its intact form through shorter loop processes or recover valuable rare-earth elements through longer loop processes. The size of the magnets could enable direct reuse of magnets, but it is complicated by changing designs. Further development of recycling and reuse pathways should be complemented by design that enables EOL management and materials circularity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Environmental implications of EV battery swapping for cascaded use and recycling in China</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Yanqiu Tao</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Nanjing University of Aeronautics and Astronautics</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Battery swapping has emerged as a commercially deployed charging paradigm for electric vehicles in</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">China, with dedicated station networks now operating at scale. Unlike conventional plug-in charging,swapping enables battery conditioning in a controlled thermal and electrical environment, attenuatingdegradation during the charging phase relative to uncontrolled fast-charging scenarios. The degradationstate accumulated over the first service life directly governs the residual performance available forcascade utilization and the environmental burden of end-of-life recycling, yet this full-chain linkageremains uncharacterized across China&#39;s climatically heterogeneous provinces, leaving critical evidence</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">gaps for infrastructure design, retirement policy, and cascade utilization planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study develops an integrated framework using a semi-empirical aging model for lithium iron</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">ohosphate (LFP), nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC), and nickel-cobalt-aluminium (NCA) batteries, driven bynourly provincial temperature data across 30+ Chinese provinces and real-world vehicle SOC profiles.Both first-life and second-life aging are simulated continuously. Retired batteries are evaluated in twosecond-life scenarios exploiting regional peak-valley tariff differentials: industrial park load managementand 5G base station backup supply. The resulting degradation profiles feed into a cradle-to-grave LCA,</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">with sensitivity analysis on battery pack size.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Swapping mode extends first-life service and yields lower per-kWh lifecycle environmental burden relative</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">to conventional charging, with the benefit most pronounced for NMC and NCA in thermally demandingprovincial conditions, while LFP exhibits more uniform performance across China&#39;s climate gradient. Thetwo second-life scenarios produce divergent utilization-recycling trade-offs: the 5G base station profileextends service duration at the cost of lower energy throughput, whereas the industrial park profileachieves higher throughput with shorter second-life service, creating chemistry-specific and regionallydifferentiated LCA outcomes. Battery pack size introduces a nonlinear optimum in lifecycle</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">environmental performance, with the optimal range shifting by chemistry and provincial climate.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">The EU&#39;s recycled content targets for batteries cannot effectively drive domestic recycling without sufficient cathode production capacity</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Zhenyu Dong</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Oxford</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The EU Battery Regulation stipulates that, beginning in 2031, all electric vehicle (EV) batteries placed on the EU market must contain certain shares of recycled materials. However, the involvement of non-EU battery producers and recyclers makes that such requirements may not effectively stimulate domestic recycling due to cross-border trade flows. To examine this issue, this study develops a dynamic material flow analysis to project global demand and end-of-life volumes of lithium, cobalt, and nickel from EV batteries. Based on these projections, recycled material flows are traced across six stages from retirement to reuse, distinguishing whether processes occur within or outside the EU. The effectiveness of the regulation is then assessed using the minimum battery collection rate required for compliance. Results indicate that under the business as usual (BAU) scenario, EU demand for lithium, nickel, and cobalt will increase from 17.0 kt, 86.8 kt, and 13.0 kt in 2031 to 25.8 kt, 131.3 kt, and 19.7 kt in 2040. If the EU is considered a self-sufficient market, meeting the requirements would require collection rates for lithium, cobalt, and nickel of 42.5%, 44.2%, and 124.9% in 2031–2035, and 31.3%, 40.5%, and 72.7% in 2036–2040. Building sufficient cathode material production capacity within the EU is crucial for achieving the recycling targets, as it represents the key link for utilizing recovered materials. We argue that more flexible mechanisms should be adopted in the Battery Regulation that reflect evolving chemistries, shifting retirement volumes, and self-sufficiency of cathode production and transboundary material flows.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Identifying roles of cities in reshaping China’s battery recycling network</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Haoyang Li</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Cambridge</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The upcoming terawatt-hours of retired batteries from electric vehicles are expected to drive substantial greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from waste treatments across cities. Using China, the world’s largest generator of retired batteries, as a case, we optimize the national recycling network under multiple emission and cost targets, integrating heterogeneities in battery retirements, energy mixes, and factor costs across 337 prefecture-level cities with 113,569 logistics paths. We reveal a trade-off between GHG emissions and costs of battery recycling across 59% of cities, while the remaining cities exhibit synergistic outcomes. As decarbonization goals tighten, the recycling capacity increasingly concentrate in southwestern cities, with five cities handling half of national retirement volume. This shift amplifies long-distance, interprovincial transport, accounting for 85% of total battery flows. Our findings provide actionable insights for leveraging the unique characteristics of various cities to accelerate cost-effective, low-carbon battery recycling network.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 190</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.2 Environmental health and emerging pollutants, including regulatory and governance responses, and policy translation of exposure science</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Urbanisation, livestock antibiotic use; dietary change; pork consumption; China</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Urbanisation’s Untapped Potential to Curb Livestock Antibiotic Use in China</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Heran Zheng</u><sup>1</sup>, Qingling Wang<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;UCL, United Kingdom; 2:&nbsp;China Agriculture University; <span class="paper_email">heran.zheng@ucl.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Urbanisation is a multifaceted process that encompasses population migration and shifts in food consumption, particularly demand for livestock meat, whose production relies heavily on antibiotics. Yet the effects of urbanisation on livestock antibiotic use remain poorly understood. Focusing on China, the world’s largest antibiotic consumer amid rapid urbanisation, we combine a multi-regional input–output (MRIO) model with household-level expenditure data to estimate the dietary antibiotic footprint of rural and urban residents separately and identify drivers of temporal trends and disparities between population groups. We found that China’s overall dietary antibiotic footprint declined by roughly half, from 33.3 kilotons to 17.1 kilotons, between 2012 and 2019. This decline was driven primarily by reductions in antibiotic-use intensity and declining pork consumption among affluent urban residents, but was partially offset by rising incomes across the population. We also found contrasting effects of income growth in rural and urban settings: it increased the antibiotic footprint among rural residents through increased pork consumption, but reduced it among urban residents through declining pork consumption. Notably, income was associated with an inverted U-shaped relationship with per capita dietary antibiotic footprint among urban residents. Counterfactual scenario analyses suggest that China’s urbanisation-induced dietary shifts could reduce aggregate livestock antibiotic use by approximately 15% (3.7–4.3 kilotons) in 2050 relative to a non-urbanisation benchmark, primarily owning to declining pork demand among affluent urban residents. These findings collectively indicate that urbanisation in China can contribute to reductions in terrestrial livestock antibiotic use through shifts in meat demand. Although projections rely on modelled consumption data and entail uncertainty, our findings suggest that exposure to urban food environments and greater dietary diversity may provide an additional pathway for reducing livestock antibiotic use alongside measures targeting antibiotic-use intensity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 191</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Child health, Public health expenditure, Institutional quality, Health-system efficiency, Central and West Africa, SDG 3</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Central and West African Child Health Outcomes: Why Efficient Spending and Strong Institutional Quality Matter</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Olaide Balogun</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Tai Solarin Federal University of Education, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">opeloyeruos@tasued.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Child survival is a major challenge to sustainability in Central and West Africa (CWA) where high infant and under-five mortality rates coexist with limited health resources and unstable health system performance. Public health expenditure has increased, but evidence on how efficiently resources are converted into health services and how institutional quality influences child health outcomes is still limited. This study examines the impact of health system efficiency and institutional quality on child survival in CWA. The study investigates 24 countries between 2002 and 2024, covering the post-Abuja declaration, COVID-19 shock and recovery. Inputs are government health expenditure per capita and doctors per 1,000 population and outputs are infant and under-five survival rates to measure technical efficiency using Slack-Based Data Envelopment Analysis (SBM-DEA). The efficiency scores from SBM-DEA are then used in two-step dynamic system Generalized Method of Moments (GMM) models of infant and under-five mortality, along with institutional quality index and GDP per capita. The results of the SBM-DEA show that none of the considered country in the study reached efficiency frontier but Togo achieved the highest relative efficiency and Nigeria was among the least efficient. Slacks associated with major expenditure, workforce and mortality suggest that availability of resources does not necessarily translate into better child survival. In the baseline GMM models, better health system efficiency is significantly associated with lower infant and under-five mortality, while good institutional quality consistently reduces both infant and under-five mortality . The findings support a shift from expenditure centered health policy to efficient resource allocation and better institutions approach. The findings have direct relevance to SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and the broader SDGs agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 192</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">systemic constraint; gender inequality; regional disparities; Mexico</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Group Inequality as the Systemic Constraint Hypothesis to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals: Evidence from Mexican regional gender data</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Aurelie Charles</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Bath, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">ac697@bath.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines how group inequality operates as a systemic constraint on achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Using regional data from thirty-two Mexican states between 2016 and 2022, gender-disaggregated indicators were constructed across nine SDGs to explore cross-goal interlinkages. Panel Generalized Least Squares regressions reveal that disparities in education, labour income, and governance participation are strongly interconnected, indicating that inequality functions as a structural bottleneck within the SDG network. Gender gaps reduce the transmission of progress across domains, slowing collective advancement toward sustainable development. The findings support the systemic constraint hypothesis, emphasizing that equity is not only a moral imperative but also a functional condition for achieving integrated and inclusive sustainability.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 193</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Resource conflicts, geopolitical instability, transboundary climate risks, climate induced migration</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate Change, Climate Action, and Human Security in Nigeria: A Comparative Analysis of Rural and Urban Vulnerability</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Asmau M Ahmed</u>, Maryam U Dikko</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kaduna State University, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">ahmed.asmau@kasu.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change has become one of the most pressing threats to human security in Nigeria, intersecting with resource scarcity, weak governance and pre-existing social vulnerabilities to produce compounding risks for livelihoods, safety and mobility. Framed within the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) - particularly SDG 13 (Climate Action), SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities) and SDG 16 (Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions), this study examines how climate change and climate action shape human security outcomes across Nigeria&#39;s rural and urban settings. The study employs a qualitative, comparative desk based methodology, synthesising peer reviewed literature, institutional reports and national policy documents on desertification and shrinking water bodies in the north, farmer herder resource conflicts, transboundary risks in the Lake Chad Basin, and urban flooding and heat stress in cities such as Lagos and Port Harcourt. Results show that rural human insecurity is driven primarily by resource based conflict and agrarian livelihood collapse, while urban human insecurity is driven by infrastructural deficits, informal settlement exposure and service overload, and that both dynamics are linked by accelerating climate induced migration and human mobility. The findings further reveal pronounced equity and justice gaps in Nigeria&#39;s climate governance architecture, with adaptation finance, planning and infrastructure investment favoured toward already better served urban and coastal areas, leaving rural and peri-urban populations structurally disadvantaged. Drawing lessons from Nigeria&#39;s SDG implementation record, the study concludes with policy recommendations for an integrated, justice centred human security framework that connects rural resilience building with urban adaptation planning and regional (transboundary) cooperation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 194</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate law, effectiveness, ecology based approach</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">An Inconvenient Legal Truth</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Lorenzo Squintani</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Groningen, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">l.squintani@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Current Climate Law suffers from systemic legal designs flaws that prevent it from achieving genuine achievement of climate goals, both in terms of mitigation and adaptation, and a fair energy transition, even when technical compliance is fully met. This institutional failure stems from a widening gap between rigid legal frameworks and dynamic ecological realities, equity imbalances in public participation that disadvantage vulnerable groups, and policy conflicts that prioritize short-term economic goals over long-term sustainability. This contribution postulates that, ultimately, relying on stagnant political limits rather than adaptive, science-based ecological standards undermines regulatory effectiveness and drives a global surge in climate litigation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 195</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Adaptation, Cultural inheritance, Heatwaves, South Asia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Heatwave Adaptation in South Asia, Rooted in Cultural Inheritance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Pracheta Acharya</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">National Institution for Transforming India [NITI Aayog], India; <span class="paper_email">acharyapracheta.kgp06@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Rising global temperatures and the increasing frequency of heatwaves worldwide have drawn growing attention from academicians, policymakers, and practitioners. Adaptation is essential to building resilience at both the individual and community levels. While numerous studies have examined the explicit knowledge that informs people&#39;s understanding of heatwaves, one important source of knowledge production remains undercommunicated: lived experience. Individuals&#39; direct experiences contribute to the formation of heatwave-related knowledge, which is then passed down across generations through cultural inheritance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This article reviews behavioural adaptation practices at the individual level and examines how these practices are rooted in culturally inherited knowledge. South Asia, home to nearly one-fifth of the world&#39;s population, has a substantial proportion of climate-vulnerable communities. Existing heatwave research in the region, however, has largely focused on climate projections, health impacts, vulnerability mapping, and policy analysis. A systematic review examining heatwave adaptation behaviour through a cultural lens has yet to be undertaken for South Asia.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) methodology, this article draws on qualitative data from 32 studies spanning eight South Asian countries, analysed through a cultural adaptation knowledge framework. The intent is not to diminish the significance of government policies and initiatives, but to position culturally rooted practices as complementary to formal heat adaptation strategies and technologies. In a region where technocratic solutions remain limited in reach, and where dedicated healthcare systems and occupational safety standards for heatwaves are still emerging, behaviourally embedded, culturally inherited practices play a significant adaptive role.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Specifically, the article explores culturally embedded dietary practices and building designs and their effectiveness, how gender differences in adaptation stem from cultural norms, and how modern adaptive measures are shaping cultural adaptive practices.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 196</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Biosphere reserve; local participation; environmental governance; equity; Morocco</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Whose priorities count? Local participation in protected areas of Morocco’s Intercontinental Biosphere Reserve</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Faiza El Mejjad</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">MOHAMMED V UNIVERSITY IN RABAT, Morocco; <span class='paper_email'>faiza_elmejjad@um5.ac.ma</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">International biosphere reserve designations encourage the reconciliation of biodiversity conservation and sustainable development. Yet their local relevance depends in part on whether residents can express their priorities and take part in decisions. This paper examines that question across protected areas on the Moroccan side of the Intercontinental Biosphere Reserve of the Mediterranean (RBIM).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on 37 interviews conducted with residents and local actors, the study explores how people describe environmental pressures, everyday needs and opportunities to participate in conservation. It pays particular attention to the relationship between locally identified priorities and the objectives associated with the biosphere reserve designation. The interviews are analysed thematically to identify areas of agreement, tensions and differences in how participation is understood across the areas studied.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper asks what meaningful participation would require in protected rural territories where conservation concerns intersect with residents’ living and working conditions. By placing local accounts at the centre of the analysis, it contributes an empirical perspective to discussions of equity in sustainability governance. Its implications for development beyond 2030 concern how conservation initiatives can recognise local knowledge and make space for residents’ priorities in decision-making.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 197</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Extreme heat, maternal health, birth weight, temperature deviation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Womb under Heat: Temperature Shocks, Maternal Care, and Birth Weight in India</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>SAPAN KUMAR PRADHAN</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">JNU, India; <span class="paper_email">sapanmpradhan@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This paper examines the effects of maternal exposure to extreme temperatures during pregnancy on children’s birth weight in India. By exploiting within-cluster variation in maternal temperature exposure, the study finds that an additional day pregnant women experienced temperatures above 32°C (relative to a baseline of 20–24°C) reduced their children’s birth weight by approximately 0.45 grams. The study also assesses the impact of temperature on birth weight, using a Temperature Deviation Index that accounts for the long-term district-season heat norm, and finds that the highest heat-exposure quintile weighs about 18 grams less than those in the lowest quintile. These findings remain robust to a series of robustness checks and alternative specifications. The study further finds that prenatal heat exposure above 32°C is associated with fewer antenatal care (ANC) visits, particularly among women exposed to the highest-temperature quintile, suggesting that reduced access to essential maternal healthcare is an important behavioural pathway through which extreme heat may adversely affect children’s birth weight. These findings highlight the unequal impact of extreme heat on early life, even before birth, emphasising the importance of integrating heat-mitigation strategies into maternal and child health policy.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 198</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.2 Lessons learned from SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">​SDG Localization, Fragile Cities, Bukavu, Fondation Anansoft, Circular Economy, Youth Innovation, Post-2030 Agenda</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Localizing the Post-2030 Agenda in Fragile Cities: Lessons from Community-Led Infrastructure, Circular Economy, and Youth Innovation in Bukavu (DR Congo)</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Joseph Bahat Nonozi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Fondation Anansoft, Congo, Democratic Republic of the; <span class="paper_email">josnonozi@fondationanansoft.org</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">​As the 2030 deadline approaches, global progress on the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) remains deeply uneven, with fragile and conflict-affected urban environments facing acute implementation gaps. Bukavu, a secondary city in Eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, exemplifies extreme multi-hazard vulnerability: steep landslide-prone topography, unmanaged plastic waste obstructing urban drainage, severe infrastructure deficits, and socio-political instability. In such volatile contexts, top-down SDG implementation models fail to address cascading, interconnected crises.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​This presentation shares operational insights and field evidence from Fondation Anansoft (FOANS) in Bukavu, examining how grass-roots innovations bridge global targets with local fragility. Key interventions include circular economy initiatives (such as "plastics for food" campaigns mitigating urban flooding), localized adaptation infrastructure (pedestrian stairways on eroded hillsides, off-grid solar lighting for public safety), and youth technological and socio-economic empowerment.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​Based on FOANS's field operations, three critical lessons emerge for shaping the post-2030 global sustainability framework:</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​Breaking Goal Silos in High-Risk Settings: Environmental action (SDG 13) through waste management directly mitigates disaster risk (SDG 11), while simultaneously enhancing food security (SDG 2) and youth employment (SDG 8). Post-2030 frameworks must prioritize systemic, multi-beneficial interventions.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​Community-Led Climate Adaptation: Building resilience in fragile cities relies on micro-level, community-engineered infrastructure to deliver immediate safety and social stability (SDG 7 &amp; 16).</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​Youth Innovation as a Resilience Catalyst: Training youth in digital skills and social entrepreneurship transforms vulnerable populations from passive aid recipients into active architects of localized sustainable development.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">​Conclusion: The post-2030 agenda must pivot toward context-sensitive localization. Empirical evidence from Bukavu demonstrates that empowering local digital and social ecosystems in fragile urban centers provides a vital blueprint for flexible, inclusive, and shock-responsive post-SDG frameworks.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 199</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Hydrological modelling, flood-risk assessment, community resilience, early warning systems, Sebeya Catchment</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Integrating hydrological modelling and flood-risk assessment to strengthen community resilience in the Sebeya Catchment, Rwanda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jean Pierre KAREKEZI</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Department of Water Resources Engineering, Faculty of Engineering, Universitas Brawijaya, Malang, Indonesia; <span class='paper_email'>k.jeanpierre1998@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Flooding in the Sebeya Catchment of Western Rwanda poses a persistent risk to communities and infrastructure along the river. This study investigates whether a calibrated hydrological model can provide a reliable scientific basis for flood-risk assessment and support more targeted community resilience measures.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A semi-distributed Hydrologic Engineering Center–Hydrologic Modeling System model was developed for the 367.26 km² Sebeya Catchment, represented by 27 subbasins and 13 routed reaches. The model combines the Soil Conservation Service Curve Number, Soil Conservation Service Unit Hydrograph, Recession baseflow, and Muskingum routing methods. Calibration used observed streamflow from 2022, while independent validation was conducted for 2023–2025 using the calibrated parameter set without re-calibration. Performance was assessed using Nash–Sutcliffe Efficiency, coefficient of determination, Percent Bias, Root Mean Square Error, and Modified Kling–Gupta Efficiency.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The model achieved NSE = 0.839, R² = 0.926, PBIAS = −3.11%, RMSE = 0.401 m³/s, and Modified KGE = 0.767 during calibration. Independent validation retained NSE = 0.793, R² = 0.933, PBIAS = 13.52%, RMSE = 0.455 m³/s, and Modified KGE = 0.810. The results demonstrate strong preservation of temporal dynamics while revealing systematic overestimation of some high-flow events and runoff volume.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings show that independent validation is essential for distinguishing model performance from calibration fit. The validated model provides a scientifically defensible baseline for flood-risk assessment and prioritization of vulnerable river sections. Linking this evidence with real-time water-level monitoring and community warning mechanisms can strengthen locally informed flood resilience and support more sustainable flood-risk management in the Sebeya Catchment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 200</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food-producing potential, urban planning, food system transformation, urban resilience, sustainability assessment</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From urban land assessment to food-producing potential: a field-based approach for resilient urban transformation</p>
<p class="paper_author"> DUSSIN Jean</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">RECOLT- Ingénierie Nourricière, France; <span class='paper_email'>contact.recolt@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Urban territories contain vacant, underused and transforming spaces whose potential contribution to local food production remains difficult to quantify at site level. RECOLT – Ingénierie Nourricière is developing a field-based approach to assess whether urban land can become food-producing and to estimate its potential production. This contribution presents the emerging RECOLT methodology and explores how field assessment can complement European-scale spatial modelling of urban food production.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">RECOLT has developed three complementary field-based tools. The PRU – Urban Resilience Potential assesses a site&#39;s capacity to support productive and food-producing use by considering its physical, environmental and territorial characteristics. The IPN-RECOLT – Food-Producing Potential Index estimates the potential annual production of edible crops, expressed in kilograms per year. RE-SOL is an operational soil tool and protocol designed, where technically and legally appropriate, to treat and make excavated soils usable and fertile directly on site, reducing the need for unnecessary soil disposal and the import of replacement soil. Together, these tools connect territorial assessment, food-producing potential and on-site soil management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">RECOLT has structured a methodological framework linking territorial analysis, field diagnosis, food-producing potential and soil management. In France, RECOLT has also developed a National Food-Producing Ranking of Brownfields without an identified redevelopment project, based on elements from the national brownfield inventory developed by Cerema. This provides a potential basis for identifying sites for future field assessment. The approach is now being prepared for testing across different European urban contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">RECOLT proposes food-producing potential as an additional dimension of urban land assessment and territorial planning. The next research step is to compare European-scale spatial modelling with field-level assessments in several cities, in order to test, refine and scientifically validate the methodology. This approach could contribute to the emergence of Food-Producing Engineering as a new dimension of resilient urban transformation and sustainable territorial development beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 201</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDGs, higher education, SDG implementation, policy translation, Uzbekistan</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Implementing the SDGs in Higher Education in Uzbekistan: From National Commitment to University Practice</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Furkat Sharipov</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of East Anglia, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">f.sharipov@uea.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Uzbekistan has increasingly incorporated sustainable development and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) into national development strategies and higher education reforms. However, translating global sustainability commitments into national higher education contexts involves processes of interpretation and adaptation shaped by existing policy priorities, institutional structures and practices. This paper presents findings from an ongoing PhD study examining how sustainable development and SDG-related commitments are translated from national policy into university-level practice in Uzbekistan. The study draws on qualitative analysis of national policy documents and semi-structured interviews with a government representative, university administrators, academics and students. The analysis traces how sustainable development and the SDGs are constructed at the national policy level, interpreted by higher education stakeholders and enacted within universities.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The findings demonstrate the increasing visibility of the SDGs across Uzbekistan’s higher education sector. National reforms have created opportunities for universities to connect sustainability with environmental initiatives, curriculum development, research, international partnerships, digitalisation and broader institutional development. At the university level, the SDGs also provide a framework through which existing activities can be organised, aligned with particular goals and communicated. However, implementation remains selective and uneven. Sustainability is frequently associated with environmental protection, the green economy and visible campus initiatives, while its social, participatory and educational dimensions receive less consistent attention. Implementation is further shaped by centralised policy priorities, differences in institutional capacity, uneven stakeholder understandings of sustainable development, and a growing emphasis on measurable performance, including international rankings. These dynamics can encourage institutional engagement with the SDGs while simultaneously narrowing what is recognised as sustainability-related action. The study therefore shows that implementing the SDGs in higher education depends not only on formal national commitments, but also on how global goals are interpreted, prioritised and translated into institutional practice.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 202</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">biodiversity, ecosystem services, nature-based solutions, landscape management, community resilience</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">NatureScaping™ for Integrated Landscape Resilience: Linking Biodiversity, Water, Agroforestry and Community Stewardship</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Satya Prakash Mehra</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Vasudha Ecosystems Global LLP (VEG-Bharat), India; RSNH; GFSRD; <span class='paper_email'>drspmehra@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Landscape sustainability is often addressed through separate interventions for biodiversity conservation, water management, land restoration, climate resilience and livelihoods. This contribution examines how these dimensions can be connected through landscape-scale practice, using NatureScaping™ as a practice-derived framework and drawing lessons from Rajasthan, India, for integrated resilience beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution synthesizes documented practice-based evidence from three Rajasthan landscape models associated with environmental action research: Abu, Dholpur and Bharatpur. The cases are comparatively examined according to relationships among biodiversity, vegetation restoration, soil-water conservation, agroforestry, ecosystem functions, livelihoods and community stewardship. Evidence is drawn from documented field experience and independent case documentation where available.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The cases illustrate complementary pathways for integrated landscape intervention. The Abu model connects conservation of the Green Avadavat and its habitat with local youth engagement and livelihood-oriented skills. The Dholpur model combines ravine rehabilitation, water-retention measures, vegetation restoration and community mobilisation; independent documentation reports substantially prolonged water retention following intervention. The Bharatpur model demonstrates cluster-based NatureScaping™ linking plant-led restoration with water, biodiversity and livelihood considerations. Together, these cases illustrate how ecological processes and local stewardship can be connected across landscape interventions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Rajasthan experience suggests that landscape-scale sustainability can complement sector-specific interventions by connecting biodiversity, water, land restoration and community action within place-based systems. NatureScaping™ is presented not as a universally validated solution, but as a practice-derived framework whose applications warrant further comparative assessment. Its documented experience offers lessons for nature-based solutions, landscape management and resilience-oriented implementation by and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 203</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">systems thinking, integrated assessment, synergies and trade-offs, sustainability indicators, SDG localization</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From SDG Interactions to Landscape Action: Lessons from Rajasthan for Integrated Sustainability Beyond 2030</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Satya Prakash Mehra</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Vasudha Ecosystems Global LLP (VEG-Bharat), India; <span class="paper_email">drspmehra@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals provide an integrated global framework, but implementation is frequently organised around individual sectoral objectives. This contribution examines what documented landscape interventions in Rajasthan, India, reveal about interactions among environmental, livelihood and community dimensions of sustainable development, and considers how such lessons may inform integrated sustainability approaches beyond 2030.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The study synthesizes practice-based evidence from site-specific socio-ecological initiatives documented across Rajasthan, including Abu Hills, Dholpur, Bharatpur and Kumbhalgarh. The cases are examined through a systems-thinking lens, mapping documented intervention pathways against relevant sustainability dimensions rather than treating individual SDGs as isolated objectives. Particular attention is given to biodiversity conservation, water management, habitat restoration, agroforestry, livelihoods, community participation and locally grounded knowledge.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Key results</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The cases illustrate that individual landscape interventions can intersect with multiple sustainability objectives. In Abu Hills, protection of Green Avadavat habitat was connected with local youth livelihoods. In Dholpur, ravine rehabilitation combined water-retention measures, vegetation restoration and community mobilisation; independent documentation reports substantially prolonged water retention following intervention. Bharatpur and Kumbhalgarh provide additional examples of connecting ecological restoration with local resource use, skills and livelihoods. The cases therefore illustrate interactions among biodiversity, water, land restoration, livelihood and community objectives while demonstrating the importance of site-specific ecological and institutional conditions.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusions</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">One lesson for the post-2030 agenda is the value of assessing sustainability interventions through interactions among objectives rather than through isolated indicators alone. Future assessment approaches could combine ecological, social and livelihood indicators with explicit analysis of synergies, trade-offs and local governance. The Rajasthan cases provide practice-based evidence for developing and testing such integrated assessment approaches while highlighting the need for further systematic measurement and comparative evaluation.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 204</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food-water nexus, water security, agroforestry, landscape management, sustainable livelihoods</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Water-Centric NatureScaping™: Integrating Agroforestry, Biodiversity and Livelihoods for Sustainable Landscape Resilience</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Satya Prakash Mehra</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Vasudha Ecosystems Global LLP (VEG-Bharat), India; <span class='paper_email'>drspmehra@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Water scarcity, land degradation and livelihood vulnerability are interconnected challenges in dryland regions. This contribution examines how water-centric landscape interventions can connect agroforestry, biodiversity conservation and livelihood development, drawing lessons from Rajasthan, India, for integrated approaches to resource security beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution synthesizes documented practice-based evidence from three Rajasthan models associated with NatureScaping™ and regenerative agroforestry: Abu, Dholpur and Bharatpur. The cases are examined through a landscape-nexus perspective, focusing on relationships among vegetation, soil-water conservation, biodiversity, agricultural and livelihood resources, and community participation. Documented field evidence is complemented by independent case documentation where available.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The cases illustrate different pathways for connecting ecological restoration with resource security. The Abu model links habitat restoration with local livelihood and skills opportunities. In Dholpur, ravine rehabilitation combined vegetation restoration, water-retention measures, conservation agriculture and community mobilisation; independent documentation reports substantially prolonged water retention following intervention. The Bharatpur model connects plant-led restoration with water, biodiversity and livelihood considerations through a cluster-based approach. Collectively, the cases illustrate how vegetation and landscape management can influence water conditions, ecological productivity and livelihood opportunities while remaining dependent on local ecological and institutional contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Rajasthan experience suggests that water, land productivity, biodiversity and livelihood challenges in drylands can be addressed more coherently when landscape restoration is planned across ecological and human systems rather than through isolated sectoral interventions. NatureScaping™ provides a practice-derived framework for organising such integration, while the cases highlight the need for measurable nexus indicators and comparative assessment. These lessons can inform place-based resource-security strategies and integrated landscape planning by and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 205</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainability committee, gender diversity, environmental performance, regulatory stringency, corporate governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">One key does not open all doors: Sustainability committee gender diversity and environmental performance under regulatory stringency</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Munir Hussain</u>, Khurshid Ali, Saira Munir</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Lincoln University, New Zealand; <span class="paper_email">munir.hussain@lincolnuni.ac.nz</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This study examines whether gender diversity within corporate sustainability committees is associated with corporate environmental performance and whether regulatory stringency shapes this relationship. Focusing on Europe and the Antipodean region, the study investigates how internal corporate governance and external regulatory conditions can contribute to environmental sustainability and support sustainable development by and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The study uses a longitudinal firm-level panel dataset covering companies in European Union countries, Australia and New Zealand. Sustainability committee gender diversity is measured by the proportion of female committee members, while corporate environmental performance is measured using an environmental performance score. Regulatory stringency is captured through a composite index reflecting relevant environmental, social and governance regulatory requirements. Panel regression models with firm and year fixed effects are employed to examine the relationship between sustainability committee gender diversity and environmental performance. An interaction term between committee gender diversity and regulatory stringency is used to assess whether the regulatory environment moderates this relationship. Firm-level characteristics are included as control variables.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: The analysis provides evidence on the relationship between sustainability committee gender diversity and corporate environmental performance and examines whether this relationship varies with regulatory stringency. The findings indicate how internal governance structures and external regulatory conditions jointly shape corporate environmental outcomes across different institutional settings. The analysis also identifies differences in the relationship between committee diversity and environmental performance across European and Antipodean firms.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: The study highlights the importance of connecting corporate governance with regulatory governance when advancing environmental sustainability. The findings provide evidence for actionable governance pathways in which more inclusive sustainability committees operate within supportive regulatory environments to strengthen corporate environmental engagement. These insights contribute to climate governance discussions and to efforts to accelerate sustainable development by 2030 while informing corporate and regulatory approaches to sustainability beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 206</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Political economy of care; childhood inequality; family well-being; care regimes; institutional pathways; Malaysia.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Political Economy of Care and Unequal Childhoods: Institutional Pathways to Family Well-being in Malaysia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>SABRI SULAIMAN</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Universiti Malaya, Malaysia; <span class="paper_email">sabrisulaiman@um.edu.my</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Malaysia’s rapid economic transformation and evolving social policies have reshaped the organisation of care, with significant implications for childhood inequalities and family well-being. This paper examines how political-economic structures and institutional arrangements influence the distribution, accessibility, and quality of care in Malaysia, with particular attention to early childhood care and education, family-based care, and vulnerable populations. Drawing on an interdisciplinary synthesis of research in political economy, social policy, childhood studies, family studies, public health, and education, the paper analyses evidence on governance, care regimes, economic inequality, parenting, and institutional inclusion. The findings indicate that centralised governance and redistributive policies have expanded access to selected services, yet persistent policy–practice gaps, public–private disparities, urban–rural inequalities, and uneven institutional capacity continue to shape unequal childhood experiences. Children in institutional care face greater psychosocial risks than those in family-based settings, while economic strain, multidimensional poverty, parental relationships, and caregiving pressures further mediate child and family outcomes. Inclusive early childhood care and education is also constrained by uneven policy dissemination, educator capacity, resources, and community participation. The paper argues that family well-being cannot be understood independently of the political economy of care. It calls for integrated and culturally responsive institutional frameworks that connect social protection, care provision, early childhood development, community participation, and support for unpaid caregiving. Such an approach offers a pathway for addressing structural inequalities while strengthening family well-being in Malaysia.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 207</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.3 AI and big data for decision-making and sustainable governance as a supporting decision tool</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">AI-assisted governance; sustainable finance; ESG auditing; SDG evidence; rural investment; human oversight.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Sustainability Data to Accountable Investment Decisions: An AI-Assisted Governance Framework for Rural Finance in Mexico.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Gerardo Trujillo</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Capitalization and Investment Fund for the Rural Sector - FOCIR, Mexico; <span class="paper_email">gtrujillo@focir.gob.mx</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Public development finance institutions face a practical governance challenge: Investment decisions must account for financial viability, environmental and social risks, and contributions to the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), while the evidence needed to assess these dimensions is often dispersed across project files, financial records, policy documents, and sustainability reports.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation proposes an AI-assisted decision-support framework for rural and agrifood investment in Mexico, using the operating context and legal faculties of the Fondo de Capitalización e Inversión del Sector Rural (FOCIR) as an illustrative case.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework combines structured project data with document analysis to support four tasks:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">1) Identification of relevant sustainability indicators;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">2) Traceability of claims about SDG contributions to verifiable evidence;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">3) Flagging of gaps or inconsistencies in environmental, social, and governance (ESG) disclosures</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">4) Comparison of investment options against transparent financial and sustainability criteria.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">It also examines how reporting requirements, including relevant European Sustainability Reporting Standards, could be mapped to the evidence collected from cross-border value chains without assuming that Mexican institutions are directly subject to the EU Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation sets out a proposed workflow, data requirements, human review checkpoints, and an evaluation protocol. The protocol would assess evidence traceability, reviewer agreement, error rates across different types of applicants, and whether the tool improves the consistency and timeliness of decisions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Particular attention is given to data quality, confidentiality, explainability, and the risk that incomplete records could disadvantage smaller producers.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution is a transferable governance model rather than a claim of demonstrated AI performance. The objective is to show how digital tools could help public and private actors make rural investment decisions more transparent and auditable, while keeping responsibility for those decisions with human decision-makers according to the Principles for Responsible Investment-PRI.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 208</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">: ecosystem services, nature-based solutions, food system transformation, sustainable resource management</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Integrated management of Red Palm Weevil using host resistance and native flora for agro-ecological resilience</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Andrew D&#39;Souza<sup>1</sup>, Rajan Shelke<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Department of Chemistry, St. Xavier’s College, Mapusa, Goa, India; 2:&nbsp;Department of Agricultural Entomology, Goa College of Agriculture, Ela Farm, Old Goa, India; <span class='paper_email'>andrewdsouza.dhe@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Abstract</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Red Palm Weevil (RPW, Rhynchophorus ferrugineus) severely threatens coconut palm (Cocos nucifera) cultivation in Goa, India, leading to yield loss and economic instability for smallholders. Current reliance on chemical management overlooks host-plant resistance and ecosystem services. This study evaluates host-plant resistance across regional coconut cultivars and investigates stimulo-deterrent properties of native weed flora to build a bio-based pest management framework for agro-ecological resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Field-collected RPW adults were reconditioned under controlled laboratory conditions. A Four-Arm Choice Olfactometer was used to evaluate behavioral antixenosis (non-preference) across 8 coconut varieties (including Benaulim, Calangute, Chowghat Dwarf variants, and hybrids). Native weed flora from local landscapes were surveyed for repellency. Volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from effective flora were isolated using steam and hydrodistillation, then characterized via GC-MS and LC-MS to identify active deterrent chemical signatures</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Behavioral assays identified varietal differences in RPW olfactory preference, distinguishing coconut cultivars with low attractiveness suitable for strategic planting. Chemical profiling of selected native flora revealed specific VOC signatures responsible for repellent properties. Combining varietal antixenosis with botanical repellents offers an integrated, non-chemical approach that effectively suppresses RPW pressure while preserving beneficial insect biodiversity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions &amp; Relevance to Sustainable Development:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This research presents a scalable, nature-based solution that operationalizes integrated transformations across agricultural and ecological systems. Replacing synthetic pesticides with varietal resistance and native flora deterrence directly enhances crop security (SDG 2), lowers chemical pollution (SDG 12), and protects biodiversity (SDG 15). Looking beyond 2030, this eco-friendly approach provides smallholders with cost-effective tools to strengthen agro-ecosystem resilience, offering a reproducible model for post-2030 sustainable food production and resource management globally.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 209</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">EU CBAM, International trade, Climate policy, Hard-to-abate sectors, Carbon pricing</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">When liabilities depend on information: Conceptualising and analysing the EU CBAM information “wedge”</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nicola Wills</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Town, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">wllnic029@myuct.ac.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The European Union’s Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (EU CBAM) imposes a carbon price on the embedded emissions of certain products imported into the EU. Where verified data for actual emissions cannot be demonstrated in accordance with EU CBAM monitoring, reporting and verification (MRV) requirements, administratively determined country-product default emission intensity values apply. Given that the EU is putting a price on a specific characteristic – embedded emissions – of products that are produced outside of its jurisdiction, default values provide a regulatory solution to imperfect information.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The default values are deliberately conservative to incentivise the measurement and reporting of verified actual emissions, with regulatory mark-ups increasing through 2028. The resulting difference between the applicable default and underlying actual emission intensity (information “wedge”) varies across countries and products, generating heterogeneous economic consequences.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper constructs and analyses this information “wedge” and its economic incidence. A novel country-product dataset is constructed at the eight-digit EU Combined Nomenclature (CN8) level for cement, fertilisers, iron &amp; steel and aluminium. Definitive-period default emission values are harmonised with product-level proxied actual emission estimates from the Commission’s Joint Research Centre. The resulting default–actual differences are examined across sectors, countries, and products. These are then linked to detailed EU import data and translated into implied differences in EU CBAM liability and ad valorem equivalents (AVEs), allowing economic significance to be assessed relative to product values.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Initial findings show substantial heterogeneity in both the magnitude and direction of the information “wedge”. Defaults do not uniformly exceed proxied actual emissions, and similar emissions discrepancies can generate different proportional exposure because product unit values differ. The results therefore show that the incidence of default-based carbon pricing depends not only on underlying emissions intensity, but also on how unavailable emissions information is treated by the regulatory framework.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 210</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">just transitions, social equity, resilience, Uzbekistan</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Just energy transition in Uzbekistan: equitable and resilient?</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Elke Herrfahrdt<sup>1</sup>, Anna Kappel<sup>2</sup>, Isomiddin Akramov<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;German Institute for Development and Sustainability (IDOS), Germany; 2:&nbsp;Deutsche Gesellschaft für Internationale Zusammenarbeit (GIZ), Germany; 3:&nbsp;United Nations Development Programme (UNDP), Tashkent, Uzbekistan; <span class='paper_email'>elke.herrfahrdt@idos-research.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Uzbekistan is highly vulnerable to the impacts of climate change, while its economy is largely based on fossil fuel extraction and is among the world’s most carbon-intensive economies. At the same time, the country has significant untapped sources of solar and wind energy. Framing decarbonisation as a pathway to long-term resilience, and in line with achieving SDG 7, Uzbekistan has recently begun transforming its energy systems by launching legislation to foster the green energy transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: Focusing on equity and resilience, this paper analyses this process by drawing on a literature review, a document analysis and semi-structured expert interviews.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: It asks how equity is addressed and whether Uzbekistan’s energy transition can be regarded a just transition. To this end, it first classifies Uzbekistan’s energy transition process according to the United Nations Research Institute for Social Development (UNRISD) framework on just transitions. It further assesses the role of equity in this process, for example through fairly distributing gains and losses and involving all stakeholders, using the energy justice approach based on distributional, recognition-based and procedural justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: The paper finds that, until recently, the transition had remained at the lower stages of the framework, showing elements of status quo and managerial reform. Equity issues are mainly addressed in terms of distributional and procedural rather than recognition-based justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: The reforms initiated first steps towards a just transition (e.g. financial compensation schemes), but have only recently encompassed the structural and institutional changes (e.g. participation processes) that are necessary for a more equitable energy sector and for generating sustained public support for decarbonisation of the economy as a pathway to long-term resilience. Reliable and clean energy production that is affordable and accessible for all is a key requisite for achieving sustainable development (SDG 7), even beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 211</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">AI-assisted governance; big data; digital governance; ESG policy auditing; CSRD compliance validation; policy-making models</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">AI and Big Data for Decision-Making and Sustainable Governance: A Decision-Support Framework</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Uthaya Santhanam</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">EpicWin, Malaysia; <span class="paper_email">pkx5679@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Abstract</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Public institutions and private organizations face growing pressure to make decisions that are effective, transparent, and consistent with sustainability goals. This paper proposes a decision-support framework that combines artificial intelligence (AI) and big data analytics to inform policy design, monitor outcomes, and assess environmental, social, and governance (ESG) performance. The framework brings together data from administrative systems, corporate disclosures, and other relevant sources to identify patterns, compare policy options, and flag potential gaps in ESG reporting. It also considers how AI-assisted checks could support the validation of disclosures against Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) requirements, while leaving interpretation and final decisions to accountable human reviewers. Rather than treating technical accuracy as the sole measure of success, the paper examines data quality, explainability, privacy, bias, and auditability as conditions for responsible use. By connecting policy-making models with reporting and review processes across the public and private sectors, the proposed framework offers a basis for more consistent, evidence-informed sustainable governance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 212</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.4 Trustworthy AI, information integrity, data autonomy, and sustainability impacts of AI</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">information integrity, trustworthy AI, early warning systems, flood risk, misinformation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Verifying floods before the water arrives: artificial intelligence for early warning and rumour control in Nairobi&#39;s informal settlements</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Samuel Kairu</u>, <u>Amani Wauye</u>, Mary Mwangi, Alvin Mugethi</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">The FloodFact AI, Kenya; <span class="paper_email">kairusamuel328@gmail.com</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">wauyeamani@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Informal settlements in Nairobi face two simultaneous emergencies: recurrent flooding and unverified messages that spread faster than official warnings. This study asks whether an artificial intelligence pipeline, combined with trained community observers, can verify flood reports and rumours within minutes and return trusted alerts through channels residents already use.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: We built and tested FloodFact AI, a six-stage pipeline. Residents and youth ambassadors report by WhatsApp, SMS or web. A language model extracts hazard, location and urgency from English and Swahili messages. A separate deterministic, unit-tested risk engine then scores evidence from rainfall telemetry, mapped flood-risk zones, historical incidents and ambassador ground-truth, and classifies each report as Verified Warning, Elevated Risk or False Information. The language model only explains the verdict; it never decides it. Low-confidence cases escalate to a human operator. We evaluated the prototype in Mukuru kwa Reuben and Mukuru kwa Njenga, Nairobi, using seeded scenarios with sandbox data.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Average verification took 120 seconds, and all test alerts met the 30-minute delivery target. A viral dam-collapse rumour was classified as false information, a flood report corroborated by rainfall, risk-zone and nearby reports was verified, and an ambiguous report was escalated to a human instead of guessed. These are prototype-stage results from a few scenarios, not field-validated performance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Separating language understanding from the safety decision, attaching evidence to every verdict and keeping humans in the loop offers a transparent model for trusted early warning in data-scarce settlements. It supports Sustainable Development Goals 11 and 13, and remains relevant beyond 2030 as climate extremes and misinformation intensify. A six-month pilot with 500 households and 30 youth ambassadors will measure alert timeliness, rumour reduction and verification accuracy, informing scale-up.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 213</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Coastal lagoons, sustainability, ecological wellbeing, governance, socio-ecological systems</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Assessment of the sustainability of coastal lagoons, a case study in favor of the Coastal Daiseas.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Henry Cadenne-Feuillette</u><sup>1</sup>, Esther Maria Leyva Ollivier<sup>1,2</sup>, Alice Newton<sup>1,2</sup>, Valérie Derolez<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of alagarve, Portugal; 2:&nbsp;Centro de investigaçao mariinhas e ambiental, Portugal; 3:&nbsp;IFREMER, France; <span class="paper_email">a90111@ualg.pt</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Coastal lagoons, such as the Thau lagoon in Occitanie (Southern France), are key socio-ecological systems which support biodiversity, economic activities and local communities. This study assesses the sustainability of the Thau lagoon using the Coastal Daiseas framework, a holistic method evolved from the Circles of Coastal Sustainability (CCS). The study evaluates four interconnected domains (environment, economy, social and governance) to provide a comprehensive analysis of the system's resilience and future prospects.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The environmental assessment reveals a mixed picture: land system alteration and ecosystem functions score well due to protected area and biodiversity recovery, but climate change (CC) impacts pose significant threats. The economical analysis highlights the lagoon’s reliance on the shellfish and tourism industries, both vulnerable to climate pressures and market fluctuation. The social domain benefits from strong community welfare and cultural identity but faces challenges like access to property, an ageing population and urbanisation. Governance structures are robust, with effective communication and stakeholder engagement, though some mechanisms around accountability require strengthening.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Key findings indicate that while the Thau lagoon demonstrates good governance and social welfare, its economic vulnerabilities and climate change impacts demand attention. The study identifies gaps in the data and emphasises the need for adaptive management strategies. By integrating scientific and non-scientific data, the research provides insights for policymakers to enhance the lagoon's sustainability, ensuring its ecological and economic viability in the future.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 214</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate change</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Integrated combat on climate change in Nepal</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Niraj karki</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">New Conscious Society, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">nirajkarki.np@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">If people are poor, they won’t be able to cope with climate change impacts, and if the country is poor, it won’t be able to help its people cope with that impact. This is the situation of my country, Nepal, and a global issue. We educate our community of the imminent global climate crisis, we promote and demonstrate green entrepreneurial solutions, and we deliver IMPACT by incubating, supporting, and sustaining social entrepreneurial initiatives. Our mission is to promote sustainable solutions to address the social and environmental challenges in economically feasible ways.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 215</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">true cost accounting; hidden costs; agrifood system; multi-regional input–output analysis; value chain</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Hidden costs across the global agrifood value chain: farm-to-fork true cost accounting</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jingyu Zhang<sup>1</sup>, Eugenia Rosca<sup>2</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen, 9747 AG, The Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Operations Management &amp; Operations Research, Faculty of Economics and Business, University of Groningen, Groningen, 9747 AG, The Netherlands; 3:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Member of the Leibniz Association, P.O. Box 60 12 03, Potsdam, D-14412, Germany; <span class='paper_email'>jingyu.zhang@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Hidden costs of the agrifood system are damages to the environment, human health and livelihoods that are not reflected in market prices. Such costs are increasingly estimated at the national scale, but where they arise along the supply chain, and how they compare with the value captured at each stage, remain poorly understood. We traced global food consumption through a multi-regional input–output (MRIO) model and attributed environmental, health and social costs to six farm-to-fork stages across 163 regions. We estimate that the global agrifood system generated US$2.6 trillion in hidden costs in 2023, comprising $1.2 trillion in environmental damage, $1.1 trillion in health impacts and $0.3 trillion associated with poverty among food-system workers. Primary production accounts for more than three-quarters of total hidden costs, despite capturing a much smaller share of value added. These findings reveal a sharp divergence between cost generation and value capture along the food chain, with primary production being approximately nine times more cost-intensive per dollar of value added than downstream stages. pressure. Our findings suggest that stage-resolved true cost accounting can reveal where hidden costs accumulate relative to value creation, providing a basis for targeting investment, procurement and policy towards the largest sources of hidden costs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 216</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Local Voluntary Review, Local and Regional Governments, Sustainable Development Goals</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Localizing the Sustainable Development Goals through Voluntary Local Reviews in The Gambia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Modou Jonga</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Banjul City Council, Gambia; <span class="paper_email">modou.jonga@banjulcity.gm</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper explores the importance of Local Voluntary Local Reviews (VLR) in the localization and acceleration of the attainment of a the most powerful global blueprint to deliver a better future. The paper regard Local voluntary Reviews as accelerators and an inclusive approach for the localization of the 2030 Agenda. According to the 2030 Agenda (A/RES/70/1: Transforming Our World: The 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development) encourages member states to “conduct regular and inclusive reviews of progress at the national and sub-national levels, which are country-led and country-driven”. The value of VLRs in advancing SDG implementation has been increasingly recognized at national and international level. The Paper used documentary sources to gather information relevant to the thematic area. These included journal articles, books, lLocal and rRegional gGovernments rReports. The researcher also examined the VLR pilot reviews in The Gambia supported by the United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs (UN DESA) and other relevant partners. The Paper strongly recommends peer-to-peer learning to enrich VLR processes at local level with a view to bolster local action.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 217</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.2 Lessons learned from SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">transdisciplinary research, SDG governance, participatory process, SDGs, Sub-Saharan Africa, North-South research partnerships</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Do We Really Work Transdisciplinarily? Lessons Learnt from 15 SDG-Related Initiatives in Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Laetitia Chapdelaine</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Nora Engel</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Dirk Essink</u><sup>1</sup>, Roel Kamerling<sup>2</sup>, Olive Dushime<sup>3</sup>, Tekalign Sahilu<sup>3</sup>, Ricky Janssen<sup>4</sup>, Pia Mager<sup>4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam, Netherlands, The; 2:&nbsp;TU Delft; 3:&nbsp;Sustainable Development Goals Center for Africa, Rwanda; 4:&nbsp;Maastricht University; <span class="paper_email">l.m.chapdelaine@vu.nl</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">n.c.engel@vu.nl</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">d.r.essink@vu.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Achieving the SDGs in Sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) requires confronting interconnected environmental, cultural, economic, and social sustainability challenges while engaging actors across sectors and governance levels. Transdisciplinary research (TDR) is increasingly promoted as a participatory approach to such challenges, with the expectation that involving non-academic actors and experiential knowledge holders in research can strengthen local ownership, relevance, and the long term viability of interventions. Yet, the language of TDR can outpace its practice, leaving participation shallow or procedural rather than leading to genuine shifts in authority and material conditions. Drawing on a multiple case study analysis of 15 SDG-related TDR initiatives across SSA, this presentation examines lessons for SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships, focusing on whose knowledge, priorities, and authority shape collaborative sustainability research.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The study combines a scoping review, document analysis, and 35 semi-structured interviews with practitioners, researchers, and community members involved in TDR initiatives across East, Southern, West, and Central Africa. Together, these sources provide insight into how institutional arrangements, power differentials, and historically rooted inequities in knowledge production shape TDR collaborations, while also highlighting participants’ experiences of the participatory process, its perceived impacts, and the conditions that enable or constrain meaningful engagement.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Preliminary findings suggest that the central challenge may not be whether participation occurs, but whether participants can fully shape problem definitions, methods, resources, and outcomes. Funder priorities and academic incentive structures can concentrate decision-making within better-resourced institutions, while insufficient investment in trust, relational labor, and contextually grounded research can marginalize local knowledge and contribute to disengagement. More promising approaches build on locally anchored leadership and relationships of trust, while giving embedded organizations meaningful roles, centering community-defined priorities, and providing flexible funding. These findings highlight the institutional and relational conditions needed for TDR to move beyond consultation toward more just, effective, and community-rooted participation in sustainable development.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 218</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Women’s economic empowerment, Cultural heritage, Local Communities, Circular Economy, Territorial resilience.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Cultural Heritage as a Tool for Sustainable Transformation: The Cultural and Creative Industries in Support of Climate Resilience in Morocco</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Asmaa Benachir</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Au Grain de Séame, Morocco; <span class='paper_email'>info@augraindesesame.org</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Because the transition concerns us all.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">How can we make ecological transitions understandable, accessible, and practically applicable to local communities and economic actors? How can we turn cultural heritage, traditional skills, creativity, and cultural exchanges into drivers of change amid contemporary environmental and social challenges?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation highlights nearly twenty years of experimentation conducted in Morocco by Asmaa Benachir, a visual artist, consultant, and founder of Au Grain de Sésame (AGS), a Moroccan social enterprise led by a woman that has been active since 2008 at the intersection of culture, the cultural and creative industries (CCI), sustainable design, the circular economy, and women’s economic empowerment. This approach has several international recognitions and collaborations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">AGS views artisanal skills not only as a heritage to be preserved, but as resources of knowledge, wealth, and innovation. Its educational approach, based on experience and learning by doing (observing, experimenting, creating, reproducing, exchanging, and passing on knowledge), enables the organization to address environmental challenges by drawing on local realities and varying levels of knowledge and skills.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Through collaborations with cooperatives and micro-enterprises in Morocco, AGS draws on local techniques (basketry, pottery, jewelry-making, leather goods, natural fibers, recycling, reuse,...). The goal is to connect heritage, creativity, and contemporary practices to promote eco-design, circularity, innovation, and local value.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper analyzes three interdependent transformations:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• ways of learning and understanding environmental issues;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• transforming production practices and value chains by leveraging the circular economy, eco-design, local techniques, and resources available in the regions;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• examining the conditions for adaptation and scaling up, as well as the potential of artificial intelligence as a complementary tool for learning, research, innovation, and design.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This initiative thus offers an integrated approach to regional resilience, linking culture, the circular economy, inclusion, innovation, and local action.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 219</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Just Circular Economy; Electric Mobility; Battery Circularity; Gender Justice; Sustainability Transitions</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Who Bears the Cost of a Clean Transition? Battery Circularity and Gender Justice in India’s Electric Mobility Shift</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sanoj Sanoj</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Jawaharlal Nehru University, India; <span class="paper_email">sanoj156@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">India’s transition to electric mobility is increasingly framed as a pathway towards decarbonisation, energy security, and sustainable development. Yet, the environmental benefits of electric vehicles cannot be understood through vehicle adoption and reduced tailpipe emissions alone. Battery production, critical-minerals dependence, resource recovery, labour, and end-of-life management create new material and social challenges. This paper examines these less visible dimensions through the concept of just circularity, asking who benefits from and who bears the environmental, economic, and social costs of battery circularity in India’s emerging electric mobility transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper builds on doctoral research on lead-acid battery waste management in Delhi-NCR, which used mixed methods and a systems-oriented approach to examine formal and informal recycling, stakeholder practices, resource recovery, and policy implementation. It extends this research to electric-vehicle batteries, with particular attention to marginalised women associated to resource-recovery systems. Rather than viewing these women solely as vulnerable workers exposed to environmental risks, the paper recognises them as economic actors, knowledge holders, and participants in circular economies. The analysis brings Just Transition Theory and Sustainability Transitions Theory into dialogue, complemented by insights from Feminist Political Ecology. This framework examines how labour, risks, resources, knowledge, recognition, and decision-making are distributed across emerging battery value chains. The paper argues that circularity does not automatically produce justice. Battery recycling may reduce pressure on primary resources while simultaneously reproducing unequal labour relations and environmental burdens. A just circular economy therefore requires attention not only to material efficiency and resource recovery but also to recognition, participation, livelihoods, and equitable distribution of transition costs and benefits. The paper contributes to debates on sustainable mobility, circular economy, environmental justice, and just transitions in the Global South.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 220</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Loss and Damage Fund, Global South, Mal-adaptation, Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Accelerating Just Transitions through SDG 2 and 13 investments in LDCs: Can Loss and Damage finance be a catalyst</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Dumisani Chirambo</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Seeds of Opportunity, Malawi; <span class="paper_email">sofopportunity@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Least Developed Countries (LDCs) suffer a double development tragedy in that (i) they have comparatively low levels of greenhouse gas emissions yet they are amongst most vulnerable groups to climate change, and (ii) they attract inadequate climate finance to support adaptation and mitigation efforts. This scenario highlights that many developing countries are at a risk of not achieving Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 2 (no hunger) and SDG 13 (climate action) due to investment gaps in their just transition programmes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Achieving “Just” and “Equitable” transitions in LDCs is also problematic due to policy conflicts and mal-adaptations. For example, Malawi’s farm input subsidy programme which focuses on providing subsidised inorganic fertiliser, has left the country more vulnerable to climate change as it constitutes 64% of the agriculture ministry’s budget, consumes 9% of national spending, and increases the country’s emissions profile.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This research employs case-study and comparative analyses to examine policy approaches for accelerating Just Transitions LDCs, particularly under conditions of unpredictable climate finance. It also conceptualises how successful mitigation practices can be adapted and introduced in LDCs through the strategic use of Loss and Damage (L&amp;D) financing.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper discovered that most financing for L&amp;D in the agriculture sector is associated with humanitarian assistance hence limiting the impact that L&amp;D finance can have on sustainable development. The paper recommends that policies advancing Just Transitions in LDCs may utilise L&amp;D finance as part of blended finance for (i) strengthening the capacity of local entrepreneurs to establish low-carbon energy and circular-economy services, and (ii) supporting farmers to restore and sustainably manage marginal and degraded lands.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 221</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Land policy; Land reform; SDGs; Policy implementation; South Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Bridging the Implementation Gap in South Africa’s Land Policy: Lessons for Sustainable Development Beyond 2030</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>John Laisani</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of South Africa, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">laisaj@unisa.ac.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">South Africa’s land reform policy embodies commitments to redress historical dispossession, reduce inequality and improve rural livelihoods, yet persistent implementation challenges raise questions about the extent to which land policy has contributed to sustainable development during the Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) era. This study examines what South Africa’s land policy experience reveals about translating sustainable development commitments into tangible outcomes, with particular attention to policy implementation, institutional coordination and beneficiary support. A qualitative document analysis was undertaken of land policy and legislative instruments, government reports, parliamentary reviews and scholarly studies published during the SDG period. The analysis examined the relationship between policy intentions, implementation arrangements and reported socio-economic outcomes. Findings reveal a persistent implementation gap: land access has not consistently been accompanied by coordinated institutional support, productive land-use opportunities, effective beneficiary participation and outcome-oriented monitoring. These shortcomings have constrained the capacity of land reform to translate access to land into sustainable livelihoods and broader rural development outcomes. The findings suggest that a post-2030 development agenda cannot rely primarily on policy commitments or output-based measures of land delivery. Sustainable land policy requires integrated implementation arrangements that connect land access with institutional accountability, productive use, livelihood outcomes and adaptive monitoring. The South African experience therefore demonstrates the importance of designing development policies around implementation systems and measurable outcomes, rather than treating policy adoption and programme delivery as proxies for sustainable development.</p>



</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 222</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">food system, sustainability assessment, indicator framework, principal component analysis, composite indices</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Construction of a global food system sustainability indicator framework</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Yizhi Deng<sup>1</sup>, Chaohui Li<sup>2,3</sup>, Yuanchao Hu<sup>4,5</sup>, Tim G. Benton<sup>6,7</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,8</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment, and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen, The Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Climate Variability and Change Group, Earth Sciences Department, Barcelona Supercomputing Center, Barcelona, Spain; 3:&nbsp;Bauhaus Earth, Bauhaus der Erde gGmbH, Berlin, Germany; 4:&nbsp;School of Ecology, Environment and Resources, Guangdong University of Technology, Guangzhou, China; 5:&nbsp;Oxford Martin School, Department of Biology, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK; 6:&nbsp;Royal Institute of International Affairs, Chatham House, London, UK; 7:&nbsp;School of Biology, University of Leeds, Leeds, UK; 8:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Member of the Leibniz Association, Potsdam, Germany; <span class='paper_email'>yizhi.deng@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Food systems underpin human nutrition and livelihoods but are currently on unsustainable trajectories, failing to ensure global food security while exerting substantial environmental pressures. However, there is still a lack of a unified indicator framework for comprehensively assessing food system sustainability. This study aims to develop a global indicator framework that integrates environmental, socioeconomic, and nutrition-health dimensions across multiple food system components.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Indicator selection was guided by four criteria: conceptual relevance, measurement reliability, cross-country comparability, and data availability. Most indicators were drawn from existing frameworks, official datasets, and relevant literature, supplemented by a small number of newly developed indicators to enhance the comprehensiveness of our framework. We employed Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to reduce indicator redundancy while preserving the main patterns of variation in the original dataset. We then constructed a set of composite indices to assess overall food system sustainability and performance across individual sustainability dimensions and food system components, enabling comparisons across countries and over time.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The assessment results are expected to reveal global trends in food system sustainability while uncovering substantial heterogeneity across countries, sustainability dimensions, and food system components. These findings will highlight uneven progress and persistent sustainability challenges that could be obscured by aggregate sustainability indices. The multidimensional analysis will further identify potential synergies and trade-offs, revealing how improvements in one sustainability dimension or food system component may coincide with stagnation or deterioration in others.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study develops an integrated framework for assessing global food system sustainability, providing a foundation for more targeted policy interventions. By capturing interconnections among environmental, socioeconomic, and nutrition-health dimensions, the framework can support policymakers and researchers in designing strategies that balance competing sustainability objectives and facilitate the transition towards more sustainable, resilient, and just food systems through 2030 and beyond.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 223</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate displacement; Climate mobility; Legal protection; Bangladesh; Post-2030 sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate Displacement and Legal Protection in Vulnerable States: Lessons from Bangladesh for the Post-2030 Agenda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Md Biplob Ahmed</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Metropolitan University, Sylhet, People&#39;s Republic of  Bangladesh,; <span class="paper_email">241113059@student.metrouni.ac.bd</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study examines whether existing international and national legal frameworks provide consistent protection for climate refugees and why fragmentation is important for fragile states affected by climate-related migration. It uses Bangladesh as a case study to assess the interaction between refugees, human rights, climate, disasters and domestic law and what this means for the governance of climate mobility on the basis of rights in 2030 and beyond.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">It adopts doctrinal and comparative methodologies. It follows up on international and Bangladeshi legislation, relevant climate, disaster risk and migration instruments, Bangladesh&#39;s internal displacement strategy and action plan, and selected comparative models of relocation and implementation based on peer-reviewed empirical evidence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The analysis reveals a meaningful but patchy protective structure. Protection of refugees is based on established treaty criteria; human rights law protects against non-refoulement without granting a qualified individual migration status; and climate law increasingly governs human mobility but does not generally provide for an individual. Bangladesh has made progress in developing a national internal displacement strategy and an implementation plan based on prevention, protection and durable solutions, but evaluations point to persisting coordination and funding problems and no specific legal status on climate change displacement. Research shows current SDGs cover aspects of migration, disaster impact, resilient cities and climate resilience and institutions, but do not provide an integrated framework that includes measures of displacement prevention, durability and sustainability.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The results reinforce strengthening the governance of mobility based on rights in existing regimes rather than creating a category of climate refugees. Bangladesh provides lessons on displacement-sensitive indicators, anticipatory measures, planned resettlement protection and land and housing protection, and social protection with participatory and accountable financing, and the maintenance of human rights standards for refugees in future-proofing development by 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 224</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Indigenous Knowledge, Climate Change, Community Based Learning, Climate Change Education</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Reimagining Climate Resilience through Community Learning: Indigenous Knowledge and Climate Change Education among Smallholder Farmers in Eastern Uganda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Muhammed Kiggundu</u>, Kellen Aganyira, Ahimbisibwe Karembe</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Makerere University, Uganda; <span class="paper_email">kiggundumuhammedhadid29@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change is increasingly challenging the livelihoods and adaptive capacities of smallholder farmers across rural Africa. While climate change responses have often emphasized technical interventions and externally generated scientific knowledge, less attention has been given to how rural communities learn about climate change through everyday experiences, social relationships, and Indigenous Knowledge systems. This paper examined how community-based learning and Indigenous Knowledge contribute to climate resilience among smallholder farmers in Eastern Uganda. Drawing on a qualitative case study in the Mountain Elgon region, the study explored how farmers acquire, interpret, share, and apply knowledge in responding to changing climatic conditions and how different forms of knowledge interact within everyday adaptation practices. Data were generated through semi-structured interviews, a focus group discussion, and observation involving smallholder farmers, local leaders, and a youth representative. The findings indicate that climate-related learning occurs through multiple formal, non-formal, and informal pathways, including intergenerational knowledge transmission, peer learning, farmer groups, local languages, radio, extension services, and community interactions. Farmers also draw on locally situated indicators of environmental change, including observations of birds, winds, clouds, seasons, and other ecological signals, alongside scientific climate information. These knowledge systems inform practices such as crop diversification, agroforestry, soil and water conservation, and other locally appropriate adaptation strategies. The paper argues that strengthening climate resilience requires greater recognition of communities as knowledge holders and active participants in sustainable development, rather than treating them solely as recipients of externally produced climate information. It concludes that integrating Indigenous Knowledge and community learning into climate change education can contribute to more locally relevant, inclusive, and equitable pathways for climate-resilient development. These lessons are particularly relevant to ongoing efforts to accelerate the Sustainable Development Goals and reimagine sustainable development beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 225</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">citizen science, participatory processes, co-design, research design, methodological trade-offs</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">More genuine participation, less robust evidence? Testing a decision matrix for citizen science in sustainability research</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Birgit Teufer</u><sup>1</sup>, Pia Riedl<sup>1</sup>, Sebastian Seebauer<sup>2</sup>, Hans-Peter Ellmer<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;FERNFH University of Applied Sciences, Austria; 2:&nbsp;JOANNEUM RESEARCH Forschungsgesellschaft mbH; <span class="paper_email">birgit.teufer@fernfh.ac.at</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective. </strong>Citizen science is increasingly promoted as a means of involving citizens in sustainability research and supporting progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Yet participatory research implies methodological choices that are rarely neutral. Increasing participants’ influence over research questions, interventions or data collection may enhance local relevance and ownership while affecting standardisation, comparability and connection to current debates in academic research. This contribution presents a structured decision matrix designed to make such choices and their trade-offs explicit rather than pitting maximum participation versus maximum methodological control against each other.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods. </strong>The matrix structures methodological decisions arising in citizen science on sustainability topics. It guides users through key design choices concerning participation and research design and prompts them to document the aims pursued, anticipated methodological consequences, benefits and risks, contextual conditions, alternatives and the reasoning behind their decisions. The matrix will be empirically tested in facilitated workshops using citizen-science cases on transportation, food consumption, or biodiversity-friendly gardening. Participants will use it to make concrete design decisions and formulate transferable “if–then–because” decision rules. Matrix outputs will be compared between the citizen-science cases.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Key results. </strong>The current methodological output is a structured decision tool that links choices about citizen participation to their implications for research design rather than prescribing a universally optimal level of participation. Empirical testing before the conference will examine its usability, the decision logics it elicits, and its dependency on specific case contexts.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusions. </strong>Achieving the SDGs and shaping sustainability agendas beyond 2030 require participatory approaches that are not only inclusive but also methodologically transparent and scientifically sound. The proposed matrix offers a structured way of making participation-related design choices explicit and open to critical reflection. Results from its empirical application will inform its further development and its potential use in citizen science in sustainability research.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 226</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">science-based target-setting, indicator development, SDG indicators, natural capital, strong sustainability</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Can the Sustainable Development Goals monitor environmental sustainability? Lessons from strong sustainability for the post-2030 monitoring framework</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Arkaitz Usubiaga-Liaño</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Basque Centre for Climate Change, Spain; <span class="paper_email">arkaitz.usubiaga@bc3research.org</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainable Development Goals (SDG) indicators are widely used to monitor national performance on the three dimensions of sustainable development. This contribution assesses whether SDG indicator sets can effectively monitor environmental sustainability, understood from a strong sustainability perspective, and what this implies for the post-2030 monitoring framework.</p>


<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">A qualitative screening of the indicators in four SDG indicator sets (United Nations, OECD, Eurostat and the Sustainable Development Solutions Network's SDG Index) assesses whether those sets can track environmental sustainability when this is understood as the maintenance of the environmental functions of natural capital. A quantitative comparison between (1) the Strong Environmental Sustainability Index (SESI) that measures performance against science-based targets, and (2) the global SDG Index and the SDG Index for Europe shows the numerical differences in EU27 countries in 2022 and the different urgency implied by those metrics.</p>


<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Results</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Depending on the set, environmental and natural capital indicators represent 32–42% and 9–20% of all indicators, respectively. Of the environmental indicators of the SDGs, between 0 and 34% rely on science-based targets to measure performance, as most reference values are directional targets, policy targets or benchmarks based on best performers. Consequently, SDG sets have limitations in representing environmental sustainability.</p>


<p class="paper_abstract">The quantitative comparison confirms this: SDG-based indices portray a considerably more favourable picture than SESI. Median environmental scores are around 78 (global SDG Index) and 79 (SDG Index for Europe) out of 100, compared with around 31 against science-based targets, with median country-level differences of 43 and 45 points, respectively.</p>


<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusions</strong></p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The current SDG framework was not designed to monitor environmental sustainability and can convey messages that downplay environmental degradation. The post-2030 monitoring framework should consider science-based targets in the selection process of environmental indicators, and invest in the data and capacity needed to apply them globally.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 227</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Capacities, climate change, gender equality, policy gaps, SSA, vulnerabilities</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">An intersectional analysis of gender equality and climate change vulnerabilities capacities and policy gaps in sub-Saharan Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>INNOCENT MUHWEZI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">UNIVERSITY OF GHANA, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">imuhwezi@st.ug.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Gender disparities in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) heighten the vulnerability of the women and marginalised groups to climate variation because they rely on climate-sensitive livelihoods. However, systemic barriers and entrenched norms hinder their roles in climate adaptation and mitigation efforts. This review paper explored how gender disparities intersect with climate change by examining vulnerabilities, the capacity of marginalised genders for adaptation and mitigation, and policy gaps hindering gender-responsive climate action in Sub-Saharan Africa. The paper methodically mapped studies on gender equality and climate change using the PRISMA-SCR methodological framework. The paper analysed twenty-five (25) peer-reviewed articles published between (2016–2024) to explore intersections between gender disparities, climate impacts, and policies. Key findings indicate that compared to men, women experience heightened vulnerabilities. These gendered vulnerabilities to climate change in Sub-Saharan Africa are rooted in systemic exclusion, limited access to resources, insecure land tenure, cultural norms, and inadequate representation in decision-making processes. Despite these challenges, women play a crucial role in climate adaptation through their local knowledge, community initiatives, and innovative solutions, highlighting significant capacities that enhance climate resilience. However, critical policy gaps of lack of gender-responsive frameworks, absence of gender-disaggregated data, and limited capacity-building initiatives, which hinder effective climate action and inclusive climate action persist. Therefore, bridging these gaps through inclusive gendersensitive policies is essential for fostering sustainable development and ensuring equitable climate resilience. Nevertheless, addressing these challenges requires coordinated global and regional efforts to mainstream gender in climate policies through targeted empowerment, inclusive governance, capacity-building, gender-inclusive and sensitive policies, and gender-disaggregated data collection.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 228</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Keywords: SDG 6, WASH coverage, rural Ethiopia, safely managed drinking water, sanitation, hygiene, climate resilience, service sustainability, operation and maintenance, and post-2030 development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for Sustainable WASH Development Beyond 2030: Gaps, Challenges, and Opportunities in Rural Ethiopia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Girma Merga</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Expertise France, Ethiopia; <span class="paper_email">keol2002@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">




ABSTRACTLessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for Sustainable WASH Development Beyond 2030: Gaps, Challenges, and Opportunities in Rural Ethiopia
<p class="paper_abstract">Access to safe and sustainable water, sanitation, and hygiene (WASH) services is fundamental to public health, human dignity, and socioeconomic development. Sustainable Development Goal 6 (SDG 6) aims to ensure universal access to water and sanitation by 2030. Despite progress in expanding WASH infrastructure in Ethiopia, significant gaps persist, particularly in rural, pastoralist, conflict-affected, and climate-vulnerable communities.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This paper examines progress, challenges, and lessons learned from SDG 6 implementation in Ethiopia, focusing on rural WASH coverage, service quality, sustainability, and institutional capacity. Major challenges include inadequate access to safely managed drinking water, limited sanitation and hygiene coverage, open defecation, unreliable water supplies, weak operation and maintenance systems, insufficient financing, limited technical capacity, and fragmented institutional coordination. Climate change, recurrent droughts, floods, poverty, conflict, and displacement further exacerbate these challenges. Additionally, prioritizing infrastructure construction over functionality, water quality, service reliability, and long-term sustainability has constrained progress toward universal access.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Lessons from SDG implementation highlight the need to shift from infrastructure-focused interventions toward integrated, inclusive, climate-resilient, and service-oriented WASH programming. Strengthening community ownership, institutional capacity, sustainable financing, operation and maintenance systems, and accountability is essential for sustaining investments. Integrating WASH with public health, environmental protection, watershed management, and climate adaptation, alongside gender-responsive and disability-inclusive approaches, is equally important.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Beyond 2030, Ethiopia's rural WASH agenda should prioritize equitable access to safely managed, reliable, and climate-resilient services through evidence-based planning, innovative financing, renewable energy, digital monitoring, and strengthened governance. The paper emphasizes that sustainable WASH development requires moving beyond infrastructure coverage toward functional, accessible, and accountable services that address rural communities' needs, strengthen resilience, and ensure long-term sustainability.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Keywords: SDG 6, WASH, rural Ethiopia, water supply, sanitation, hygiene, climate resilience, sustainability, operation and maintenance, post-2030 development.</p>







</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 229</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change, Sexual and reproductive health and rights, Yahoo Plus, Reproductive precarity, Gendered vulnerability, Nigeria</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Yahoo Plus, Ritual Anxiety, and Reproductive Health in the Era of Climate Change, Evidence from a Selected Community in Southwestern Nigeria</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Olawale Akinrinde</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Osun State University; <span class="paper_email">olawale.akinrinde@uniosun.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change poses significant threats to sexual and reproductive health and rights (SRHR) across Africa. Yet, scholarship has largely emphasised direct pathways involving food insecurity, displacement, disease, and weakened healthcare systems. Less attention has been paid to the indirect socioeconomic mechanisms through which climate-induced livelihood disruptions reshape gender relations and reproductive vulnerabilities. This paper addresses this gap by examining the intersection of climate change, the cybercrime economy popularly known as Yahoo Plus, and women’s SRHR in a selected community in Osogbo, Southwestern Nigeria.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study argues that livelihood insecurity associated with climate variability, declining agricultural productivity, and shrinking economic opportunities can encourage alternative income-generating strategies, including illicit ones, among urban youth. Within this context, narratives surrounding Yahoo Plus include allegations of ritual practices, transactional sexual relationships, reproductive exploitation, psychological coercion, and symbolic violence against women. Although such claims remain contested, their social consequences may shape perceptions of risk, bodily autonomy, intimate relationships, and reproductive decision-making.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper develops Climate-Induced Reproductive Precarity Theory (CIRPT), proposing that climate-related economic stress can expand informal and illicit survival economies that generate reproductive vulnerabilities. Through reproductive survival chains, climate stressors become indirectly connected to SRHR risks through economic desperation, gendered power asymmetries, and the commodification of intimate relationships.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using a qualitative case-study design, the study draws on semi-structured interviews, focus groups, key informant interviews, and media discourse analysis involving women, youth, community leaders, health practitioners, and civil society actors. The study contributes to climate justice and SRHR scholarship by showing how climate vulnerability, digital informal economies, and gendered reproductive risks intersect in contemporary African societies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 230</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.4 Trustworthy AI, information integrity, data autonomy, and sustainability impacts of AI</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Epistemic agency; artificial intelligence; sustainable development; epistemic justice; peace and security</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Enabling or Defining? AI, Digital Infrastructures and the Epistemic Politics of Sustainability in an Age of Emerging Security Technologies</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Qurat ul ain Qurat ul ain</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">CECOS College London, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">qurat.tutor@cecos.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">If artificial intelligence, big data and digitalisation are to enable sustainable development, we must ask who determines what &quot;sustainable development&quot; means once these systems help define it. Dominant accounts treat such technologies as neutral instruments for delivering the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). This conceptual paper argues instead that they are epistemic infrastructures: systems that shape what counts as knowledge, which risks become visible, and whose priorities inform policy.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The opportunities are considerable. Advanced analytics, satellite observation and decision-support systems can strengthen evidence-based policymaking, improve monitoring of progress, enable earlier identification of environmental, humanitarian and security risks, and open new possibilities for conflict prevention and peacebuilding. Yet the same systems can reproduce unequal data infrastructures, embed algorithmic bias, marginalise situated and community-based knowledge, amplify disinformation, obscure accountability through opacity, and concentrate technological power. Growing reliance on AI-generated knowledge risks eroding the epistemic agency of the people and institutions expected to interpret and act on it.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on feminist theory, epistemic justice and critical security studies, the paper develops epistemic agency as its analytical lens. It asks whether development can be inclusive when the tools that generate evidence and guide decisions also determine whose knowledge is recognised as legitimate. The emerging AI–space–nuclear nexus serves as an illustrative case rather than a primary focus. It shows how the convergence of AI, satellite data and strategic systems can enhance analytical capacity while complicating human responsibility, blurring civilian and military applications.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Looking beyond 2030, the paper argues that future sustainability agendas must treat data governance, algorithmic accountability and epistemic pluralism as core conditions of sustainable development, not technical add-ons. It contributes to debates on AI, digitalisation, peace and security by reframing digital enablement as a question of epistemic justice and human agency, offering a critical framework for assessing whose knowledge and decisions shape sustainability futures.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 231</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.1 Modeling Earth system interactions, including climate impacts on development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">land use change, DPSIR framework, causal chains, systematic review</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Understanding Causal Chains of Land Use Change: A Systematic Review Based on the DPSIR Framework</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Xiaochun Xu</u><sup>1</sup>, Kshitij Dahal<sup>2</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen, The Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;School of Sustainable Engineering and the Built Environment, Arizona State University, Tempe, AZ, USA; 3:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Member of the Leibniz Association, P.O. Box 60 12 03, Potsdam, 14412, Germany; <span class="paper_email">xiaochun.xu@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Land use change is a key driver of environmental change, yet its drivers and impacts are interlinked through feedback that studies rarely capture within a unified framework. We systematically reviewed 383 studies through the Driver-Pressure-State-Impact-Response (DPSIR) framework adapted to land use, providing the first synthesis that links fragmented causal chains of land use and locates where evidence is concentrated or missing. The evidence shows that urbanization and cropland expansion reduced forest and grassland, causing ecological loss and intensifying natural hazards. Human activities act in both directions: anthropogenic pressure degrades forest and grassland, whereas land use policy promotes their recovery. Beyond changes in area, land use intensity changes with both land conversion and land use management, and the resulting ecological impacts are nonlinear and spatially heterogeneous. However, the evidence is dominated by short causal chains that link land conversion under single pressures to ecological impacts. Compound pressures, land use intensity, socioeconomic impacts and feedback from land use change to natural hazards remain underexplored, and anthropogenic pressure and policy responses are rarely assessed together. As a result, it remains unclear whether land use change forms vicious or virtuous cycles. We argue that future research should extend short causal chains into complete causal loops by quantifying how far responses offset the ecological damage caused by anthropogenic pressure, tracing how land use change feeds back to natural hazards and evaluating the combined impacts of changes in land use quantity and quality.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 232</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Africa, Climate-Resilient, carbon cycle modeling, Sustainable Renaissance, Sectoral</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Quest for a Sustainable African Renaissance: Sectoral Determinants and Synergies for Climate-Resilient Development</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Felix Aberu</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Tai Solarin Federal University of Education, Ijagun, Ogun State, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">felixaberu@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Africa’s push for sustainable development beyond 2030 will increasingly rely on mainstreaming economic, social and environmental agenda within a shared development paradigm. However, evidence on the joint drivers of these sustainability objectives is scanty. Based on the Triple Bottom Line (TBL) theory, which suggests that sustainability is a process of moving towards achieving economic, social, and environmental goals concurrently, this research adds to the discussion on the trajectory of development in Africa by examining whether electricity, education, agriculture, and industrial performance lead to environmental sustainability in five African countries (2000-2025). The dynamic panel Generalized Method of Moments (GMM) was estimated to address issues of persistence and endogeneity. Results indicate significant persistence in environmental performance (EP), evidenced by a lagged coefficient of 1.02 (p&lt;0.01). Electricity and education performance have positive and significant coefficients of (0.46; p=0.05) and (0.48; p=0.01) respectively. Marginal effects suggest that a 1% improvement in electricity and education is associated with approximately 0.46% and 0.48% increase in EP, respectively. Improvements in carbon emissions are associated with declines in EP (-0.07; p&lt;0.01). One percent increase in carbon emissions will reduce EP by 0.07%. Similarly, industrial performance crowds out EP by approximately 0.01% (-0.01; p=0.01). The agriculture coefficient is negative but insignificant (-0.05; p=0.22). Hence, the results corroborate the TBL hypothesis, as environmental sustainability is closely related to the energy system, human capital, and economic activities. The results underscore the implementation of coherent policies that involve economic transformation and social development in combination with the environment, if Africa&#39;s inclusive, resilient and sustainable development agenda after 2030 is to be strengthened.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 233</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Policy uncertainty, carbon markets, clean energy, MS-MIDAS, sustainable finance.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Policy Uncertainty and Market Resilience in Carbon and Clean Energy Markets: A Regime-Switching Perspective</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Damilola Aboluwodi</u><sup>1</sup>, Kerry Mccullough<sup>2</sup>, Paul-Francois Muzindutsi<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of KwaZulu-Natal; 2:&nbsp;University of KwaZulu-Natal; 3:&nbsp;University of KwaZulu-Natal; <span class="paper_email">aboluwodid@ukzn.co.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Carbon and clean energy markets support the low-carbon transition, yet their responses to policy uncertainty may differ across stable and stressed conditions. Understanding these differences is relevant to financing climate action and sustaining progress towards SDGs 7 and 13. This study examines regime-dependent relationships between policy uncertainty and returns in the European Union Emissions Trading System (EU ETS) and the S&amp;P Global Clean Energy Index (SPGCEI).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using Bloomberg data, a Markov-switching mixed-data sampling (MS-MIDAS) framework combines daily market returns with monthly measures of Climate Policy Uncertainty, Global Economic Policy Uncertainty, and Energy-Related Uncertainty. The model preserves the original data frequencies while estimating latent market states, regime-specific uncertainty effects, and time-varying volatility.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings reveal pronounced differences in market dynamics. Clean energy returns exhibit a persistent low-volatility bull regime, with a continuation probability of approximately 98.7% and an expected duration of 77 trading days, compared with 16 trading days for the high-volatility bear regime. The EU ETS displays greater asymmetry, characterised by persistent low-volatility conditions interrupted by brief but more intense high-volatility episodes. Policy uncertainty coefficients vary in direction and magnitude across regimes, but their generally insignificant estimates provide limited evidence of a systematic direct effect on returns.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">These results highlight distinct risk profiles within the low-carbon investment universe and caution against treating carbon and clean energy assets as interchangeable. The study contributes to discussions on sustainable transformation by showing how market instability differs across instruments supporting decarbonisation. Its findings inform portfolio diversification and carbon risk management, while providing a basis for further research on how policy credibility can support resilient transition finance beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 234</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">business models, strategic sustainability management, stakeholder legitimacy, value chains, post-2030 agenda</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Connecting the missing links: how business model innovation integrates capabilities, legitimacy, and adaptation for post-2030 corporate sustainability</p>
<p class="paper_author"> HANDE KARADAG</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">MEF University, Turkey (Türkiye); <span class='paper_email'>hande.karadag@mef.edu.tr</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Corporate contributions to the Sustainable Development Goals often stall because firms pursue sustainability through fragmented initiatives: capabilities that fail to earn stakeholder legitimacy, stakeholder engagement without operational substance, or adaptation to systemic change without the capabilities to sustain it. Management theory mirrors this fragmentation by treating resource-based, stakeholder, and systems perspectives as separate lenses. This paper asks how the three perspectives reinforce one another and which organizational mechanism activates their integration.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The study follows a conceptual framework development approach. It combines a structured review of the three theoretical traditions, a comparative analysis of prior integration efforts, and logical derivation of causal mechanisms, illustrated through four organizational configurations using public sustainability disclosures.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: The resulting Dynamic Integration Framework treats capability development, legitimacy building, and adaptive integration as interdependent dimensions linked by three reinforcing dynamics. Early adaptation to systemic change builds legitimacy through credible commitment signals; legitimacy supports capability development through resource access and protection from imitation; and capability development funds and informs further adaptation through slack resources and learning. Business model innovation, through value proposition reconfiguration, operational redesign, multi-stream value capture, and ecosystem governance, activates all three dynamics at once. The illustrative configurations show that emphasis on a single dimension produces characteristic failures, including greenwashing risk when legitimacy claims outpace operational change.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: For a post-2030 agenda, the framework shifts attention from strengthening individual sustainability capabilities to governing the connections among capability development, stakeholder legitimacy, and systemic adaptation, with business model innovation as the organizational vehicle. Policy can support this by strengthening the links between dimensions: transparency requirements, including double materiality reporting, connect capabilities to legitimacy; preferential procurement connects legitimacy to capability; and collaborative research connects capability to adaptation. Because these mechanisms operate differently where formal verification is weak, integration strategies must be adapted to institutional context.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 235</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.2 Environmental health and emerging pollutants, including regulatory and governance responses, and policy translation of exposure science</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change, male reproductive health, semen quality, environmental exposure, sustainability</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate-Sensitive and Environmental Threats to Male Reproductive Health in Low- and Middle-Income Countries: Implications for Sustainable and Climate-Resilient Health Systems</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Samuel Kofi Arhin</u>, Isaiah Kofi Arhin</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast School of Medical Sciences, Ghana, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">samuel.arhin2@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background: Climate change and environmental deterioration are increasingly acknowledged as threats to human health; however, their impacts on male reproductive health have not been sufficiently integrated into discussions on sustainability and climate-related health concerns. Rising temperatures, heat exposure, air pollution, pesticides, heavy metals, and other environmental stressors may negatively influence spermatogenesis, semen quality, and male fertility potential.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A systematic scoping review was carried out following the JBI framework for scoping reviews and the PRISMA-ScR reporting standards. Studies published from 2000 to August 2026 were identified through major electronic databases and other pertinent sources. Eligible studies involved males aged 18–70 years in LMICs and examined associations between climate-related or environmental exposures and reproductive outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: The collected evidence highlights a wide range of environmental and climate-dependent threats to male reproductive health. Notably, increased ambient temperatures and occupational heat exposure are major concerns, linked to decreased sperm concentration, motility, count, and other semen quality indicators. Additional findings point to air pollution, pesticides, heavy metals, and various environmental pollutants as contributors to adverse reproductive effects. Agricultural, industrial, and outdoor workers may face significant exposure burdens. Nevertheless, the data is uneven geographically, with significant gaps particularly in African nations and other LMIC regions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusion: It is crucial to explicitly incorporate male reproductive health within the framework of sustainability and climate resilience initiatives. Preventive actions may involve reducing occupational heat exposure, controlling environmental pollution, implementing climate-adaptive occupational health policies, enhancing reproductive health surveillance, and integrating environmental risk assessments into fertility and reproductive services. Strengthening research infrastructure and monitoring systems in LMICs, especially in Africa, is vital for identifying at-risk populations and developing tailored interventions. Overall, these findings indicate that male reproductive health is an underrecognized aspect of climate-related vulnerabilities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 236</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">well-being; sustainable development; SDG; social foundation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Global progress in social foundations remains uneven over time and across dimensions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jing Li</u><sup>1</sup>, Anne Warchold<sup>1</sup>, Klaus Hubacek<sup>1</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Groningen, Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Germany; <span class="paper_email">jing.li@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Achieving and sustaining human well-being requires progress across multiple dimensions of social foundations, yet existing measures provide limited evidence on whether such progress has been broadly shared across countries, balanced across dimensions, and sustained over time. We developed a Social Foundation Index (SFI), which combines four themes including self-identity, societal interaction, social provision, and macro-level measurement, based on a systematic literature review that integrates 31 indicators across 19 dimensions for 171 countries from 2000 to 2022. By grouping countries according to their average attainment in 2000-2005, we tracked how social foundation attainment evolved across national trajectories and its association with environmental and peace-related boundary conditions. Results show that despite improvements in social foundations in 155 countries, national trajectories varied substantially, even among countries with similar initial levels. Average annual progress has clearly slowed down after 2015. Improvements were concentrated in basic provision and infrastructure, whereas relational and institutional dimensions changed more modestly. Moreover, the pattern is asymmetric if accounting for environmental and peace boundaries over time. Countries with lower SFI generally had higher climate vulnerability and conflict exposure, while exerting much less ecological pressure. Our findings suggest that ensuring social foundations for long-term sustainability therefore requires differentiated policies to close remaining provision gaps, strengthen relational and institutional capacities, and respond to different climate and conflict vulnerabilities across countries.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 237</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Football, Resilience, Education, Adaptation, Community</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Defending Our Future : Using Football to Mitigate and Adapt to Climate Change</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Grace Elizabeth James</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Swansea University, United Kingdom; <span class='paper_email'>2528800@swansea.ac.uk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This project examines the effectiveness and feasibility of using football as a medium for climate communication, with a particular focus on the role of trusted non-expert messengers such as professional football players. The original research design aimed to explore the use of Swansea City AFC players as climate communicators to engage audiences who are typically less responsive to traditional scientific messaging. However, due to limited engagement from the club despite extensive outreach, the study evolved to instead investigate the institutional barriers influencing football clubs’ willingness to engage with climate communication initiatives.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The revised research therefore combines two strands of inquiry: first, an analysis of football club engagement with sustainability and climate-related communication across multiple league levels in the United Kingdom; and second, an assessment of football fans’ perceptions of using the sport as a vehicle for climate messaging. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with football clubs that agreed to participate, focusing on their existing sustainability practices, communication strategies, and perceived constraints in engaging with climate change communication. In parallel, a questionnaire was distributed to football supporters to evaluate levels of climate awareness, attitudes towards climate messaging within football, and demographic differences in responses.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study highlights the tension between the potential of football as a trusted communication platform and the institutional barriers that limit its use for climate messaging. It contributes to broader debates on climate communication, trusted messengers, and informational vulnerability by assessing both organisational willingness and public receptiveness within a major cultural institution.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 238</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Water-energy-food nexus, water footprints, food-system transformation, energy transition, nexus governance</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Understanding the water-energy-food nexus in an era of increasing resource competition</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Junjie Yi</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Chinese Academy of Sciences, China, People&#39;s Republic of)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Winnie-Gerbens Leenes</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Paola Guzmán-Luna</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Univ Montpellier)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Santiago Vaca Jimenez</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Escuela Politécnica Nacional)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Water, energy and food systems are deeply interconnected, yet they are often assessed and governed separately. Agriculture is the largest global user of freshwater, energy production depends increasingly on water availability, and food production and consumption place substantial pressure on both water quantity and quality. Climate change, groundwater depletion, extreme weather, geopolitical conflicts and growing resource competition further intensify trade-offs among water, food and energy objectives. This session addresses Conference Theme 2.1 by moving beyond sector-specific assessments towards integrated water–energy–food (WEF) nexus approaches. It focuses on how nexus thinking can be translated into quantitative evidence, practical interventions and policy action. The session is organised around four sub-themes: (1) water, energy and carbon footprints in agriculture; (2) water use and greenhouse gas trade-offs in the energy transition; (3) water quality, food consumption and food-system transformation; and (4) nexus methods, data and policy, including industrial water use and circular water management. The 120-minute interactive session will feature five invited talks, a moderated panel and breakout discussions, and can be adapted to 90 minutes if required. By bringing together speakers from different disciplines and regions, the session aims to identify synergies, manage unintended trade-offs and inform resilient water, energy and food security strategies for the post-2030 agenda and the 2027 SDG Summit.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 239</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Nexus approaches, sustainable resource management; food system transformation, energy transition; smart grids</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Strengthening the Water-Energy-Food-Ecosystem Nexus through Participatory Planning of Renewable Energy Systems in Rural Communities.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Frank Akowuge Dugasseh<sup>1</sup>, Lucy Pius Kyauke<sup>2</sup>, Moses Mengu<sup>3</sup>, Richard Gyimah<sup>4</sup>, Hans Sanderson<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Aarhus University, Denmark; 2:&nbsp;Norwegian University of Science and Technology, Norway; 3:&nbsp;Partnership for Participatory Development, Ghana; 4:&nbsp;Forestry Commission, Ghana; <span class='paper_email'>fad@envs.au.dk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Achieving food, water, and energy security requires integrated approaches that recognize the interdependencies among natural resources, local institutions, and community priorities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Objective</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study presents a participatory framework to assess and enhance the sustainability of renewable energy interventions within the water-energy-food-ecosystem nexus in rural Ghana.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on conceptual underpinnings and principles of Community-Based Natural Resource Management, Social Life Cycle Assessment, and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), the framework was applied to evaluate a renewable energy initiative comprising a solar microgrid, solar-powered irrigation, and a biomass combined heat and power system. Stakeholder consultations involving community members, local authorities, value chain actors, and technical experts were used to identify, prioritize, and benchmark needs and preferences against localized SDG indicators.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results reveal strong linkages between renewable energy access, poverty reduction, food security, livelihood diversification, and institutional strengthening. Stakeholders ranked SDG 1 (No Poverty) and SDG 7 (Affordable and Clean Energy) as top priorities, while food production, irrigation services, and productive use of energy emerged as critical pathways to improve resilience and socio-economic well-being. The framework further identified trade-offs and social hotspots, including farmer-herder conflicts and inequitable access to resources, highlighting the importance of inclusive governance in nexus planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusion</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings demonstrate that integrating renewable energy systems with water management, food production, and community-led governance can enhance resource efficiency, strengthen local economies, and accelerate progress toward multiple SDGs simultaneously.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By and Beyond</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study offers a practical nexus-based approach to sustainable resource management and insights for policymakers, planners, and practitioners seeking to support food system transformation, energy transition, and fragile ecosystems in vulnerable rural contexts for and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 240</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Demand-side mitigation, Just transition, Input-output linear programming, Regional employment, Gender equity</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Demand-Side Decarbonisation and a Just Transition in Ireland: Regional and Gendered Employment Impacts</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Tianning Zhao</u><sup>1</sup>, Valeria Andreoni<sup>1</sup>, Hanah Daly<sup>2,3</sup>, John Eakins<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Department of Economics, University College Cork, Ireland; 2:&nbsp;Sustainability Institute, University College Cork, Ireland; 3:&nbsp;School of Engineering and Architecture, University College Cork, Cork, Ireland; <span class="paper_email">tianning.zhao@ucc.ie</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate mitigation can generate uneven employment impacts across regions and social groups. This study examines how sectoral final demand in Ireland can be restructured to achieve emissions reductions while prioritising regional and gender employment equity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We combine an environmentally extended input-output model with vulnerability-weighted, two-stage linear programming. Ireland’s 2022 domestic input-output table, emissions accounts, and employment data covering 20 sectors and 26 regions are used to trace direct and indirect employment effects. The first stage minimises the maximum weighted relative employment loss, giving additional protection to structurally vulnerable regions and female employment. The second minimises net final-demand contraction while preserving the optimal equity outcome.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Three counterfactual scenarios apply production-side emissions reduction targets of 13.9%, 28.9%, and 48.8% relative to 2022. The first two take reduction rates derived from emissions projections under existing and additional measures as benchmarks; the third converts Ireland’s statutory 2030 target to a 2022 baseline. Rather than simulating those policy measures, we examine how equivalent reductions could be achieved entirely through final-demand adjustment under fixed technical, emissions, and employment coefficients.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results show that the least stringent target can coexist with aggregate employment gains through sectoral reallocation, whereas tighter targets entail broader contraction and larger employment losses. Regional industrial dependence, gendered employment structures, and supply-chain linkages shape the distribution of these impacts. Gains in selected service sectors and construction coexist with losses elsewhere, demonstrating why aggregate employment outcomes alone cannot establish transition fairness.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework embeds distributional priorities in demand-side mitigation planning and identifies needs for regionally targeted and gender-sensitive transition support. It provides normative adjustment scenarios rather than forecasts of realised unemployment, connecting climate mitigation with decent work and reduced inequalities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 241</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">post-2030 agenda, lessons learned, environmental governance, SDGs</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Reimagining Sustainable Development After 2030: Emerging Priorities, Governance Reforms and Future Directions</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Neele van den Bongardt</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (SDSN Netherlands)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Philipp Pattberg</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (VU Amsterdam)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Anna Pot</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Ministry of Foreign Affairs)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">As the 2030 deadline approaches, attention is increasingly turning to the future of global sustainable development governance. While the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) have provided an unprecedented common framework for action, implementation has revealed persistent challenges related to policy coherence, financing, accountability, and the ability to address rapidly evolving global risks. At the same time, emerging issues such as climate resilience, biodiversity loss, digital transformation, artificial intelligence, social cohesion, geopolitical instability, and intergenerational justice raise important questions about whether the current framework remains fully fit for purpose.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This interactive session contributes to the discussion on a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030 by focusing on missing links and potential reforms. Rather than conducting another assessment of SDG performance, the session seeks to generate forward-looking insights on what should be retained, strengthened, reformed, or added in a future global agenda. It will explore how lessons from a decade of implementation can inform more effective, science-based, inclusive, and action-oriented approaches to sustainable development.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The session consists of a plenary round of short statements by representatives from government, municipalities, academia, business, and civil society, followed by a participatory World Café. Invited speakers will each address the question: <em>What is the most important missing link that should be addressed in a post-2030 agenda?</em> This will serve as input for four discussion tables focusing on: 1) emerging sustainability priorities, 2) governance and institutional reform, 3) measuring success beyond current indicators and 4) guiding principles for a future agenda.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">By bringing together stakeholders from different discplines and sectors, the session aims to identify shared priorities, areas of divergence, and practical recommendations for future agenda design. The outputs will contribute to ongoing international discussions on how a post-2030 framework can better address interconnected sustainability challenges while remaining actionable, inclusive and responsive to changing global agenda.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 242</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">resilience, monitoring and evaluation, development, humanitarian</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Building a Resilience Monitoring Framework: From Theory to Application</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Roeland Hemsteede</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">IOD PARC, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">roeland@iodparc.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Resilience is at the same time both a straightforward and intuitive concept and an incredibly illusive concept, and key to the 2030 Agenda. Building resilience to climate change requires a multipronged approach, yet development actors often only focus on a single aspect. Development actors who take a more holistic approach to resilience face challenges in understanding what their interventions add up to, and how to use Monitoring and Evaluation data to adaptively manage their portfolio because it can feel like comparing apples to oranges.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">In this presentation I discuss a custom-built Resilience Monitoring Framework centred around three themes: the Resilience of People, Services, and Institutions. The framework was designed for a large bilateral donor to Nepal and applied to monitor a multimillion-dollar portfolio spanning covering both development and humanitarian interventions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Systematic literature reviews underpinned the identification of relevant dimensions within each of the three resilience themes. Once the framework was developed, project implementers provided regular reporting on their contributions to the Resilience of People, Services, and Institutions after which the author and his team further broke this down across the different dimensions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">As a result of the framework, the donor gained better insight into how its overall portfolio contributed to resilience and where there were blind spots in its programming. These insights facilitated accountability, but they also stimulated further discussion within programme teams and between programmes teams and delivery partners.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation gives the audience insight into the development of a theory-based resilience framework and its subsequent practical application in a Nepal covering a development and humanitarian portfolio. People will leave with a fuller appreciation of the challenges involved in understanding stakeholders’ contributions to building resilience to climate change in a complex and dynamic environment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 243</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">locally-led adaptation, climate action, climate finance, inclusion, governance.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Locally-Led Adaptation in Nepal: Principles and Practice</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Roeland Hemsteede</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">IOD PARC, United Kingdom; <span class="paper_email">roeland@iodparc.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Locally-Led Adaption (LLA) is a key approach to dealing with the ongoing climate crisis. International development actors such as bi- and multilateral donors and climate funds spent $23 billion on adaptation in 2023 (UNEP, 2025), of which a significant portion goes to LLA. However, we should question whether this finance reaches its objectives and ask ourselves how much more resilient LLA makes communities?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation draws on extensive fieldwork in Nepal between 2020-2025 across dozens of LLA projects. Data were collected through interviews and focus group discussions with beneficiaries, local politicians, project implementers, and other key stakeholders. While the analytical framework differed across fieldwork periods, the nine Principles for Locally-Led Adaptation play a key role in understanding how LLA is implemented in practice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Findings show that while the Government of Nepal and key development partners have officially endorsed the LLA Principles, their implementation is – at best – varied. This has direct implications for how inclusive LLA projects are as well as their long-term sustainability. The researched LLA projects often provided valuable short-term support to community members as they were delivered using a cash-for-work modality. At the same time, project selection rarely addressed the systemic climate risks communities faced, and the climate finance to maintain or scale these adaptation actions remains lacking.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Local politicians rarely prioritise climate adaptation interventions. They are happy to endorse external donor-funded projects which bring resources into the community but are loath to structurally commit (local) government resources to them in practice. The inclusion and consultation aspects of the LLA principles are also rarely implemented effectively, let alone mainstreamed. As a result, while LLA in Nepal offered short-term benefits, it failed to address the structural risks and processes that leave people vulnerable to the impacts of climate change.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 244</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Physical AI, Circular Economy, Clothing Resale, EPR, ESPR</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Does unit economics work for Profitable Resale of clothes at scale? Can EPR and ESPR Succeed Without Profitable Resale?</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Kishor Rimal</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">VNYX B.V., Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">kishor@vnyx.ai</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Since July 2026 the Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation (ESPR) bans the destruction of unsold textiles. The textile EPR Directive makes producers pay for what happens to their clothing after sale, and the Dutch UPV Textiel sets rising reuse targets. These rules assume that a garment kept out of the bin can find a second life.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Most used, unsold and returned garments are processed by hand. Manual handling takes eight people, eight steps and about 19 minutes per item, at around €11. A garment must sell for roughly €49 to break even, and only about 4.5% of secondhand clothing clears that bar. For the rest, reuse costs more than it earns. A ban on destruction does not change that arithmetic. And therefore industry finds it cheaper option of burn or dump clothes rather than resale.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The session draws on the experience of VNYX, an Amsterdam-based startup founded in 2025 that builds physical AI robotic systems for resale processing. Robotic handling of soft, deformable textiles is an open problem in robotics. VNYX combines a robotic E-Mannequin, AI item assessment and automated listing to process a garment in under a minute for €2.50 to €3, lowering the break-even price to about €17.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We argue that EPR and ESPR can only succeed if resale becomes profitable at item level. Policy creates the obligation. Automation creates the margin that makes reuse the cheaper choice than burning or dumping.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 245</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">social forestry, Indonesia, socioeconomic, climate mitigation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Linking Indonesia’s social forestry characteristics to climate mitigation outcomes</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nurul Silva Lestari</u><sup>1,2</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Groningen, Netherlands, The; 2:&nbsp;National Research and Innovation Agency (BRIN), Indonesia; 3:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Member of the Leibniz Association, Germany; <span class="paper_email">n.s.lestari@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Social forestry has become an Indonesia’s climate strategy in the forestry and land use (FOLU) sector. However, the extent to which socioeconomic characteristics may affect climate mitigation outcomes remains unclear. This study examines the spatial distribution of social forestry at the village level and assesses the characteristics of villages containing social forestry areas and their associations with aboveground biomass. We find that indicative social forestry areas occur in 18,654 villages, representing approximately 22% of all villages in Indonesia, and are distributed across 37 provinces. Land cover within these areas consists of forest (59 %), cropland (22 %), grassland (15 %), and other land cover types (4 %). Population density shows a consistent negative correlation with aboveground biomass across the years. Meanwhile, main livelihood, distance to forests, and community dependence on forests are significantly associated with aboveground biomass, although the magnitude of the associations is small. Agriculture and forestry sectors are the main sources of livelihood in most villages. Villages with greater dependence on forests also tend to have higher aboveground biomass. Farmer groups are present in most villages, indicating potential institutional capacity to support climate mitigation actions. Panel regression analysis will be conducted to examine the association between changes in village characteristics and forest biomass over time. This study can inform more context-specific social forestry policies by identifying socioeconomic conditions that may support or constrain climate mitigation. In addition, it contributes to Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 1 (No poverty), SDG 10 (Reduced inequality), SDG 13 (Climate action) and SDG 15 (Life on land).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 246</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Keywords: Corporate Governance, Sustainable Development Goals, Sustainability</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sustainable Governance: Exploring the Integration of Corporate Governance Practices with Sustainable Development Goals</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Abdullah Ahmadzai</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Trieste, Italy; <span class="paper_email">abdullah.ahmadzai@phd.units.it</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Role of corporate governance in achieving sustainability has gained the attention of all <br>stakeholders such as investors, customers and policy makers during the current era that is marked by <br>heightened public expectations, social inequalities and increasing environmental challenges. At this <br>era, stakeholders do not only evaluate organizations based on their financial performance, but they <br>also evaluate them on their contribution to sustainable development and social responsibility. It is <br>argued that corporate governance should integrate sustainability into its business practices in order <br>to achieve the UN SDGs. These goals aim to eradicate poverty, reduce inequality, and protect the <br>planet. The literature states that a small number of firms (28%) of Eurostoxx 50 companies reported<br>SDGs in their annual reports. Previous studies have focused on corporate governance and earning <br>management, CEO remuneration, financial performance etc. There is still a research gap to explore <br>the impact of internal corporate governance strength on a firm’s tendency to address SDGs in its <br>sustainability report. This is significant research that will address the current gap through the lens of <br>stakeholder and sustainable development theory to investigate how corporate governance practices <br>can be evolved to meet both tradition businesses objectives and to achieve the UN SDGs. This <br>research will use both qualitative and quantitative data to address the current gap and provide deep <br>insights into the challenges and opportunities for improving a corporate governance model that will <br>help achieve both financial goals and sustainable development goals. This research potentially has <br>significant implications beyond finance, with the potential interdisciplinary impacts for management <br>and organizational studies research as well as policy and sustainability implications. Moreover, this <br>research also has significant implications for responsible leadership within a corporation by <br>exploring how corporate governance can play a critical role in promoting responsible leadership and <br>advocating for sustainable business practices to achieve sustainability.<br></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 247</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Green industrialisation, Inclusive growth, Instrumental variable methods, Sub-Saharan Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Effect of Green Industrialization on Inclusive Growth in Sub Saharan Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> RAYANGUENEWENDE ABDOUL KADER BOUDA, TIBI DIDIER ZOUNGRANA</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Université Thomas SANKARA, Burkina Faso; <span class='paper_email'>kbouda292@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This research analyses the effect of green industrialization on inclusive growth in 27 sub-Saharan African countries. The research covers the period from 2002 to 2021 and uses fixed effect IV regression, double least squares, and Lewbel&#39;s approach as its main methods. It concludes that green industrialization has a positive influence on inclusive growth in the 27 countries studied. A one percentage point increase in green industrialisation leads to an increase in inclusive growth of 0.00482 points at the 1% threshold. Green industrialization significantly contributes to the achievement of sustainable development goals, particularly economic growth and the reduction of inequalities. The main result of this research is in line with the theory of weak sustainability and structural transformation of the economy, which postulates green growth if appropriate mechanisms are employed. We suggest that the authorities in sub-Saharan African countries promote the development of green industrialization by applying taxes to polluters and granting tax breaks and subsidies to green industries. They can also invest in renewable energies to encourage a transition to clean energy use. It is also necessary to raise awareness among businesses about the need to protect the environment and encourage consumers to favor products from green businesses.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 248</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Green industrialisation; Sustainable development; Institutions; Two-step system GMM; Sub-Saharan Africa</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Green industrialisation and sustainable development in sub-Saharan Africa: role of institutions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> RAYANGUENEWENDE ABDOUL KADER BOUDA, TIBI DIDIER ZOUNGRANA</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Université Thomas SANKARA, Burkina Faso; <span class='paper_email'>kbouda292@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainable development has become a key factor for countries seeking continued growth. It is in this context that this study analysed the effects of green industrialisation on sustainable development in Sub-Saharan Africa. Particular emphasis was placed on the role of institutional quality. The study covered 40 countries in Sub-Saharan Africa over the period from 2000 to 2024 and employed the two-step system GMM estimation method. Our findings indicate that green industrialisation makes a positive and significant contribution to sustainable development in sub-Saharan Africa. The results suggest that this positive effect is amplified more than fivefold in an environment characterised by higher-quality institutions. These findings suggest that the authorities in sub-Saharan African countries should promote green industrialisation by investing in green technologies and reducing taxes on green investments in order to facilitate the transition. They should also improve the quality of institutions by enhancing their credibility through the fight against corruption, as well as by strictly enforcing property rights and contracts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 249</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">responsible innovation, emerging governance frameworks, barriers, opportunities, risks</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From technology adoption to development outcomes: A systematic review of artificial intelligence’s contribution to sustainable development in Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Joshua Simuka</u><sup>1</sup>, Evelyn Derera<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Harare Institute of Technology, Zimbabwe; 2:&nbsp;University of KwaZulu Natal, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">jsimuka@hit.ac.zw</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This review will examine whether, how and under what conditions artificial intelligence contributes to sustainable development in Africa. It will distinguish technology adoption and technical performance from demonstrated social, economic and environmental outcomes, addressing the gap between technological promise and development impact.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Following PRISMA 2020 reporting guidance, the review will systematically search Scopus and Web of Science, supplemented by targeted Google Scholar searches and citation tracking. Eligible empirical studies will examine artificial intelligence applications in African environments and report development-related outcomes. Study quality will be assessed using design-appropriate appraisal criteria. A structured narrative synthesis will compare sectors, countries, beneficiaries and outcome measures, examining how institutional capacity, infrastructure, data governance and stakeholder participation shape reported outcomes. Particular attention will be given to causal attribution, distributional effects and environmental trade-offs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The planned outputs are an evidence map distinguishing adoption indicators, service and organisational improvements, and demonstrated development outcomes; an assessment of evidence strength and geographical coverage; and a framework explaining the conditions under which artificial intelligence produces inclusive and sustained benefits. The synthesis will also identify adverse effects, uneven access and gaps between reported efficiency gains and population-level impact.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The review will inform decisions about where artificial intelligence warrants investment, which safeguards are necessary and how development impact should be evaluated. Its relevance to sustainable development by and beyond 2030 lies in connecting technological interventions to measurable outcomes, equitable benefit distribution and institutional accountability. The resulting framework will support responsible innovation and help policymakers, universities, industry and civil society distinguish promising applications from interventions supported by credible evidence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 250</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Urban forest management, target-oriented models, warming cities</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A roadmap for target-oriented urban forest management models in warming cities</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Dani Broitman<sup>1</sup>, Stephan Pauleit<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Technion - Israel Institute of Technology, Israel; 2:&nbsp;Strategy and Management of Landscape Development - Technical University of Munich; <span class='paper_email'>danib@technion.ac.il</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The transformation of urban areas into sustainable cities and communities (SDG11) in a threatening climate change era is a key challenge. The development of a high-performance Urban Green Infrastructure (UGI) and its integration within a densely built urban environment is a central tenet of this challenge.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Good practices and principles for developing high-quality UGI in cities are well established. However, urban UGI guidelines usually focus on a combination of two intertwined approaches: On one hand, local interventions based on demonstrated microclimate assessments, such as green facades or tree planting recommendations. On the other hand, highly stylized guidelines at the neighborhood or city level suggest what can be achieved with a systematic application of these local interventions. However, such guidelines sometimes fall short when dealing with questions such as what the (measurable) objectives of the UGI development are, whether (or not) these objectives are realistic in the specific urban setting, and, finally, the cost and benefits of concrete implementations of UGI in specific sites. We suggest that this results from a lack of scientific models supporting target-oriented urban forest management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The proposed model begins with a defined target required from UGI in the future, in terms of ecosystem services provision. For example, a certain level of thermal comfort for a share of the people moving daily on the city’s streets, considering local warming forecasts. Starting from the current urban setting, population, and green infrastructure status, the model will run forward suggesting a small set of economically feasible, physically possible, and timely manageable UGI alternative plans towards the required target. The main challenges of the model would be dealing with a vast universe of implementation possibilities, accounting for economic externalities, and managing UGI as a living stock that evolves over time. We will present a model development roadmap and initial results.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 251</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Net-zero pathways, just transition, stakeholder co-design, desirable futures, integrated assessment modelling</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Whose Future? Innovative workshop for Co-Designing Desirable Decarbonised Futures</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Tadeusz Rudek</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Jagiellonian University, Poland)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Arkadiusz Szlaga</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Jagiellonian University, Poland)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Aleksandra Wagner</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Jagiellonian University, Poland)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jaime Nieto</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Universidad de Valladolid)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Monica Contestabile</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Nature Portfolio)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Reaching net zero is not only a question of identifying technically feasible pathways, but also of asking what desirable goals these pathways should serve, what kind of post-decarbonised societies they should help create, and what trade-offs they generate. This innovative session will function as a Knowledge Arena of Horizon Europe project PANTHEON, which develops decarbonisation scenarios together with the stakeholders, using the WILIAM integrated assessment model and complementary CGE/MRIO modelling. This session brings together researchers, policymakers, NGOs, civil society organisations, industry and business representatives to critically engage with these scenarios.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">After a short, non-technical presentation of the forecasting and backcasting pathways, participants disuses them in facilitated groups, asking which desirable goals they reflect, which remain missing, what assumptions shape the results, and what social, economic, environmental or political consequences are underrepresented. Particular attention will be paid to goals beyond emissions reduction, including social justice, wellbeing, affordability, health, employment, environmental protection and resilience. They identify trade-offs, inequalities and unintended effects, and consider how goals, assumptions and scenarios could be revised.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The reflections will inform the policy roadmaps and recommendations that PANTHEON will deliver for the European Union and the People’s Republic of China. The session therefore moves beyond asking how to reach net zero and creates a science-policy-society dialogue around the questions of net zero for what, for whom, and through which pathways, which are central to sustainable development beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 252</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">System dynamics modeling, emission reductions, environmental policies</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Using the iSDG model to explore sustainable policy pathways for the Netherlands</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Teun Kluck</u><sup>1</sup>, Matteo Pedercini<sup>2</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Groningen University, Netherlands, The; 2:&nbsp;Millennium Institute, United States; <span class="paper_email">t.kluck@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Netherlands’ economic trajectory is unsustainable, with existing policies falling short of addressing environmental degradation. This study evaluates whether drastic structural changes are necessary to achieve EU and national environmental targets.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">We use the iSDG model, a national-level system dynamics model, to analyse the effects of policies across environmental and socio-economic sectors. Our study is the first to consider indicators across all 17 SDGs for the Netherlands. We explore policies that change economic incentives, such as a carbon tax and investments in green energy, as well as a policy pathway involving direct emission reductions through structural interventions, such as limiting livestock numbers, reducing pesticide and chemical fertiliser use, and phasing out machinery dependent on fossil fuels.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Preliminary findings suggest that drastic transformations are needed for the Netherlands to limit its contribution to environmental degradation. Public investments in green practices, while aligned with the values of the Green Deal and the Dutch National Recovery and Resilience Plan, are insufficient and require substantial scaling up. Alternatively, enforcing emission reductions to the required levels results in limited trade-offs with socio-economic indicators.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Combined with social reforms, direct emission reductions could lead to a truly sustainable society. This research highlights the urgency of structural economic and environmental reforms to meet sustainable development goals by and beyond 2030.</p>


</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 253</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">social-ecological system, Nature-based Solutions, urban rivers, water governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From River Burial to Daylighting in Brussels: Renewed Human-River Interactions for Just Urban Adaptation</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yixin CAO</u><sup>1</sup>, Ann van Griensven<sup>2</sup>, Piotr Sadowski<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Department of Water and Climate, Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Belgium; 2:&nbsp;Department of Water and Climate, Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Belgium; 3:&nbsp;Volonteurope, Brussels, Belgium; <span class="paper_email">yixin.cao@vub.be</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Historically, cities developed within river floodplains. With industrialization, stream burial, i.e., the enclosure of watercourses in pipes and culverts, became a widespread strategy in European cities to address flooding, sanitary crises, and demand for buildable land. While enabling urban expansion, it disrupted hydrological connectivity and weakened socio-cultural relationships with rivers. As climate change intensifies flood and drought risks, river restoration has expanded since the 1980s; more recently, stream daylighting has emerged as a nature-based solution (NbS) that restores freshwater ecological functions, moderates urban microclimates, and reconnects people with water heritage.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Brussels illustrates how successive transformations of its river system have reshaped human–nature interactions. The city expanded within the flood-prone Senne valley, where the river was covered between 1867 and 1871 in response to recurrent flooding and public-health crises, its tributaries were diverted into the sewer network, and two constructed canals facilitated navigation and trade. These historical interventions still shape today’s hydrology, concentrating stormwater along former valley floors and increasing sewer overload and pluvial flood risk. Their effects are also unevenly distributed. Densely built, lower-income neighbourhoods face greater exposure to overheating and flooding, while combined sewer overflows contaminate the Senne, and canal-zone regeneration intersects with migration, social exclusion, and green gentrification.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Since the mid-2010s, Brussels has increasingly reframed its waterways as natural assets, with stream daylighting efforts underway, e.g., the reopening of 200m of the Senne near Buda Bridge in 2021 and a 650m planned under the ongoing Max-sur-Zenne project. In this context, our project explores river restoration as a coupled social–ecological intervention that reshapes land use policy, redistributes diverse riverine ecosystem services, and strengthens climate-adaptive capacity within civil society. Supported by digital citizen science, the project seeks to engage structurally under-represented residents along the Senne and canal. Ultimately, we ask how restored urban rivers can foster just urban transitions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 254</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Healthcare Disparities; Sustainable Rural Healthcare, Urban-Rural Disparity; Sustainable Developement; Health Insurance Coverage</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Urban-Rural Healthcare Disparities in Ghana: Strategies for Promoting Sustainable Rural Healthcare.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Eric Kojo Wu Aikins</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">eaikins@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">In most developing countries, including Ghana, urban–rural health disparities persist. Although Ghana has made significant progress toward achieving Sustainable Development Goal 3 (SDG 3) (Government of Ghana VNR, 2025; United Nations, 2025), substantial urban–rural disparities remain in the provision of healthcare facilities and healthcare professionals, the prevalence of non-communicable diseases, and access to health insurance (Government of Ghana VNR, 2025; WHO Country Profile, 2026).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The progress Ghana has made so far toward achieving Sustainable Development Goal 3 (SDG 3) in the areas of primary healthcare delivery, maternal and child healthcare, immunization, and infectious disease control has primarily benefited the urban population. For instance, the urban–rural doctor-to-patient ratios—0.57 doctors per 1,000 population in the Greater Accra Region (predominantly urban) and 0.03 doctors per 1,000 population in the North East Region (predominantly rural)—demonstrate this disparity (Government of Ghana, 2025; Ministry of Health, 2024; Ghana’s Healthcare Sector Report, 2022).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study employed a systematic review methodology, involving a review of global, Government of Ghana, and local private health institution reports, to assess the extent of regional disparities in healthcare delivery in Ghana. Also, it provides strategies for fostering sustainable healthcare delivery in Ghana.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">In all, the study proposed four main strategies to reduce urban–rural disparities in healthcare delivery in Ghana:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">1. Encourage private investment in the provision of healthcare facilities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">2. Encourage community initiatives to provide scholarships for qualified and competitive individuals pursuing medical and nursing education.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">3. Encourage private investment in the provision of pharmaceutical services.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">4. Expand the scope of the National Health Insurance Scheme to cover vulnerable persons who can make weekly premium payments.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 255</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Driver drowsiness detection  Edge AI, Low-powered devices, Data balancing techniques, YawDD dataset, Small deep learning models</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Comparative Analysis of Data Balancing Techniques for Small Deep Learning Models on Low-Powered Devices Using the YawDD Dataset for Driver Drowsiness Detection.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Kwadwo Sarbeng-Baafi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">kwadwo.sarbeng-baafi@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Driver drowsiness detection is critical to preventing road crashes and improving traffic</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">safety, yet real-world implementation of deep learning paradigms is hindered by dataset</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">class imbalance and automotive system embedded computational limitations. This</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">study undertakes a systematic comparative investigation of diverse data balancing</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">schemes comprising data augmentation, automatic/manual class weighting, focal loss,</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and their hybrid settings in the context of lightweight MobileNetV2 framework on the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">YawDD driver yawning dataset. Experimental results suggest that, if sensitivity to</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">minority yawning incidents is drastically improved by class weighting in any form, it</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">culminates in a balance between recalling such incidents and false positives through</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">manual tuning. Data augmentation by itself does not improve recognition of the minority</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">class sufficiently, whereas focal loss pays off to some extent only when aligned with</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">class weighting. A combination of focal loss with manually weighted classes offers the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">best balanced performance significantly reducing false negatives with tolerable false</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">alarm rates. These findings are invaluable for developing effective, sensitive, and</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">deployable driver drowsiness detection architectures running on battery-powered</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">devices, with implications for both theoretical research and practical deployment in</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">vehicles.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 256</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainability, labour well-being, mining health, inner development, inequalities.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Localising Subjective Well-Being and Inner Development Among Settlements in the Mining Heartland of India</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Arindam Mondal</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Indian Statistical Institute, India; <span class="paper_email">mondalari10@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This study analyses how mining settlement residents living on the edge conceptualise subjective well-being and inner development while battling everyday societal, economic, and environmental injustices.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: This research was conducted across three different settlements in the proximity of mines in Raniganj, West Bengal, applying a mixed-methods approach, where qualitative ethnographic methods were employed to gather information about life history, narratives regarding livelihood precarity, occupational health hazards, and coping mechanisms. To complement this, stratified random sampling was used to obtain quantitative data regarding demographic profiles, household economic conditions, and family health information. This combination provided a rich ground-level perspective on the lived realities in resource extractive regions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Findings reveal a complex psychological normalisation of severe environmental and occupational hazards among the labourers. Tuberculosis, fungal infections, anaemia, cataracts, heart issues, breathing issues, and hypertension are a few key issues found. Dealing with these, they treat physical morbidity as an inevitable sacrifice made to secure household survival, instead of looking at it as a mere health deficit. A solidified community network to tackle societal deficiencies is visible; so is how the women in the families had to deal with the dual burden of managing the household and daily impacts of toxicity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: acknowledgement and integration of certain ground-zero survival strategies are required to achieve true sustainable development. Marginalised perspectives of well-being and inner development, if incorporated with the broader spectrum of sustainability frameworks, can be vital in looking beyond theoretical models of equity and development. Prioritising the subjective realities of vulnerable workforces and anchoring justice and transition policies in the lived experiences of those most impacted by resource extraction environments will ensure equitable progress by and beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 257</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Environmental Education, Environmental Stewardship, Water Pollution, Community Participation, Sustainable Development Goals.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Empowering Children as Environmental Stewards: Lessons from a Community Based Environmental Education Approach to Water Pollution and Environmental Sustainability in Ghana</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Enoch Donkor<sup>1</sup>, Augustina Sena Afordofer<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Earth Stewards Foundation, Ghana; 2:&nbsp;GAT KADOSH FOODS; <span class='paper_email'>earthstewards26@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Ghana faces significant environmental challenges, including illegal small-scale mining pollution of rivers and coastal environments, improper waste disposal, plastic pollution and degradation of natural ecosystems. While regulation, enforcement and technological interventions remain essential, long-term environmental sustainability also requires changes in environmental knowledge, attitudes and behavior. Earth Stewards Foundation (ESF) believes that environmental education, particularly among children and young people, can contribute significantly to this transformation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This practitioner-based presentation shares lessons from the emerging work of ESF, a Ghanaian environmental non-governmental organisation promoting environmental stewardship through child-centred education, community engagement and practical environmental action. ESF views children not only as beneficiaries of environmental protection but also as potential agents of behavioral change within their schools, families and communities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation draws on ESF&#39;s school and community activities, including environmental awareness programmes, community clean-up exercises and household education on waste segregation. A practical example is ESF&#39;s engagement in the coastal community of Asemko in Ghana&#39;s Western Region, where community clean-up activities have been combined with household education on separating organic waste for potential use in backyard gardens and plastics for recycling.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on these experiences, the presentation examines opportunities and challenges in translating environmental awareness into community-level action and highlights lessons concerning environmental education, community participation and local partnerships. It also considers how international knowledge exchange can strengthen locally appropriate environmental education.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation argues that progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals and the post-2030 sustainability agenda requires greater recognition of children, schools and community-based civil-society organisations as partners in environmental transformation, behavioral change and intergenerational environmental responsibility.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 258</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.2 Environmental health and emerging pollutants, including regulatory and governance responses, and policy translation of exposure science</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Environmental exposureair; pollution; lung cancer risk; spatial analysis.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Examine the Environmental Determinants of Lung Cancer Risk - A Spatiotemporal Study in Bagmati Province</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Basanta Kumar Neupane</u><sup>1</sup>, Min Xu<sup>2</sup>, Chunxiang Cao<sup>3</sup>, Pramod Joshi<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Nepal Health Research Council, Nepal; 2:&nbsp;Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences; 3:&nbsp;University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing; <span class="paper_email">basanta.neupane@nhrc.gov.np</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Lung cancer, primarily driven by environmental pollutants, is a growing public health concern in Bagmati Province, Nepal. While numerous studies have linked environmental toxins to lung cancer, their spatial associations at the local level remain underexplored. This study investigates the municipal-level relationship between lung cancer incidence and environmental determinants using spatiotemporal analysis. We analyzed 2,184 lung cancer cases recorded between 2017 and 2021 across 119 municipalities, integrating 25 geographic, environmental, and socioeconomic variables. Spatial clustering was assessed using Moran&#39;s I Index, and associations were modelled using Ordinary Least Squares and Spatial Log Models. Our results reveal that air pollution (particularly SO2 and kerosene use) is significantly associated with increased lung cancer risk, whereas NDVI and soil organic matter show protective associations. High-risk clusters were identified in the eastern and central hilly districts, including Kabhrepalanchok, Ramechhap, and Sindhupalchok, while low-risk areas were concentrated in the southern plains. These findings highlight the importance of spatial epidemiology in identifying environmental risk zones and informing targeted interventions. This study offers evidence to guide resource allocation, risk mitigation, and prevention strategies for lung cancer in Nepal.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 259</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">integrated assessment model; beyond GDP; subjective well-being</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">What does it take to ensure well-being for all? Eliminating subjective well-being poverty in the EU using WILIAM</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Rebecca Blevins</u>, Iñaki Arto</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Basque Centre for Climate Change, Spain; <span class="paper_email">rebecca.blevins@bc3research.org</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The transition to a sustainable and low-carbon economy requires 1) new policies that decrease emissions, provide minimum standards of living, and reduce inequalities, and 2) the use of appropriate indicators to ensure that these goals are met. Most models currently used in policy evaluation only take into account traditional economic indicators such as income and consumption as proxies of people’s well-being, and policy goals are set using these indicators. However, these purely economic metrics may fail capture well-being accurately and should be complemented with subjective measures of well-being (life satisfaction, happiness) to provide more comprehensive insights into the distributional impacts of these policies. We introduce a newly incorporated subjective well-being indicator measuring life satisfaction in WILIAM (Within Limits Integrated Assessment Model), representing a beyond-GDP measure of well-being.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We assess the changes in income needed to eliminate subjective well-being poverty in Europe by 2050, using the macroeconomic multi-regional input-output model WILIAM. With the objective of ensuring high levels of well-being for all 27 EU member states by 2050, we model the effects of a progressive redistribution of income through wealth/income taxes and transfers to analyze the changes in income needed to reach this objective. Subjective well-being poverty is defined as a response of 6.5 or lower on the 11-point life satisfaction scale (0 = Not at all satisfied, 10 = Completely Satisfied), as recorded in the 2025 EU-SILC survey. We compare the redistribution scenario to a redistribution + carbon tax scenario and to a business as usual scenario, and evaluate the effects on subjective well-being, economic indicators, inequality, and carbon emissions. We additionally introduce an analysis of the GINI of subjective well-being to explore inequalities in well-being in the EU, and explore further integration within the WILIAM model, connecting life satisfaction to health, education, and employment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 260</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Energy communities; energy security; geopolitics of energy transition; EMMENA; policy translation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Power to the people, or power for the state? Energy communities and the geopolitics of the energy transition in the EMMENA region</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Marios Kalemis<sup>1</sup>, Paul Upham<sup>1</sup>, Sophie Nyborg<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Groningen, The Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Technical University of Denmark; <span class='paper_email'>m.a.kalemis@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Energy communities are the European Union&#39;s flagship instrument for a decentralised, citizen-owned energy transition. In the Eastern Mediterranean, Middle East and North Africa (EMMENA) region, however, the transition is being made under conditions the instrument was not designed for: contested maritime boundaries, offshore gas discoveries, planned interconnectors with Europe, islanded or state-dominated grids, and an EU energy diplomacy driven since 2022 by the search for alternatives to Russian supply. These conditions favour large, centralised, export-oriented projects and cast energy as an attribute of sovereignty rather than a sector of the economy. This article asks what space such a geopolitical environment leaves for energy communities, and whether they can be made to serve, rather than compete with, the security agendas that dominate it. Drawing on a comparative analysis of energy strategies, regulatory frameworks and EU policy instruments in selected EU and non-EU states of the region, complemented by expert interviews with policymakers, regulators, utilities and civil-society actors, we trace how the EU energy community template is translated, adapted or set aside as it meets national imaginaries of energy security. We conceptualise energy communities as potential instruments of resilience and sovereignty rather than solely as market innovations, and examine how they interact with rentier and utility-dominated models, with EU conditionality and funding, and with regional rivalry over resources and infrastructure. The article offers recommendations for national governments and the EU, and contributes to debates on the geopolitics of decentralised renewable energy.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 261</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">multi-stakeholder engagement, nature-based solutions, multi-level governance, policy instruments, Indonesia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From national commitments to provincial regulations: How Indonesia&#39;s climate policy mix integrates stakeholder engagement into nature-based solutions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yoga Hadiprasetya</u><sup>1,2</sup>, Anne Warchold<sup>1</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>1,3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen, 9747 AG, The Netherlands; 2:&nbsp;Directorate General of Sustainable Forest Management, The Ministry of Forestry, The Republic of Indonesia, Jakarta, 10270, Indonesia; 3:&nbsp;Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK), Member of the Leibniz Association, P.O. Box 60 12 03, Potsdam, Germany; <span class="paper_email">y.hadiprasetya@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Our earlier review of 840 studies from 170 countries found that research on Indonesia gives climate governance twice the usual attention and engagement processes half. No study has examined which actors Indonesian climate policies name in nature-based solutions (NbS), what roles they assign, or what depth of participation they grant. Any post-2030 agenda that expects ecosystems to deliver mitigation, adaptation, and equity needs those answers, and the literature does not supply them. We read the policies instead, analyzing instrument composition, NbS conceptualization, and engagement structures across both tiers of government. We compiled more than 200 climate-related policies issued between 1990 and 2026 from legal databases, the portals of six national agencies and 38 provinces, and international repositories, then traced each one onward through the legal bases it cites. Documents qualify when they address climate mitigation or adaptation and reference NbS, explicitly or implicitly. We code engagement within them rather than searching for it. We apply a five-branch codebook in ATLAS.ti, scoring instrument type, ecosystem coverage against a 16-type classification, and engagement depth on the International Association for Public Participation (IAP2) Spectrum, which grades a stakeholder&#39;s role from being informed to holding delegated decision rights. Across Indonesia&#39;s five Nationally Determined Contribution (NDC) submissions, the phrase &quot;nature-based solutions&quot; never appears. Policymakers write about landscape approaches and the forestry and other land-use sector. We report how each instrument conceptualizes NbS, which actors it names, what role it assigns them, and at what depth on the IAP2 Spectrum. The study addresses SDG 13 on climate action; SDG 6, 14, and 15 on freshwater, marine, and terrestrial ecosystems; SDG 16 on inclusive decision-making; and SDG 17 on multi-stakeholder partnerships. Mapping where engagement obligations sit and where they are missing shows national and provincial governments which instruments to amend before the next NDC cycle.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 262</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Co-design of future agendas, indicator development, multi-stakeholder participatory processes, negotiation, institutional innovation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Participatory indicators for inclusive Sustainable Development Goals</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nikki Theeuwes</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Utrecht University, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">n.j.j.theeuwes@uu.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals are perhaps the most prominent example of governance by goal-setting. As part of this strategy, indicators are used as a policy tool to support earth system governance from local to global scales. Indicators are assumed to offer objective decision-making input across all dimensions of sustainable development while Leaving No One Behind.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Yet research shows that indicators are heavily influenced by who develops them and how, risking existing exclusion mechanisms to become exacerbated if existing power asymmetries remain unchallenged in negotiations about indicators. Several scholars have therefore called for multi-stakeholder participatory processes of indicator development. Such studies tend to rely on deliberative notions of direct engagement. However, this may be difficult to achieve in highly contentious settings where structural barriers to direct deliberation exist.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">In this article, we therefore developed and tested a multi-stakeholder participatory indicator development method which facilitates participation in highly contentious settings. We applied and tested this method in developing indicators for gender equality under the SDGs in the district of Faridabad in India through a series of workshops. Gender is a highly contentious issue in this area, with controversies surrounding gender equality leading to mass protests in the weeks prior to the workshops. We collected and analyzed data of eight workshop sessions, interviews with all 20 participants prior to and after the sessions, six expert interviews, facilitator meeting notes, and workshop outputs.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Our results show that our method that combines geographically and temporally dispersed participation with reflexive co-design, enables different groups that otherwise do not see eye to eye to deliberate about gender equality and develop indicators, thereby facilitating more inclusive development of sustainability indicators. In doing so, we contribute to the development of a methodology to democratize earth system governance, in order to foster the building of just and sustainable futures.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 263</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG localization, national and subnational strategies, monitoring progress, implementation gaps, enabling conditions</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Barriers and opportunities in local implementation of the SDGs</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nikki Theeuwes</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Utrecht University, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">n.j.j.theeuwes@uu.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are the most prominent example of sustainable development governance, including a set of SDG indicators that are supposed to be localized from global to subnational and local levels. Indicators are believed to be an effective monitoring tool to objectively inform policy decisions across sectors, scales and decision-making arenas. Yet in developing indicators, choices are made about what and how to measure, and how to display this. This influences what issues are deemed legitimate and what not, making indicator development inherently political. These politics of indicators has received little attention in governance of the SDGs. We argue this is problematic in contexts of growing diversities, power disparities among agents, and a growing importance of indicators.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">We have developed and applied a framework for understanding the politics of indicators. The framework captures the socio-material nature of indicator politics along indicator design, collection, and presentation. We applied the framework to uncover the indicator politics of national and subnational SDG localization strategies in India. We specifically focus on the development of SDG indicators for gender equality. We collected and analyzed 33 in-depth interviews, participant observations, and 57 (policy) documents as part of two months in-depth fieldwork in Tamil Nadu and Haryana states. </p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Our findings show that in both states, indicator politics are socio-materially shaped by ideas embedded in governance (data infra)structures that severely lack gender-sensitivity. This resulted in indicators that are especially challenging for marginalized genders and local contexts. We argue that these often-overlooked issues of representation need to be made visible in order to make indicators more inclusive. We suggest that (sub)national governments actively seek ways to capture locally-specific inequalities by critically interrogating how and why issues are understood, framed and interpreted, and what and how is revealed and obscured.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 264</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">equity and justice</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A Critical Analysis of Civil Society’s Engagement in South Africa’s Just Energy Transition: Dynamics and Debates</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>lerato ramasesane</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Town, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">lramasesane@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">South Africa is currently undergoing a Just Energy Transition (JET) that seeks to design and create a clean and just energy industry in South Africa. This process is underpinned by a rapid revision of energy sector policies, regulatory frameworks and debates around energy reform. The aim of the study is to critically analyse the debates and dynamics underpinning Civil Society Organisations’ (CSOs) participation as key stakeholders in the JET process taking place within South Africa: How is civil society engaging in the energy reform process in general and specifically with regards to debates around JET in South Africa? Currently, civil society engages in state-sanctioned and self-created platforms around JET, however the extent to which it participates, the challenges it faces, and the dynamics of this civil space are not well understood. The research is based on a qualitative research design and involved in-depth interviews with representatives from several CSOs involved in JET debates, as well as desktop research. Strategic and purposive sampling was used as a method for choosing respondents best suited to provide answers for the research question. The research found that CSOs actively participate in energy transition debates in South Africa using a variety of strategies and tactics. These include litigation, public participation platforms, media, resistance campaigns and grassroots community activism. Whilst there have been considerable victories - including advocating for energy access, justice and broader consultation - CSOs face resource related challenges, threats and power dynamics whilst engaging in JET processes. There is also a disconnect between local and international CSOs working in this space. Recommendations are made for more funding and collaboration opportunities to increase the scope, depth and participation of civil society in the JET discourse. The research may assist policy makers, CSOs and researchers to better understand the current participation of civil society</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 265</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Artificial intelligence, Sustainable development, Twin transition, Water–energy–carbon nexus, Energy transition</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Artificial Intelligence and the Water-Energy-Carbon Nexus: A Time-Varying Connectedness Analysis</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>DASAUKI Caleb Musa</u>, Sin-Yu Ho</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University Of South Africa, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">musadc@unisa.ac.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Artificial intelligence (AI) and digitalisation are increasingly recognised as enabling technologies for sustainable development and the twin transition towards digitalisation and environmental sustainability. Beyond their direct technological applications, AI-related developments can provide new opportunities for understanding interactions across interconnected resource and environmental systems. This study examines the role of AI within the water–energy–carbon (WEC) nexus and explores how AI-related developments interact with the broader transition towards cleaner and more sustainable resource systems. Using daily data over a decade, the study applies a time-varying multivariate connectedness framework to examine the transmission and direction of shocks among AI, water, energy-transition, and carbon-related indicators, while decomposing connectedness across different investment horizons. The results show that AI has a measurable but structurally modest footprint within the WEC system. In contrast, the clean-energy transition emerges as the dominant transmitter of shocks, while water represents the principal net receiver of spillovers, particularly during the post-2020 period. Approximately 88.7% of total connectedness occurs at short-run frequencies corresponding to one-to-five-day horizons, highlighting the rapid and information-driven nature of interactions within the system. The findings demonstrate how AI-enabled analytical approaches can contribute to a more integrated understanding of the interdependencies underlying sustainable development and the twin transition. They also highlight the importance of considering digital, energy, water, and carbon systems jointly when assessing the opportunities and emerging sustainability pressures associated with AI-driven transformation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 266</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate-development-health nexus; Commercial determinants of health; Global South; Planetary health; Social determinants of health; Vulnerability; Water crises.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Layered, Relational Vulnerability in Coastal Khulna’s Water Crises: A Conceptual Synthesis Toward a Politicized Climate-Development-Health Nexus</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Aisha Siddika</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">McMaster University, Canada; <span class="paper_email">siddia71@mcmaster.ca</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Despite growing recognition that the climate crisis is a health crisis, climate-health scholarship has yet to adequately account for how development processes shape the co-production of these crises. Addressing this gap, this paper draws together interdisciplinary scholarship to develop a three-layered, relational framework of vulnerability spanning macro-structural political economy, meso socio-ecological transformations, and micro embodied inequalities. Integrating commercial determinants of health (CDOH) – the profit-driven entities and practices that restructure environments – with social determinants of health (SDOH) – the structural positionalities that dictate daily exposure – the framework traces how global market paradigms and institutional arrangements, such as export-oriented aquaculture and depoliticized adaptation and water governance, redistribute environmental risks. Building further on this interdisciplinary foundation and informed by nexus scholarship, the paper then advances a politicized Climate-Development-Health (CDH) nexus that connects these processes across scales through three interrelated dynamics: (i) global-local connections driven by export-led commercial aquaculture and transboundary hydropolitics, (ii) the depoliticization of adaptation and water governance, and (iii) the structural inscription of intersecting inequalities onto marginalized bodies. The coastal Khulna case illustrates how salinized and degraded water landscapes are not merely environmental consequences of climate change but material expressions of historically uneven commercial relations whose effects become socially and biologically embedded in marginalized bodies. By situating these localized water crises within the broader production of structural vulnerability across scales, the synthesis highlights their implications for planetary health.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 267</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Monitoring, Reporting and Verification (MRV), digital appraisal, public investment planning, climate risk screening, evidence-based adaptation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digitalising Public Investment Appraisal, Monitoring, Reporting and Verification (MRV) for Climate Resilience: Lessons from Pakistan</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Shakeel Imtiaz</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">GIZ Pakistan, Pakistan; <span class="paper_email">shakeel.imtiaz@giz.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change is increasingly challenging governments to ensure that public investments are resilient, sustainable and responsive to evolving climate risks. However, climate considerations are often addressed separately from mainstream public investment planning, appraisal and monitoring. This study explores how digitalising public investment appraisal and Monitoring, Reporting and Verification (MRV) can provide an institutional mechanism for mainstreaming climate resilience into development decision-making. Drawing on Pakistan as a case study, the study examines opportunities to integrate climate-risk screening, resilience measures and climate-related performance indicators throughout the public investment cycle.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study reviews existing federal and provincial digital appraisal processes and identifies key institutional, technical, data and capacity requirements for systematically incorporating climate considerations into investment decisions. It proposes a phased approach that integrates Climate and Hazard Initial Risk Assessment (CHIRA), climate-resilient project requirements, climate-risk information and MRV into digital appraisal platforms. This approach can enable decision-makers to identify climate risks at an early stage, assess appropriate adaptation measures, track implementation and generate evidence on resilience outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Pakistan experience demonstrates that digitalisation can move climate adaptation from a largely policy-level commitment towards a more systematic, transparent and measurable component of public investment management. At the same time, successful implementation requires interoperable data systems, reliable climate information, institutional coordination, clear responsibilities, strengthened technical capacities and common indicators for monitoring adaptation outcomes. The study also highlights the importance of considering gender equality, social inclusion and the needs of vulnerable populations in climate-informed investment decisions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings offer lessons for other climate-vulnerable developing countries seeking to align public investment with climate-resilient and sustainable development pathways. The study argues that integrating climate risk, digital appraisal and MRV can strengthen the quality, accountability and long-term resilience of public investments while supporting evidence-based adaptation planning and more effective use of public and climate finance. https://www.giz.de/de/downloads/giz2025-en-digital-appraisal-system-study-2025.pdf.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 268</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.2 Environmental health and emerging pollutants, including regulatory and governance responses, and policy translation of exposure science</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Cocoa farmer co-operatives, ICA Principles, Perceived effectiveness, Illegal Mining, Co-operative Governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Cocoa Farmer Co-operatives as Social and Economic Institutions: Perceived Effectiveness under Illegal Mining in Ghana.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Lewis Adom Onumah</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology, Kumasi, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">laonumah1@st.knust.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The study assessed the perceived effectiveness of the International Co-operative Alliance (ICA) principles among cocoa farmer co-operatives in the Amansie South District of Ghana. Specifically, it described the nature of the co-operatives, assessed members’ perceptions of the effectiveness of the ICA principles, examined factors influencing these perceptions, and identified and ranked the main challenges facing members. A convergent parallel mixed-methods design was used. Quantitative data were collected from all 33 cocoa farmer co-operatives and 371 farmers selected from a population of 2,039. Descriptive statistics, multiple linear regression and Kendall’s Coefficient of Concordance were used for the quantitative analysis. Qualitative data from six Focus Group Discussions and three Key Informant Interviews were analysed using Braun and Clarke’s thematic analysis. The results showed that the co-operatives were formally established but weak in several areas, including enforcement of by-laws, accountability, transparency, member participation, financial management and service delivery. The overall mean index for perceived effectiveness of the ICA principles was 2.19, indicating general ineffectiveness. Voluntary and open membership was the only principle rated as moderately effective, while the other six were rated ineffective. Age, sex, education, household size, landownership, availability of training resources and whether a farmer’s cocoa farm had been affected by illegal mining significantly influenced perceived effectiveness. Major challenges included declining cocoa production, forceful takeover of cocoa lands, reduced interest in cocoa farming, fewer extension visits, leasing of land for illegal mining and preference for mining because of its immediate financial returns. The qualitative findings supported the quantitative results and confirmed generally weak co-operative effectiveness among members. The study concludes that improving co-operative governance alone may not be enough unless cocoa lands and farmers’ livelihoods are also protected. It recommends regular training on ICA principles and stronger support for the protection and rehabilitation of cocoa farms.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 269</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Public asset management, legacy wells, carbon capture and storage</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Public Assets in the Energy Transition: Evidence from Indonesia’s Upstream Oil and Gas Sector</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mega Dipta Praditya</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Ministry of Finance of The Republic of Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">mega.dipta@kemenkeu.go.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The energy transition raises questions about how governments manage existing fossil-fuel infrastructure and its associated obligations. In Indonesia, upstream oil and gas operations use a substantial portfolio of state-owned assets, including wells, platforms, pipelines and land. As fields mature and decarbonisation progresses, these assets face changing uses and potential retirement costs, while some may support carbon capture and storage (CCS). This study examines the institutional conditions for repurposing Indonesia’s state-owned upstream assets, particularly legacy wells, for CO₂ storage. It combines publicly reported government financial data and sector statistics with a structured review of asset-management and decommissioning regulations and Presidential Regulation No. 14/2024 on CCS. The analysis focuses on ownership, access to existing assets, funding arrangements and post-closure responsibilities. The regulatory framework provides a route for converting existing wells into CO₂ injectors, but implementation requires coordination between energy regulation and public asset management. The analysis identifies issues requiring clarification: access to state-owned wells by storage operators, the sequencing of abandonment and reuse assessments, valuation and revenue arrangements for repurposed assets, and the implications of continued government oversight after operator responsibilities end. Lifecycle framework covering asset recording, recovery, repurposing and retirement are proposed. It recommends assessing storage suitability and well integrity before abandonment decisions, clarifying asset-use arrangements, and coordinating funding across successive uses. These proposals offer an Indonesian perspective on the institutional requirements of net-zero pathways. Their relevance to other resource-producing developing countries depends on comparable ownership and governance arrangements. Integrating public asset stewardship into transition planning could help preserve reuse options while making long-term obligations and distributional concerns more transparent.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 270</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.1 System perspective for addressing one health nexus within ecological ceilings</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">ambient temperature, emergency department, meteorological exposure, distributed lag non-linear model, time-series forecasting</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Ambient Temperature and Emergency Department Visits: Association and Short-Term Prediction</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Syed Ghazanfar</u><sup>1</sup>, Muhammad Osama<sup>1</sup>, Saima Ali<sup>1</sup>, Rakhshanda Ayub<sup>1</sup>, Paul Charlton<sup>2</sup>, Ammad Fahim<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Indus Hospital and Health Network, Pakistan; 2:&nbsp;University of Colorado Anschutz School of Medicine; <span class="paper_email">ghazanfar.saleem@tih.org.pk</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Extreme and changing environmental conditions can influence emergency healthcare demand, making the ability to understand and anticipate ED utilization a global public health priority. However, evidence on temperature-related ED utilization and short-term forecasting remains limited in low- and middle-income settings, particularly in South Asia. We examined the association between ambient temperature and daily adult emergency department (ED) visits and evaluated meteorological and historical demand variables for short-term ED forecasting in Karachi, Pakistan. We conducted a retrospective time-series study of 1,322,537 adult ED visits recorded at Indus Hospital and Health Network over a 10-year period (2016–2025). Lagged Pearson correlations were used to assess associations between meteorological variables and ED visits, followed by a quasi-Poisson distributed lag non-linear model (DLNM) to characterize nonlinear and delayed temperature effects. SARIMA, SARIMAX, XGBoost, and LightGBM were trained using temporally ordered data and evaluated on an unseen test period. Temperature showed the strongest correlation with ED visits among the evaluated meteorological variables. Using 28.3°C as the reference, cumulative RR was 0.839 (95% CI 0.792–0.889) at 18.5°C and 1.062 (95% CI 1.034–1.091) at 31.7°C, demonstrating a nonlinear temperature-associated pattern. For forecasting, models incorporated meteorological conditions, calendar variables, and historical ED demand. LightGBM achieved an MAPE of 6.55% and R² of 0.56, followed by XGBoost (6.63%; R² 0.54). SHAP analysis showed that recent ED demand, particularly the seven-day rolling mean and recent lags, contributed most to predictions. This large, decade-long dataset provides evidence from a resource-constrained setting and demonstrates the potential of integrating environmental and demand signals to support short-term emergency healthcare planning under changing climatic conditions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 271</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable entrepreneurship; entrepreneurial ecosystems; sustainability transitions; sustainable business models; social innovation</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Sustainable Entrepreneurship for Transformative Development: From the SDGs to 2030 and Beyond</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Rita Klapper</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Paul Upham</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Rita Klapper</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Paul Upham</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Entrepreneurship has increasingly been recognised as a potential contributor to addressing environmental, social and economic challenges. Yet, as the 2030 deadline for the Sustainable Development Goals approaches, important questions remain about when, how and under what conditions entrepreneurial activity contributes to meaningful sustainability transformations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We invite conceptual, empirical, methodological and interdisciplinary contributions that critically examine the role of entrepreneurship in advancing sustainable development during the remaining years of the SDGs and in shaping post-2030 sustainability agendas. We also welcome contributions that move beyond the assumption that entrepreneurship is inherently beneficial for sustainability and examine the conditions, tensions, trade-offs and unintended consequences associated with entrepreneurial approaches to sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This track invites conceptual, empirical, methodological and interdisciplinary contributions that critically examine the role of entrepreneurship in advancing sustainable development during the remaining years of the SDGs and in shaping post-2030 sustainability agendas. The track aims to connects entrepreneurial agency with the wider institutional, societal and ecological systems in which entrepreneurship takes place.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A central question for the track is not simply whether entrepreneurship can contribute to sustainable development, but how, for whom, under what conditions and with what consequences. We invite academic researchers, corporate professionals, startup founders, government officials to take this as an opportunity to talk about their work, to explore the challenges and provide reflections on practical solutions. Contributions are particularly welcome that generate lessons from the SDG era and offer insights into the role sustainable entrepreneurship may play in shaping more inclusive, resilient and sustainable futures beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Qualitative experiences of educators learning sustainability education techniques</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Rita Klapper</u>; Paul Upham</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Groningen</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change is accelerating, yet business and management education still largely reproduces epistemic regimes built around profit maximisation. This paper argues that business schools could instead become catalysts for societal transformation, but only if education moves beyond knowing (Head) and doing (Hands) to include being (Heart). Drawing on transformational learning theory, in particular Freire&#39;s critical pedagogy, and on Sipos et al.&#39;s Head, Hands and Heart framework, we examine how Arts-based Learning (ABL) and contemplative methods inspired by kundalini yoga can foster the Heart dimension of a sustainability mindset in educational leaders.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The empirical case is a workshop for educators at a German higher education institution. Participants took part in a mindfulness exercise, an out-of-the-box thinking task and a sensorial activity that linked nature connectedness to climate change education. Eight educational leaders from German and Danish institutions, all with sustainability or climate education responsibilities, were later interviewed in semi-structured interviews. The data were analysed inductively.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings point to four themes: multi-level connections between university and society; transformational education that is practice-oriented and impactful; a &quot;reset&quot; of the educator&#39;s profile and personal journey, involving courage, openness and self-reflection; and obstacles at both structural and individual levels. Participants valued the sensorial and contemplative exercises as memorable &quot;eye openers&quot; that interrupt routine and invite critical reflection. They also warned that novelty can wear off, that mindset change cannot be achieved in a single session, and that fear of losing students&#39; respect can deter educators from using such methods.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study contributes by showing that ABL and contemplative methods are complementary tools for epistemic change, by documenting educators&#39; reactions to them, and by identifying institutional tensions that hinder change. We conclude that these pedagogies can empower educators to reshape curricula and teaching practice, and we call for further research on their use beyond higher education.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">In need of another story – personal narratives of sustainable entrepreneurship journeys</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Rita Klapper</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Groningen</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This article examines sustainable entrepreneurship in international contexts. It draws on Freire&#39;s concept of conscientisation and John Shotter&#39;s withness thinking as its theoretical foundations. It aims to deepen understanding of the lived experiences of sustainability entrepreneurs in both developed and developing countries. Two research questions guide the study. What drives entrepreneurs to pursue sustainability through the economic, social and environmental innovations in their business models? What barriers do they face, and what solutions do they find?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methodologically, the study elicits and analyses in-depth personal narratives from entrepreneurs seeking to make social and/or sustainability contributions. Following Shotter&#39;s phenomenological approach, the narratives are generated dialogically, so that entrepreneurs&#39; experiences are understood in their own terms. The study makes no claims of wider generalisability, although structural commonalities may be shared by similar individuals.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The article makes four contributions. It identifies the factors motivating the pursuit of social innovation in sustainability-oriented enterprises. It explores the role of conscientisation in those motivations. It examines the barriers entrepreneurs experience and the solutions they develop. Finally, it advocates a methodological approach based on withness thinking, which moves away from treating marginalised subjects as research objects towards co-created meaning-making between entrepreneurs and researchers.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The empirical case is a workshop for educators at a German higher education institution. Participants took part in a mindfulness exercise, an out-of-the-box thinking task and a sensorial activity that linked nature connectedness to climate change education. Eight educational leaders from German and Danish institutions, all with sustainability or climate education responsibilities, were later interviewed in semi-structured interviews. The data were analysed inductively.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Panel description repeat</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>tbc1 tbc1</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Groningen</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Entrepreneurship has increasingly been recognised as a potential contributor to addressing environmental, social and economic challenges. Yet, as the 2030 deadline for the Sustainable Development Goals approaches, important questions remain about when, how and under what conditions entrepreneurial activity contributes to meaningful sustainability transformations.</p>
