</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 272</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.1 Advances in AI methods, big data, and digital technologies for sustainability analytics</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Argania spinosa; LULC; Object-Based Image Analysis; Deep Learning; Digitalization.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digitalizing Dryland Conservation: A Machine and Deep Learning Framework for High-Precision LULC Mapping of the Moroccan Argan Forest</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Hoda Benazun</u>, Farid El Wahidi, Lassiane Youssef, Hassan Ait Naceur, Mohamed Reda Najem</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Cadi Ayyad University, Morocco; <span class="paper_email">h.benazun.ced@uca.ac.ma</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Moroccan argan forest (Argania spinosa) is a vital biosphere reserve, severely threatened by climate change and anthropogenic pressure. Until now, its spatial representation has been limited to rough, small-scale botanical sketches due to the complex challenges of dryland forest mapping, such as low canopy density, strong soil background reflectance, and extreme structural heterogeneity. To bridge this critical gap, this study presents the first-ever high-resolution, detailed land use land cover (LULC) map of the argan ecosystem, leveraging the synergy of remote sensing and artificial intelligence. We developed a novel two-step hierarchical mapping workflow. First, an Object-Based Image Analysis (OBIA) approach coupled with the Random Forest algorithm was deployed to distinguish forest ecosystems from all other classes identified and classified as non-forest. Second, a parallel classification was applied to the core argan ecosystem, comparing machine learning models (Random Forest, XGBoost) against a deep learning architecture (CNN). The comparative analysis reveals that the CNN (OA = 95%) significantly outperformed Random Forest (91%) and XGBoost (87%). Specifically, the pixel patch-based CNN effectively resolved the radiometric confusion caused by dense vegetation structures, dominating complex classes like Pure Thuya and Mixed Argan. By merging the highly accurate non-forest matrix with the best-performing CNN ecosystem classification, we produced an unprecedented final map. This first-of-its-kind mapping of the entire argan spatial distribution provides a crucial, highly accurate decision-making tool to support the conservation and sustainable management of this endemic forest.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 273</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate finance; multilevel governance; community-based climate action; maladaptation; gender-responsive finance; rural banking; Ghana</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Banking on Her Resilience: Integrating Gender into Climate-Related Financial Risk Management in Ghana&#39;s Rural and Community Banks</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Thelma Frimpong Mensah</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">ARB Apex Bank PLC, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">tmensah@arbapexbank.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change and gender inequality meet at the point of rural finance. In Ghana, women make up the majority of smallholder farmers, market traders and micro-entrepreneurs, and are the primary clients of Rural and Community Banks (RCBs). They are among the most exposed to climate shocks, yet land-title collateral rules, spousal guarantees and thin credit histories limit their access to adaptation finance. As the Bank of Ghana&#39;s Climate-Related Financial Risk Directive extends to RCBs from January 2027, will climate risk management reinforce women&#39;s exclusion or support a just transformation?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper presents a conceptual framework drawn from the ARB Apex Bank Gender Policy (2026), which sets standards for more than 144 RCBs, treating this apex-to-RCB structure as multilevel climate governance. It argues that climate risk and gender-based financial exclusion should be assessed together, through three components: gender-responsive climate risk screening in credit decisions; products that turn adaptation needs into bankable demand, including climate-smart agricultural loans, crop insurance, collateral beyond land title and community-based Susu group finance; and gender-disaggregated loan data reported alongside climate returns, so supervisors can see whether climate-driven credit tightening falls disproportionately on women.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that SDG 5 and SDG 13, largely pursued in parallel, can be joined up in climate finance beyond 2030, and sets out unresolved tensions, including maladaptation through de-risking and the limited capacity of small rural banks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 274</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">value chains, material efficiency, production and consumption, physical input-output table, critical raw materials</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A Multi-regional Physical Input-Output Table Framework for Gallium and Indium</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Thi Thu Huong Do, Thomas Grebel</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Technical University of Ilmenau, Germany; <span class='paper_email'>thi-thu-huong.do@tu-ilmenau.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Resource-efficiency targets under the SDG rely on aggregate material indicators that are blind to by-product critical metals: where they originate, how they are transformed, and where they are lost. We develop a physical input-output table (PIOT), in kilograms, tracing gallium and indium from extraction to final use. Both are by-products of aluminum and zinc production, indispensable for photovoltaics, light-emitting diodes and electronics. Methods: The table is built from observed physical data, not derived from a monetary table. Geological survey statistics anchor extraction and refining across 15 regions for 2000-2022. Stoichiometric closure with partner elements (nitrogen, phosphorus, arsenic, antimony) generates compound flows. Customs trade statistics and balancing synthetic flows link refineries, and final demand shares from a multi-regional monetary database allocate devices to consuming regions. Because by-product supply is fixed by host-metal output, the system is solved with a supply-driven (Ghosh) model closed by a terminal loss node. Key results: Mass is conserved at every refining and synthesis node in every year, with residuals below one microgram, and total extraction equals total loss for each element-year. Supply concentration is traceable with visualizations, especially for by-products with routes and in physical units, a distinction a monetary model with one coefficient per sector cannot separate. Conclusions: The framework is transferable to other metals and delivers what expert-based criticality frameworks cannot: measured supply concentration, losses, scenario-conditioned extraction requirements and substitutability. For sustainable development by and beyond 2030, this could complement material footprint indicators under the responsible consumption and production goal and inform secure metal supply for the energy transition. Further research could be extended to secondary production of by-products, taken into account of lifetime of final products and recycling rates for new and old scraps.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 275</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Ascophyllum nodosum, biostimulants, waterlogging, tomato, metabolomics</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Evaluating the Efficacy of Ascophyllum nodosum-Mediated Priming of Tomato Seedlings Under Waterlogging: Physiological, Biochemical, and Metabolic Responses</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Sadia Tabassum Tanni<sup>1,2</sup>, Dr. Leilei Zhang<sup>2</sup>, Dr. Luigi Lucini<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Scuola Universitaria Superiore IUSS Pavia; 2:&nbsp;Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Italy; <span class='paper_email'>sadia.tanni@iusspavia.it</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background: Waterlogging (WL) is an increasingly important abiotic stress that limits crop productivity by inducing soil hypoxia, disrupting root development, and promoting oxidative stress. Seaweed extracts are widely used as plant biostimulants, but their efficacy against WL stress remains poorly understood. Ascophyllum nodosum (AN) contains diverse bioactive compounds, including polysaccharides, minerals, and hormone-like compounds, which may enhance plant stress resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: This study evaluated the effects of AN application on cherry tomato (Solanum lycopersicum L. cv. Heinz 1301) seedlings exposed to 72 h of WL followed by an 11-day recovery period. Plants were primed twice with AN at 7-day intervals through soil drenching before WL stress initiation. Physiological, biochemical, and root architectural responses were evaluated. Untargeted metabolomic profiling using UHPLC-QTOF is currently underway to further investigate metabolic responses to AN priming under WL.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: WL induced pronounced oxidative and membrane damage in untreated plants, with H₂O₂ and malondialdehyde (MDA) levels increasing 1.5- and 1.7-fold, respectively, compared with untreated controls. Although photosynthetic responses did not differ significantly between untreated and AN-treated WL plants after 72 h, AN-treated plants exhibited improved protection during the recovery period. WL also markedly altered root architecture, significantly reducing primary root length by 34.48% and increasing average root diameter by 50.5% compared with CK; AN treatment reduced this WL-induced increase, with root diameter 9.38% lower than in untreated WL plants. Adventitious root formation was also prominent under WL. Interestingly, AN-treated plants showed partial protection against these WL-induced root alterations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusion: AN priming appears to enhance tomato resilience to WL, particularly during post-stress recovery. Ongoing UHPLC-QTOF metabolomic analysis will provide further insight into the primary and secondary metabolic pathways underlying AN-mediated stress tolerance. These findings highlight the potential of AN-based biostimulation as a sustainable approach for improving crop resilience to WL under changing climatic conditions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 276</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.1 Modeling Earth system interactions, including climate impacts on development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Salinity, waterlogging, combined stress, metabolomics, tomato</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Does stress timing matter? Biochemical and metabolomic insights into stage-dependent tomato responses to waterlogging, salinity and their combination</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sadia Tabassum Tanni</u><sup>1,2</sup>, Dr. Leilei Zhang<sup>2</sup>, Dr. Hajar Salehi<sup>2</sup>, Dr. Luigi Lucini<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Scuola Universitaria Superiore IUSS Pavia; 2:&nbsp;Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore, Italy; <span class="paper_email">sadia.tanni@iusspavia.it</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Salinity (S) and waterlogging (WL) frequently co-occur in agricultural systems, yet their combined (WL+S) effects across early reproductive developmental stages remain poorly understood. This study investigated stage-dependent physiological, biochemical, photosynthetic, and metabolomic responses of cherry tomato (Solanum lycopersicum L. cv. Ciliegino a Terra) under S, WL and WL+S. The plants were exposed to 36 hours of stress in controlled environmental conditions during early flowering and early fruiting stages. Combined stress reduced shoot growth at both developmental stages, whereas photosynthetic responses were relatively similar across stages. In contrast, oxidative and antioxidant responses differed between stages. Lipid peroxidation showed antagonistic effects under combined stress at early flowering but synergistic increases at early fruiting. At final harvest, WL+S induced coordinated upregulation of catalase (CAT), superoxide dismutase (SOD), and ascorbate peroxidase (APX) activities in early flowering, whereas no significant antioxidant enzyme response was observed at early fruiting. Importantly, untargeted metabolomic profiling (UHPLC-QTOF) further revealed stage- and treatment-dependent metabolic reprogramming. At early flowering, only two metabolites were shared among the stress treatments, while combined stress produced the highest number of unique metabolites. In contrast, 41 metabolites were shared among the stress treatments at early fruiting, indicating greater metabolic convergence at this developmental stage. Consistently, AMOPLS analysis identified developmental stage as the primary source of metabolic variation. Overall, the integrated responses highlighted the complexity of combined stress alongside contrasting biochemical and metabolic adjustments, demonstrating that developmental stage is a key factor influencing tomato growth and yield, particularly under changing climate.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 277</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Nature Relationship Index, Sustainable Development, Aspirational Approach, Transformation</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Nature Relationship Index: An Aspirational Approach to Planetary Futures</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Junze Zhang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Research Center for Eco-Environmental Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, CAS)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jing Li</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Erle Ellis</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Maryland, Baltimore)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Junze Zhang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Research Center for Eco-Environmental Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, CAS)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Krushil Watene</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Auckland)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jort Fiecheter</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">How can we measure progress toward a future in which people and the rest of nature thrive together? Existing global metrics, from GDP to the Human Development Index, capture human wellbeing but say little about the health of our relationship with the living world. To address this gap, Ellis et al. (2025) proposed an aspirational approach to planetary futures based on the Nature Relationship Index framework. It argues that metrics should not merely track ecological decline, but measure human agency, i.e., what societies are doing right, in shaping mutually beneficial relationships with nature. This framework underpins the NRI as a new metric of national progress along three dimensions: nature that is thriving and accessible, nature used with care, and nature safeguarded through governance and investment.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This session brings together contributors to the NRI's international development to showcase its latest advances. This includes indicator design, global data integration, and early applications across diverse social-ecological contexts. The session comprises presentations on the NRI and its derivatives, elaborating its conceptual foundations and demonstrating its potential to shift policy narratives from ecological crisis to positive, achievable action. </p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The session will also seek to stimulate discussion on the post-2030 global development agenda: how can aspirational, nature-centred metrics inform the next generation of global goals? It will conclude with an interactive discussion, inviting the GSC participants to explore these metrics as tools for inspiring and steering planetary transformation beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 278</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Inclusive wealth convergence, institutional quality, foreign direct investment, sustainable development, beyond GDP</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Inclusive wealth convergence in Africa: the role of institutional quality and foreign direct investment</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Nxalati Baloyi</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Stellenbosch Business School, South Africa; <span class='paper_email'>nxalati.baloyi@icloud.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The increasing recognition of the inadequacy of per capita GDP convergence in capturing the environmental sustainability of economic growth and societal aspects of well-being, necessitates convergence metrics that extend beyond GDP. This paper examines how institutional quality and foreign direct investment (FDI), along their interactive effects, influence convergence beyond GDP (inclusive wealth) in 40 African countries. Employing the two-stage system generalised method of moments (2SGMM) to a panel data ranging from 1995 to 2020, the empirical results reveal persistent unconditional and conditional divergence in inclusive wealth among numerous African countries. This divergence is attributed to many nations being ensnared in suboptimal institutional environments, along pronounced regional and sectoral disparities associated with FDI flows to Africa. Further, the reported results posit that FDI can effectively synergise with institutional quality to promote inclusive wealth convergence in Africa. Achieving inclusive wealth convergence is crucial for enhancing sustainable development in Africa. Enhanced levels of sustainable development break persistent cycles of low-growth and alleviate conditions of extreme poverty and inequality. Evidence of inclusive wealth convergence signifies progress towards an inclusive and sustainable development pathway, consistent with the Sustainable Development Goals and the African Union’s Agenda 2063. The findings emphasise that inclusive wealth convergence in Africa is conditional rather than absolute, necessitating robust governance structures that align external capital investment with sustainability and development objectives.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 279</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Responsible AI, Human rights evidence, Data governance, Conflict-affected settings, Sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Human Rights Evidence to Responsible AI for Sustainable Development: Lessons from Conflict-Affected Yemen for the Post-2030 Agenda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>KHALED AYESH. SAGHEER</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">National Forum for Human Rights, Yemen; <span class="paper_email">khaledsakheer44@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals have demonstrated that progress towards sustainable development depends not only on the availability of data, but also on the quality, inclusiveness, governance and usability of evidence. This challenge is particularly acute in conflict-affected and fragile settings, where institutional disruption, information gaps and unequal access to digital technologies can undermine evidence-based decision-making. At the same time, the rapid expansion of artificial intelligence (AI) creates new opportunities to analyse complex evidence while introducing significant risks related to bias, information integrity, privacy, accountability and exclusion.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This contribution examines the potential of responsible AI and evidence governance to support sustainable development beyond 2030, drawing on lessons from human rights documentation and information management in conflict-affected Yemen. It explores how systematically collected and verified human rights evidence can contribute to more effective data-driven analysis and decision-making, while identifying the safeguards required to prevent AI from amplifying weaknesses in source data or reproducing existing inequalities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper proposes an evidence-to-AI framework linking data quality and verification, responsible data governance, contextual analysis, human oversight, accountability and sustainable development outcomes. It argues that the post-2030 agenda should move beyond a narrow focus on producing more data towards building trustworthy, contextualised and actionable evidence ecosystems. In conflict-affected settings, this requires treating affected communities not merely as data sources, but as participants in responsible data governance and the interpretation and use of evidence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution concludes with practical lessons for designing inclusive, rights-respecting and accountable AI-enabled approaches to sustainable development beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 280</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate Change, Mitigation, Nature-based solutions, Community resilience, Codesign</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A Novel Framework for Codesigning Nature-based Solutions for Climate-resilient and Sustainable Communities across Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Lambert Takang</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Buea, Cameroon; <span class="paper_email">anton.nada2030@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Africa faces accelerated convergence of climate change, biodiversity loss, ecosystem degradation, and socioeconomic vulnerability, placing unprecedented pressure on communities, livelihoods, food and water security, and human well-being. Nature-based Solutions (NbS) provide a promising pathway for addressing these interconnected challenges by harnessing ecosystems to deliver climate adaptation and mitigation, biodiversity conservation, disaster-risk reduction, and societal benefits. However, NbS implementation across Africa remains constrained by fragmented planning, limited integration of Indigenous and local knowledge, inadequate stakeholder participation, weak science–policy linkages, and insufficient consideration of diverse ecological, cultural, socioeconomic, and institutional contexts. This study proposes a novel transdisciplinary framework for codesigning NbS with African communities, positioning communities, knowledge holders, scientists, practitioners, policymakers, and other stakeholders as co-creators of solutions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework integrates climate-risk and vulnerability assessment, ecosystem and biodiversity diagnostics, socioeconomic analysis, Indigenous and local knowledge, participatory geospatial approaches, scenario modelling, multicriteria decision analysis, and stakeholder engagement within an iterative decision-support architecture. It guides NbS development from risk and ecosystem assessment through opportunity-identification, collaborative design, spatial prioritisation, feasibility and trade-off analysis, implementation, monitoring, evaluation, and adaptive learning. NbS interventions were evaluated against interconnected dimensions of climate resilience, biodiversity, ecosystem services, livelihoods, social equity, cultural relevance, economic viability, governance feasibility, and long-term sustainability.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By connecting scientific evidence with Indigenous and local knowledge and strengthening collaboration among communities, researchers, policymakers, practitioners, and financing institutions, the framework seeks to transform NbS from fragmented interventions into locally owned, evidence-based, scalable, and adaptive strategies for sustainable development. This transferable methodological framework and decision-support approach capable of supporting context-specific NbS planning across diverse African landscapes. Ultimately, it will demonstrate how integrating ecological science, local knowledge, participatory governance, and spatial decision-making can unlock the transformative potential of NbS to strengthen climate resilience, conserve biodiversity, enhance livelihoods and well-being, and advance inclusive, equitable, and sustainable Africa communities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 281</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG localization, youth leadership, multilevel governance, sustainable development, regional cooperation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond 2030: From Sustainable Development Goals to Individual Development Goals through Local Action and Regional Cooperation</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Syed Zeeshan Haider KAZMI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">The Central Asia Pakistan Afghanistan Sustainable Development Initiative, Pakistan; <span class="paper_email">kazmi.zeeshan.haider@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals have provided an unprecedented global framework for sustainable development, but their implementation has often been constrained by limited local ownership, institutional capacity, fragmented interventions and insufficient political will. As the international community considers sustainable development beyond 2030, this contribution proposes a more targeted, people-centred and locally grounded approach.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on practical experience from Azad Jammu and Kashmir (AJK), Pakistan and regional cooperation initiatives, the presentation explores how SDG implementation can become more effective by prioritizing selected goals according to local needs, defining specific and measurable objectives, and concentrating resources on achievable outcomes rather than attempting to address all goals simultaneously. Particular attention is given to people with fewer opportunities, including marginalized and underserved communities, whose participation is essential to meaningful development.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation further introduces the complementary perspective of Individual Development Goals (IDGs): recognizing that sustainable societies depend not only on institutional and environmental progress, but also on the development of individuals’ capacities, agency, skills, resilience and ability to participate in development processes. Linking SDGs with IDGs can help connect global targets with individual and community-level transformation.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The contribution argues that effective implementation beyond 2030 requires stronger political will, accountable multilevel governance, meaningful youth participation and locally designed solutions. It also emphasizes that local approaches should be connected through regional cooperation, allowing neighbouring countries and communities to exchange knowledge, mobilize resources and address shared challenges.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The Central Asia–Afghanistan–Pakistan Sustainable Development Initiative (CAPSDI) is presented as an emerging regional platform for translating these principles into dialogue, partnerships, knowledge exchange and sustainable investment, connecting local priorities with regional cooperation.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation concludes by proposing a targeted SDG–IDG approach in which global goals provide direction, local communities define priorities, individuals become agents of change, and regional cooperation enables scale and continuity beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 282</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.3 AI and big data for decision-making and sustainable governance as a supporting decision tool</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Artificial intelligence, SDG monitoring, big data, digitalization, regional data cooperation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">AI-Enabled SDG Intelligence: From Global Indicators to Micro-Level Monitoring, Local Action and Regional Data Cooperation</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Syed Zeeshan Haider KAZMI</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">The Central Asia Pakistan Afghanistan Sustainable Development Initiative, Pakistan; <span class="paper_email">kazmi.zeeshan.haider@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Artificial intelligence, big data and digitalization can fundamentally change how progress toward the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) is understood, measured and acted upon. This contribution proposes an AI-enabled SDG intelligence framework that moves beyond aggregated national statistics toward granular, real-time and actionable monitoring of individual targets, indicators, initiatives, gaps, impacts and time-bound objectives.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The proposed approach uses AI modelling to assess each SDG target and indicator, identify implementation gaps, track progress and impact, estimate emerging risks, and recommend areas for improvement. It seeks to narrow monitoring to the micro level—from individual communities and development projects to snow and glaciers on mountains, rivers and water systems, forests and plants, climate conditions, public health, education, human rights, governance, peace and livelihoods. Such granular intelligence can reveal changes and inequalities that conventional aggregate datasets may overlook.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A central component is an accessible digital dashboard that presents progress against selected goals and specific objectives over defined timeframes. The platform would enable governments, researchers, organizations, communities and individuals to access information and contribute observations, evidence and local data, creating a participatory model of sustainable-development monitoring.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework also proposes regional data-sharing mechanisms among countries, regions and organizations, particularly for shared challenges such as climate change, water, migration, biodiversity and peace. Special attention is given to developing countries, underprivileged communities and conflict-affected regions, where data gaps and limited institutional capacity can constrain evidence-based policymaking.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Central Asia–Afghanistan–Pakistan Sustainable Development Initiative (CAPSDI) is proposed as a potential regional cooperation platform for piloting such approaches, connecting local data and AI-enabled monitoring with cross-border knowledge exchange, policy dialogue and sustainable investment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presentation argues for a transition from data collection to data intelligence, from national averages to micro-level realities, and from passive reporting to participatory, AI-driven sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 283</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Small Languge Model, Inclusion, Explainable AI (xAI), Data Soverignty, Superintelligence</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Advancing Sustainable Development and Inclusion Using AI in Low-Resource Language Contexts</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Israel Olatunji Tijani</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">ChatVE, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">israel@chatve.co</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: AI is trained where data is abundant, not where the need is greatest. The &quot;Geography of AI for Peacebuilding&quot; dataset (Harvard Dataverse, 2026) indicates that 90% of AI training data originates in Europe and North America, while 83% of conflicts occur in the Global South. This study critically examines four roles of AI, big data, and digitalisation (analytical method, implementation tool, decision aid, and agenda-setter) through voice-first, sector-specific AI for Nigeria&#39;s low-resource languages, weighing opportunities against challenges.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: We assemble an open developer stack from N-ATLaS, Nigeria&#39;s Llama-3-based multilingual model; Meta&#39;s NLLB-200 for translation; and Google&#39;s WAXAL corpus for speech-to-text and text-to-speech. Each component is audited for language coverage, licensing, compute, energy and data costs. The stack is mapped onto services in healthcare, digital democracy, financial literacy, agriculture, information integrity, peacebuilding and education. SHAP and LIME attributions are generated in English, translated and voiced, then tested for whether explanations remain faithful across languages.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results: Each role carries an opportunity and a constraint. As method, explainable AI exposes bias, yet attributions computed on English proxies may not hold for Hausa, Igbo or Yoruba. As implementation, voice lowers literacy barriers, yet WAXAL offers text-to-speech but no transcribed speech for these languages. As decision aid, spoken explanations make outputs contestable, yet NLLB-200&#39;s non-commercial licence limits local enterprise. As agenda-setter, digitalisation competes with poverty hindering affordability of mobile data prices, poor internet penetration, and grid electricity averages 456 gCO₂/kWh before diesel back-up.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Beyond 2030, and as capabilities approach superintelligence, trustworthy AI depends on who owns language data, who can audit explanations and who bears the energy cost. We propose data autonomy commons, permissive licensing, multilingual explainability standards and privacy-preserving, carbon-aware deployment as practical levers for SDGs 2, 3, 4, 5, 13, 16 and 17.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 284</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Coastal ecosystem services; Kumayam; shell-lime mortar; nature-based resource management; circular material systems; traditional ecological knowledge; coastal resilience.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Kumayam: Reviving Traditional Shell-Lime Mortar for Coastal Ecosystem Resilience and Sustainable Landscapes along India’s Malabar Coast</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Hasna Sal</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">LMSAI Harvard University, United States of America; <span class='paper_email'>hsalam@gsd.harvard.edu</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Along India’s Malabar Coast, Kumayam, a traditional shell-lime mortar derived from calcium-rich mollusk shells, represents an enduring relationship between coastal ecosystems, vernacular architecture, and local ecological knowledge. Historically employed in Kerala’s built environment, Kumayam embodies a resource-based construction practice connecting aquatic biodiversity, artisanal livelihoods, and regional material traditions. However, industrial cement production, changing coastal economies, and declining intergenerational knowledge have contributed to its gradual disappearance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This research investigates Kumayam as a potential model for regenerative material practices within sustainable coastal landscape and seascape management. Focusing on Kerala’s Vembanad Lake ecosystem, the study examines the historical interdependence of shell resources, aquatic habitats, traditional lime production, and vernacular construction. It explores how locally sourced biological materials have shaped architectural practices while supporting coastal communities and their cultural identities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Employing an interdisciplinary methodology integrating field documentation, oral histories, traditional production techniques, and comparative material analysis, the research evaluates Kumayam’s ecological significance and its potential contribution to circular construction economies. Particular attention is directed toward responsible shell sourcing, the reuse of discarded shells, biodiversity conservation, and the environmental implications of lime calcination compared with conventional cement production.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study further considers how reviving traditional material knowledge can strengthen community resilience, preserve artisanal livelihoods, and encourage environmentally responsive architectural practices. Rather than treating coastal ecosystems solely as extractive resources, it proposes a reciprocal framework linking ecological stewardship with sustainable material production.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By positioning Kumayam within the discourse of nature’s contributions to people, this research demonstrates how traditional construction knowledge can inform contemporary sustainability strategies. It advocates integrating cultural heritage, aquatic ecosystem protection, and circular material innovation to advance regenerative coastal landscapes, strengthen human–nature relationships, and support resilient communities confronting ecological degradation and accelerating environmental change across vulnerable coastal regions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 285</span>
<br />
