<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 312</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate-Development Nexus, Just Transitions, Climate Justice, Real Resilience, Systems Change, Structural Equity, Community Governance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Architecture of Just Resilience: Navigating the Intersections of Climate Disruption, Economic Vulnerability, and Transformative Global Governance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Anowar Hossen</u><sup>1</sup>, Mohammd Abu Talha<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Bangladesh Meteorological Department, Bangladesh, People&#39;s Republic of bangladesh; 2:&nbsp;Jamila Ainun Ananda School and College; <span class="paper_email">salim.anowar@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The dual crises of accelerating climate disruption and deepening economic inequality are not separate, parallel problems; they are fundamentally entangled. For decades, global policy has treated climate action and human development as isolated agendas on a checklist. This siloes approach has yielded top-down, technocratic adjustments—such as localized engineering fixes or carbon offset markets—that consistently fail because they ignore the political and economic systems driving vulnerability in the first place. This paper provides a comprehensive evaluation of the climate change–development nexus, arguing that we cannot build a physically resilient world on a foundation of structural injustice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on the intersections of political ecology and development economics, we analyze how climate shocks act as a severe crisis multiplier. These disruptions follow historical fault lines, disproportionately harming marginalized communities across both the Global South and North who possess the least resources to adapt but bear the heaviest burdens of historical carbon debts. We contend that true, lasting resilience requires moving far beyond incremental survival strategies. Instead, a dual framework of climate justice and equitable development must be operationalized to actively dismantle systemic exclusion, unfair resource distribution, and top-down institutional mandates.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By evaluating emerging pathways—specifically community-led adaptation metrics, just energy transitions, and regenerative economic models—this paper outlines actionable mechanisms to align urgent decarbonization targets with foundational human rights and poverty alleviation. Ultimately, we demonstrate that a sustainable planet is entirely impossible without an equitable society. Successfully navigating this nexus demands a profound transformation in global governance, shifting power and funding away from distant boardrooms and handing them directly to frontline communities to guarantee procedural, distributional, and recognitional justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 313</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Youth participation, Climate governance, Kenya, Sustainable Development Goals, Tokenism, Resource mobilisation.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From passion of the youth to progress in societies: Lessons and critical reflections on youth participation, climate governance and Sustainable Development in Kenya</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Gabriel Dinda</u>, Ethemcan Turhan, Stephen Adaawen, Chris Zuidema</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Groningen, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">g.d.olewe@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Kenya’s demographic structure has been described as a “youth quake”, with roughly a third of the population aged between 18 and 35. Article 10 of the Constitution of Kenya requires public participation in governance and young Kenyans have used that opening to press into sustainable development and climate policy at local, national and international levels. Kenya’s climate commitments make this participation consequential. Under its Second Nationally Determined Contribution (NDCs), submitted in April 2025, Kenya undertakes to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 35% by 2035 relative to a business-as-usual scenario, superseding the 32% by 2030 target of the updated first NDC. Meeting a commitment of this scale requires <em>all hands-on deck </em>since economic losses from weather and climate-related disasters are estimated at 3–5% of GDP annually. This chapter asks how youth participation in Kenyan climate governance is enabled and constrained and what that reveals about the relationship between climate action and the wider development agenda. Drawing on documentary analysis of scholarly, policy, civil-society and media sources published between 2020 and 2025, and on two illustrative cases, the Ondiri Wetland Botanical Garden in Kiambu County and the One Million Trees for Kilifi project, we construct a critical narrative synthesis of resource mobilisation, policy engagement and public outreach. We argue that constraints on youth climate participation are not specific to sectors but compound. Deficits in climate education, employment, institutional inclusion and regional equity reinforce one another and jointly suppress the capacity for climate action. We name this the compound-deficit model and use it to show why interventions framed around a single Sustainable Development Goal reliably underperform.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 314</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG implementation, corporate governance, sustainability reporting, board expertise, Saudi Arabia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Does board sustainability expertise make governance work? Environmental, social, and governance disclosure in Saudi Arabia&#39;s energy sector</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Hassan Mousa</u>, <u>Hussam Sharawi</u>, <u>Shatha AlQurashi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Jeddah International College, Saudi Arabia; <span class="paper_email">hassan@jicollege.edu.sa</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">h.sharawi@jicollege.edu.sa</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">s.alqurashii@hotmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective:</strong> Energy companies in resource-dependent economies are pivotal to achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), yet it remains unclear which board mechanisms produce credible environmental, social, and governance (ESG) disclosure rather than symbolic reporting. This study examines how board size, independence, gender diversity, ESG committees, and government ownership affect ESG disclosure quality in Saudi Arabia's listed energy sector under Vision 2030. It also tests whether board ESG expertise is the boundary condition that determines when these governance structures become effective.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods:</strong> Grounded in stakeholder, agency, and resource dependence theories, a cross-sectional survey collected primary data from 378 finance directors, chief sustainability officers, board members, and senior executives across the population of energy companies listed on the Saudi Exchange. Partial least squares structural equation modeling estimates direct and moderating effects, controlling for firm size, profitability, leverage, age, ownership concentration, and international exposure. Procedural and statistical remedies address common method bias.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Key results</strong>: Hypotheses predict that ESG committees and gender diversity improve disclosure quality, while larger boards reduce it through coordination costs. Board ESG expertise is expected to amplify the effects of independence, diversity, and committees and to offset the costs of board size, indicating that board capability, not composition alone, drives disclosure quality.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusions:</strong> Governance reforms that mandate board structures without building sustainability competence risk producing compliance-driven rather than substantive disclosure. The study offers regulators and energy companies evidence for linking board appointment, training and committee design to disclosure outcomes. Beyond 2030, credible disclosure by hydrocarbon producers is a precondition for tracking progress on clean energy, responsible production, climate action and strong institutions (SDGs 7, 12, 13 and 16) and for holding resource-dependent economies accountable in post-2030 sustainability frameworks. Lessons extend to other Gulf and emerging energy economies.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 315</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Traditional medicine, Africa, medical pluralism, comparative medicine</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Towards Decolonizing Medicine and Healthcare: The Place of African Health and Healing Traditions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Geoffrey Nwaka</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Abia State University, Uturu, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">ginwaka@abiastateuniversity.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Global health science today needs to integrate the health and healing traditions of local communities in Africa. To achieve the ideal of Universal Health Coverage in Africa, we need to promote comparative medicine, and synergy between the practice of traditional and modern medicine.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Traditional medicine and therapeutic techniques have a long history of treating a wide range of human and animal health conditions in Africa. . This rich body of knowledge has been undervalued because of the dominance of Eurocentric mindsets and practices. But current research confirms that many of today’s medicines are derived from tropical African medicinal plants, and that traditional medicine can provide a lead to scientific breakthrough in modern medicine and drug discovery. With colonialism, traditional medicine has been discredited because it does not always appear to conform with the scientific principles of modern medicine, least of all the spiritual and cultural aspects of healing that sometimes involve divination and traditional rituals. Many doctors, scientists and government officials distrust traditional medicine, and hesitate to provide the regulatory and legislative framework for integrating traditional medicine into the national healthcare system until it is possible to validate, codify and standardize its practice to ensure greater safety and efficacy. Unfortunately, modern medicine, with all its obvious merits, is not readily accessible and affordable to a large percentage of the populations, especially in the rural areas; and even in cities, most people combine traditional and modern medicines, especially in response to epidemics and ailments like HIV/AIDS EBOLA, insanity, and COVID19, for which orthodox medicine has not provided ready cure. We argue that global health science today needs to integrate the healing traditions and innovations of local communities in Africa. But the medical pluralism and synergy we desire need to be regulated in order to avoid the indiscriminate mixture of traditional and modern medicine.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 316</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate Mitigation, Policy, Gender, Transport, Just transition</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Gender and Climate change mitigation in transport: A Just transition Perspective on Policy and Practice in India</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nirmali Baruah</u>, Anamika Barua, C. Mallikarjuna</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, India; <span class="paper_email">b.nirmali@iitg.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Reducing carbon dioxide emissions is increasingly urgent in the context of climate change, with the transport sector being a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions. However, mitigating transport emissions is complex and challenging, involving various interests and complexities such as mobility, access, gender, age among others particularly in emerging economies facing dual challenges of economic growth and climate change impacts. Although transport systems are essential for enabling mobility and access to opportunities, it remains unevenly distributed across gender, raising concerns about equity in climate mitigation efforts. This complexity demands holistic policies that reduce emissions while ensuring equitable mobility access and addressing diverse stakeholders&#39; needs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Focusing on India as a key emerging economy in the Global South, this paper, therefore, critically examines how gender is incorporated within transport-related climate mitigation policies. Employing a qualitative approach from a just transition lens- analyzing existing 35 policy documents of related Ministries of India and by conducting 20 interviews with government officials and civil society, the study aims to analyze the national level policies and challenges in addressing the gender concerns and identifying practical solutions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The findings reveal a lack of meaningful gender inclusive participation from pre-policy phase onward, limiting integration of women’s experiences, mobility needs, and perspectives into policy formulation and implementation. Integration of gender perspectives into transport decarbonization is largely missing from transport policy landscape. Furthermore, assumptions that low-carbon transitions benefits are equally distributed risk overlooking structural barriers women face in accessing mobility or participating in climate action. The paper argues that without embedding gender-sensitive perspectives, climate mitigation policies may inadvertently reproduce structural inequities, underscoring the need for more inclusive and justice-oriented policy frameworks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 317</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable interventions, Climate action, Great Green Wall Initiatives, Climate resilience, Rural adaptation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Building Climate-Resilient Livelihoods Beyond 2030: Evidence from Nigeria’s Great Green Wall Initiative</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Cecy Edijala Balogun</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Johannesburg, Centre on the Study of Race, Gender and Class, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">olusanjo2019@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change intensifies livelihood vulnerability across rural communities in Nigeria, undermining resilience and progress towards sustainable development, especially in the northern states where desert encroachment and land degradation are evident. Although adaptation often prioritises environmental restoration, livelihood-centred interventions are equally important for strengthening households’ capacity to withstand climate shocks. This study examines how livelihood interventions implemented under Nigeria’s Great Green Wall initiative improve livelihood capitals and identifies the interventions most capable of supporting Sustainable Development Goal achievement by and beyond 2030. Primary survey data were collected from 262 beneficiaries and non-beneficiaries in Gombe, Kebbi and Sokoto States in Northern Nigeria. Analysed interventions included capacity-building programmes, community development initiatives, household support and distributed starter packs to enhance livelihood diversification in farm and non-farm livelihood activities. Descriptive and inferential statistics were used to assess changes in livelihood capitals and determine which interventions produced the strongest improvements to guide interventions beyond 2030. Results show an overall improvement in respondents’ livelihood capitals, with significant changes in physical and human capital. Among capacity-building interventions, poultry and goat rearing, lubricant production and perfume making significantly improved livelihood capital. Community interventions including solar street lighting, water tanks and food seedling orchards also contributed to improvement. Household-targeted support, particularly energy-efficient cookstoves, gas cylinders and fruit seedlings, further strengthened livelihood capitals. Starter packs, including tailoring machines and kits, veterinary drugs, and soap and lubricant-making kits, similarly generated significant gains. This evidence demonstrates that climate adaptation is more sustainable when ecological restoration is integrated with locally relevant livelihood support. The study concludes that targeted livelihood interventions can reduce vulnerability, expand productive opportunities and reinforce the human, social and material foundations of resilience. Prioritising scalable, inclusive and context-sensitive interventions within climate programmes can accelerate progress towards climate action, poverty reduction, clean energy and resilient communities by 2030 and beyond.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 318</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate resilient water innovations, Household water security, Inclusive water access, fog harvesting direct water reuse</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Sustainable Development Goal 6: Scaling Inclusive Water Innovations for Climate-Resilient Communities in Sub-Saharan Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Cecy Edijala Balogun</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Johannesburg, Centre on the Study of Race, Gender and Class, South Africa; <span class='paper_email'>olusanjo2019@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Achieving universal and equitable access to safe drinking water remains a major sustainable development challenge in sub-Saharan Africa as climate change, population growth, urbanisation, pollution and infrastructure deficits intensify household water insecurity. This paper examines progress towards Sustainable Development Goal 6 and identifies climate-resilient water innovations capable of accelerating inclusive water access by 2030 and sustaining progress beyond the current framework. Specifically, it compares household access to improved drinking water in Ethiopia, Kenya, Namibia, Nigeria and South Africa and assesses innovative responses to persistent water scarcity. The study employs descriptive and comparative case-study designs, drawing on secondary data from the World Bank and the WHO/UNICEF Joint Monitoring Programme for 2013–2022, supplemented by institutional reports and academic literature on water innovations. Indicators include access to basic drinking water, water availability on premises and when needed, freedom from contamination, and piped and non-piped provision across urban and rural areas. The findings reveal progress in basic water coverage but substantial inequalities in service quality and location. Namibia and South Africa record comparatively higher household access, including piped water, while Ethiopia, Kenya and Nigeria continue to experience limited on-premises provision and pronounced rural–urban disparities. Nigeria demonstrates particularly low piped-water access in both urban and rural areas. Fog harvesting, renewable-energy-powered desalination, community water kiosks and direct potable reuse offer viable pathways for diversifying water supplies and strengthening climate resilience. However, high costs, weak regulation, maintenance failures, uneven infrastructure and urban-centred implementation constrain their transformative potential. The paper concludes that progress beyond 2030 requires location-sensitive combinations of water technologies, public investment, quality regulation, community participation and deliberate rural inclusion. Scaling proven innovations while safeguarding affordability and water quality can advance the Sustainable Development Goals SDGs 6, 3, 5, 10, 11 and 13 and support a more equitable post-2030 sustainability agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 319</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.2 AI as an enabling technology for understanding sustainable development and twin transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Green and digital transition; occupational skills; labour markets; artificial intelligence; comparative analysis.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Skills for the Green and Digital Transition: A Comparison of Major Labour Markets</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Meltem UCAL</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kadir Has University, Turkey (Türkiye); <span class="paper_email">msengun@khas.edu.tr</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Renewable energy employment reached 16.6 million worldwide in 2024. China accounted for approximately 7.3 million jobs, compared with 1.8 million in the European Union, 1.4 million in Brazil, 1.3 million in India and 1.1 million in the United States (IRENA and ILO, 2026). This uneven distribution raises questions about how training priorities differ across labour markets.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study proposes a comparative analysis of occupational skill requirements and employment structures, focusing on these five markets where comparable data are available. It asks which occupational groups combine green and digital skill requirements and how their employment shares differ across economies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The analysis will use ESCO and O*NET occupational descriptions, the published correspondence between these systems, and employment statistics classified by ISCO. Selected occupations in renewable energy, manufacturing and technical services will be examined to identify shared skills and potential pathways between related occupations. Employment shares will indicate their relative importance in each market. Comparisons will use a common reference period and compatible levels of occupational aggregation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study will distinguish occupational requirements from workers’ actual skills. It will examine the limitations of applying European and US occupational descriptions to economies with different technologies, production practices and employment conditions. Alternative mappings will be tested to assess robustness.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The intended contribution is a transparent basis for identifying potential training priorities and informing AI-supported skills matching. Particular attention will be paid to which occupational relationships can be transferred across national contexts and which require local evidence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This work is supported by the European Union’s Horizon Europe programme through the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Staff Exchanges project AI4SUSTAIN, under Grant Agreement No. 101296730.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 320</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.2 Lessons learned from SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainability education, Sustainable Development Goals, Environmental pollution, Student perceptions, International collaboration</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Student Perspectives on Environmental Pollution and Sustainability Priorities: Lessons from an International Educational Initiative</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Idrissa Diédhiou</u><sup>1</sup>, Jorge Alonso Alcalá Jáuregui<sup>2</sup>, María Flavia Filippini<sup>3</sup>, Daniela Cónsoli<sup>3</sup>, Matias Venier<sup>3</sup>, Eduardo Martínez Carretero<sup>4</sup>, Ángel Natanael Rojas Velázquez<sup>2</sup>, Fabiola Villegas Rodríguez<sup>2</sup>, Oscar Iván Guillén Castillo<sup>2</sup>, Heriberto Méndez Cortés<sup>2</sup>, Juan Carlos Rodríguez Ortiz<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;EARTH University, Costa Rica; 2:&nbsp;Universidad Autónoma de San Luis Potosí (UASLP) Mexico; 3:&nbsp;Universidad Nacional de Cuyo (UNCuyo) Argentina; 4:&nbsp;Instituto Argentino de Investigaciones de las Zonas Áridas (IADIZA–CONICET) Argentina; <span class="paper_email">idiedhiou@earth.ac.cr</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Objective:</strong> Understanding how university students perceive environmental challenges and the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) is essential for strengthening sustainability education and identifying priorities beyond 2030. This study examined students’ perceptions of environmental pollution, SDG priorities, environmental data management, and preferred learning approaches within an international educational initiative.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Methods:</strong> A virtual workshop on environmental pollution data management was organized in June 2025 through collaboration among universities in Costa Rica, Mexico, and Argentina. A structured, anonymous online questionnaire was administered to 155 undergraduate and postgraduate students from more than 15 countries. Responses were analyzed using descriptive statistics to identify priorities related to the SDGs, environmental information systems, professional training, and educational strategies.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Results:</strong> Responsible consumption and production (SDG 12, 15%) and climate action (SDG 13, 11%) emerged as leading sustainability priorities. Students identified agricultural production systems (21%), climate change (17%), and environmental engineering technologies (14%) as important professional training areas. Environmental impact assessment (17%) was the most frequently highlighted application of pollution data management. Students also expressed interest in remediation technologies (21%) and environmental impact assessment (19%) as specialized educational topics. Collaborative teamwork (11%) and statistical and digital tools (8% each) featured among preferred learning approaches.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>Conclusions:</strong> The findings identify student-defined priorities for strengthening practice-oriented sustainability education. International university collaboration provides a promising framework for integrating environmental data literacy, applied learning, and interdisciplinary perspectives. These insights can inform educational approaches that support SDG implementation and contribute to a more inclusive sustainability agenda beyond 2030.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 321</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Cocoa rehabilitation; particulate organic carbon; mineral-associated organic carbon; carbon sequestration; soil fertility</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Soil carbon and nutrient cycling in cocoa rehabilitation scheme in low latitude environment</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Francis Adu Adjei</u>, Vincent Logah, Joshua Abisah</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Ghana Cocobod/ Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">aduagyeifrancis@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Cocoa (Theobroma cacao L.) production is vital to the economies of many tropical countries but faces persistent challenges, including long-term soil fertility decline and swollen shoot virus disease. These challenges are usually addressed through complete removal of infected trees and replanting with hybrid cocoa varieties and shade trees, often with minimal soil management. Although rehabilitation is expected to alter litter and root inputs that stabilise soil organic carbon (SOC) and consequently nutrient cycling, its impact in low-latitude environments remains poorly quantified.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Consequently, this exploratory paired-plot study in the Bia West District of Ghana compared rehabilitated and non-rehabilitated farms at fixed depths of 0–15, 15–30, and 30–45 cm. In both farms, we sampled four 20 m × 20 m sub-plots and evaluated partitioning of bulk or total SOC into mineral-associated organic carbon (MAOC) and particulate organic carbon (POC) pools. We also assessed the extent to which rehabilitation influences soil nutrient stocks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">At 0–15 cm, bulk SOC and nitrogen stocks were respectively 19% (p&lt;0.01; 42.44 Mg C ha−1) and 15% (p=0.05) greater in rehabilitated plots. Cumulative total SOC stock to 45 cm, determined by the equivalent soil mass (ESM) approach, was 82.89 Mg C ha−1 in rehabilitated plots, compared with 83.31 Mg C ha−1 calculated through the fixed-depth method. At 0–15 cm, MAOC increased by 17% (p=0.01) in the rehabilitated farm, suggesting a shift towards long-term carbon stabilisation in topsoil, but this effect appeared compounded by greater clay content. At 15–30 cm, the rehabilitated farm exhibited a significant decrease of 33.3% in POC (p=0.02) relative to the non-rehabilitated farm type. Topsoil available phosphorus (p=0.03) and effective cation exchange capacity (p=0.001) increased significantly under rehabilitation. Although the study was limited to one site, it provides valuable information on carbon and nutrient cycling in cocoa rehabilitation schemes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 322</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">waqf, donor transition, primary healthcare, Nigeria</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A Waqf Framework for Financing Recurrent Costs of Primary Healthcare During Donor Transition in Nigeria</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Aishatu Usman Muhammad</u><sup>1</sup>, Murni Yusoff<sup>2</sup>, Nura Mohammed<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Gombe State University, Nigeria; 2:&nbsp;Universiti Sains Malaysia; 3:&nbsp;Gombe State University, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">aumummad@gsu.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The primary healthcare system in Nigeria depends mostly on funding from foreign donors. As uncertainty about donor support continues, it becomes difficult to pay staff salaries, medicines, supplies, utilities, and general routine maintenance. Funding capital projects can improve health facilities, but to keep them running requires regular and sustainable funding. This paper examines how waqf (Islamic endowment) could help cover these recurrent costs during and after donor transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Waqf could help meet these costs because the endowed asset is kept intact while the income it generates provides continued funding instead of one-off grants. A recent study in Gombe State found that waqf institutions build health facilities, equip clinics, and support staff. However, reliance on one-off donations, poor asset management, and limited coordination with the government make it difficult to keep paying salaries and buying supplies (Muhammad et al., 2025). Building from this problem, the paper examines how income from waqf could be used to pay for the regular costs of running primary healthcare facilities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using a conceptual analysis and policy review, the paper groups the recurrent costs of primary healthcare and examines how income from property, cash, and productive waqf could help cover them. The proposed framework has three parts, which include clear rules on which regular costs the waqf income will cover, professional management with open financial reporting, and formal arrangements with the state authorities to include waqf funding in health budgets and plans.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper argues that waqf can provide additional funding alongside government and donor support. It suggests practical steps that policymakers and waqf institutions can take to help primary healthcare services run beyond 20230.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 323</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">governance, occupational eye health, commercial drivers, eye-care seeking, sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond licence renewal: Eye-care seeking among commercial drivers in Cape Coast, Ghana, and implications for sustainable development</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Eunice Agyare, Samuel Bert Boadi-Kusi, Enyam Morny, <u>Emmanuel Ekow Ampiah</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">emmanuel.ampiah@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">To distinguish licensing-related from non-licensing eye-care attendance among commercial drivers in Cape Coast, Ghana, and examine associated occupational and health-belief factors, with implications for licensing governance and the integration of eye care into sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">An analytical cross-sectional study recruited 239 male commercial drivers aged 25–60 years through convenience sampling at five purposively selected transport stations. A structured questionnaire assessed examination history, attendance reasons, occupational characteristics, ocular symptoms and Health Belief Model constructs. Presenting visual acuity was measured. Logistic regression examined factors associated with self-reported eye examination within the preceding 24 months.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Overall, 130 drivers (54.4%) reported a recent eye examination. Of these, 100 (76.9%) attended solely for licence renewal; 30 (12.6% of the full sample) attended for routine checks, eye pain or visual problems. Ocular symptoms were reported by 62.8%, while 99.2% had presenting visual acuity of 6/12 or better in the better eye. Auto-rickshaw drivers had lower adjusted odds of recent examination than taxi drivers (adjusted odds ratio 0.36; 95% confidence interval 0.15–0.89), although the overall regression model was not statistically significant. The overall Health Belief Model was also not significant. Exploratory analysis identified higher cues to action among non-licensing attenders.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Recent eye-care attendance was predominantly licensing-related, highlighting the importance of distinguishing regulatory contact from attendance for other health-related reasons when interpreting service coverage. Licence-renewal encounters could provide entry points for comprehensive assessment, referral and follow-up. Linking licensing authorities, transport unions and eye-care providers offers a practical governance approach to integrating eye health into occupational health and transport systems, with potential relevance to worker well-being and sustainable development by and beyond 2030. Non-probability sampling and the cross-sectional design limit generalisability and causal interpretation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 324</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">digital eye strain, occupational eye health, well-being, governance, sustainable development.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digital eye strain and sustainable working lives: A biopsychosocial framework for occupational eye health</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Emmanuel Ekow Ampiah</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">emmanuel.ampiah@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">To synthesise evidence on digital eye strain within a biopsychosocial framework and identify implications for occupational eye health, worker well-being and sustainable development as workplaces become increasingly digital.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A narrative review used structured searches of MEDLINE, Scopus, Web of Science and African Journals OnLine, supplemented by Google Scholar and citation searching. The latest search was conducted in June 2026. Primary studies and reviews were synthesised thematically across biological, psychological and social or occupational domains. Engel’s biopsychosocial model, Karasek’s job demand–control model and Andersen’s model of health-services use informed conceptual integration. No new meta-analysis was undertaken.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Published meta-analytic evidence reported a pooled digital eye strain prevalence of 69% across 103 studies, although definitions, instruments and populations varied. Biological vulnerability included tear-film instability, incomplete blinking and accommodative or binocular-vision demands. Occupational factors included prolonged screen exposure, glare, workstation design, work–rest schedules and job demands. Psychological factors included perceived stress, cognitive load, anxiety and disturbed sleep. The proposed framework links these domains with symptom burden, functional consequences and access to eye care. Evidence was predominantly observational, limiting causal interpretation, and integrated occupational studies from African settings remained scarce.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainable digitalisation should incorporate workers’ ocular health into workplace design and occupational health provision. The framework supports evaluating coordinated approaches combining appropriate eye care, ergonomic improvements, scheduled breaks and attention to psychosocial working conditions. Employer-supported access and clear referral pathways could help embed eye health within healthier working lives. These proposals require prospective testing, particularly in resource-constrained settings. Positioning ocular health within the health and governance dimensions of sustainable development offers a research and policy agenda extending beyond individual screen-use advice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 325</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.4 Trustworthy AI, information integrity, data autonomy, and sustainability impacts of AI</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Afrognosis, AI-driven education, Eurocentric biases, Culturally responsive AI, Sustainable Development, Decolonisation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Deconstructing Epistemological and Eurocentric Biases in AI-driven Education: An Afrognosis Counter-perspective AI for Sustainable Development</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Anthony Kola-Olusanya</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Peter ADEWALE</u><sup>2</sup>, <u>Victor Braimoh</u><sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Osun State University, Nigeria; 2:&nbsp;Osun State University, Nigeria; 3:&nbsp;Osun State University, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">anthony.olusanya@uniosun.edu.ng</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">peter.adewale@uniosun.edu.ng</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">victor.braimoh@uniosun.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This study aims to identify and critique the perceived dominant Eurocentric AI-driven educational frameworks and community leaders and develop an Afrongnosis (African culturally responsive) counter-perspective that centres on African Indigenous knowledge and cultural values for environmental education. A mixed methods approach was used for the study. Questionnaire and interviews were used to draw data from educators, learners and community leaders from diverse backgrounds and analyse AI-driven educational platforms and tools used in different regions and contexts. Eurocentric biases in AI-driven educational platforms revealing limitations in representing diverse cultural perspectives were identified. The result channelled dominant epistemologies and promote Afrognosis as a viable alternative. Culturally responsive AI frameworks for environmental education can empower African communities through inclusive knowledge representation. Therefore, to promote inclusive and equitable quality education in Africa, we recommend adopting an Afrognosis counter-perspective that centres African Indigenous knowledge and cultural values in the development of culturally responsive AI solutions for sustainable development education.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 326</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Africa health systems, health financing, air pollution, health workforce, equity and post-2030 agenda</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Health as Foundation, Not Footnote: Evidence for Equitable, Self-Reliant Health Systems in Africa</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Francis Ohanyido</u><sup>1,2,3</sup>, Seyilnan Gushe<sup>2,3</sup>, Thomas Adoyi<sup>3</sup>, Chioma Ohanyido<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Africa Future of Health Commission, Nigeria; 2:&nbsp;United Nations SDGs Professional Support Group for Africa , Nigeria; 3:&nbsp;West African Institute of Public Health, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">fohanyido@un-sdgs.africa</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Africa&#39;s health gaps are development challenges. Healthy people make sustainable development possible, yet health is still funded as an afterthought once growth arrives. The Africa Future of Health Commission follows the Theme 4 pathway, from systems to risks to solutions to equity, using the best available public evidence.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Foundations are thin. The WHO African Region has about 1.55 physicians, nurses and midwives per 1,000 people against a threshold of 4.45, and faces a projected shortfall of 6.1 million health workers by 2030 (WHO, 2022). Only South Africa sustained the Abuja target of 15% of government spending on health between 2014 and 2020 (WHO AFRO, 2023). Africa CDC reports that development assistance fell 70% between 2021 and 2025, while public health emergencies rose from 152 to 213 between 2022 and 2024 (Africa CDC, 2025).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Many risks sit outside the clinic. Air pollution contributed to an estimated 1.1 million deaths in Africa in 2019, 63% from household air pollution (Health Effects Institute, 2022). Africa imports about 99% of its vaccines, against a continental target of producing 60% by 2040 (Africa CDC; Gavi).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">African experience shows what works. Rwanda&#39;s community-based insurance and community health workers took coverage from 43% in 2005 to 87% in 2020 (Exemplars in Global Health), as life expectancy rose from about 50 years in 2001 to about 70 in 2022 (Think Global Health). Nigeria&#39;s National Health Act (2014) ring-fenced at least 1% of federal revenue for primary care; in April 2026 the Senate passed an amendment to double that share.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The Commission&#39;s central argument is that equitable outcomes require health within energy, climate, urban, education and fiscal policy, not only health ministries. We offer this evidence to help shape a post-2030 agenda in which African institutions lead the solutions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 327</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change, climate finance, Sustainable Development Goals</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Strengthening Climate Resilience Through Domestic Law: Assessing Zimbabwe&#39;s Path Toward Paris Agreement Alignment and SDG 13.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jabulile Tshaliba</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">City of Bulawayo (Local Authority), Zimbabwe; <span class="paper_email">tshalibajj@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">This paper investigates the legal and institutional readiness of Zimbabwe in implementing its Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) under the Paris Agreement and Sustainable Development Goal 13 (SDG 13). Specifically, it examines how the historical lack of a dedicated statutory framework has constrained cross-sectoral coordination, climate finance mobilisation, and domestic climate resilience in a highly vulnerable developing economy. Employing a doctrinal and comparative legal analysis, the research evaluates international climate change instruments including the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) and the Paris Agreement alongside Zimbabwe’s national policy documents. It critically reviews contemporary legislative proposals, including the emerging Climate Change Management Bill, to identify institutional bottlenecks and enforcement gaps across public and private sectors. The study reveals that while Zimbabwe demonstrates international commitments through voluntary policy frameworks and set emission reduction targets, the absence of a binding, overarching legislative architecture creates severe implementation hurdles. Key obstacles include sector fragmentation across agriculture, mining, and finance ministries, inadequate Measurement, Reporting, and Verification (MRV) systems, and legal barriers to scaling up domestic and international climate finance. Furthermore, the findings indicate that existing policies insufficiently mandate public participation and climate justice for marginalised rural communities. Achieving SDG 13 in Zimbabwe requires a transition from voluntary policy guidelines to a binding domestic legislative framework anchored by a specialised Climate Change Act. Enacting strong legal mandates with clear enforcement mechanisms, transparent accountability frameworks, and decentralized institutional structures is critical for bridging the climate finance gap and ensuring legal alignment with international commitments. Looking beyond 2030, a formalised legal framework ensures long-term regulatory certainty, de-risking green investments in renewable energy and sustainable land-use practices. This institutionalised approach lays the groundwork for intergenerational climate justice and sustainable economic transformation well into the future, aligning national growth with a low-carbon, climate-resilient trajectory.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 328</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Administrative Data; Capabilities Approach; SDG Indicators; Sustainable Development Measurement; Well-being; Post-2030 Agenda</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Outcome Indicators: A Capabilities-Based Approach to Measuring Sustainable Development for the Post-2030 Agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Jhoner Perdomo</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Central University of Venezuela. Institute of Economic and Social Research.; <span class="paper_email">jhonerperdomo@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) rely on official statistical systems, including administrative records, to monitor progress through indicators largely designed to measure observable outcomes. Although these data provide essential information for policy evaluation, their coverage and measurement frameworks may limit our understanding of the conditions required to sustain progress over time. This research examines how a capabilities perspective can complement existing approaches to measuring sustainable development.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The study compares a selection of official UN SDG indicators with alternative indicators developed from a capabilities perspective, using Spain as a case study. Drawing on available official statistics, including administrative data where applicable, the analysis examines how both approaches represent the same dimensions of sustainable development. Particular attention is paid to differences in the information captured by outcome-based indicators and by alternatives designed to reflect the capabilities and conditions underlying people's opportunities to sustain and improve their well-being.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The comparison explores how the choice of indicators and underlying data sources shapes the interpretation of social progress. While conventional indicators describe achieved outcomes, capability-oriented alternatives seek to identify conditions and opportunities that may remain less visible in existing measurement systems. The Spanish case provides an empirical basis for examining the potential contribution of this complementary approach.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The research argues that improving sustainable development measurement requires not only reviewing indicator values and data coverage, but also reconsidering what official statistical systems are designed to capture. Integrating a capabilities perspective into the use and interpretation of administrative and other official data could help inform the development of a post-2030 indicator framework better suited to assessing the conditions required to sustain well-being over time.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 329</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">affordable housing; climate justice; extreme heat; flood vulnerability; spatial analysis; New York City</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Affordable Housing at Risk: The Geography of Heat and Flood Vulnerability in New York City</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yakubu Alhassan Zakaria</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Binghamton University, United States of America; <span class="paper_email">yzakaria@binghamton.edu</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">New York City has financed about 290,000 affordable homes since 2024, but few studies ask what climate hazards those investments face. The study joined 6,886 geocoded buildings supported by the Department of Housing Preservation and Development to the city&#39;s Heat Vulnerability Index, tract level Flood Vulnerability Index, and stormwater flood maps. About 69% of affordable units sit in neighborhoods with high heat vulnerability. The share units in tracts with high coastal flood vulnerability rises from 14.1 to 25.8 by the 2080s, and 40.1% units lie within 50 feet of projected extreme rainfall flooding. Preserved and homeownership units were more coastal flood vulnerability than new construction and rentals. Buildings with deeper affordability were not more exposed once borough and project size were held constant. Tracts with high heat vulnerability contained 2.3 times more affordable units per square kilometer. The study recommends that climate risk should be treated as a condition of housing affordability in both sitting and preservation decision.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 330</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Structural Transformation, MLI, NAV</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Measuring the Dynamics of Structural Change in India: An MLI and NAV Approach</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Suman Rani</u>, Balakrushna Padhi</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">BITS Pilani Rajasthan, India; <span class="paper_email">p20230033@pilani.bits-pilani.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract"><em>India has witnessed a complex interplay between economic expansion and employment opportunities over the past four decades. Despite high economic growth, India's labour market has struggled to absorb the surplus workforce, raising concerns about employment growth and a weak process of structural transformation. This study examines the magnitude of structural transformation in output and employment using the Modified Linen Index (MLI), Norms of Absolute Value Index (NAV). The study further employs a time series analysis to examine the relationship between economic growth and structural transformation at the aggregate level. This study utilize the NSSO-Employment and Unemployment Survey and Periodic Labour Force Survey employment datasets from 1993-94 to 2023-24. The findings of the study reflects that despite the high output growth, the rate of employment is very low, indicating that the growth has not been absorbing the labour entering the market. It also suggests that the variation in employment allocation is much more higher compared to the output allocation. The regression results further indicates that HDI, social sector expenditure, Money supply and the urban population are the majore drivers of structural transformation in India. This study indicates that the process of structural transformation remains weak in India, which underscores the need for targeted policy for the provision of decent work and economic growth.</em></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 331</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Procedural justice; Sustainable transformation; Stakeholder inclusion; Decision-making; Diagnostic challenges.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Same Dilemma, Different Reasoning: Making Hidden Assumptions Visible in Sustainability Decisions</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Emilia Sutton</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Sutton Decision Lab, Denmark; <span class='paper_email'>emiliasutton@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Sustainable transformation is not only a question of choosing solutions, but also of examining whose interests, assumptions and perspectives shape those choices. Participants may support the same sustainability objective while overlooking different consequences for affected communities. How can these differences in reasoning be identified and constructively challenged?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation introduces a practice-informed, four-stage diagnostic-challenge framework for examining reasoning in complex sustainability decisions, with particular attention to stakeholder inclusion and procedural justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework is informed by exploratory educational sessions involving approximately 40 students in Warsaw, organized into four groups addressing two dilemmas, with each dilemma assigned to two groups. Informal facilitator observations suggested variations in participants&#39; interpretations and priorities. These observations informed the framework&#39;s development but did not constitute an evaluation of its complete mechanism.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The proposed process begins with an evidence-based decision, followed by identification of its most consequential reasoning weakness. A challenge is then selected to test that specific weakness, such as an overlooked stakeholder interest, an unsupported assumption or an unequally distributed impact. Participants subsequently defend, qualify or revise their decision, with the reasoning behind their response documented.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework&#39;s contribution lies in explicitly connecting initial reasoning, diagnostic interpretation, targeted challenges and subsequent justification. It does not assume that changing one&#39;s position necessarily indicates better reasoning or greater justice.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using an illustrative renewable-energy dilemma, the presentation will demonstrate the framework and propose criteria for assessing its feasibility, diagnostic consistency and relevance of matched challenges. It will also identify priorities for evaluating reasoning quality and stakeholder consideration in future empirical research.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The central question is not simply which sustainability decision participants make, but what they recognize, overlook and reconsider in reaching it.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 332</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digitalization, Labor mobility, Regulation, Migration</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digital Labor Opportunities and (Im)Mobility: Technological promise and legal paradoxes of digital remote for migrants and refugees</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Charles Martin-Shields</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">German Institute Of Development And Sustainability (IDOS), Germany; <span class="paper_email">charles.martin-shields@idos-research.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Countries need workers and workers need jobs, however it is often the case that these two needs are not spatially matched. This paper will explore the current conditions that limit the ability of employers and workers to match remotely across borders, and lays out a research agenda focused on making digital remote labor a viable solution for including those who cannot, or prefer not to, move across border for jobs that can be done remotely. In migration policy the increased use of bilateral labor agreements (BLAs) between countries is evidence that innovative approaches to growing the global labor pool are needed. However, the success or failure of BLAs remains contingent on the coherence of the employment country’s immigration policies and the country providing labor having a large pool of people who are willing and able to relocate to the country where the employer is incorporated. There are cases where people have made digital remote work possible, but the reasons it has worked are often very idiosyncratic. This paper will focus on the experience of an urban refugee who was able to work digitally from Malaysia, while being subject to social security, taxation, and insurance in the United States. The idiosyncrasies of his case help point to spaces for reforming social security, tax, and insurance rules to reduce their ‘bordered-ness’, potentially making digital remote work more systemically viable for migrants and refugees.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 333</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.2 Lessons learned from SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Institutional arrangements, coordination mechanisms, accountability, collaborative governance, stakeholder co-creation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">When sustainability meets existing organizational systems: Pathways to integration and disconnection</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Lali Giorgidze</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Neuchâtel, Switzerland; <span class="paper_email">lali.giorgidze@unine.ch</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">As sustainability becomes increasingly embedded in institutional policies, standards, and governance frameworks, an important question is what happens when new sustainability requirements encounter existing organizational systems. While formal requirements can create opportunities for strengthening sustainability within organizations, their implications depend on how they interact with established structures, responsibilities, and expertise. Understanding these interactions is particularly relevant to discussions about effective sustainability governance beyond 2030.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines how sustainability and quality assurance (QA) interact within higher education institutions under a common external accreditation requirement. Drawing on institutional documents and 32 interviews with university leaders, sustainability officers, QA professionals, and student representatives across 13 Swiss public higher education institutions, the analysis explores how two organizational domains with distinct expertise and responsibilities engage with one another. QA, combining accountability and continuous improvement with stakeholder participation, provides an established organizational system through which sustainability requirements are addressed.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The findings identify four recurring organizational dynamics. First, QA can strengthen the organizational position of sustainability by increasing its formalization, visibility, and institutional leverage. Second, where sustainability is already established, QA may draw on existing sustainability expertise, goals, and monitoring practices without determining their substantive direction. Third, QA and sustainability can mutually reinforce one another through processes adaptation. Fourth, the two domains may remain organizationally disconnected, interacting primarily around formal review requirements while developing in parallel.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">These findings show that incorporating sustainability requirements into existing organizational systems does not necessarily produce integration. Instead, interactions depend on existing organizational arrangements, capacities, and expertise. For sustainability governance beyond 2030, the study highlights the importance of recognizing different organizational starting points and enabling governance and improvement systems to engage with sustainability expertise without prescribing a single organizational model.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 334</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">children&#39;s participation, capability approach, right to the city, participatory urban planning, SDG 11.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Participation to Agency: Children&#39;s Capabilities and the Right to the City in Sustainable Urban Development</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Özlemnur Ataol</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University Of Groningen, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">o.ataol@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">SDG 11 and the New Urban Agenda recognize inclusive and participatory urban development as essential to sustainable cities. Yet, translating participation into meaningful influence remains a challenge, particularly for young people. This study examines how participatory approaches can advance children's agency and right to the city, contributing to a more inclusive sustainability agenda beyond 2030. It brings together insights from participatory work with young people in urban planning, climate adaptation, everyday mobility, and circular urban development. Participatory work includes co-design, photovoice, participatory mapping, and artwork elicitation. The Lundy model, capability approach, and right to the city provide complementary theoretical lenses for examining substantive opportunities to participate in and influence urban development. As a result, first, child-centered participatory approaches reveal valuable situated knowledge, spatial experiences, and capacities for addressing urban challenges that conventional adult-centered planning and design may overlook. However, recognizing environmental problems or possessing relevant knowledge does not necessarily translate into collective agency. Second, meaningful participation depends on enabling conditions extending beyond participatory methods, including educational opportunities, supportive policies, and institutional responsiveness. Advancing SDG 11 beyond 2030 requires shifting attention from participation as procedural inclusion toward participation as an expansion of substantive capabilities and shared decision-making power. In alignment with the New Urban Agenda's vision for sustainable urban development toward 2036, this study argues for institutionalizing children's participation in urban decision-making and recognizing children as present urban citizens and agents of sustainable transformation, rather than postponing the recognition of their citizenship until adulthood.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 335</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">social equity, just transitions, social vulnerability, leave no one behind</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond Solar Lamps: Women’s Pathways to Economic Empowerment and Intrahousehold Bargaining Power</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Uuriintsolmon Narantsetseg</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Institute of Development Studies, United Kingdom; <span class='paper_email'>uuriintsolmonsu@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: To investigate how clean energy entrepreneurship empowers women economically and impacts their agency and intrahousehold bargaining power, addressing the critical roles of social equity and just transitions within sustainable energy interventions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: This research employs a mixed-methods approach, utilizing an anonymized secondary dataset from 60 Decibels encompassing surveys of women clean energy entrepreneurs in Kenya. Quantitative analysis of self-reported impact metrics is triangulated with qualitative key informant interviews to systematically assess shifts in household decision-making, financial independence, and social vulnerability while maintaining strict analytical independence from programme stakeholders.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Results: The analysis demonstrates that women&#39;s active participation in clean energy micro-enterprises significantly enhances their agency and intrahousehold bargaining power. Increased economic agency directly correlates with greater influence over household expenditures, resource allocation, and strategic life choices. However, findings also reveal that while economic empowerment is a critical catalyst, entrenched socio-cultural norms remain persistent barriers to achieving full social equity within the home, illustrating the localized complexities of ensuring we leave no one behind.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Measuring intrahousehold power dynamics is essential for designing genuinely gender-transformative climate action and interventions. To advance sustainable development by and beyond 2030, global energy policies must transcend technical implementation to actively address and mitigate localized social vulnerabilities. Integrating intersectional feminist frameworks and rigorous monitoring and evaluation metrics into environmental programming ensures that just transitions actively dismantle existing inequalities rather than merely equipping women with clean technologies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 336</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">social values; social attitudes; climate action; sustainability</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Social Values and Sustainability: Across Societies and Over Time</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Guodong Ju</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Nanjing University, China, People&#39;s Republic of)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Jing Li</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Guodong Ju</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Nanjing University, China, People&#39;s Republic of)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Advancing sustainability requires more than institutional commitments, technological advances and economic resources. Social values, attitudes and beliefs shape how individuals and communities perceive sustainability challenges, what they consider important, and how they respond to societal and policy changes. They provide an important social foundation for public support, collective action and behavioural change. Yet these orientations are neither uniform across societies nor static over time, raising important questions about how they shape, reflect and respond to sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Understanding and tracking social values is therefore important for examining the social conditions that enable or constrain sustainability transitions. Existing research has investigated values and attitudes associated with specific domains, including environmental protection, climate action, gender equality, health and social trust. Broader conceptual frameworks and measurement approaches have also sought to capture multidimensional patterns of social values. However, important questions remain about how these orientations differ across countries, social groups and generations, how they evolve over time, and how they interact with broader socioeconomic and institutional changes and sustainability outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This session invites theoretical, empirical and methodological contributions examining social values in relation to sustainability, particularly from cross-national, longitudinal and comparative perspectives. We welcome research focusing on specific sustainability domains, individual value dimensions, relationships and trade-offs among different priorities, as well as comprehensive approaches to measuring and understanding multidimensional social values. Relevant topics include, but are not limited to:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Cross-national and longitudinal patterns of social values, attitudes and sustainability priorities;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Development and validation of indicators, composite indices and measurement frameworks;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Relationships between social values, sustainability outcomes, policy support and behavioural change;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Socioeconomic, demographic, cultural and institutional differences in value orientations;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Intergenerational value change and broader processes of social transformation;</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Competing sustainability priorities, public engagement and implications for sustainability governance.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 337</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.1 Modeling Earth system interactions, including climate impacts on development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Flood inundation, Flood management, HEC-RAS, Flood Risk, Climate Change</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">FLOOD HAZARD MAPPING AND ANALYSIS USING HYDRODYNAMIC MODEL HEC-RAS IN BHARATPUR METROPOLITAN CITY, CHITWAN: CHURE CONSERVATION AREA OF NEPAL</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Suzata Karki</u><sup>1</sup>, Subodh Upadhaya<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Pokhara University, Nepal; 2:&nbsp;Pokhara University, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">karki.suz@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Flood is the most common natural disaster in Nepal. Climate Change and the fragile geology of Nepal to be the main contributing factor of increasing frequency and severity. This study was carried out in Bharatpur Metropolitan City within the confines of the Chitwan district, adjacent to left bank of the snow-fed Narayani River encompassing an expanse of 418.8 square kilometers. Out of 28 wards of Bharatpur Metropolitan City, 8 wards namely 3, 4, 16, 17, 18, 26, 27, 28 were studied in this research and involved systematic process of preparation of 2D flood plain maps using HEC-RAS and QGIS. This study aimed to assess the flood inundation area and to access the flood risk associated with different return period such as 2, 5, 10, 50, 100 years respectively. The HEC-RAS river flow model of the Narayani River was calibrated against measured water depth for the year 2016. The produced inundation map indicates direct relationship between the inundation area increases significantly with flood discharge of various return periods. The discharges predicted by Gumbel’s distribution were 10212.68 m 3 /s, 11495.5m 3 /s, 12726.02 m 3 /s, and 14318.8 m 3 /s, 15512.36 m 3 /s for the 5, 10, 20, 50, 100 YRPs, respectively. The context of spatial impact analysis, Ward 28 emerges as a focal point, consistently reflecting substantial repercussions of the identified phenomenon across diverse return periods. In 10-year return period (10YRP), the ward experiences impact, covering 9.61 km² and constituting 75.64% of its total area. This heightened impact persists into the 50-year return period (50YRP), with an extent of 10.23 km², representing a substantial 80.48% of Ward total area. Even in the 100-year return period (100YRP), the impact remains remarkably high at 10.56 km², covering an impressive 83.11% of the ward total area.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 338</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">artificial intelligence, data-center energy efficiency, co-packaged optics, sustainable digitalization</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sustainable AI starts with the network: cutting the energy cost of data-center interconnect</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Susmita Adhikari Joshi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Credo Semiconductor Inc., United States of America; <span class="paper_email">susmita.joshi@credosemi.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Artificial intelligence is now basic infrastructure of daily life, and that ubiquity carries a fast-growing infrastructure footprint: data centers consume 1-2% of global electricity, with AI training and inference the fastest-growing load. When the SDGs were conceived in 2015, neither this technology nor its energy demands existed; the post-2030 agenda must explicitly account for the cohabitation of humanity and AI. Yet policy debate still focuses on compute hardware, while a growing share of power goes to moving data, not processing it. Network equipment draws roughly 5-10% of data-center electricity, but inside the machines most energy metered as compute is spent moving data between processors and memory: over 60% of total system energy in profiled large-scale workloads. As AI clusters scale to hundreds of thousands of accelerators, interconnect becomes a first-order sustainability problem.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This talk traces the industry&#39;s answer from copper through pluggable optics to near-packaged optics (NPO) and co-packaged optics (CPO). Each step moves optics closer to the switch silicon, shortening the energy-intensive electrical link: NPO designs, which eliminate the pluggable module&#39;s DSP, are reported at around 50-60% lower power, and CPO platforms now entering volume production at 51.2 terabits at up to 70% below pluggable transceivers. The second half turns to governance: energy-per-bit metrics and disclosure, efficiency criteria in procurement, coordinated roadmapping. It closes by proposing that industry and policymakers jointly set the first targets for the AI era, beginning with the energy per bit of AI data-center interconnect: reduce it by 2035 against a 2025 baseline, under standardized public disclosure.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 339</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG 1; poverty durability; post-2030 agenda; means of implementation; global assessment; sustainable development goals</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Poverty Reduction to Poverty Durability: Global Lessons from SDG 1 for the Post-2030 Agenda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Sushobhan Joshi</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Groningen, United States of America; <span class="paper_email">sushobhan.joshi@rug.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">A decade of SDG 1 implementation yields a striking lesson for the post-2030 agenda: reducing poverty and keeping it reduced are different achievements. Analyzing 37,926 SDG 1 indicator records across all seven targets for 2000-2024, we find poverty outcomes near the observed performance frontier; 98.6% of robust Target 1.1 trajectories improving; while the systems meant to sustain those gains lag badly: financing and implementation targets sit farthest from the frontier, with 38.2% of trajectories worsening. Organized into outcome, intermediate, and enabling layers, the asymmetry is stark: enabling systems worsen at 7.6 times the rate of outcome systems. Country contrasts sharpen the point: Indonesia, Costa Rica, Bangladesh, Cabo Verde, and Liberia show durable broad-based progress, while Honduras, Equatorial Guinea, Côte d'Ivoire, Kenya, and Jordan regress across multiple dimensions, with trajectories diverging from current levels and income alike.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">We propose a poverty durability perspective: the post-2030 framework should treat durability of gains as an explicit objective, strengthen financing, institutional, and resilience systems alongside outcome targets, and monitor the direction of enabling systems, not just outcome levels.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 340</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">RegionsAdapt, Climate Change Adaptation, Subnational Governments, Multilevel Climate Governance, Sustainable Development Goal 13 (SDG 13).</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">RegionsAdapt: a global initiative supporting subnational governments in adapting to climate change</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Fernando Rei</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Catholic University of Santos, FAAP University Center; <span class="paper_email">fernandorei@unisantos.br</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Launched in 2015 at COP21 in Paris by the Governments of Catalonia and Rio de Janeiro, RegionsAdapt celebrates ten years of promoting climate change adaptation through subnational governments. Coordinated by Regions4, the initiative strengthens regional capacities, fosters knowledge exchange, and encourages concrete adaptation measures, connecting governments across five continents. RegionsAdapt contributes directly to Sustainable Development Goal 13 (SDG 13) of the 2030 Agenda, which calls for urgent action to combat climate change and its impacts. By promoting climate resilience, integrating adaptation into public policies, and strengthening institutional capacities, the initiative helps translate the objectives of the Paris Agreement and the 2030 Agenda into practical territorial action. Its distinctive contribution lies in its method of subnational implementation, which connects global climate commitments with regional priorities and local realities. This approach can be understood through five interconnected steps: (1) political commitment and institutional engagement; (2) assessment of climate risks and vulnerabilities; (3) integration of adaptation into regional planning and public policies; (4) implementation of measures, supported by technical expertise and financial resources; and (5) monitoring, reporting, and knowledge-sharing to assess progress and improve performance.This collaborative framework recognises that subnational governments are strategically positioned to address climate risks through land-use planning, infrastructure, public services, and ecosystem protection. Peer-to-peer learning and the exchange of methodologies and good practices help transform political commitments into institutionalised and measurable adaptation policies. After ten years, RegionsAdapt illustrates the importance of multilevel and polycentric climate governance. Its experience demonstrates that achieving SDG 13 requires more than international agreements: it depends on institutional capacity, territorial knowledge, cooperation, and sustained political engagement. Regional governments are not merely implementers of global agendas; they are active agents in building climate resilience and advancing sustainable development.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 341</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.3 Integrated sustainability assessment of progress made on the SDGs</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">sustainable development goals (SDGs), regression discontinuity design (RDD), evenness, network analysis</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Global sustainable development trajectories did not improve with the adoption of the SDGs</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Xu Luo</u><sup>1</sup>, Xutong Wu<sup>1</sup>, Shuai Wang<sup>1</sup>, Ting Guan<sup>2</sup>, Jianguo Liu<sup>3</sup>, Bojie Fu<sup>4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Faculty of Geographical Science, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China; 2:&nbsp;School of Government, Beijing Normal University, Beijing, China; 3:&nbsp;Department of Fisheries and Wildlife, Michigan State University, East Lansing, MI, USA; 4:&nbsp;Research Center for Eco-Environmental Sciences, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China; <span class="paper_email">luoxu@mail.bnu.edu.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The world remains alarmingly off track to achieving the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) by 2030, highlighting the urgency and necessity of reflecting on implementation outcomes and identifying specific accelerating actions. Here, using global SDG data for 2000–2024 covering 167 countries, we show that trajectories of SDG performance, evenness, and interactions did not improve overall with the adoption of the SDGs; instead, post-2015 progress largely reflects a continuation of pre-existing trends. Nevertheless, countries with greater attention to the 2030 Agenda and stronger economies tend to exhibit a larger number of SDGs with improved performance trajectories. By distinguishing lagging and leading goals and their systemic influences, we identify country-specific SDGs requiring prioritized advancement or structural reform to catch up or maintain progress, and reveal that current implementation is rarely transformative. These findings indicate that the SDGs have fallen far short of the ambition of “transforming our world”, and provide critical evidence to guide SDG acceleration and the design of post-2030 sustainability initiatives.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 342</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">emerging governance frameworks, responsible innovation, international cooperation, risks, Ubuntu</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Ubuntu Alignment Index: survival governance to safeguard humanity from autonomous weapons and technocratic artificial intelligence dominance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Patience Nomonde Mchunu-Sussmann</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of KwaZulu-Natal, South Africa; <span class="paper_email">892134949@stu.ukzn.ac.za</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Artificial intelligence (AI) is being embedded in lethal autonomous weapons, mass surveillance and state decision systems faster than binding governance can follow. Leading scientists warn of catastrophic, potentially extinction-level risk from loss of human control, while technocratic states and corporations race for AI dominance. Sustainable development is impossible without human survival. This contribution proposes Ubuntu, operationalised as the Ubuntu Alignment Index (UAI), as a survival governance mechanism.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The methodological approach is conceptual and comparative policy analysis. The UAI assesses AI systems and their deploying institutions on ukuthembana (relational trust), ukubambisana (participatory consensus) and ubuntu bokuphila (communal wellbeing), across five pillars (People and Culture; Fairness and Justice; Privacy and Security; Technical Rigour; Environmental Stewardship). It is contrasted with technical audit tools and with state-centred, security-led AI governance, and illustrated with South African cases in financial inclusion and marketing.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">It is propounded that prevailing tools measure statistical fairness, not who holds power over life-critical decisions. Three governance failures potentially emerge: absent consent from affected communities, decision authority concentrated in technocratic elites, and no measure of communal harm. The UAI aims to convert these into auditable thresholds: no deployment without participatory consensus, accountable human authority over life-and-death decisions, and demonstrable communal benefit.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The UAI is designed to be a testable proposition, not a proven safeguard. Validation against high-risk systems is the next step. The argument is nonetheless forceful, governance placing relational accountability above technical capability and state power is a precondition for a post-2030 agenda in which humanity stays in command of its tools. Ubuntu, &quot;a person is a person through other persons&quot;, offers the Global South a leading voice in international AI cooperation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 343</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">water-energy footprints, Pakistan, Irrigation, groundwater pumping</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Water-energy-food nexus solutions for climate-resilient and sustainable irrigated agriculture in Pakistan</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Abdul Wahab Siyal<sup>1</sup>, Swarts Thomas<sup>2</sup>, P. Winnie Gerbens-Leenes<sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Pakistan Agricultural Research Council (PARC), Pakistan; 2:&nbsp;Electrical Energy Systems (EES), Eindhoven University of Technology, Eindhoven, Netherlands.; 3:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment and Society (IREES), University of Groningen, Groningen, Netherlands; <span class='paper_email'>wahab_siyal@hotmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Ensuring food security while reducing pressure on scarce water and energy resources is a major sustainability challenge in irrigated agriculture. This study examines water-energy-food nexus interactions in Pakistan’s Indus Basin Irrigation System and explores how spatially informed crop redistribution can reduce water use, energy demand and associated carbon emissions while maintaining food production.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The analysis builds on spatial assessment of irrigation water supply, energy requirements and carbon emissions for four major crops: wheat, rice, cotton and sugarcane. Irrigation hotspots were identified based on crop water requirements, surface and groundwater use, groundwater depth and pumping energy sources. A multi-objective optimization model was subsequently applied to redistribute these crops across 170 sub-canal command regions in Punjab and Sindh while maintaining crop production. Water, energy and carbon outcomes were evaluated under average conditions and an extreme-weather scenario representing the 2022 floods.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Irrigation energy use is highly spatially concentrated, with ten hotspot districts accounting for 42% of the four crops&#39; energy footprint. Under average conditions, optimized crop redistribution reduces the agricultural blue water footprint by 6.3%, energy footprint by 10.9% and carbon footprint by about 9.3%. Energy-focused optimization achieves about 75% of the potential water savings while producing substantially greater energy and carbon reductions than water-focused optimization. Under extreme flood conditions, however, reducing the blue water footprint can increase energy use and carbon emissions, revealing important nexus trade-offs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Sustainable food, water and energy security requires integrated planning rather than optimizing individual resources independently. Beyond 2030, climate-resilient cropping patterns, combined with efficient irrigation and carefully managed clean-energy transitions, can help avoid shifting environmental burdens between water, energy, food and climate goals while strengthening the long-term resilience of irrigated agriculture.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 344</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Global Value Chains, Biodiversity, Environmentally Extended Multi-region Input-output Analysis, Value Added, Employment</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Decoding Mechanism and Inequity of Biodiversity Loss and Socioeconomic Benefits Along Global Value Chains</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Yiqing Huang<sup>1,2</sup>, Ludi Liu<sup>1,2</sup>, Ailin Kang<sup>1</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>2</sup>, Xin Tian<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;School of Environment, Beijing Normal University, Beijing 100875, China; 2:&nbsp;Integrated Research on Energy, Environment, and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen, 9747 AG, Netherlands; <span class='paper_email'>y.q.huang@mail.bnu.edu.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Biodiversity is facing an unprecedented loss crisis. The expansion of global value chains (GVCs) and vertical specialization has obscured the sources of biodiversity loss, complicating conservation and creating an inequal distribution of biodiversity impacts and socio-economic benefits. Few studies have traced biodiversity impacts across different production stages from a value chain perspective, while inequalities between biodiversity impacts and socio-economic benefits across countries remain insufficiently understood, limiting the development of pathways that jointly advance biodiversity conservation and sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study applies an environmentally extended multi-regional input-output model and GVCs analysis to quantify biodiversity and socio-economic impacts and examine their inequalities. The results show that GVCs are important drivers of biodiversity impacts and contribute to a systematic mismatch between biodiversity impacts, social benefits, and economic gains. Specifically, 78.3% of GVC-related biodiversity impacts are concentrated in ecologically sensitive regions, including Latin America, Sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia, while 71.6% of the associated value added accrues to developed economies, particularly North America and Europe. Employment is relatively concentrated in populous developing countries. Biodiversity-sensitive regions bear 33.5% of GVC-embedded biodiversity impacts through upstream primary production, with intermediate production accounting for more than three-quarters of these impacts, whereas downstream-oriented regions capture substantial employment and value added. Spatially, final demand from developed regions drives 55.3% of biodiversity impacts toward developing regions, while the associated value added is mainly retained within developed economies. This creates a pronounced separation between ecological pressures and economic benefits, placing developing regions at a structural disadvantage in many bilateral trade relationships, with the imbalance intensifying along the value chain.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">These findings highlight the need to consider GVCs contribution when allocating conservation responsibilities. Restructuring production, promoting industrial upgrading, and establishing multidimensional responsibility-sharing mechanisms could help reduce the inequality and better align global biodiversity conservation with sustainable development.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 345</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Financial frictions, macroprudential policy, transition risk, permit trading, intensity target</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Emission trading, financial frictions, and transition risks</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Saurav Kumar</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Indira Gandhi Institute of Development Research, India; <span class="paper_email">saurav@igidr.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">I examine how a carbon permit market affects financial stability by using a two-sector Environmental Dynamic General Equilibrium model with emission intensity target and financial frictions. The Ramsey-optimal environmental target and macroprudential policy (taxes or subsidies on bank assets) are contingent on initial permit allocation method and financial frictions. I find that the asymmetric effect of permit trading between firms due to their differing policy compliance propagates from firms’ profit maximization to banks’ portfolio composition. Permit trading and macroprudential policy shift lending toward permit sellers. When policy is implemented without prior announcement, bank net worth falls. Auctioning generates larger losses and a recession, while free allocation entails smaller losses and avoids recession under optimal targets but can be recessionary under stringent targets. However, macroprudential policy limits these losses. Advance policy announcements mitigate transition risk by reducing net worth losses under auctioning and increasing bank net worth under free allocation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 347</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">E-DSGE, Permit market, Intensity target, Business cycle, Energy transition</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Macroeconomic Implications of Permit Market: Evidence from India’s Carbon Credit Trading Scheme</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Saurav Kumar</u><sup>1</sup>, Taniya Ghosh<sup>1</sup>, Shesadri Banerjee<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Indira Gandhi Institute of Development Research, India; 2:&nbsp;Reserve Bank of India, India; <span class="paper_email">saurav@igidr.ac.in</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">We study how a carbon permit market shapes firm-level emission reduction incentives and aggregate environmental and macroeconomic outcomes. We develop a novel and tractable microeconomic framework in which firms endogenously decide whether to become a permit seller. We find that a higher previous period abatement or current green energy adoption increases a firm&#39;s probability of becoming a permit seller but reduces equilibrium share of permit sellers as overall compliance improves, revealing a distinction between individual and aggregate participation. We embed this mechanism in an Environmental-Dynamic Stochastic General Equilibrium model of India&#39;s emission intensity target policy. We find that it achieves energy transition at lower economic costs than a carbon tax. A stricter target and cheaper green energy can accelerate this transition. Finally, endogenous permit price adjustment accommodates shocks that increase fossil-fuel dependence from translating into higher fossil-fuel use, providing a novel stabilization mechanism for permit markets.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 348</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">AquaCrop, APSIM, multiplier onion, Ilocos Norte, irrigation interval, net irrigation requirements, crop-water stress, climatic variability, scenario analysis, sustainability assessment.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Comparative Evaluation of APSIM and AquaCrop for Multiplier Onion Yield Simulation and Irrigation Strategy Assessment under Climate Variability in Ilocos Norte, Philippines</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Jilves Jimenez</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Asian Institute of Technology, Thailand; <span class='paper_email'>st125826@ait.asia</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Efficient irrigation management is essential for sustaining crop productivity and conserving water under climate variability. This study compared APSIM and AquaCrop for simulating multiplier onion yield in Ilocos Norte, Philippines, and applied the model with better validation metrics to assess irrigation strategies across ten historical growing seasons (2015–16 to 2024–25). Both models were calibrated and independently validated using field datasets from Batac, Pasuquin, and Badoc under varying management conditions. AquaCrop achieved lower root mean square error than APSIM (1.066 versus 2.090 t ha⁻¹), higher R² (0.916 versus 0.678), and greater agreement with observed yields. The models also differed in simulated crop–water dynamics, with AquaCrop producing higher mean relative transpiration and fewer transpiration-limited days. Historical simulations identified reference evapotranspiration and climatic water deficit as climatic variables consistently associated with simulated yield under farmers’ irrigation practices. Irrigation scenarios combined 100%, 80%, and 60% of net irrigation requirement (NIR) with 10-, 15-, and 20-day application intervals. Applying 100% NIR at 10- and 15-day intervals produced the highest mean simulated yields (39.77 and 39.60 t ha⁻¹, respectively). However, 80% NIR at these intervals offered a favorable balance among yield, water conservation, irrigation water productivity, and interannual reliability. Relative to farmers’ practices, these strategies reduced simulated irrigation requirements by approximately 67% while increasing mean simulated yield by approximately 11–13%. Although 60% NIR achieved the greatest irrigation-water savings and highest irrigation water productivity, it increased crop water stress, reduced yield, and weakened reliability across seasons. These findings support AquaCrop as a decision-support tool for evaluating irrigation trade-offs and highlight the importance of balancing irrigation amount and timing to sustain agricultural productivity and water-use efficiency under historical climate variability.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 349</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.4 Measures for urban transformation, including sustainable mobility</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Extreme Heat, Urban Mobility Resilience, Built Environment, Bike-sharing, Metro</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Keeping Cities Moving During Extreme Heat: Built Environment and Cycling–Metro Resilience in Shanghai</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Abdulkarem Qasem Abdo Derhem Dammag, Yongping Zhang, Jiali Zhou, Xingang Zhou</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">College of Architecture and Urban Planning, Tongji University, Shanghai, China; <span class='paper_email'>karimdammaj103@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Extreme heat is becoming a critical challenge for sustainable urban mobility, yet the ability of cycling and metro systems to maintain travel activity under thermal stress remains insufficiently understood. This study investigates how built-environment characteristics relate to the heat-related resilience of bike-sharing and metro travel within 282 metro-station areas in Shanghai, China.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Using approximately 4.9 million bike-sharing trips and 32.5 million metro trips, we compared travel activity during a sustained heatwave with thermally normal conditions, separately for weekdays and weekends. Resilience was expressed as the ratio of heatwave to normal trip activity. Building enclosure and greenery ratio were derived from street-view imagery, and geographically weighted regression was applied to examine associations with resilience while accounting for other station-area characteristics.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Bike-sharing activity declined by 16.7% overall, with greater reductions during off-peak and weekend periods. Metro activity remained relatively stable on weekdays but decreased by 7.1% on weekends. Building enclosure and greenery ratio were positively associated with bike-sharing resilience across the study areas, while greenery ratio also showed consistently positive associations with metro resilience. Distance to the city centre was negatively associated with cycling resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">These findings highlight the relevance of station-area environments to climate-resilient urban mobility. Integrating human-scale streetscape characteristics into sustainable transport planning may help identify priorities for adaptation to extreme heat. However, the observed associations do not establish causal effects or directly demonstrate the effectiveness of specific interventions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 350</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">European food trade, food system, externalities, FABIO, true cost accounting</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From environmental pressures to economic damages in European food trade</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Henri Contor<sup>1</sup>, Molla Tigabu Kasaw<sup>1,2</sup>, Erik Mathijs<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Division of Agricultural, Food and Resource Economics (DAFRE), Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences, KU Leuven, Leuven,  Belgium; 2:&nbsp;Social Science Unit, Flanders Research Institute for Agriculture, Fisheries and Food (ILVO), Merelbeke-Melle, Belgium; <span class='paper_email'>henri.contor@kuleuven.be</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective. European food trade separates consumption from the environmental pressures and prospective economic losses associated with production across global value chains. This study examines where these pressures and damages occur, whose demand drives them, and whether monetary damage valuation changes the hotspots identified from physical pressures alone.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods. We link FABIO, a product-resolved physical input-output model of global food supply chains, with SPIQ-FS, which provides country-, pathway-, scenario- and uncertainty-specific marginal damage costs. Producer-commodity-consumer tracing attributes environmental pressures generated along food supply chains to European and non-European final demand. By connecting physical pressures with prospective economic damages, the approach develops an alternative metric for assessing production and consumption beyond conventional measures of economic activity. We distinguish domestic, intra-EU, import and export channels and estimate distributions of prospective economic losses across SSPs. Robustness tests assess uncertainty and alternative allocation assumptions. We additionally compare damage-cost exposure with value added associated with the same demand.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results. Preliminary results use FABIO’s environmental extensions to map the physical pressures embodied in European food production, consumption and trade. We compare non-EU production serving EU demand, EU production serving foreign demand, and intra-EU trade, identifying the producer countries, commodities and bilateral links that dominate environmental pressures. Further analysis will examine their resulting economic damages using SPIQ-FS. At this point, results will only reveal how the geography and composition of environmental pressures differ across European food-trade relationships.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions. By connecting the geography of food production to the demand that ultimately drives environmental pressures, the study will make visible economic damages that conventional physical footprint accounts do not capture. It contributes to economic approaches beyond GDP by complementing value-added measures with indicators of environmental pressure and prospective economic loss across food value chains. This integrated perspective can inform more accountable and resilient food-system strategies beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 351</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">regional climate action, multilevel governance, carbon farming, agricultural policy, state legislation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Partisan control drove United States state carbon farming legislation, 2009 to 2025: lessons for subnational climate governance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>ROCK ABOUJAOUDE</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Florida, United States of America; <span class="paper_email">raboujaoude001@ufl.edu</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This study evaluated how United States state legislatures used law to promote agricultural carbon sequestration and which state characteristics predicted legislative activity.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A systematic review of all 50 states used LegiScan to identify enacted carbon farming legislation from 2009 through 2025. Each bill was coded as a substantive statute or a non-binding resolution and scored against five policy design criteria. A negative binomial regression fit by maximum likelihood tested state-level predictors of enacted bill counts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: The review identified 193 relevant enacted bills: 163 substantive statutes and 30 non-binding resolutions. Only 24 states enacted any relevant legislation. Eleven bills in seven states (California, Colorado, Hawaii, Maryland, Minnesota, Oregon and Washington) met all five design criteria. Democratic trifecta control was the sole significant predictor of legislative activity, with an incidence rate ratio of 10.82.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: State carbon farming policy remained concentrated in a small group of states and tracked partisan control more than any other tested factor. Comprehensive frameworks were rare even where legislation existed. For sustainable development by and beyond 2030, these results showed that voluntary subnational leadership produced an uneven patchwork rather than a national floor. Durable agricultural mitigation will likely require multilevel governance arrangements that pay farmers for verified sequestration without depending on single-party control of state government.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 352</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">life-cycle assessment, business models, alternative metrics, dairy farming, environmental valuation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Beyond conventional farm performance: integrating True Cost Accounting and business-model heterogeneity in farming systems</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Molla Tigabu Kasaw<sup>1,2</sup>, Henri Contor<sup>2</sup>, Liselot Bourgeois<sup>1</sup>, Erik Mathijs<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;ILVO-Institute for Agricultural, Fisheries &amp; Food Research, Belgium; 2:&nbsp;Division of Agricultural, Food and Resource Economics, Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences, KU Leuven,  Belgium; <span class='paper_email'>MollaTigabu.Kasaw@ilvo.vlaanderen.be</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Sector-level averages can obscure farm-system heterogeneity and weaken the policy relevance of environmental sustainability metrics. This study develops a comparative farm-level True Cost Accounting approach to assess how environmental impacts and external costs vary across dairy-farm business-model (BM) configurations, which impact categories and processes drive these differences, and whether conclusions depend on expressing performance per unit of milk or per farm.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: Environmental inventory data from Flemish dairy farms that completed a Klimrek climate scan are matched to four previously identified dairy BM profiles using structural and managerial characteristics. The farm remains the analytical unit, while the profiles provide a comparative interpretive lens. We conduct a cradle-to-farm-gate life-cycle assessment using different functional units. ReCiPe 2016 midpoint indicators characterise environmental pressures, while endpoint indicators estimate damage to human health, ecosystems and resource scarcity. These damages will be monetised using documented valuation factors and harmonised to a common price year; midpoint, endpoint, and monetary results remain separate. Sensitivity analyses examine allocation, system boundaries, profile matching and valuation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Preliminary results show that across 114 farms, each represented by one annual inventory, the median climate-change impact was 902 t CO₂-eq per farm (IQR: 647–1,309). For the included pathways, median modelled endpoint damage was 2.81 DALYs (IQR: 1.95–3.87) for human health and 9.97 × 10⁻³ species·yr (IQR: 7.18 × 10⁻³–1.42 × 10⁻²) for terrestrial and freshwater ecosystems combined. When endpoint damages were pooled across farms, fine particulate matter contributed 70% of human-health damage, land use 47% of terrestrial-ecosystem damage, and freshwater eutrophication 90% of freshwater-ecosystem damage.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: By linking farm heterogeneity to physical impacts, endpoint damages and monetised environmental costs, the study moves beyond sector-average and carbon-only assessments. It provides a transparent basis for differentiated farm transition strategies and environmental policy within agricultural and natural-resource economics.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 353</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.2 Actions for climate change adaptation to build societal resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Barriers; Pathways; Climate Smart Agriculture; Gilgit-Baltistan, Pakistan</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Navigating barriers and pathways to climate-smart agriculture in high-mountain areas: Farm-level evidence from Gilgit-Baltistan, Pakistan</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Iftikhar Ali</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Leibniz Centre for Agricultural Landscape Research (ZALF), Germany, Germany; <span class="paper_email">Iftikhar.Ali@zalf.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate Smart Agriculture (CSA) practices such as adopting disease-tolerant crop varieties and shifting from traditional cropping to diversified horticulture and extensive livestock herding have been widely promoted to ensure sustainable agriculture in climate-sensitive regions. However, the adoption of CSA remains limited in Pakistan’s high mountain regions, restrained by diverse underlying barriers. This study examines the barriers and pathways to CSA adoption in Gilgit-Baltistan, Pakistan, employing a mixed method approach. Quantitative data were collected using a structured household survey, involving 388 randomly selected farm households, while qualitative data were obtained via face-to-face key informant interviews and focus group discussions. Descriptive statistics, non-parametric tests, and thematic analysis were used to analyze the collected data. The study findings show that the adoption of climate smart agriculture is restrained by diverse socio-economic, institutional and technological barriers. Poverty and limited financial resources emerged as the most severe socio-economic barriers, reported by 73.4% of farmers, followed by a lack of awareness about climate change (64.2%). In addition, 59.4% identified limited access to credit services as a major institutional barrier, while 39.9% reported lack of technical know-how about CSA as a key barrier. The results further reveal several pathways to improve CSA adoption suggested by farmers, including farmer awareness programs (90.3%), monetary subsidies to offset adoption costs (87.9%), and farmer training programs (71%). The findings underscore the urgent need to mainstream locally adapted CSA frameworks into regional and local development planning to ensure sustainable agricultural development in the high mountain region of Gilgit-Baltistan in Pakistan.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 354</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Resource security, infrastructure governance, political economy, institutional coordination, sustainable development</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Strategic Resource Dependencies and the Governance of Infrastructure Connectivity: A Global South Perspective</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Raheel Zaman</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Government of Pakistan, Pakistan; <span class='paper_email'>rehanakarimullah2017@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: This paper examines how strategic dependencies involving energy, water, transport and urban infrastructure shape the governance of sustainable development in Global South metropolitan systems. It asks how resource interdependence becomes a source of vulnerability or resilience, and under what institutional conditions infrastructure connectivity can support progress towards the Sustainable Development Goals beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The paper develops a conceptual framework linking resource dependencies, infrastructure interdependence, political economy and institutional coordination. Karachi, Pakistan, serves as an illustrative metropolitan case through a proposed qualitative analysis of relevant policy documents, publicly available institutional materials and secondary literature. The analysis distinguishes material interdependence from the governance arrangements through which dependencies are managed.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: The framework identifies three analytical mechanisms: resource dependence can transmit external and domestic disruptions across interconnected systems; fragmented institutional mandates can impede coordinated responses; and effective coordination can improve the capacity to manage trade-offs between resource security, infrastructure investment and equitable development. These are conceptual propositions to be examined through the case analysis, rather than assumed empirical findings.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Sustainable development requires more than expanding infrastructure or diversifying resource supplies. It also depends on institutions capable of coordinating interdependent systems across administrative and territorial boundaries. By connecting resource security with metropolitan governance and political economy, the paper offers a framework for examining how Global South cities can strengthen resilience and inform integrated sustainability policies beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 355</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">database, organizational learning, development cooperation, adaptive cooperation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">AI’s Potential to Facilitate More Agile Development Cooperation: Introducing a New Database</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Semuhi Sinanoglu</u>, Michael Roll</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">German Institute of Development and Sustainability, Germany; <span class="paper_email">Semuhi.Sinanoglu@idos-research.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">How does development cooperation use artificial intelligence (AI), and does this use help the sector learn? While AI can free up capacity for the local, political work at the heart of development, it also risks crowding that work out. To assess who deploys AI in the development cooperation sector, and for what purposes, we built an original primary-source database of AI deployments by donor ministries, implementing agencies, development banks, and international and humanitarian organizations. Our database shows that program delivery, such as using AI for more efficient forecasting and targeting, and internal operations, such as staff chatbots, make up more than half of existing AI deployments in the sector. By contrast, the functions closest to organizational learning, such as monitoring, evaluation, coordination and governance, account for less than 1/5th. In addition, maturity remains modest: 23 percent operate at scale, and 17 percent are still pilots. Our results suggest that the sector uses AI to do its existing work faster, not to change how it works and learns. We call this “plug-and-play efficiency.” In response, we propose a framework that turns information into institutional change. For sustainable development by and beyond 2030, AI adoption must be paired with reforms toward iterative, adaptive cooperation so freed capacity flows to local political work.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 356</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.4 Trustworthy AI, information integrity, data autonomy, and sustainability impacts of AI</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">responsible AI; cognitive autonomy; AI literacy; higher education; social sustainability.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Cognitive autonomy and responsible artificial intelligence use in higher education: lessons from NeuroIA in Uruguay</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nelly Beguerie</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">UTEC, Uruguay; <span class="paper_email">nelly.beguerie@utec.edu.uy</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: To describe NeuroIA, an interdisciplinary higher education course in Uruguay, and examine participants’ perceptions of its contribution to critical and responsible artificial intelligence (AI) use. Cognitive autonomy is considered in relation to socially sustainable digitalization.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: NeuroIA was delivered in 2026 as a 30-hour, primarily self-paced course integrating cognitive psychology, neuroscience and ethics. This descriptive case study examined 165 voluntary end-of-course questionnaires, including 132 open-ended responses, and records of 214 final-project submissions. Open responses underwent preliminary thematic interpretation, compared with a non-random exploratory sample of projects across three assignment formats. The datasets could not be linked at participant level.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Among valid questionnaire responses, 94.5% (154/163) rated the course positively, 95.7% (156/163) reported that it connected neuroscience and AI, and 90.8% (148/163) perceived that they achieved its learning objectives. Additionally, 66.9% (109/163) rated its perceived influence on their interaction with AI highly. Open responses emphasized cognitive delegation, critical judgment and attention management. Sampled projects proposed verifying AI outputs, protecting focused attention and maintaining human oversight. Some projects also contained unsupported claims about neurological harm.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: NeuroIA offers an educational example for discussing cognitive autonomy, accountability and digital well-being within socially sustainable digitalization. Findings concern self-reported perceptions and proposed practices, not observed behavioral change, objectively measured cognitive improvement or causal course effects.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 357</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">natural disasters, taxation, fragile states, Haiti, climate finance</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Collective Threats and Fiscal Demands in Fragile States: Experimental Evidence from Haiti</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ana Isabel Lopez Garcia</u><sup>1</sup>, Sarah Berens<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Maastricht University, Netherlands, The; 2:&nbsp;University of Bremen; <span class="paper_email">a.lopezgarcia@maastrichtuniversity.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Existing research associates wars, disasters, and pandemics with greater solidarity, stronger demand for protection, and increased willingness to pay taxes. Most evidence however comes from contexts in which the state is seen as a credible provider of protection. Focusing on fragile states, we ask whether, and under what conditions, collective threats can generate support for taxation. We argue that the fiscal effects of collective threats depend on whether citizens understand them as exogenous or endogeneous to government action. We test this using a survey experiment fielded in autumn 2026 (N = 2,000) in Haiti. Respondents are randomly exposed to a neutral prompt, a natural-disaster prompt, or a gang-violence prompt. We then examine effects on the ranking of competing local spending priorities and support for both higher and more progressive property taxation. We expect the disaster prime to increase support for spending on preparedness and security, as well as the taxation required to finance it. The gang-violence prime should have the opposite effects because armed violence in Haiti is closely associated with political collusion.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 358</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Haiti, climate resilience, disaster preparedness, taxation, nonstate provision, foreign aid.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Financing Climate Resilience: Evidence from a Survey Experiment in Haiti</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ana Isabel Lopez Garcia</u><sup>1</sup>, Sarah Berens<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Maastricht University, Netherlands, The; 2:&nbsp;University of Bremen; <span class="paper_email">a.lopezgarcia@maastrichtuniversity.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">In fragile states, public services are often financed and delivered by non-state actors, including communities, migrants, and international donors. This paper asks whether governments can nonetheless build support for taxation by performing visible administrative roles in such projects (e.g. by issuing permits, monitoring implementation, and ensuring compliance with agreed rules). It also explores whether the effects of government involvement vary with project transparency and the identity of the external funder. We address this using a vignette experiment embedded in an online survey in Haiti in autumn 2026 (N = 2,000). Participants are randomly assigned presented a community-led disaster-preparedness project that varies in whether the local government helps regulate and oversee the project, whether funding comes from the Haitian diaspora or international donors, and whether information about project spending and implementation is made publicly available. Respondents are then asked about their willingness to pay higher local taxes for funding similar disaster-preparedness projects. We test whether government involvement changes willingness to contribute because citizens give local authorities more credit for the project or because they expect the project to be more effective.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 359</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">diaspora finance; remittances; climate adaptation; disaster resilience; public goods; foreign aid; fragile states; conflict-affected states</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Diaspora Finance for Climate Resilience in Fragile, Conflict-Afflicted States</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ana Isabel Lopez Garcia</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Maastricht University, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">a.lopezgarcia@maastrichtuniversity.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">As foreign aid contracts, migrant remittances and diaspora finance are increasingly viewed as potential sources of funding for climate resilience. However, diaspora resources are not politically neutral. By substituting for public provision, diaspora finance may loosen the fiscal relationship between citizens and governments. Diaspora contributions often respond to community obligation, and, where armed actors hold territory, are subject to extraction and licensing by them. This project asks whether the way climate-resilience projects are designed can turn diaspora contributions into a force for state-building rather than -erosion. It makes use of a conjoint experiment embedded in parallel online surveys of diaspora members resident in Canada. Respondents are presented hypothetical climate-resilience projects in their country of origin. Projects vary the role of public authorities and the degree of armed control over access to the project site, alongside other attributes such as who selects the project, who monitors implementation, how expenditure is reported, whether diaspora contributions are matched by an external funder and the project site’s exposure or not to a severe climate-related hazard. The outcomes of interest are: (i) project choice and (ii) willingness to contribute to the chosen project. The focus is on diaspora members from Haiti and Somalia, both extremely fragile countries featuring heavy aid dependence, recurrent climate risks and fragmented public authority but differing in the types of hazards they face and in the character of armed authority. Results will shed light on: (i) what combinations of institutional features practitioners could use to attract diaspora investment in climate finance while simultaneously keeping a role of state institutions in contested areas and (ii) whether arrangements preferred in one setting can travel to another. By producing experimental evidence on what drives diasporas to finance public goods in conflict-afflicted states, the study expands research on diaspora finance, informal taxation, hybrid governance and environmental politics.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 360</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">machine learning; renewable energy; waste management; hydrothermal liquefaction</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Data-driven process simulation and techno-economic evaluation of microalgae hydrothermal liquefaction</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Yongdong XU</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">National university of Singapore, Singapore; <span class='paper_email'>ceev358@nus.edu.sg</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Hydrothermal liquefaction (HTL) is a promising route for converting wet microalgae into biocrude oil, but its process evaluation can be strongly affected by the yield and higher heating value (HHV) values selected from individual experiments. This study developed a data-driven workflow integrating literature-scale machine learning (ML), laboratory validation, and Aspen Plus-based gate-to-gate energy, cost, and carbon assessment. A</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">dataset containing 406 microalgae HTL records was used to predict biocrude yield and HHV, screen feedstockcondition-specific candidate scenarios, and identify key influencing factors. The optimized models achieved testing R2 values of 0.7313 for HHV and 0.7909 for yield. The ML-screened conditions were then experimentally validated using Spirulina and Chlorella, with prediction errors ranging from 1.98% to 15.0%. The process assessment showed that the scenario with the highest feedstock-based energy recovery efficiency (ERE) did not provide the highest overall energy conversion efficiency (OECE). Chlorella treated at 270 ◦C, 30% solid concentration, and 60 min achieved an ERE of 63.71%, an OECE of 58.00%, a gate-to-gate process-energy-derived cost of $85.03 per barrel equivalent, and direct process-energy-derived CO2 emissions of 57.03 kg per barrel equivalent. These results indicate that yield or HHV alone is not sufficient for HTL condition selection. A feedstock-specific energy-yield screening step, followed by experimental validation and process-boundary-aware simulation, provides a clearer basis for comparing microalgae HTL scenarios under a defined gate-to-gate boundary</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 361</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.3 Climate change mitigation and net-zero pathways for a just transition</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Evapotranspiration, Geographic contexts, Water management, Photovoltaic farms, Vegetation recovery</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Water management regulates vegetation recovery in photovoltaic farms across China</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yueran Hu</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Chinese Academy of Sciences, China, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class="paper_email">yrhu_st@rcees.ac.cn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Expanding photovoltaic (PV) energy is central to climate change mitigation, but its ecological benefits depend on how solar farms are managed. In particular, vegetation restoration may require additional water, creating trade-offs between renewable energy development, ecosystem recovery and water conservation. Yet the role of water management in shaping vegetation responses remains poorly understood.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We assessed vegetation changes in PV farms across China using satellite observations and an integrated modelling framework. We adjusted the normalized difference vegetation index to account for PV panels and their shadows, and estimated human-induced evapotranspiration change as a proxy for water management intensity, separating it from natural and microclimatic contributions. Machine learning and dose-response modelling were used to examine vegetation responses and compare observed, no-management and optimized-management scenarios.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Vegetation responses to water management were nonlinear and varied across climatic conditions. The estimated optimal human-induced evapotranspiration change increased from 92 mm per year in humid regions to 200 mm per year in arid regions. Under the optimized scenario, the improvement in vegetation greenness was 2.3 times that under observed management, while total water consumption decreased by 21.5%. These modelled benefits indicate that better water allocation, rather than uniformly increasing water inputs, could improve ecological outcomes. Estimated optima should be interpreted as indicative ranges rather than precise prescriptions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Our findings support incorporating climate-specific water management into PV planning and operation. This approach could help reconcile renewable energy expansion with ecological restoration and sustainable water use, contributing to more environmentally sustainable low-carbon transitions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 362</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">urban boundary areas; land surface temperature, natural factors, human factors, interaction effects</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Interactive Effects of Natural and Human Factors on LST in China’s Urban Boundary Areas</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Rui Huang</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Nanjing Normal University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class='paper_email'>huangrui4420@163.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Urban boundary areas in China are thermally sensitive zones shaped by urban expansion and jobs–housing separation, where land surface temperature (LST) is jointly influenced by complex interactions between natural and human factors. Based on MODIS LST products from 2003 to 2023, combined with multi-source data including DEM, meteorological data, nighttime lights, population density, road network density, land use composition, two-dimensional landscape metrics, and three-dimensional building morphology, this study constructs a natural–human factor system and employs Geodetector, interaction detector, random forest, and SHAP to analyze the spatiotemporal differentiation of LST and its driving mechanisms in China’s urban boundary areas. The results show that: (1) LST in urban boundary areas exhibits significant diurnal and seasonal differences; daytime LST is strongly affected by natural factors and two-dimensional landscape patterns, while nighttime LST is dominated by human factors, with notable nighttime warming in winter. (2) Nighttime lights, impervious surface proportion, building density, and road network density are the main warming factors, whereas DEM, NDVI, water body proportion, and wind speed have significant cooling effects. (3) Natural and human factors show significant interaction enhancement effects; the q-statistics of interaction terms such as nighttime lights ∩ DEM, building morphology ∩ vegetation cover, and road network density ∩ water bodies are generally higher than those of single factors, indicating that the thermal environment in boundary areas is a coupled result of natural background and human activities. (4) The direction and strength of factor effects vary by season; natural factors contribute more in summer, while human factors become more influential in winter. This study suggests that thermal environment governance in China’s urban boundary areas should integrate natural background protection with human morphology optimization and implement diurnal- and seasonal-specific spatial regulation strategies to mitigate thermal risks and support sustainable urban planning.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 363</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.4 Missing links and reforms for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Culture &amp; language, Sustainability indicators, Education for sustainable development, Civic participation, Post-2030 agenda.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Can sustainability afford to overlook culture? Evidence for the post-2030 agenda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mar Gaitán</u>, Indra Ramírez, Alba Gandía, Arabella Leon, Ester Alba</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Universitat de València, Spain; <span class="paper_email">m.gaisal@uv.es</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">If museums can help young people understand environmental challenges and imagine different futures, how should their contribution count towards sustainable development? As debates on the post-2030 agenda gather momentum, the place of cultural institutions in sustainability education deserves closer examination.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">This paper draws on TOWCHED, a Horizon Europe project coordinated by the Universitat de València, to investigate what museum-based education can contribute to future sustainability priorities. It presents findings from nine educational pilots in Spain and Latvia, evaluated through retrospective self-assessments from 159 children and young people, a survey of 36 museum professionals and educators, and 12 interviews.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Participants reported statistically significant improvements in sustainability-related competencies (r = 0.78), with the strongest results in environmental sustainability. Yet outcomes varied considerably across museums. The evaluation identified a recurring condition for effective learning: the relationship between the museum collection, the sustainability issue being explored and the participants' own experiences. Qualitative findings also revealed forms of civic engagement and attachment to place that the original assessment framework captured only partially. Meanwhile, collaboration between museums and schools emerged as an important contribution to the continuity of educational activities.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">These findings raise a question for the post-2030 agenda: how can cultural contributions to sustainability be recognised without overlooking the conditions that make them meaningful? The paper proposes two directions for future policy: explicitly recognising cultural institutions within sustainability education frameworks and developing evaluation indicators that capture locally situated learning and civic participation. It also considers the need to assess whether educational partnerships can endure beyond individual interventions.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">Although the evaluation cannot establish long-term or causal effects, its findings provide empirical grounds for reconsidering how cultural learning is represented in future sustainability priorities.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 364</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">energy transition risk; financial development; financial inclusion; renewable electricity; oil and gas producing economies</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Financial Development, Transition Risk and Decarbonization Policy in Oil and Gas Producing Economies: Evidence from Egypt</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mohamed Ibrahim</u><sup>1</sup>, Asmaa Harraz<sup>2</sup>, Rasha Mandouh<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Faculty of Graduate Studies for Statistical Research ,Cairo University, Egypt; 2:&nbsp;University of Science and Technology, Zewail City of Science, Technology and Innovation, Egypt; <span class="paper_email">eng.moh.saad315@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Oil and gas producing developing economies must decarbonize while their fiscal revenues, banks and industry remain built around hydrocarbons. This study asks whether financial development accelerates their transition to renewable electricity, and introduces a new tool for measuring the risks that can derail that transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A balanced panel of Egypt, Indonesia, Kazakhstan and Malaysia (2015–2024) links the renewable share of electricity to banking depth, stock market size and financial inclusion, using pooled, fixed-effects and random-effects estimators selected by Hsiao and Hausman tests. A Bayesian network maps Egypt’s systemic transition risks, and these feed an original Energy Transition Risk Index (ETRI) covering policy, economic-financial, technological and geopolitical risk.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Once country heterogeneity is controlled for, domestic credit and market capitalization have no significant effect on the renewable share. Financial inclusion has a significant negative effect (β = −0.27, p &lt; 0.01), a “Financial Inclusion Paradox”: where fossil fuels are subsidized, new access to finance funds energy-intensive consumption supplied by fossil generation. GDP growth and FDI show no association. The Bayesian network classifies five risks as unacceptable, including debt arrears, gas field depletion and energy security crisis. The ETRI scores Egypt at 72.3, in the high-risk band.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: In hydrocarbon economies, finance follows policy. General financial deepening will not shift the power mix; targeted green finance, sequenced fossil fuel subsidy reform and risk-informed policy will. The ETRI offers governments and lenders a transparent way to diagnose transition risk and decide where to act first.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 365</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">datacenters, sustainability, measurement, heat reuse, flexibility</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Modular, Integrated, Sustainable Data Centers</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Magiel Bruntink</u>, <u>Bram van der Waaij</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">TNO, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">magiel.bruntink@tno.nl</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">bram.vanderwaaij@tno.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">How can we build the next generation of data centers that are not only sustainable and secure; but also modular, decentralized, and deeply integrated with our energy systems?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The MISD (Modular, Integrated, Sustainable Data Centers) project is a national flagship initiative supported by the Dutch government under the IPCEI-CIS program, which was instated by the EU. With a target of deploying 10,000 highly secure and climate neutral edge nodes by 2030, the EU’s focus is on creating a sovereign cloud and data infrastructure for Europe’s digital independence. Under the umbrella of the IPCEI-CIS program, MISD is developing a new concept for edge data centers that are close to users, sustainable, energy-flexible, and secure-by-design.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">From orchestration algorithms to liquid cooling, from cybersecurity frameworks to field labs, MISD is laying the foundation for a new ecosystem.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">We will share a first look at the distributed network of data centers of the future and invite you to co-develop it.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Whether you’re driving energy innovation, shaping policy, building tech, or using data services, MISD offers a platform for strategic collaboration and co-creation with:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">- Utility providers and grid operators to optimize integration with the grid</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">- Policymakers and governments to align climate and sovereignty goals</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">- Vendors and suppliers to co-develop modular and scalable solutions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">- Consultants, operators, and developers to share their own expertise and views</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">- Users who benefit from modular, sustainable, and secure-by-design data center solutions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Join us to shape the future of green data centers in Europe and be part of shaping it!</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 366</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">systemic risk; Bayesian network; energy transition risk index; resilience; Egypt</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Mapping Cascading Risks in Egypt’s Energy Transition: A Bayesian Network and Energy Transition Risk Index</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Mohamed Ibrahim<sup>1</sup>, Asmaa Harraz<sup>2</sup>, Rasha Mandouh<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Faculty of Graduate Studies for Statistical Research ,Cairo University, Egypt; 2:&nbsp;University of Science and Technology, Zewail City of Science, Technology and Innovation, Egypt; <span class='paper_email'>eng.moh.saad315@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Energy transitions in fossil-fuel-dependent developing economies rarely fail because of one risk. They fail because financial, operational, geopolitical and policy risks interact and amplify each other. Using Egypt’s oil and gas sector as a case, this study maps how these risks cascade and proposes a practical way to measure and act on them.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A qualitative Bayesian network links 14 risk sources, 5 initiating events and 5 consequences. Each element is assessed for probability, severity and strength of knowledge, and evaluated against ISO 31000-based tolerance criteria. The results feed a new Energy Transition Risk Index (ETRI) that scores country-level transition risk from 0 to 100 across policy, economic-financial, technological-infrastructure and geopolitical-security dimensions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Five risks are classified as Unacceptable: high debt burden and payment arrears, natural field depletion, failure to maintain export capacity, energy security crisis, and economic and fiscal crisis. Four critical pathways drive the system, the most immediate being a production-decline cascade from field depletion to energy-security and fiscal crisis. Three feedback loops (economic, investment-deterrent and production-constraint) turn sector shocks into self-reinforcing decline. The ETRI scores Egypt at 72.3 (high risk), driven by economic-financial (76.7) and geopolitical-security (76.3) risk.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Building resilience in such systems requires breaking feedback loops, not treating risks one by one. We propose a Diagnose–Map–Target framework: diagnose the dominant risk dimension with the ETRI, map its causal pathways with a Bayesian network, then target precise interventions such as arrears resolution, investment guarantees and binding renewable targets. The approach is transferable to other resource-dependent economies.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 367</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Ecosystem services, Local perceptions, Livelihood dependence, Environmental pressures, Núi Chúa Biosphere Reserve</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">PERCEPTIONS OF ECOSYSTEM SERVICES IN RELATION TO CLIMATE CHANGE AND LIVELIHOOD ADAPTATION AMONG LOCAL COMMUNITIES IN THE NUI CHUA BIOSPHERE RESERVE, VIETNAM</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Gitte Neyns</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">vub; <span class="paper_email">gitte.neyns@telenet.be</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Climate change and increasing human pressures are intensifying ecosystem degradation, with severe consequences for communities that depend on natural resources for their livelihoods. Understanding how these communities value ecosystem services and perceive environmental change is essential for effective conservation and climate adaptation. The Núi Chúa Biosphere Reserve in south-central Vietnam provides a relevant case study, where high biodiversity coincides with substantial climate stress and environmental degradation. This study investigates local perceptions and valuations of ecosystem services, differences across livelihood groups and socio-demographic backgrounds, and perceived environmental changes and adaptation strategies. Structured household surveys were conducted at five coastal sites in the Reserve’s buffer zone (N = 107).</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">All ecosystem service categories were rated highly, with provisioning and regulating services valued slightly more than cultural services. Among households with calculable natural resource dependence index (NRDI) scores (N = 44), resource dependence was high and significantly greater among older respondents. However, NRDI scores were not significantly associated with ecosystem service valuation. Agricultural and forestry households showed somewhat stronger preferences for terrestrial ecosystem services, whereas fishing households placed greater emphasis on marine ecosystem services. Reported environmental problems, including poor waste management, water pollution and declining fish stocks, were associated with lower ecosystem service valuations, suggesting a potential link between perceived ecological degradation and the value attributed to natural resources. Most respondents reported changes in weather patterns, particularly increasing drought frequency and severity. Despite widespread climate awareness, few respondents had received climate adaptation training, and reported adaptation strategies were largely reactive rather than preventive. These findings highlight a gap between awareness of climate change and access to practical adaptation support. Effective management of UNESCO Biosphere Reserves should therefore integrate ecosystem protection with livelihood support and targeted climate adaptation training.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 368</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Food system vulnerabilities; climate change; biodiversity loss; resilience assessment; adaptive capacity; policy pathways; systematic risk mapping</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Mapping Food Security Drivers through Resilience Capacities: An Integrated Resilience Framework for European Food Systems</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Giri Kandel<sup>1</sup>, Markus Dettenhofer<sup>1</sup>, Marjana Vasiljevic<sup>2</sup>, Ioannis Manikas<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Czech University of Life Sciences, Czech Republic (Czechia); 2:&nbsp;Institute of Field and Vegetable Crops; Novi Sad, Serbia; <span class='paper_email'>kandelg@af.czu.cz</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Food security is a complex and evolving challenge shaped by climate change, biodiversity loss, and the nexus among biophysical, technological, economic, political, supply chain, and socio-demographic drivers. Addressing these challenges requires system-level approaches that go beyond analysing individual sectors. This study aims to develop and apply a structured, resilience-oriented framework to systematically classify and analyse food security drivers across European food systems. The analysis is based on a structured integrative literature review, complemented by participatory insights from ten EU Living Labs, to categorise food system risks into six thematic domains. Using the four Global Food Security Index pillars as outcome dimensions, the framework qualitatively maps drivers across availability, affordability, quality and safety, and sustainability and adaptation, linking them to absorptive, adaptive, and transformative resilience capacities. The results indicate that biophysical and environmental, economic and market, and food supply chain drivers are prominent across the European contexts examined. In contrast, institutional, technological, and socio-cultural drivers vary across contexts. The findings further indicate that availability and sustainability may be particularly affected where climate-induced shocks, biodiversity loss, and market volatility interact. Mapping drivers through resilience capacities identifies potential intervention points that require further empirical validation, including governance coordination, supply-chain diversification, and investments that enhance adaptive and transformative capacity to manage trade-offs within food systems. By integrating food security drivers, resilience capacities, and GFSI pillars within a single analytical framework, this study offers an analytically grounded, conceptually derived tool intended to support, rather than replace, context-specific policy design, collaborative problem-solving by food-system practitioners, and future research on systemic food-security risks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 369</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digital solutions, agrochemical independence, one-stop-shop, Common Agricultural Policy, precision agriculture, EU strategies</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The role of digitalisation in supporting farmers and strategic policies for food security and sustainability in Europe</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Giri Kandel</u><sup>1</sup>, Jana Poláková<sup>1</sup>, Pavel Hamouz<sup>1</sup>, Adam Hruška<sup>1</sup>, Ioannis Varvaris<sup>2</sup>, Ioannis Manikas<sup>1</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Faculty of Agrobiology, Food, and Natural Resources, Ceska zemedelska univerzita v Praze, Kamycka 129, 16500 Praha, Suchdol, Czech Republic; 2:&nbsp;Department of Environment, ERATOSTHENES Centre of Excellence, 3012 Lemesos, Cyprus; <span class="paper_email">kandelg@af.czu.cz</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Ensuring food security while reducing dependency on agrochemical inputs has become a strategic priority for European agriculture amid climate change, geopolitical instability and environmental degradation. This paper examines the role of digitalisation in supporting sustainable and resilient agri-food systems across the European Union. Drawing on an extensive literature review, we analyse the current state of agricultural digitalisation, the systemic barriers constraining its uptake, and the effectiveness of key EU policy instruments, primarily the Common Agricultural Policy. Our study shows that several digital technologies, such as precision agriculture, sensor-based monitoring, and decision-support systems, have reached high technological readiness levels, yet adoption remains uneven. In terms of precision agriculture, several technologies exist, including variable-rate technology for crops, which can increase wheat production by 1% to 10% and offer savings in nitrogen fertilisation ranging from 4% to 37%. However, there are trade-offs to consider, such as power asymmetries, rebound effects, and a digital divide stemming from uneven digital literacy among farmers. The paper explores three detailed implementation pathways, including mechanisms, resources, feasibility and time horizon. The pathways include 1) EU framework for agrochemical independence and agricultural data digitalisation; 2) CAP Eco-schemes for low-input digitalisation; and 3) Cooperative digital inclusion for small and medium farms. Overall, the findings underscore that digitalisation alone is not a panacea, but its contribution to food security depends on governance, inclusive policy design and long-term investment in enabling conditions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 370</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">climate change; development nexus; climate justice; transformative adaptation; subnational governance; Kano State; Nigeria</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">ating the Climate Change–Development Nexus for Resilient, just and Equitable Transformations in Subnational Entities: A Study of Kano State</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Abdulkadir Inuwa Abdulkadir</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Federal University Dutsinma Katsina State, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">iabdulkadir@fudutsinma.edu.ng</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Abstract</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Subnational governments are where global climate commitments meet everyday development outcomes. In Nigeria, however, state-level climate action remains thinly theorised and unevenly resourced, even though the effects of a warming climate are felt most directly in farms, neighbourhoods, markets and households. This paper examines how the climate change–development nexus is playing out in Kano State, northern Nigeria’s most populous state and a major commercial, agricultural and industrial centre, and asks what would make its response resilient, just and equitable. Drawing on qualitative document analysis of national and state policy instruments, multilateral and project documents (including the Agro-Climatic Resilience in Semi-Arid Landscapes, ACReSAL, programme) and the recent peer-reviewed literature, and read through the lenses of climate justice and transformative adaptation, the paper advances three findings. First, climate stressors in Kano (erratic rainfall, heat, flooding, land degradation and water stress) compound pre-existing development deficits in agriculture, urban services, waste management, energy access and livelihoods rather than operating as a separate environmental problem. Second, the institutional response is fragmented, project-driven and heavily dependent on external finance, which limits its capacity to deliver transformation rather than incremental coping. Third, the distribution of risk and benefit is uneven: smallholders, women, peri-urban poor households and displaced people carry disproportionate burdens yet have limited voice in planning. The paper proposes a five-pillar framework (integrated planning, locally led adaptation finance, inclusive governance, nature-based and circular-economy investment, and data and learning systems) for moving Kano from reactive coping towards resilient, just and equitable transformation, and draws lessons for other Nigerian states and subnational entities in comparable drylands.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Keywords: climate change; development nexus; climate justice; transformative adaptation; subnational governance; Kano State; Nigeria</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 371</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">nature-based solutions, ecosystem services, marine management, biodiversity, limits to adaptation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Climate-risk analytics for nature-based solutions: assessing resilience, risk reduction and adaptive management in Portugal&#39;s marine Natura 2000 network</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Gardawheh Gad Boe</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Doctoral Programme in Climate Change and Sustainable Development Policies, University of Lisbon and NOVA University Lisbon, Lisbon, Portugal; <span class='paper_email'>boegardawheh@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Salt marshes, seagrass meadows, dunes and intertidal flats are promoted as natural coastal defences, yet their protective effect is conditional and the habitats are themselves exposed to the hazards they buffer. Area-based targets such as SDG 14.5 measure protected coverage, not whether protected habitats keep functioning as the climate changes. In Portugal, many such habitats lie in EU Natura 2000 sites whose conservation objectives are rarely climate-informed. This doctoral research asks where and under what conditions these habitats reduce coastal hazards, whether that role will persist, and how evidence can guide site management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The study couples two risk perspectives usually assessed separately: climate risk to habitats, and habitats&#39; role in reducing risk to people and assets behind them. A systematic evidence map will yield graded rules on when habitats reduce hazards, feeding a spatial risk assessment to 2100 under three climate scenarios, using satellite, climate-projection and census data. All mainland coastal sites will be screened, with Ria Formosa, the Tagus estuary and Ria de Aveiro analysed in detail. Policy analysis, interviews and workshops will test translation into management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: So far, this ongoing research has built a framework adjusting each habitat&#39;s protective contribution for its likelihood of persisting. Review of 2026 European Commission guidance on Natura 2000 and climate change shows it leaves site-level vulnerability methods to Member States and habitats&#39; risk-reduction role to case-by-case assessment. Expected outputs are maps of where protection is durable, at risk or overstated, and who lives behind it, plus draft climate-informed objectives and monitoring triggers.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Linking risk evidence to measurable site objectives offers a transferable route to keeping nature&#39;s contributions to people durable, helping protected areas serve biodiversity and adaptation goals by 2030 and informing post-2030 targets that track ecological function, not just coverage.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 372</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sugar, Nepal, Sugar Sweetened Beverages</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Nutrition profile, sugar disclosure, and marketing practices of sugar-sweetened beverages in Nepal: A cross-sectional package analysis</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ranjeeta Subedi</u>, Ghanshyam Gautam, Sujan Sapkota, Bishnu Dulal, Tara Aryal, Shalabh Shah, Sushil Baral</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">HERD International, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">ranjeeta.subedi@herdint.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background: Sugar sweetened beverages (SSBs) are the major contributors of excessive sugar intake resulting in increased obesity and non-communicable diseases (NCDs), including diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, and dental caries. Despite the increasing consumption of SSBs in Nepal, evidence on their nutritional composition, labelling practices, and regulatory environment remains limited.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A cross sectional Package label analysis of 449 unique SSB products available across Kathmandu valley, Lumbini and Madesh province was done</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Findings: The study found high compliance with basic mandatory labelling requirements given by Food Hygiene and Quality Act, 2024. However, nutrition information of public health importance was inadequately disclosed. While approximately three-quarters of products reported total sugar content, only 37·6% disclosed added sugar information, limiting consumer’s ability to make informed dietary choices.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The sugar levels in beverages available in Nepal was substantial. The median total sugar content was 11 g/100 ml. Most products exceeded the WHO South-East Asia Region nutrient profile thresholds for restricting marketing to children. Products with higher sugar content were also found to be relatively less expensive, potentially increasing their accessibility among lower-income populations.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Approximately one in seven products employed child-appealing marketing techniques, and nearly two-thirds of these products exceeded WHO nutrient thresholds. Furthermore, only around 16% of products displayed any form of warning label, while sugar-specific warnings and front-of-pack labels were virtually absent. Existing excise taxes appeared insufficient to meaningfully discourage consumption of high-sugar beverages.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Interpretation: These findings highlight the need for comprehensive policy reforms in Nepal, including mandatory disclosure of total and added sugars, implementation of interpretive front-of-pack warning labels, restrictions on child-directed marketing of high-sugar products, and adoption of sugar-tiered taxation. Strengthening market surveillance and public awareness initiatives would further support healthier dietary environments and contribute to reducing consumption of SSBs and the growing burden of non-communicable diseases in Nepal.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 373</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.5 Climate governance, finance, and negotiation for sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">intermediaries, legitimacy, democracy, pre-decisional influence, municipal planning</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Shaping Decisions Before They Are Made: Intermediary Influence and Democratic Legitimacy in Energy Transition Governance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Adelheid Böckmann</u>, Neneh Braum</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Universität Vechta, Germany; <span class="paper_email">adelheid.boeckmann@uni-vechta.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Intermediaries in sustainability transitions can shape the conditions under which collective decisions are prepared. Brokerage may structure access to governance arenas and resources; translation may affect which forms of knowledge become accessible and relevant; mediation may shape how conflicting interests are articulated and processed; and sense-making may influence problem definitions and the range of options considered. These forms of pre-decisional influence are democratically consequential because political influence can operate through the structuring of agendas and alternatives before formal decisions are taken (Bachrach &amp; Baratz, 1962).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Existing research has examined how intermediaries obtain and maintain legitimate positions within transition processes (van Lente et al., 2020) and has raised questions about the democratic nature and legitimacy of place-based intermediation (Loeber &amp; Kok, 2024). What remains less systematically developed are normative democratic criteria for evaluating the influence exercised through intermediary practices. This paper asks: How do intermediary roles shape the pre-decisional conditions of energy transition governance, and what are the democratic implications of such influence? Under what conditions can such pre-decisional influence be democratically justified?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The paper distinguishes the democratic relevance of intermediary influence from its democratic justification. It draws on democratic theory to examine how intermediary practices may structure access, knowledge, conflict, problem definitions, resources, and the range of available options before formal decisions are made, and why influence over these conditions matters democratically. It then takes Beetham’s (1991) account of legitimacy as a starting point and combines it with democratic accounts of accountability, contestation, and public justification. These considerations inform an evaluative framework that is applied to and further specified through cases of municipal energy transition planning in Germany. We argue that intermediary influence generates distinct legitimacy requirements when it shapes politically consequential conditions of decision-making without being directly subject to the authorisation and accountability mechanisms associated with formal decision-making authority.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 374</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Biogas adoption; Policy incentives; Clean cooking; Household energy transition; SDG 7.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Socio-economic factors and policy incentives for household biogas adoption in Gulu City, Uganda</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Julius Odong, Charles Oneka</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">CARITAS GULU ARCHDIOCESE, Uganda; <span class='paper_email'>odongjulius2@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The study investigates the determinants of biogas technology adoption among households in Gulu City, Uganda, emphasizing the importance of decentralised renewable energy for achieving Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 7. The research utilised a mixed-methods approach, surveying 88 households and conducting interviews with key informants. Findings revealed high awareness of biogas benefits, such as cost savings and improved air quality, but low actual adoption. Economic barriers, notably high installation costs and limited financing, were significant obstacles, while social factors had minimal impact. Policy incentives, including subsidies and training, enhanced the positive effects of socio-economic factors on adoption. The study concludes that successful biogas adoption requires affordable technology, accessible financing, reliable support services, and strong policy frameworks, urging local stakeholders to implement targeted strategies for fostering sustainable energy transitions in urban settings.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 375</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.3 Developing solutions and innovations for healthier societies</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sugar Sweetened Beverage, Taxation, Labelling, regulation</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sugar-Sweetened Beverage Consumption in Nepal: Evidence, Policy Gaps, and Governance Challenges</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Ranjeeta Subedi</u>, Ghanshyam Gautam, Sushil Baral</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">HERD International, Nepal; <span class="paper_email">ranjeeta.subedi@herdint.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Changing global food environment marked by rapid rise of ultra-processed foods (UPFs) consumption, particularly sugar-sweetened beverages (SSBs), reflects broader governance and market challenges. SSB consumption is strongly associated with obesity, diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, and other non-communicable diseases (NCDs). In Nepal, NCDs account for 71% of total mortality, with dietary risks playing a major role.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study used a mixed evidence-synthesis approach combining: scoping review of national and global literature; secondary analysis of nationally representative datasets, and review of national policies, laws, and regulations shaping food environment.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">In Nepal, 68% of the population consumed SSBs. Consumption is higher in urban and among wealthier households. Furthermore, 86.8% of commercially available food and beverage products commonly consumed by children exceed at least one threshold, indicating early exposure to excessive sugar intake. SSBs are widely available, with low unit prices and small package sizes encouraging frequent purchases. Marketing exposure remains substantial, over 70% of food advertisements aired during children’s television programming promote products high in sugar, fat, and/or salt.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Nepal has established a legal and policy foundation for regulating SSBs through food safety legislation, consumer protection provisions, advertising regulation, and commitments under MSNP III. However, current excise taxes remain low and volume based. Mandatory front-of-pack warning labels, standardized nutrient thresholds, and systematic enforcement mechanisms are not fully operational. Policy ambiguity and competing economic interests further limit decisive action, reflecting broader governance challenges in regulating commercial determinants of health.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Rising SSB consumption represents a critical yet under-addressed public health challenge in Nepal. While policy commitments exist, weak implementation, limited financing, and insufficient regulatory capacity constrain their impact. Strengthening fiscal measures, enhancing regulatory enforcement, and improving policy coherence across sectors are essential. Addressing SSB consumption requires navigating complex governance and power dynamics, and leveraging coordinated, multisectoral responses to advance health equity in an era of polycrises.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 376</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">SDG 6; open data governance; open ML models; AI; digital twins; Multi-agent simulations</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Accelerating SDG 6 and Related Goals through Open Data</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Bhanu R Neupane</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (UNESCO)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Maria Fasli</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (UofSussex)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Progress on SDG 6 depends on using evidence to improve water services, allocate resources and protect ecosystems. Yet fragmented data, limited access and uneven institutional capacity often prevent available knowledge from informing decisions. Climate pressures and competing demands make these gaps more consequential. Addressing these gaps requires multistakeholder cooperation and a shift towards managing water as a finite, shared resource through reliable evidence and collective responsibility.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Open data, open solutions and open machine learning models can widen access to knowledge and analytical tools. Artificial intelligence can support analysis, while digital twins can integrate observations and models to visualize changing conditions and test alternative responses. Multi-agent simulations can explore interactions among water users and institutions, helping assess policy choices and their consequences.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">These opportunities depend on five domains of open data practice: leadership and commitment; open data governance and management; resources and institutional capacity; risk management and trust; and implementation and demonstrated results. Together, these domains connect political support and clear responsibilities with reliable, interoperable data, sustained financing and skills. They also require safeguards for privacy and security, transparency about uncertainty and bias, and evidence that applications improve decisions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The session will use these five domains to structure a panel discussion on how openness and digital approaches can accelerate SDG 6 and advance related goals. It will examine government understanding of openness, readiness to share and use data, and barriers to implementation. Discussion will connect data governance with practical decisions on water services, resource allocation and long-term water security and sustainability. It will also consider how public participation and accessible evidence can strengthen responsible water use. The panel will identify capacity-building needs and draw lessons across health, food, energy and climate priorities, providing insights for the 2028 United Nations World Water Development Report and preparedness beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Open Solutions Approach: Sharing Knowledge to Advance the SDGs</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Lampros Stergioulas</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>THUAS</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">This presentation will introduce UNESCO’s Open Solutions approach, which connects open educational resources, open access to scientific information, open data, and free and open-source software. It will examine how these components can support SDG 6 by making water knowledge and tools easier to access, adapt and reuse. Discussion will address the policies, licensing arrangements, institutional capacity and partnerships needed to sustain their use. The presentation will connect openness with practical water decisions, highlighting its contribution to education, innovation, equitable access to knowledge and cooperation across the SDGs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Testing Water Policy Choices through AI and Multi-Agent Simulations</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Maria Fasli</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>UofSussex</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Water policies affect users and institutions with different needs, priorities and capacities to respond. This presentation will explore how AI, digital twins and multi-agent simulations can help examine these interactions and compare policy options for advancing SDG 6. It will consider how simulated responses to allocation rules, conservation measures and service disruptions can inform decisions and reveal potential trade-offs. Emphasis will be placed on transparent assumptions, uncertainty and stakeholder participation, connecting water management with reduced inequalities, effective institutions and climate resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Testing Policy Choices through AI and Multi-Agent Simulations</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Majdi Sabe Mofadi Owda</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>AAUP</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Open machine learning models can expand access to analytical tools for advancing SDG 6 and related sustainability goals. This presentation will examine their potential to assess water availability, identify water-quality risks and inform resource allocation. It will consider how model sharing, transparent documentation and local adaptation can support use across different institutional settings. Particular attention will be given to data quality, validation, technical capacity and the resources needed to maintain models. The discussion will connect these requirements with practical decisions linking water security, sustainable agriculture and climate resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Digital Twins for Water: Connecting Observations with Decisions for SDG 6</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'><u>Dev Niyogi</u></span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>UTA</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Digital twins can connect observations and models to represent changing water conditions and test management responses. This presentation will examine their practical use in advancing SDG 6, with attention to urban water systems, resource availability and climate-related risks. It will explore how scenario testing can inform infrastructure planning, service continuity and ecosystem protection. The discussion will address monitoring requirements, interoperability, model validation and institutional ownership, including when a digital twin adds value. Applications will be considered through their contribution to water security, sustainable cities and climate adaptation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 377</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">sustainable development; regional sustainability transformation; evidence-based decision-making; monitoring system; surveys</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A policy-oriented monitoring system for regional sustainability transformation. Insights from a German case study</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Harald Heinrichs</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Leuphana University, Germany; <span class="paper_email">harald.heinrichs@leuphana.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">The UN Transformation Agenda 2030 including the global sustainable development goals (SDGs) are not on track and will likely fail to meet its ambitious targets till 2030. Given that un-sustainable developments keep on going and - in some areas - are worsening, policy-oriented research and support for evidence-based decision-making in politics, public and private sectors as well as society at large remain important. In particular indicator-based international and national sustainability monitoring systems are crucial for orienting sustainability policy and governance and need to be further developed within the Post-2030-Agenda, for example regarding data availability, cross-cutting indicators, impact-orientation and policy relevance. Even though improved indicator-based monitoring systems will be essential, they are not sufficient. In this presentation it is argued, that beyond "objective" data, insights on "subjective" assessments of sustainability transformation is needed, in order to secure social acceptance and allow for resonant, adaptive transformation governance. Based on a pilot project on the subnational regional level of Lower-Saxony in Germany, a monitoring system for surveying decisionmakers from public and private sector as well as citizens regarding their perceptions, attitudes and actions on sustainability transformations is presented. Finally it is discussed, how this monitoring information may complement indicator-based monitoring systems, support evidence-based decision-making for sustainable development, and what its implementation at the science-policy-interface would require.    </p>



</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 378</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.2 Lessons learned from SDG governance and multi-stakeholder partnerships</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Household food waste; food-waste awareness; awareness–action gap; circular economy; sustainable consumption; household behaviour; resource efficiency; Oman.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">From Awareness to Action: Household Food Waste and the Behavioural Challenge of Circular Consumption in Oman</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Behnaz Saboori</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Sultan Qaboos University, Oman; <span class="paper_email">b.saboori1@squ.edu.om</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Reducing household food waste is essential for advancing sustainable consumption, resource efficiency, and circular-economy transitions. However, increasing awareness does not necessarily translate into changes in actual household behaviour. This study examines the relationship between food-waste awareness and actual household food waste in Oman, focusing on how socioeconomic, demographic, educational, employment, housing, and geographic characteristics shape this relationship.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The empirical analysis uses survey data from 347 households. Actual household food waste is measured using household food-related expenditure and the reported proportion of food wasted, providing an outcome-oriented measure rather than relying exclusively on attitudes or perceptions. The analysis examines the association between awareness and actual food waste while accounting for differences in household circumstances.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The findings indicate that higher food-waste awareness is associated with lower actual household food waste. Nevertheless, this relationship is heterogeneous across educational groups, employment categories, residential locations, and housing conditions. Household income is also positively associated with actual food waste, suggesting that greater purchasing capacity may increase the volume of food available for consumption and the potential for unnecessary disposal. Differences in household composition further highlight the importance of demographic context in food purchasing, preparation, consumption, and leftover management.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">These findings demonstrate that awareness alone is insufficient as a comprehensive food-waste reduction strategy. Effective interventions must translate knowledge into practical and sustained behavioural change. For Oman, this requires targeted communication, improved meal planning and storage practices, responsible purchasing, portion management, and locally adapted initiatives involving households, educational institutions, and municipalities. Integrating measurable food-waste outcomes into policy evaluation would also help distinguish improvements in awareness from actual reductions in waste.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The study contributes to the sustainable-consumption and circular-economy literature by highlighting the importance of household heterogeneity in the awareness–action relationship. Its policy message is that effective food-waste reduction requires moving beyond information provision towards context-sensitive interventions.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 379</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Greenspace Change, Urban Flood Risk, Land Use/Land Cover, GIS, Remote Sensing</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">SPATIO-TEMPORAL ANALYSIS OF GREENSPACE CHANGE AND ITS IMPACT ON URBAN FLOOD RISK IN KUMASI, THE MIDDLEBELT OF GHANA</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Francis Adarkwah</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Ghana, Ghana; <span class='paper_email'>francisadarkwah34@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Rapid and largely unplanned urbanisation in Kumasi, Ghana, once celebrated as the &quot;Garden City of West Africa,&quot; has progressively eroded the city&#39;s greenspace cover, weakened natural flood mitigation capacity and intensified urban flood risk. This study employed GIS and remote sensing techniques to investigate the spatio-temporal relationship between greenspace change and flood risk in the Greater Kumasi Metropolitan Area between 2015 and 2025. Landsat and Sentinel-2 imagery were classified using a Random Forest algorithm, achieving overall accuracies of 84% and 86% for 2015 and 2025 respectively, while NDVI analysis and a DEM-derived hydrological model were used to assess vegetation health and flood susceptibility. Results showed that built-up land expanded from 63% to 82% of the study area, while total greenspace declined by over 33 km², with dense vegetation losing more than half its 2015 extent. Spatial overlay analysis revealed substantial coincidence between areas of high greenspace loss and zones of high to very high flood risk, particularly in Aboabo, Anloga, Moshie Zongo, Asawase, and parts of Bantama, with emerging flood risk also evident in peri-urban areas such as Ahinsan and Kentinkrono. The findings indicate that flooding in Kumasi is driven not only by climatic factors but substantially by land cover change, underscoring the need for stricter land use enforcement, greenspace restoration, and nature-based flood mitigation measures to support the city&#39;s long-term hydrological resilience.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 380</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.1 Advances in AI methods, big data, and digital technologies for sustainability analytics</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Electricity demand forecasting, photovoltaic generation forecasting, ensemble learning, power-system operation, renewable energy integration</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Operational ensemble forecasting of electricity demand and post-curtailment photovoltaic generation in Cyprus</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Panagiotis Herodotou</u><sup>1,2</sup>, Ilia Neofytos<sup>1</sup>, Viken Davidian<sup>1</sup>, George Makrides<sup>1,2</sup>, George Georghiou<sup>1,2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Phaethon Center of Excellence, Nicosia, Cyprus; 2:&nbsp;University of Cyprus, Nicosia, Cyprus; <span class="paper_email">herodotou.panagiotis@ucy.ac.cy</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Increasing photovoltaic (PV) penetration creates a growing need for reliable electricity-demand and generation forecasts to support power-system operation. This study presents machine-learning ensembles for forecasting aggregated demand and post-curtailment PV generation in Cyprus. The ensembles are deployed through the “Loadcaster” online platform for the Transmission System Operator of Cyprus, supporting scheduling, reserve planning, and PV-curtailment management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Separate forecasting ensembles combine XGBoost, LightGBM, and ExtraTrees regressors through wighted averaging. Models are retrained daily using 360 days of historical data and generate forecasts at 15-minute resolution over a five-day window comprising the current day and four subsequent days. Inputs include lagged demand or generation, weather information from five regions of Cyprus, solar-position variables, and calendar features. These predictors capture meteorological influences and temporal patterns, including daily and weekly cycles and holidays. Operational performance is evaluated for the first 24 hours against measured data over approximately 45 days.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Demand forecasts achieved root mean squared errors (RMSEs) of 33.00 MW on weekdays and 35.10 MW on weekends, corresponding to normalized RMSEs of 2.70% and 3.26%, respectively. For post-curtailment PV generation, RMSEs were 41.24 MW on weekdays and 34.32 MW on weekends, with normalized RMSEs of 5.41% and 5.70%, respectively. Relative to the reference demand forecast, the ensemble reduced RMSE by 13.4% on weekdays and 39.2% on weekends.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The proposed ensemble provides an applied example of digital forecasting infrastructure supporting electricity-system operation during the energy transition. Anticipating demand and renewable generation can inform resource scheduling and reserve requirements while supporting curtailment management. Such capabilities are relevant to affordable and clean energy and climate action, supporting the integration of renewable electricity by 2030 and beyond. Longer evaluation periods are needed to establish performance across seasons and operating conditions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 381</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">ecosystem mapping, digitalization, circular infrastructure</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">The Textile Ecosystem Living Lab: AI-assisted mapping of the sectoral activity in the Netherlands</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Francesco Sollitto</u>, Troy Nachtigall</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">HvA, Netherlands, The; <span class="paper_email">f.sollitto@hva.nl</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The Textile Ecosystem Living Lab (TELL) is a novel effort to improve visibility into local ecosystems in the Textile, Clothing, Leather, and Footwear (TCLF) sector, offering an open-source tool to different stakeholders (businesses, governments, research and network organizations, etc.) pursuing their sustainability and economic performance targets. Focusing on the Netherlands, this research aims to illustrate the challenges and opportunities for organizations engaging in the Twin Digital and Green Transition, providing a more complete picture of local and national actors in the textile sector.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The presented approach combines 1) a dataset (or business registry) of companies, 2) an interactive dashboard, 3) an AI data scraper, and 4) an interactive input form. Users of the TELL can locate organizations on a map and search for potential collaborators using the keywords obtained from companies&#39; websites through the AI data scraper. Providing a semantic basis for business classification and identification, the tool works as a search engine specifically for the textile sector.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The key result of the TELL development is an accessible, regularly updated inventory enriched with keywords and supporting classification methods beyond standard industry classifications (i.e., NACE). Since the first version was launched, public institutions and subsidy providers have acknowledged the variety of textile activities and SMEs that can innovate and contribute to the circular transition.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">In conclusion, ecosystem mapping is gaining prominence in shaping future generations of e-government applications and circular ecosystem orchestration, supporting the large-scale coordination of actors engaged in Climate Action, Innovation, and Infrastructure development (i.e., recycling). Beyond 2030, with the Digital Product Passports (DPP) and Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) fully rolled out across the EU, the quality of the sectoral mapping can support policymaking around sustainability and improve the effectiveness of (differentiated) incentive systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 382</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">social well-being, social equity, land surface temperature, NDVI, urban greenery, urban heat island</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Heat, Density, and Green Space as Indicators for Development Priorities in Dense Urban Area of Palembang, Indonesia</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Harrini Hapsari<sup>1</sup>, <u>Hardayani Hardayani</u><sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Department of Landscape Architecture, University of Illinois at Urbana Champaign, United States of America; 2:&nbsp;Civil Engineering Study Program, Faculty of Engineering, Universitas IBA, South Sumatra, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">yeniharuno@yahoo.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Dense neighborhoods in Palembang, Indonesia, face growing climate stress as the city expands. Green cover keeps shrinking, and land surface temperature (LST) rose by about 2°C between 2008 and 2018. Rising heat harms both physical and mental health, and the harm falls hardest on low-income residents of overly dense areas. These residents also have the least access to green space. Urban greenery is often proposed as a nature-based solution for heat and well-being. But green space is usually placed without first asking where social welfare is most at risk, and its health benefits are measured only after it is built. This study offers recommendations for where urban greenery would do the most for health equity. Using Landsat 8–9 imagery, the study derives the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) for vegetation cover, LST from the thermal band for heat exposure, and the Normalized Difference Built-up Index (NDBI) for building density. The result is validated against a kernel density map of census population. The three layers are scaled from 0 to 1 and combined at the sub-district level into a Social Wellbeing–Density Index (SWDI). The output index is tested with a sensitivity analysis. The results show that sub-districts with high SWDI tend to fall mostly within Palembang’s older, dense urban core, where urban heat island effects, high population density, and low vegetation overlap, making these places urgent targets for greening to support the equal health of the population. By treating social wellbeing as a planning input rather than a final outcome, this study links environmental exposure to health equity. The approach can be used in other fast-growing cities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 383</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">health inequalities, pain management, administrative law, right to health, universal  health coverage</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Pain Inequity by Design? Administrative Gaps in Indonesia’s Universal Health Coverage and the Right to Pain Relief: Evidence from Knee Osteoarthritis</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Mailinda Eka Yuniza</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Muhammad Yusuf Hisam</u><sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Universitas Gadjah Mada, Indonesia; 2:&nbsp;Universitas Islam Indonesia, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">mailinda@ugm.ac.id</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">107110102@uii.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Chronic pain constitutes a major cause of disability and diminished well-being, yet access to effective pain management remains highly unequal. The Lancet Commission on Palliative Care and Pain Relief identifies an “access abyss” in which the majority of individuals in low- and middle-income countries lack basic pain relief. This inequity arises not only from clinical factors but also from legal and administrative structures. Pain equity should therefore be established as an explicit objective of universal health coverage (UHC) in the post-2030 agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">First, through doctrinal legal analysis, we examine how Indonesian law shapes access to pain care: constitutional and international right-to-health norms, the narcotics regime (Law 35/2009; Constitutional Court Decision 106/PUU-XVIII/2020), and the national health insurance (JKN/BPJS Kesehatan) benefit package, including formulary restrictions and case-based tariffs. Applying the general principles of good administration (Law 30/2014), we assess whether coverage exclusions are evidence-based, transparent and contestable, and what remedies exist.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Second, evidence is drawn from a doctoral clinical study at Universitas Gadjah Mada (ClinicalTrials.gov NCT07355712) involving patients with knee osteoarthritis. Prolotherapy combined with personalized physical activity demonstrated superior outcomes compared to prolotherapy alone in reducing matrix metalloproteinase-3 (MMP-3) and improving WOMAC pain, stiffness, and function scores; both interventions reduced interleukin-1β (IL-1β). However, these low-cost, non-surgical pain interventions are not explicitly included in the JKN benefit package, resulting in cost burdens for older patients and exacerbating inequalities in healthy aging.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">A pain equity framework is proposed: (1) transparent, health technology assessment (HTA)-based benefit decisions with published reasoning; (2) administrative appeal pathways for denied pain care; (3) inclusion of evidence-based chronic pain and palliative services in UHC packages; and (4) balanced narcotics regulation that safeguards medical access. Integrating pain relief into UHC governance links SDG 3.8 and SDG 16 to promoting healthy aging beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 384</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Innovative Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.5 Methods and processes for a sustainable development agenda beyond 2030</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Use of ICT tools in teaching and learning of STEM education</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Fostering Sustainable Science Education: Practical STEM Instruction and Low-Cost Pedagogical Strategies for Resource-Constrained Contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Edwin Agbegoe</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Ghana education service)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Presenter(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Edwin Agbegoe</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Ghana education service)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">Quality STEM education is central to achieving Sustainable Development Goal 4 (Quality Education) and equipping the next generation with problem-solving skills for sustainable development. </p>

<p class="paper_abstract">However, delivering practical physics and integrated science lessons in resource-constrained environments often presents significant challenges due to limited laboratory infrastructure and materials.</p>


<p class="paper_abstract">​This presentation explores actionable strategies for implementing hands-on, low-cost practical instruction in secondary science classrooms.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">By integrating accessible experimental setups such as low-voltage solar kits, practical circuit mechanics, and visual instructional materials educators can effectively demystify abstract physical concepts while fostering environmental awareness and technical competence.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The session highlights practical approaches to activity-based learning, student engagement strategies, and the integration of digital tools to support science pedagogy. Ultimately, enhancing practical STEM literacy at the secondary level serves as a crucial driver for sustainable community development and capacity building.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 385</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Agricultural sustainability, land-use change, carbon emissions, nutrient flows, human health</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Agricultural land and food-system transitions for sustainability beyond 2030 (p1)</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Yuanchao Hu</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Guangdong University of Technology)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Xue Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Zhen Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (College of Resource &amp; Environment, Huazhong Agriculture University)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Yanxian Li</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (College of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University,)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jia Ning</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Yuanchao Hu</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Guangdong University of Technology)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jia Ning</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Agricultural and food-system transitions reshape land use, resource flows, and ecological processes, with far-reaching implications for sustainability. Their consequences extend beyond production sites: policies promoting domestic food self-sufficiency can redistribute global land-use pressures, changes in vegetation cover can generate climate feedbacks, and food consumption can drive nutrient pollution in distant inland and coastal waters. As the international community looks beyond 2030, understanding these connections is essential for advancing sustainability while avoiding unintended consequences across regions and environmental systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Proposed by Yuanchao Xue, Zhen, and other colleagues, this session will examine how changes in agricultural production, food demand, and resource management interact with land-use dynamics to shape sustainability outcomes across local, regional, and global scales. From seasonal underutilization of farmland and long-term abandonment to shifts in crop production and aquatic food consumption, these changes link food security to climate regulation, biodiversity conservation, and nutrient cycling. This session brings together research on farmland underutilization and abandonment; agricultural policy, food self-sufficiency, and global land demand; vegetation–climate feedbacks; and opportunities to improve protected-area connectivity through food-system transformation and ecosystem stewardship. The session also explores the management of agricultural residues, nutrient surpluses and deficits, phosphorus depletion and conservation, and consumption-driven eutrophication. By linking these perspectives, we aim to identify how coordinated policies, spatially differentiated management, and integrated food-system strategies can reconcile productive land use with resource conservation, ecosystem protection, and environmental quality. We have invited eight speakers, and we welcome more relevant speakers to join our discussions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Incorporating seasonal and yearly farmland underutilization to reshape global abandonment hotspots and future projections</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Xue Wang</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, Chinese Academy of Sciences</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Farmland underutilization has become an increasing challenge to global food security and sustainable agricultural development, with yearly and seasonal abandonment representing two important but distinct forms. However, their spatiotemporal dynamics, key drivers, and interrelationships remain insufficiently understood at the global scale, limiting comprehensive assessments of farmland underutilization and future abandonment risks. To address these gaps, we developed an integrated global assessment framework combining long-term high-resolution land-use and cropping-intensity datasets, a three-stage least squares (3SLS) model, and an XGBoost-SHAP framework to characterize the spatiotemporal dynamics of yearly and seasonal abandonment, disentangle their interactions and underlying drivers, and project future yearly abandonment risk under multiple SSP-RCP scenarios. The results indicate that: (1) Yearly and seasonal abandonment exhibited contrasting spatiotemporal dynamics during the study period. Seasonal abandonment generally declined across major agricultural regions, whereas yearly abandonment continued to expand globally. Despite these contrasting trends, both forms of abandonment displayed pronounced spatial clustering and significant spatial associations. (2) The two forms of abandonment were governed by distinct environmental and socioeconomic drivers. Seasonal abandonment was more responsive to climatic variability and extreme events, whereas yearly abandonment was primarily shaped by landscape fragmentation, accessibility, and broader socioeconomic conditions. (3) Both contemporaneous and lagged seasonal abandonment significantly shaped yearly abandonment across several regions, with these effects further modulated by landscape configuration and topographic constraints. (4) Under all SSP-RCP scenarios, the future risk of yearly abandonment is projected to increase by 2050, with high-risk areas becoming increasingly spatially clustered.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Integrated policies could raise China’s soybean self-sufficiency and ease global land-use pressure</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Fei Lun</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>China Agricultural University</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Growing demand for animal-sourced foods is intensifying global reliance on soybeans, with China, the largest consumer and a low self-sufficiency rate (SSR) country, driving global largest imports and associated environmental spillover. To improve its SSR, China has implemented policies including rehabilitating salt-affected land, approving genetically modified soybeans and promoting healthy dietary transition. Here we evaluated these interventions using a scenario-based framework benchmarked against a no-policy baseline reconstructed soybean demand. China’s SSR declined from full self-sufficiency in 1961 to 17% by 2022 and is projected to decline to 12% by 2050 without intervention. Each 10% implementation of salt-affected land and genetically modified soybeans alone could increase soybean SSR by 1.4% and 1.0%, whereas a 50% healthy dietary implementation alone could raise to 17%. Although individual policies yield modest improvements, their combined implementation could increase SSR to 74% by 2030 and 80% by 2050. This shift could free soybean sufficient to feed 1.89 billion additional people globally while avoiding cropland expansion in tropical frontiers, enabling reforestation and carbon sequestration.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Inland and coastal eutrophication driven by aquatic food consumption</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Mengyuan Hao</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>School of Ecology, Environment, and Ocean, Guangdong University of Technology</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Aquatic foods provide high-quality protein and essential micronutrients, with considerable potential to reduce global nutritional inequalities. Yet soaring aquatic food production stresses local environments through intensive nitrogen and phosphorus losses to aquatic ecosystems. Meanwhile, production and consumption of aquatic foods are unevenly distributed across regions. International trade moves aquatic foods and their nutritional benefits across borders, while nitrogen and phosphorus pollution from aquaculture remains largely in producing regions and can contribute to eutrophication. How additional demand driven by dietary and nutritional equity goals will reshape global supply chains and their environmental impacts remains unclear. Here, we build a spatially resolved aquaculture system model to quantify nitrogen and phosphorus burdens embodied in global aquatic food trade. We combined a novel dynamic trade network with region-specific material-flow and environmental-fate models to reveal local eutrophication driven by aquatic food demand from elsewhere. We then simulate changes in production and trade under dietary and nutritional equity scenarios, accounting for production capacity and existing trade relationships. We also assess the resulting eutrophication-related ecosystem damage in inland and coastal waters. This study is expected to identify key production regions supplying additional demand, shifts in nitrogen and phosphorus burdens, and potential hotspots of ecosystem damage. The findings could inform efforts to reconcile nutritional equity with aquatic ecosystem protection, advancing Sustainable Development Goals 2, 6 and 14 beyond 2030.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Needs for more integrated, inclusive, and policy-Relevant modeling of food system transitions: Insights from a systematic review</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Yizhi Deng</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Integrated Research on Energy, Environment, and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Food systems underpin human nutrition and livelihoods but are currently on unsustainable trajectories, failing to ensure global food security while exerting substantial environmental pressures. Transitioning towards sustainable food systems therefore requires robust, integrated modeling frameworks to assess impacts, pathways for change, and trade-offs. In this study, we conduct a systematic review of food system modeling studies and synthesize 850 sustainability indicators from 150 publications. We show that indicator use is highly fragmented across the reviewed literature: of the 850 indicators identified, 436 are unique, with limited convergence in definitions, metrics, or reporting practices. This lack of standardization substantially constrains comparability across models, scenarios, and regions, hindering cumulative knowledge building and cross-study synthesis. We further find that around one-third of studies apply model coupling or integrated assessment frameworks, yet such approaches remain unevenly adopted across spatial scales and transition strategies. Our findings highlight the need for harmonized, transparent, and policy-relevant indicator frameworks to enable systematic and comparable assessment of food system transition outcomes.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Future Climate Feedbacks of Vegetation Greening under Land-Use Change on the Loess Plateau</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Jia Ning</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS)</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Land-use change driven by ecological restoration can substantially alter vegetation greenness and land–atmosphere interactions, yet the future climate feedbacks of vegetation greening under different restoration configurations remain poorly understood. Here, we coupled the Weather Research and Forecasting (WRF) model with the Future Land Use Simulation (FLUS) model to investigate the seasonal temperature responses to vegetation greening associated with the Grain for Green Program (GFGP) on the Loess Plateau and to assess future climate feedbacks under alternative land-use scenarios. The results showed pronounced seasonal differences in temperature responses, with the strongest cooling occurring in summer and weaker responses in other seasons. Summer cooling was associated with changes in surface energy partitioning and boundary-layer processes, and its magnitude increased along the precipitation gradient, indicating that background hydroclimatic conditions modulate the thermal effects of vegetation greening. Among the future land-use scenarios, Scenario III produced the strongest regional mean summer cooling, while the precipitation-based Scenario VI most closely reproduced this cooling among Scenarios IV–VI. These findings demonstrate that the future climate feedbacks of vegetation greening depend on both land-use configuration and regional precipitation conditions. Accounting for these interactions can improve the spatial allocation of ecological restoration and support climate-informed land-use planning on the Loess Plateau.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Synergizing climate change adaptation and mitigation in agriculture through crop switching</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Chen Zeng</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Huazhong Agriculture University</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) has become a vital approach to adapting to and mitigating climate change while safeguarding food security. Future climate change is projected to shift suitable cropping areas northward in China. Guided by the CSA framework, this study establishes a refined spatial linear optimization model for multi-cropping at national and provincial scales to explore the potential of crop switching in synergizing climate change adaptation and mitigation under the RCP4.5 and RCP6.0 scenarios in the 2050s. Results indicate that under national planning compared to the future baseline scenario without spatial crop reallocation, an adaptation optimization strategy increases crop production by 24.1% to 24.6% and achieves staple crop self-sufficiency (wheat, maize, rice, and soybean) by the 2050s under RCP4.5 and RCP6.0, but at the cost of a 19.2% to 23.3% increase in greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions. Alternatively, the mitigation optimization strategy can reduce direct cropland GHG emissions (CH4 and N2O) by 30.8% (86.0% from CH4)to 52.0% (79.9% from CH4) while maintaining crop production. While trade-offs exist, specific weighting regions of the Pareto front reveal co-benefits, including crop production (+4.0% to +21.4%), economic profits (+13% to +28.3%), reduced GHG emissions (−52% to −16.2%), and irrigation water savings (−12.8% to −0.7%). Acknowledging implementation constraints alongside modeling uncertainties, these optimal outcomes indicate that carefully designed crop-switching strategies such as expanding soybean cultivation in North China and replacing rice with wheat, maize, and soybean in the Yangtze River Plain could both enhance climate adaptation and mitigation and help meet China&#39;s food demand in the 2050s without cropland expansion.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 386</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Agricultural sustainability, land-use change, carbon emissions, nutrient flows, human health</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Agricultural land and food-system transitions for sustainability beyond 2030 (p2)</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Yuanchao Hu</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Guangdong University of Technology)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Zhen Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (College of Resource &amp; Environment, Huazhong Agriculture University)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Xue Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS))</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Yanxian Li</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (College of Environment and Ecology, Chongqing University)</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Jia Ning</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS))</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Xue Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS))</span>; <span class="paper_symposium_author">Zhen Wang</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (College of Resource &amp; Environment, Huazhong Agriculture University)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Agricultural and food-system transitions reshape land use, resource flows, and ecological processes, with far-reaching implications for sustainability. Their consequences extend beyond production sites: policies promoting domestic food self-sufficiency can redistribute global land-use pressures, changes in vegetation cover can generate climate feedbacks, and food consumption can drive nutrient pollution in distant inland and coastal waters. As the international community looks beyond 2030, understanding these connections is essential for advancing sustainability while avoiding unintended consequences across regions and environmental systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Proposed by Yuanchao Xue, Zhen, and other colleagues, this session will examine how changes in agricultural production, food demand, and resource management interact with land-use dynamics to shape sustainability outcomes across local, regional, and global scales. From seasonal underutilization of farmland and long-term abandonment to shifts in crop production and aquatic food consumption, these changes link food security to climate regulation, biodiversity conservation, and nutrient cycling. This session brings together research on farmland underutilization and abandonment; agricultural policy, food self-sufficiency, and global land demand; vegetation–climate feedbacks; and opportunities to improve protected-area connectivity through food-system transformation and ecosystem stewardship. The session also explores the management of agricultural residues, nutrient surpluses and deficits, phosphorus depletion and conservation, and consumption-driven eutrophication. By linking these perspectives, we aim to identify how coordinated policies, spatially differentiated management, and integrated food-system strategies can reconcile productive land use with resource conservation, ecosystem protection, and environmental quality. We have invited eight speakers, and we welcome more relevant speakers to join our discussions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Enhancing global protected area connectivity via food-system transformation and targeted ecosystem stewardship</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Zhen Wang; Zhongci Deng</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>College of Resource &amp; Environment, Huazhong Agriculture University</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Functional connectivity of protected areas (PA) is critical to global biodiversity yet faces growing threats from land-use change. Using an integrated framework, we evaluate how future PA connectivity, measured by mammal movement probability (MMP) and protected area isolation (PAI), responds to food-system transformation and targeted ecosystem stewardship (EcoS) strategies through 2070. Without interventions, global PA connectivity is projected to decline from 2020 to 2070 (-2.0% in MMP and +3.7% in PAI), with substantial regional variability (e.g., PAI ranging from 8.49% decline in Europe to 16.9% increase in Africa). However, coupling food-system transformation with EcoS strategies can not only fully counteract these trends but restore connectivity to above 2020 levels. These integrated strategies also deliver great co-benefits for food security and human health. Our study underscores the necessity to align food-systems transformation with conservation policies to reconcile ecological connectivity with global food security and sustainability.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Unmasking Multi-scale Trade-offs in Energy Transitions: Navigating Health, Affordability, Carbon, and Equality in Rural China</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Mingxing Sun</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS)</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">While household energy transitions are critical for climate mitigation and public health, reliance on macro-scale averages often obscures micro-scale trade-offs in health benefits, affordability, carbon footprints, and distributional equity. This study leverages rural household surveys from five Chinese provinces to evaluate electricity and natural-gas substitution scenarios against a 2022 baseline. Historical data confirm a clear shift toward non-solid fuels—rising from 12.2% in 2018 to 30.5% in 2022—which corresponded with a 33.0% drop in direct household PM2.5 emissions. Scenario modeling reveals that no single transition rate optimizes all policy objectives simultaneously. Electricity substitution minimizes household expenditure and the high-burden share at a 70% transition rate, while maximizing net benefits at 84% (CNY 749.52 per household). Conversely, natural-gas substitution reaches its maximum net benefit at 70% (CNY 537.31 per household). At full substitution, natural gas drives 15.92% of households into high-burden status and generates negative net benefits and elevated carbon footprints in 20 and 47 villages, respectively. Furthermore, although overall expenditure inequality decreases across both pathways, energy-burden inequality experiences a localized rebound effect at higher transition rates. These findings demonstrate that effective transition policies must move beyond broad regional targets to explicitly monitor threshold-crossing affordability risks and village-level carbon dynamics.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">The Tearing of Calm: Potassium Surplus and Deficit in China&#39;s Rice Cultivation</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Xiangbo Xu</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS)</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Potassium (K) is a crucial factor limiting global food security. However, K imbalance on a large spatial scale has received relatively little attention. Here, we developed a K material flow analysis framework integrating long-term farm survey data from more than 400,000 farms during 2003-2022 to quantify the evolution of K balance and K use efficiency (KUE, K absorption-to-input ratio) in China’s rice cultivation systems. We found that China underwent a substantial transition from widespread K deficit in 2003 towards K surplus in 2022. Spatial K imbalance remains severe. Potassium-supplementing regions have shifted from an extreme K deficit (KUE=2.14) in 2003 to a K balance in 2022, but potassium-stabilizing regions are experiencing K surpluses with a markedly aggravating trend. Scenario analyses showed that optimizing fertilizer nutrient composition provided the greatest potential to correct future K imbalances. These findings highlight the significance of spatially targeted strategies for sustainable K management.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Hidden costs across the global agrifood value chain: farm-to-fork true cost accounting</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Jingyu Zhang</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Integrated Research on Energy, Environment and Society (IREES), Energy and Sustainability Research Institute Groningen (ESRIG), University of Groningen, Groningen</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Hidden costs of the agrifood system are damages to the environment, human health and livelihoods that are not reflected in market prices. Such costs are increasingly estimated at the national scale, but where they arise along the supply chain, and how they compare with the value captured at each stage, remain poorly understood. We traced global food consumption through a multi-regional input–output (MRIO) model and attributed environmental, health and social costs to six farm-to-fork stages across 163 regions. We estimate that the global agrifood system generated US$2.6 trillion in hidden costs in 2023, comprising $1.2 trillion in environmental damage, $1.1 trillion in health impacts and $0.3 trillion associated with poverty among food-system workers. Primary production accounts for more than three-quarters of total hidden costs, despite capturing a much smaller share of value added. These findings reveal a sharp divergence between cost generation and value capture along the food chain, with primary production being approximately nine times more cost-intensive per dollar of value added than downstream stages. pressure. Our findings suggest that stage-resolved true cost accounting can reveal where hidden costs accumulate relative to value creation, providing a basis for targeting investment, procurement and policy towards the largest sources of hidden costs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Global phosphorus depletion and conservation pathways</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Jiexin Deng</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>School of Ecology, Environment, and Ocean, Guangdong University of Technology</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Phosphorus is an irreplaceable nutrient essential for sustaining agricultural production and global food security. However, growing demand from agriculture and clean-energy technologies, the uneven distribution of phosphate rock resources, and concentrated trade expose countries to varying risks of phosphorus depletion and supply disruption. Meanwhile, phosphorus losses throughout production and consumption further intensify resource pressures and environmental pollution. This study will develop a country-level dynamic material flow model to project phosphorus demand from agriculture and batteries through 2050. By integrating phosphate rock reserves, extraction, international trade, and soil legacy phosphorus, the model will assess regional phosphorus depletion risks and supply vulnerabilities. Scenarios involving improved phosphorus-use efficiency, dietary shifts, reduced food loss and waste, and waste recovery will then be developed to quantify the phosphorus conservation potential, environmental benefits, and interactions among these pathways. The study will identify regions vulnerable to future phosphorus supply risks, reveal how global trade alleviates or amplifies regional scarcity, and determine combinations of strategies suited to different resource endowments and demand structures. The findings will inform efforts to secure food and energy supplies, reduce phosphorus releases to the environment, and advance the Sustainable Development Goals.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Pull and push: The impact of the labor opportunity cost of farming and land fragmentation on cropland abandonment in China’s mountainous areas</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Hengfei Song</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research (IGSNRR), Chinese Academy of Sciences (CAS)</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Cropland is critical to food security and agricultural development. As a result of adjustments in farmers’ livelihood strategies, their abandonment behavior is jointly influenced by the labor opportunity cost of farming (LOCF) and land fragmentation. However, existing research on the relationships among LOCF, land fragmentation, and cropland abandonment remains insufficient in terms of analyzing farmers’ behavioral decision-making mechanisms. To address this gap, this study developed a theoretical framework based on push–pull theory and cost–benefit analysis to explain the relationships between these, and employed multiple econometric models to estimate the effects using survey data from 2540 mountainous farmers. The findings reveal that both LOCF and land fragmentation could significantly promote farmland abandonment, although the significance of LOCF is relatively low, likely due to the influence of other factors. This suggests that, for mountainous areas, managing land fragmentation remains an important approach to addressing abandoned farmland. Furthermore, LOCF and land fragmentation affect cropland abandonment by promoting labor migration, and LOCF also plays a role by encouraging cropland transfer. The effects of LOCF and land fragmentation on abandonment vary by cropping system and location, with stronger and more pronounced impacts in multi-cropping regions and regions closer to county towns. Therefore, to effectively manage cropland abandonment, improving land markets, developing new agricultural business entities, and developing specialty agriculture in mountainous areas are important strategies for promoting sustainable agricultural development in mountainous areas.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 387</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.3 Roles of equity & justice for sustainable transformation and resilience</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Decentralization, local government, equity, justice, community resilience, sustainable transformation, Nepal</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Decentralized Justice and Community Resilience: Nepal&#39;s Local Government as a Vehicle for Equitable Sustainable Transformation</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Upakar Bhandari</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Mid West University, Nepal; <span class='paper_email'>bhandariupakar@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background &amp; Rationale: Nepal&#39;s transition to a federal democratic republic through the 2015 Constitution established three tiers of government—federal, provincial, and local—fundamentally reshaping governance structures and enabling localized approaches to sustainable development. With 753 local government units comprising rural municipalities, municipalities, and district assemblies, Nepal presents a critical case study of how constitutional decentralization can advance equity, social justice, and community resilience while addressing the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key Question: How can Nepal&#39;s local government institutions serve as instruments of just transition and equitable sustainable transformation, particularly in ensuring that vulnerable populations—including smallholder farmers, Indigenous Peoples, and marginalized communities—are not left behind in the implementation of sustainable development?</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Focus &amp; Approach: This paper examines the enabling potential and structural challenges of Nepal&#39;s three-tier governance system in fostering:</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Equity &amp; Justice: How decentralized governance facilitates locally responsive, culturally appropriate solutions to sustainability challenges while addressing historical inequities and systemic vulnerabilities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Community Resilience: The role of local government in building adaptive capacity, social cohesion, and livelihood security in agricultural and rural communities facing climate variability and development pressures.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">• Leave No One Behind: Mechanisms and gaps in inclusive participation and benefit-sharing at the local level, particularly for marginalized and Indigenous communities.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Evidence &amp; Implications: Drawing on Nepal&#39;s experience implementing decentralized structures and initiatives linking research, extension, and education (REE) at local levels, the analysis identifies how grassroots governance can embed sustainability principles while advancing substantive justice. Insights from agricultural extension, market-led development, and community-based adaptation inform recommendations for strengthening the role of local institutions in resilient, equitable transformation aligned with national and provincial policy frameworks and SDG targets.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 388</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.1 Modeling Earth system interactions, including climate impacts on development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Hailstorms, Potential Hail Track, Bayesian Beta Regression, MONICA, yield loss</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">A spatial framework for quantifying hail impacts on crop production in Germany</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Surendra Giri</u><sup>1,2</sup>, Marlene Palka<sup>1</sup>, Markus Augenstein<sup>3</sup>, Michael Kunz<sup>3</sup>, Bruno Merz<sup>4,5</sup>, Claas Nendel<sup>1,2,6</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Leibniz Centre for Agricultural Landscape Research (ZALF), Eberswalder Str. 84, 15374, Müncheberg, Germany; 2:&nbsp;Institute of Biochemistry and Biology, University of Potsdam, Am Mühlendamm 3, 14469 Potsdam-Golm, Germany; 3:&nbsp;Institute for Meteorology and Climate Research, Karlsruhe Institute of Technology (KIT), Hermann-von-Helmholtz-Platz 1,   D-76344 Eggenstein-Leopoldshafen, Karlsruhe, Germany; 4:&nbsp;GFZ Helmholtz Centre for Geosciences, Telegrafenberg, 14473 Potsdam, Germany; 5:&nbsp;Institute for Environmental Science and Geography, University of Potsdam, Karl-Liebknecht-Straße 24/25, 14476 Potsdam-     Golm, Germany; 6:&nbsp;Global Change Research Institute of the Czech Academy of Sciences, Bělidla 986/4a, 603 00 Brno, Czech Republic; <span class="paper_email">surendra.giri@zalf.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Hailstorms are among the most destructive convective hazards affecting agriculture, yet their high spatial and temporal variability makes it difficult to represent hail damage in crop models. This study developed a spatial framework to estimate hail-induced crop losses by combining radar-derived hail tracks, simulated crop maps and phenological stages, and insurance claims into crop-specific damage. Hail damage was expressed as a reduction factor representing the proportion of effective yield loss and estimated using Bayesian Beta Regression. The model accounted for hail severity, crop type, phenological stage, their interactions, and parameter uncertainty.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The model showed strong predictive performance, explaining 80% of the variation in the training dataset and 77% in an independent test dataset. Yield losses generally increased with hail severity, although crop responses varied across growth stages. Potatoes were particularly vulnerable to moderate and severe hail, while winter rye and spring barley showed greater susceptibility under the most severe hail conditions. The Bayesian approach also provided credible intervals to quantify uncertainty in predicted damage.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The framework was subsequently applied to eight crops, including four winter and four summer crops, across Germany under +2 K and +3 K global warming scenarios. Monthly downscaled hail-occurrence probabilities, regional hail-intensity distributions, and phenology-specific damage factors were combined with MONICA-simulated yields to estimate hail-related yield losses and damage percentages from April to September. When multiple growth stages occurred within a month, the highest stage-specific loss was retained. This approach allowed spatial and seasonal comparisons of projected hail impacts under different warming levels.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">By linking observed crop damage relationships with future hail hazards and simulated crop development, the framework extends hail damage assessment from individual events to regional climate change applications. It offers a practical basis for incorporating hail-related losses into crop modelling and evaluating agricultural vulnerability under future climate conditions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 389</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Nature Language Processing, Sentiment analysis, Speech Recognition, Environmental pollution management, AI Inference</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Application of AI and Knowledge Graphs to Environmental Complaint Management for Sustainable Urban Governance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Yen Hsun Chuang</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Shih Hsuan Wei</u><sup>2</sup>, <u>Zi You Ho</u><sup>3</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Chinese Culture University, Taiwan; 2:&nbsp;National Taichung University of Education, Taiwan; 3:&nbsp;Tung Hai University, Taiwan; <span class="paper_email">zyx27@ulive.pccu.edu.tw</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">scottiewei@mail.ntcu.edu.tw</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">ziyouhou@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Environmental complaints provide important information on pollution impacts experienced by urban residents. However, complaints often contain ambiguous descriptions and overlapping pollution issues, making it difficult for local authorities to identify specific environmental problems and determine appropriate responses. This study aims to apply artificial intelligence (AI) and knowledge graphs to analyze citizen complaints and support environmental problem identification for sustainable urban governance. A total of 200 environmental complaint calls were collected and divided into 259 audio samples. Speech recognition and semantic analysis were applied to extract pollution-related information from citizen descriptions. Environmental complaints were classified into four major categories: water pollution, odor and air pollution, waste pollution, and noise pollution. Knowledge graphs were constructed to establish relationships among complaint descriptions, pollution characteristics, potential sources, and environmental impacts. The results showed that environmental complaints frequently involved multiple pollution characteristics, particularly those related to odor, waste, and noise. The developed knowledge graphs enabled the identification of relevant pollution categories and potential environmental problems from unstructured complaint descriptions, including cases involving overlapping pollution issues. Furthermore, incorporating environmental terminology improved the reported speech recognition accuracy from approximately 85% to 95%. These findings indicate that integrating AI with knowledge graphs can transform citizens&#39; descriptions of pollution impacts into structured environmental information. This approach provides a basis for improving environmental complaint assessment and supporting local authorities in identifying potential environmental problems, thereby contributing to sustainable urban governance</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 390</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.5 Health as both an outcome and driver of sustainable development</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Healthy ageing; SDG interactions; health equity; care-seeking; post-2030 agenda; multilevel analysis</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Ageing at the Margins: How SDG-Linked Deprivations Accumulate to Shape Health and Care-Seeking Among Older Adults</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Joseph Kojo Oduro</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">University of Cape Coast, Ghana; <span class="paper_email">joseph.oduro@ucc.edu.gh</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background: Population ageing is rapidly increasing in low- and middle-income countries, yet older adults remain largely invisible in SDG monitoring. This study examines how multiple SDG-linked deprivations accumulate to shape health and care-seeking among older adults, offering lessons for the post-2030 agenda.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: We analysed nationally representative data on 4,753 adults aged 60+. Five SDG-linked indicators were constructed: SDG 1 (poverty), SDG 3 (recent illness), SDG 4 (no formal education), SDG 10 (rural residence), and SDG 13 (Savannah ecological zone). We quantified co-occurring deprivations through an accumulation matrix and composite deprivation score (0–5) and fitted multilevel logistic regressions with random intercepts for enumeration areas.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: Disadvantages were common, geographically concentrated, and accumulated additively. Only 14.5% had zero deprivations; 24.5% had four; 4.0% had five. Mean deprivation rose from 0.98 (Coastal) to 3.75 (Savannah), where 95.3% had high deprivation (score ≥3). Rural residence increased illness odds (OR=1.83, 95% CI [1.39, 2.42]) but reduced care-seeking by nearly half (OR=0.54, 95% CI [0.37, 0.79]); lack of education also reduced care-seeking (OR=0.65, 95% CI [0.47, 0.90]). Interaction terms were non-significant (poverty × education: β=0.27, p=.414; poverty × rural: β=0.06, p=.907), but a four-indicator deprivation score was strongly associated with reduced care-seeking (OR=0.79 per additional deprivation, 95% CI [0.70, 0.88], p&lt;.001): each additional deprivation reduced care-seeking by approximately 21%. Intraclass correlations (0.18–0.29) confirmed strong community clustering.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Older adults experience SDG-linked deprivations as accumulating, geographically concentrated burdens. Because deprivations compound additively, every removal of a single deprivation yields a proportional improvement in care-seeking. Three lessons emerge: SDG monitoring must be age-inclusive; health systems must be reoriented toward communities and toward reducing specific barriers (education, rural access); and social protection must be integrated with health policy. As the post-2030 framework takes shape, older adults must move from the margins of sustainable development to its centre.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 391</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Full Panel Session</span><br />
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.5 Economic approaches beyond GDP, including circularity and  bioeconomy</span><br /><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">circular economy, circularity, regeneration, supply chains</span><br />
<p class="paper_title">Perspectives on circular and regenerative supply chains</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Chair(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Eugenia Rosca</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (University of Groningen, Netherlands, The)</span></p>

<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class="paper_symposium_label">Discussant(s): </span><span class="paper_symposium_author">Dirk Pieter van Donk</span><span class="paper_symposium_organisation"> (RUG)</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Circular supply chains are becoming a reality in many places, yet, many open questions remain about how to design, coordinate, and govern them. This special session brings together research on circular supply chains addressing themes such as governance of inter-organizational relationships needed for circularity, the role of orchestration and digital platforms as enablers, and best practices from several countries. These presentations of empirical insights from ongoing research present various tensions and trade-offs emerging in the implementation of circular economy. Participants will present new findings, exchange perspectives across methods and contexts, and help shape an agenda for the next generation of circular supply chain research.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_label">Presentations of the Full Panel</p>
<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Regeneration and circularity in B-Corps: Theorizing collective action through positive deviance</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Eugenia Rosca</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>RUG</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The transition to circular and regenerative supply chains requires inter-organizational participation. This study uses an inductive, exploratory multiple case study approach to examine the supply chains of 12 award-winning B Corps to understand how they build collaborations to support regeneration within and outside their supply chains. Based on insights from extensive qualitative data, we then deploy collective action theory as an interpretive lens to generate theoretical propositions situated in the empirical data. The findings unpack mechanisms needed to activate stakeholders for regeneration and the use of generosity to reinforce collaborations for regeneration. Using metaphorical imagination, we propose a theoretical explanation for how positive deviants in the regenerative domain develop collaborations for regeneration within and beyond their supply chains. We theorize positive deviance for addressing societal challenges through inter-organizational collaboration by revisiting the B Corp practices through the lens of collective action theory.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">A Value-Chain-Bootstrapping Approach for Regional Scaling of Regenerative Business Models</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Sven Kilian</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Campus Friesland</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Pressing ecological and social challenges demands a fundamental shift from a take-make-waste to a circular economy, where value creation is interconnected across value networks. However, promising circular business models often face structural lock-ins: their diffusion requires synchronized changes across supply systems, infrastructures, regulations, and adoption behaviours. This interdependence creates a systemic chicken-and-egg problem, stalling transformative innovations in pilot phases. Our study explores how a regional ecosystem in Friesland, Netherlands, overcame this lock-in through a value-chain-bootstrapping approach. In 2023–2024, over 30 organizations—farmers, processors, construction firms, municipalities, and provincial authorities—collectively committed to scaling a regenerative, biobased insulation value chain. Despite absent market demand or supportive frameworks, 110 farmers pledged to cultivate hemp on peatland, processors invested in new technologies, and adopters committed to integrating hemp insulation into 1,000 projects within three years. This synchronous alignment of diverse actors in the innovation ecosystem contradicts incremental niche development theories, warranting an in-depth case study. Using a qualitative, process-oriented design, we triangulate semi-structured interviews with actors across the value chain, archival materials (policy documents, reports, agreements), and internal consortium documents. Inductive coding, informed by grounded theory, surfaces mechanisms of coordination, issue framing, and trust-building. Theoretically, we introduce value-chain bootstrapping as a means to pre-construct market configurations, extending literature on circular entrepreneurship and ecosystem orchestration. Practically, we offer actionable insights for policymakers and entrepreneurs to design initiatives scaling circular solutions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">A network governance perspective on circular supply chains</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Juno Joseph Ninan</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>RUG</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Circular economy transitions require supply chains to develop reverse flows, recovery processes</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and open-loop material exchanges that involve a wider and more diverse set of actors than</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">traditional linear chains. Achieving collective circular outcomes requires these autonomous</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">actors to work together often across organizational and sectoral boundaries. Traditional supply</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">chain governance which focuses on vertical buyer-supplier relationships managed by a focal</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">firm, cannot address the governance challenges of multi-actor, cross-sectoral circular networks</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">where no single actor holds authority over the whole. However, there is limited empirical</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">research on network governance mechanisms in circular networks, with literature being</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">fragmented across sectors and contexts as most studies are often limited to single industries or</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">specific network types. This paper asks what network governance mechanisms are documented</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">in published empirical cases of circular networks, and what patterns emerge across different</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">network types and contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">To answer this, we conduct a two-phase study. In the first phase, we conduct a systematic literature study using the PRISMA framework to identify published empirical case studies of circular networks from Web of Science and Scopus. From a final sample of approximately 25 papers, we extract governance mechanisms, actor types, relationship types, network characteristics and governance challenges, wherever specified. We analyze the data through an iterative, abductive approach, moving between analytical constructs from network governance theory and emerging themes from data to identify recurring patterns and develop categories. In the second phase, we conduct 10-12 semi-structured interviews with practitioners and network orchestrators to complement and extend the literature findings to identify mechanisms under-reported in published cases and providing richer understanding of how governance operates in practice. Together, these phases produce an integrative framework of network governance mechanisms in multi-actor circular networks.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Digital platforms and sustainability knowledge in supply chains</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Giovanna Culot</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>University of Udine, Italy</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Due diligence regulations, carbon reporting and circular economy requirements are</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">increasing the need for information on suppliers and products. Digital platforms seek to meet this need by providing supplier ESG ratings, carbon footprints and traceability data. In this study, we examine how these platforms represent suppliers, products and material flows, and how buying firms use this information. We analyzed 22 cases, each involving a platform provider and a client firm, based on 44 interviews and public documents. We find that platforms produce sustainability</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">information in different ways. Supplier assessment platforms mainly use supplier declarations to generate ratings based on their own scoring methods. Carbon accounting platforms combine buyer data with emission factors and estimates when supplier data are unavailable. Traceability platforms record the movement of materials and products. Across these platforms, providers check documents, methods and records, but only partly verify the accuracy of the underlying data.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Buying firms therefore need to check, interpret and combine platform outputs with other information. These outputs are more likely to inform decisions when integrated into existing processes, such as supplier qualification, tenders and product design. Product-level data are particularly relevant for sourcing and design choices, while circularity applications remain largely at the pilot stage. Our findings show that the value of platform-based sustainability information depends not only on how platforms produce it, but also on how buying firms assess and use it.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>

<div style="font-size:4pt; clear:both;">&nbsp;</div>
<p class="paper_title">Corporate engagement in the circular economy: evidence for Romanian companies</p>
<p class="paper_symposium_authors"><span class='paper_symposium_author'>Mirela Panait</span><br /><span class='paper_symposum_organisation'>Petroleum-Gas University of Ploiesti, Romania</span></p>

<div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: The transition to a circular economy is central to Sustainable Development Goal 12</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and the European Green Deal, yet evidence on how companies in emerging European capital</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">markets integrate circularity into corporate strategy remains limited. This study examines the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">extent, evolution and quality of circular economy engagement among companies listed on the</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Bucharest Stock Exchange between 2015 and 2025, a period covering the shift from voluntary</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">disclosure to the Non-Financial Reporting Directive and the Corporate Sustainability</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Reporting Directive.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: A content analysis was applied to sustainability, non-financial and integrated</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">reports published by companies listed on the regulated market. A disclosure index was</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">developed around circular economy principles (reduce, reuse, recycle, recover, redesign),</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">covering waste management, resource and material efficiency, eco-design, industrial</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">symbiosis, circular business models, targets and performance indicators. Disclosures were</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">scored by depth (narrative, quantitative, target-based) and compared across years, industries</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and company size.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results: Circular economy disclosure increased markedly after mandatory non-financial</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">reporting was introduced and again during preparation for the European Sustainability</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Reporting Standards. However, reporting remains dominated by waste management and</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">recycling, while redesign, product life extension and circular business models are rarely</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">addressed. Energy, utilities and manufacturing companies report more extensively than</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">financial and service firms. Quantitative indicators and time-bound targets remain scarce,</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">suggesting a gap between symbolic commitment and substantive implementation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: Companies listed on the Bucharest Stock Exchange are increasingly engaged in</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">circularity, but mainly through compliance-driven, end-of-pipe practices. Stronger regulatory</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">guidance, sector-specific metrics and investor pressure are needed to move toward</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">transformative circular strategies. The findings inform the sustainable development agenda by</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">and beyond 2030, showing that mandatory reporting is an enabler, but not a guarantee, of</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">corporate contributions to sustainable production and consumption in emerging markets.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 392</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.1 Advances in AI methods, big data, and digital technologies for sustainability analytics</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">AMR, antimicrobial resistance, identification and prediction</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">MicroBIS A novel AI/ML based platform for bacterial and antimicrobial resistance identification and prediction</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>DAVID OTIENO</u>, BEATRICE OTIENO, Evans Martin</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Kenya Medical Research Institute, Kenya; <span class="paper_email">daveouma56@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Background</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Antimicrobial Resistance (AMR), one of the top 10 global health threats, poses a critical public health challenge with a projected 10 million deaths annually by 2050 if left unchecked1,2. In 2019, 1.27 million deaths were attributed to AMR, with data gaps in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) impeding global efforts3,4. Fragmented databases limit the understanding of regional AMR burdens and hinder the development of data-driven interventions. Datasets like the Pfizer-ATLAS program are essential for real-time tracking of antibiotic resistance and for building predictive models to forecast AMR.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The microBIS platform combines a web server, AI/ML processing, and a user-friendly client interface. The MERN Stack workflow was used for web development and Python-based tools like Random Forest and PyBact for AI/ML modeling. Biochemical test results simulated and the Pfizer ATLAS datasets were used to train the platform&#39;s models to identify bacteria and predict AMR.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Results</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The microBIS app offers intuitive interfaces for bacterial identification and AMR prediction. It uses biochemical test panels (8 or 20 tests) for bacteria identification, achieving a 79% accuracy with the 20-panel and 31% with the 8-panel. When predicting the top 5 bacterial species, accuracy increased to 99% for the 20-panel and 74% for the 8-panel. Certain tests, like citrate production, showed higher predictive value.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">For AMR prediction, the Machine Learning (ML) model trained on the Pfizer ATLAS dataset achieved 56% accuracy in predicting antimicrobial susceptibility profiles . Key predictors included bacterial phenotypes and species.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">microBIS bridges the gap between accessible biochemical assays and advanced ML tools, offering a cost-effective, scalable solution for bacterial identification and AMR prediction. It empowers healthcare providers and researchers in resource-limited settings with rapid diagnostic capabilities, enhancing clinical decision-making and AMR surveillance. By integrating AMR datasets and predictive analytics, microBIS enables</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">better care delivery and supports the global fight against AMR.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 393</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Digitalisation, AI, climate adaptation, climate policy</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Digitalisation in Global Climate Adaptation Governance</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Niyanta Shetye</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">BTU Cottbus-Senfenberg, Germany; <span class="paper_email">niyantasheye@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Digital technologies from early warning systems to artificial intelligence are increasingly presented as enablers of climate adaptation. However, systematic evidence on how governments plan to use them, which technologies are planned for different adaptation themes remain scarce. The study offers a near global text-as-data based assessment of digitalisation in national adaptation planning under the Paris Agreement. We analyse the main adaptation documents submitted to the UNFCCC namely the National Adaptation Plans (81 NAPs), Adaptation Communications (71 ACs), and Nationally Determined Contributions (167 NDCs and the EU) to extract 1622 distinct passages which we distinguish using a codebook on technology family, adaptation themes, climate hazards, implementation status, evidence strength, and intensity of digital climate links. Evidence was triangulated across all three documents, duplicate passages were counted once and false positives were removed by reassessing passages, unsure interpretations in documents submitted in Spanish, French, and Arabic. This approach gave us a holistic view on digitalisation in adaptation governance as reliying solely on one document set would have given a broad overview instead of an in-depth view needed to assess the extent to which digitalisation is included in climate adaptation planning. Our findings suggest that digital technology features in 89% of countries, 76% of countries link it a specific climate application. Data platforms and information systems are among the highest use cases (80%) followed by decision support systems (76%) and remote sensing (75%). AI appears in 19% of country submissions and is the most aspirational technology for adaptation. Countries mainly deploy or want to attain technologies for risk assessment and early warning systems specifically for floods (58%) and droughts (57%) with sea-level rise being the main theme in Small Island Developing States. Additionally most of the coded measures utilising technology are conditional goals while only 27% are operational.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 394</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">rights of nature, water governance, Europe, France, multispecies justice</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Failure of Translating Rights of Nature into French Water Governance? Insights from the Loire and Tavignanu Cases</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Yixin CAO</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Vrije Universiteit Brussel, Belgium; <span class='paper_email'>yixin.cao@vub.be</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">The global water crisis has intensified calls to move beyond anthropocentric water governance. Inspired partly by Indigenous cosmologies that understand rivers/nonhumans as living beings, the Rights of Nature (RoN) movement has led to the legal recognition of rivers in countries including Colombia, New Zealand, Australia, etc. Yet translating RoN into Europe’s highly institutionalized and technocratic water-governance systems remains challenging. While some RoN initiatives strengthen stakeholder engagement and empower riverine communities (e.g., the Mar Menor lagoon in Spain), others remain largely symbolic, with limited effects on formal policy and implementation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines how RoN is translated into the French context through two pioneering initiatives: the Parlement de Loire (2019) and the Déclaration des Droits du Fleuve Tavignanu in Corsica (2021). The Parlement de Loire, the first major RoN initiative in France, reimagines the Loire as a subject and creates spaces for dialogue among scientists, stakeholders, artists, and citizens. In 2026, a bill proposing recognition of the Loire as a natural legal entity was submitted to the French National Assembly. In Corsica, the Tavignanu Declaration emerged from civil-society mobilization against a landfill project in 2015 and was subsequently endorsed by the Corsican Assembly, representing the first formal political recognition of river rights in France. After more than a decade of legal battles, the dispute culminated in a 2026 court ruling that halted the landfill project.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on 38 interviews conducted in 2025 and participant observation at meetings and events, the study analyses both the opportunities and limitations of RoN in France. It highlights persistent difficulties in translating river personhood into French institutional frameworks and in establishing legitimate forms of nonhuman representation. Although neither initiative has secured enforceable legal status, both cases create new socio-political spaces for renegotiating human–river relationships and advancing discussions on ecocentric transformations in water governance in France.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 395</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.1 Ensuring food, water, and energy security and understanding their nexus</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Climate change, emission accounting, food consumption, age groups</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Youth lead current dietary emissions while seniors increasingly drive total emission growth</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Yanxian Li<sup>1</sup>, Jin Yan<sup>2</sup>, Pan He<sup>3</sup>, Prajal Pradhan<sup>4</sup>, Shuai Fang<sup>5</sup>, Yuli Shan<sup>6</sup>, Franco Ruzzenenti<sup>4</sup>, Klaus Hubacek<sup>4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Chongqing University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; 2:&nbsp;Fudan University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; 3:&nbsp;The University of Hong Kong, China, People&#39;s Republic of; 4:&nbsp;University of Groningen, Netherlands; 5:&nbsp;Soochow University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; 6:&nbsp;Nanjing University, China, People&#39;s Republic of; <span class='paper_email'>yanxian.li94@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Diets across age groups vary in both nutritional quality and greenhouse gas emissions, yet their relationship remains poorly understood in the context of population aging and evolving dietary patterns. We evaluated dietary emissions associated with 141 products across 22 age groups in 149 countries from 2000 to 2019, combining age-specific dietary intake from the Global Dietary Database with a consumption-based emission inventory derived from FAOSTAT. We quantified changes in emissions alongside shifts in nutritional quality and examined contributions of demographic and dietary factors. Adolescents and young adults generally had the highest per-capita dietary emissions, while seniors contributed most to global emission growth, particularly in high- and upper-middle-income countries. Increased meat intake was a leading contributor to rising emissions and frequently coincided with declining nutritional quality. These findings highlight the need for dietary-emission mitigation efforts across age groups that account for demographic shifts, nutritional needs, and national development contexts.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 396</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 3: Navigating the climate change–development nexus for resilient, just and equitable transformations</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">3.4 Understanding climate change and climate actions for human security</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">equity and justice, transboundary climate risks, human mobility</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Counting Care in Thailand&#39;s Smoky Season</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Chidi Emenike</u><sup>1</sup>, Martina Padmanabhan<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;University of Passau, Germany; 2:&nbsp;University of Passau, Germany; <span class="paper_email">emenik01@ads.uni-passau.de</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Environmental governance under the SDGs relies on instruments such as air quality indices and hotspot maps that make harm visible and measurable. This study asks what those instruments leave out. Drawing on three months of fieldwork in Ban Pong, northern Thailand, I examine how the annual smoky season organises gendered care work and who pays for breathable air. I argue that unpaid reproductive labour during the season is what keeps households habitable through the atmospheric crisis, and that current governance instruments are designed in ways that systematically overlook this labour. I adopted a qualitative design informed by feminist political ecology. My study draws on eleven individual interviews, five of which are life-history depth, a focus group with eight elders, and three key informants from government and NGOs. Coding was abductive and done in MAXQDA. The analysis develops two concepts. Seasonal habitability describes the differentiated capacity to stay, work, care or leave during recurring environmental degradation. Institutional displacement refers to the shift in responsibility from state instruments onto households. Residents describe the season as pakati, or ordinary. Exposure becomes normal when livelihood and care obligations make leaving impossible. Women absorbed most of the additional domestic work, which included cleaning and sealing rooms. Men were also drawn into care, though disproportionately. Their public and technically organised work, such as fire patrol and monitoring, was recognised and equipped by state agencies. Intimate care, mostly carried out by women, remained invisible. None of the instruments I reviewed, from PM2.5 standards to clean-air rooms, treated households as reproductive units. This study argues that haze policy should count and support the reproductive labour that sustains lives during the smoky season. Sustainability metrics that ignore reproductive labour produce unjust outcomes. So, this paper calls for a post-2030 agenda that counts care as a precondition for equitable transformation.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 397</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 2: Building resilience through integrated sustainable transformation across natural and human systems</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">2.2 Sustainable landscape and seascape for nature's contribution to people</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">biodiversity, biosphere integrity, landscape management, indigenous people, conservation area</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Towards Fire-informed Conservation Management in Wasur National Park: Integrating Human and Ecological Dimensions of Fire</p>
<p class="paper_author"> Kushartati Budiningsih<sup>1,2</sup>, Mohammad Sidiq<sup>3,4</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;National research and innovation agency, republic of Indonesia, Indonesia; 2:&nbsp;Department of Forest Management, Faculty of Forestry- IPB University, Bogor, Indonesia; 3:&nbsp;Study Program of Natural Resources and Environmental Management Science (NREMS), IPB University, Bogor, Indonesia; 4:&nbsp;GIZ ( Deutsche Gesellschaft für Internationale Zusammenarbeit) Forclime, Indonesia; <span class='paper_email'>raku001@brin.go.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Objective: Fire is an integral component of the landscapes of Wasur National Park (Wasur NP) and is closely connected with Indigenous livelihoods, ecological processes, and landscape management. This study aims to understand fire as a social-ecological process and develop implications for conservation management that integrate ecological dynamics with Indigenous knowledge, fire practices, and institutions.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Methods: The study was conducted in Wasur NP, South Papua, Indonesia. The park is a designated Ramsar Site and forms part of the Trans-Fly biogeographic region, a distinctive landscape characterized by extensive savannas, wetlands, and forests in southern New Guinea. A mixed-methods approach examined Indigenous fire practices and traditional ecological knowledge, fire regime dynamics and their relationships with climate and human activities, ecological changes associated with repeated burning, and institutions governing traditional fire use.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Key results: Fire was found to be embedded in Indigenous livelihood practices and landscape management rather than functioning solely as an ecological disturbance. Traditional fire practices are informed by knowledge of vegetation, landscape conditions, seasonality, and livelihood needs. Fire regime dynamics reflect interactions between human activities and environmental conditions, while repeated burning is associated with changes in ecological conditions across the landscape. Institutional arrangements governing fire use differ among situations, with variations in rules, actors, monitoring, compliance, and enforcement shaping institutional effectiveness. These findings indicate that uniform fire suppression may not adequately reflect the social-ecological complexity of fire in Wasur.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Conclusions: The study proposes fire-informed conservation management as a basis for integrating human and ecological dimensions of fire into protected-area governance. This approach can support context-sensitive conservation while recognizing Indigenous knowledge, livelihoods, and local institutions. Beyond 2030, integrating fire dynamics, biodiversity conservation, Indigenous knowledge, and institutional capacity can contribute to more resilient and equitable pathways for sustainable management of social-ecological systems.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 398</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 1: Lessons from the Sustainable Development Goals for sustainable development beyond 2030</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">1.1 National and local implementation of SDGs, including barriers and opportunities</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Biodiversity conservation, community-based ecotourism, Sustainable Development Goals, participatory governance, local livelihoods</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">COMMUNITY-BASED ECOTOURISM FOR THE SUSTAINABLE DEVELOPMENT GOALS: EVIDENCE FROM NINH BINH, VIETNAM</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Thi Thu Ha Tran</u><sup>1</sup>, <u>Thi Phuong Do</u><sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Vietnam National University of Forestry (VNUF), Vietnam; 2:&nbsp;University of Economics-Technology for Industry, Vietnam; <span class="paper_email">hattt@vnuf.edu.vn</span><span class='paper_organisation'>, </span><span class="paper_email">dtphuong@uneti.edu.vn</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">
<p class="paper_abstract">This study examines the evidence on the contributions of community-based ecotourism (CBET) to Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) 1, 8, 12, 15, and 16 across three protected areas in Ninh Binh, Vietnam including Cuc Phương and Xuan Thuy National Parks, and Van Long Nature Reserve. The SDG 14 is considered specifically for Xuan Thuy where evidence relating to coastal ecosystems and aquatic resources is available.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract"><strong>T</strong>he study combines secondary document analysis with a comparative case study approach. The documents are analysed across four dimensions: (i) livelihoods and employment; (ii) responsible tourism practices; (iii) ecosystem and biodiversity conservation; and (iv) governance, coordination, and community participation in decision-making. Each finding is classified into five evidence levels: potential, activities, outputs, outcomes, and impacts. This classification distinguishes between anticipated contributions, implemented actions, and verifiable changes.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">The three cases exhibit different approaches to organising tourism activities. In Cuc Phuong, tourism services are linked to national park management and buffer-zone communities. In Van Long, boat-tour services are organised through a cooperative and a rotational system. In Xuan Thuy, tourism services are provided by local households within the context of coastal livelihoods. These arrangements indicate preliminary contributions to the SDGs by creating opportunities for community members to provide tourism services, supporting local livelihoods, and linking tourism activities with resource management. However, the extent of these contributions varies across the sites. Available evidence remains insufficient to establish actual outcomes and impacts in terms of increased household income, employment quality, equitable benefit distribution, responsible tourism practices, participation in decision-making, and conservation effectiveness.</p>

<p class="paper_abstract">CBET can contribute to advancing the SDGs, but the extent to which these contributions are realised needs to be assessed through verifiable socioeconomic and environmental outcomes. These dimensions warrant particular attention in monitoring progress towards the 2030 Agenda and in designing sustainable development programmes for the post-2030 period.</p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 399</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.4 Trustworthy AI, information integrity, data autonomy, and sustainability impacts of AI</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">AI spillover effects, data governance, water–energy nexus, data centres, Indonesia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Who pays for the cloud? Data centre expansion, water and energy trade-offs in Indonesia’s digital transition</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Haekal Al Asyari</u><sup>1</sup>, Alda Izzati<sup>2</sup></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">1:&nbsp;Faculty of Law, Universitas Gadjah Mada, Indonesia; 2:&nbsp;Center for Human Rights Studies Universitas Islam Indonesia (PUSHAM UII); <span class="paper_email">haekal.al.asyari@ugm.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Indonesia is positioning itself as Southeast Asia’s next artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure hub. Data localisation rules, tax holidays and special economic zones attract record data centre investment, framed as progress towards Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 9. This paper asks who bears the costs of that expansion, and whether current governance distributes them in line with the SDGs.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The study combines doctrinal and policy analysis of Indonesia’s legal framework (Government Regulation 71/2019 on electronic systems, the Personal Data Protection Law, investment incentive and special economic zone rules) with the non-binding ASEAN Guide for Sustainable Data Centre Development (2025). Industry and market reports supply data on capacity, energy and water demand. A distributional lens maps benefits and burdens across SDGs 6, 7, 9 and 13.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Preliminary findings show a structural mismatch: legal instruments reward installed capacity, while environmental performance remains voluntary and largely undisclosed. Coal supplied two-thirds of national electricity in 2024, yet data centres alone could require about 19,000 GWh by 2030. Their projected water use more than doubles, from roughly 38 to 87 billion litres between 2025 and 2030, with capacity concentrated almost entirely in Greater Jakarta and Batam. Forecasts for 2030 range from under 1 GW to almost 12 GW, so grid, water and incentive planning rests on unverified projections. Burdens fall on grid users, local water users and public budgets; benefits accrue mainly to operators and foreign cloud demand, including load displaced from Singapore.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Data centres can enable sustainable development only if growth is conditional. The paper proposes linking incentives to efficiency and clean-energy thresholds, mandatory disclosure of energy, water and carbon performance, water-stress-sensitive siting, and local benefit-sharing. These measures offer a transferable model for governing digital infrastructure in a post-2030 agenda, ensuring the digital transition does not undermine the green one.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 400</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.5 AI, big data, and digitalization for sustainable development by and beyond 2030, including opportunities and challenges</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">emerging governance frameworks, data governance, digital sovereignty, Third World approaches to international law, Indonesia</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Sovereign for whom? Digital sovereignty, the postcolonial state and the blind spots of Sustainable Development Goal 16</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Haekal Al Asyari</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Faculty of Law, Universitas Gadjah Mada, Indonesia; <span class="paper_email">haekal.al.asyari@ugm.ac.id</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Digital sovereignty has become a rallying claim for Global South states resisting data extraction by foreign platforms. Yet sovereignty can be turned inward, against the very people it claims to protect. This paper asks how the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) shape this tension, using Indonesia as a case study.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Drawing on Third World Approaches to International Law (TWAIL), the paper combines doctrinal analysis of Indonesia’s digital governance framework with a critical reading of the SDG targets and indicators that govern data and connectivity. It examines the 2019 internet shutdowns in Papua, later declared unlawful by the Jakarta Administrative Court, the 2022 platform blocking under the electronic systems operator registration regime, and state data integration through the One Data Indonesia policy.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">The SDG framework strengthens the data-hungry state. Through the “data revolution” agenda and targets 17.18–17.19, it treats national governments as trusted custodians of data for development. The same sovereignty, exercised against citizens, undermines targets 9.c on universal internet access, 10.2 on inclusion and 16.10 on public access to information and fundamental freedoms. The burden falls hardest on marginalised regions such as Papua. These harms remain invisible: no SDG indicator measures shutdowns, blocking or surveillance, and indicator 16.10.2 records only whether access-to-information guarantees exist. A state can therefore report progress while cutting entire regions offline. This reproduces a pattern TWAIL has long identified, in which the postcolonial state accumulates power in the name of development and its people.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract">Digital sovereignty supports sustainable development only when it is anchored in people rather than the state alone. For the post-2030 agenda, the paper proposes a people-centred data sovereignty principle: a digital rights target with indicators on shutdowns and blocking, community data governance inspired by Indigenous data sovereignty, and independent monitoring beyond state self-reporting.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 401</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 4: Health, well-being, and societal foundations as key drivers of sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Oral Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">4.4 Health equity, well-being, inner development, and societal foundations as societal outcomes</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Sustainable Urbanization, Public Health, Nigeria, Clean Sanitation, Affordable Housing.</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Blueprinting Nigeria&#39;s Future: Sustainable Urbanization for Healthier, Equitable, and Resilient Cities</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Akongbowa Bramwell Amadasun</u></p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Federal University Wukari, Wukari,  Taraba State, Nigeria, Nigeria; <span class="paper_email">ab.amadasun16@gmail.com</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Nigeria&#39;s rapid demographic shift has triggered unprecedented urban growth, presenting a dual challenge: managing vast population influxes while safeguarding public health and ecological integrity. This paper explores the intersection of sustainable urbanization and public well-being in Nigeria, positioning healthy cities as the foundation for the nation&#39;s long-term sustainable development. Specifically, it examines the synergistic impacts of three core pillars within the Nigerian context: clean sanitation, integrated green spaces, and affordable housing. Employing a mixed-methods research design, this study synthesizes qualitative policy evaluations with quantitative infrastructure deficit data across three major Nigerian metropolitan hubs: Lagos, Kano, and Abuja. The methodology includes a systematic review of federal and state urban development frameworks alongside empirical field observations of sanitation distribution, urban canopy coverage, and housing affordability indices. Spatial analysis via GIS mapping is utilized to evaluate the accessibility of green spaces and sanitation facilities relative to population density and socioeconomic strata. The findings indicate that isolating these infrastructure components leads to fragmented, inefficient urban governance. Conversely, a holistic, health-centric planning paradigm yields compounding benefits across multiple United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), particularly SDG 3 (Good Health and Well-being) and SDG 11 (Sustainable Cities and Communities). Circular economy solutions in waste management can significantly mitigate endemic disease risks, while urban greening combats the sub-Saharan heat island effect. Ultimately, this paper provides Nigerian policymakers, urban planners, and public health advocates with an actionable framework to transition from speculative real estate growth toward human-centric, climate-adaptive urban ecosystems</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

</div>
</div>
</div>
<br clear=all style='page-break-before:always'>
<div class="paper"><span class="paper_id">ID: 402</span>
<br />
<span class="paper_type">Theme 5: Artificial Intelligence, big data, and digitalization as enablers for sustainable development</span><br />
<span class="paper_format">Poster Presentation</span><br>
<span class="paper_label">Topics: </span><span class="paper_topics">5.1 Advances in AI methods, big data, and digital technologies for sustainability analytics</span><br><span class="paper_label">Keywords: </span><span class="paper_keywords">Scope 3 emissions, healthcare sustainability, greenhouse-gas accounting, carbon accounting; automated emissions calculation, spend-based method, digital health innovation, hospital carbon footprint</span><br>
<p class="paper_title">Establishing an EHS Wide Scope 3 Emissions Baseline: A Data-Driven Digital Platform for Healthcare Carbon Accounting</p>
<p class="paper_author"> <u>Nouf Alloghani</u>, Yomna Al ketbi, Maitha Khoori, Zakaria Alattal</p>

<p class="paper_organisation">Emirates health services, United Arab Emirates; <span class="paper_email">nouf.alloghani@ehs.gov.ae</span></p>

<div ><div class="paper">

<p class="paper_abstract">Healthcare Scope 3 emissions arise across complex procurement and operational activities, making measurement difficult. At Emirates Health Services (EHS), fragmented data and no standardised process limited portfolio visibility. Costly commercial platforms offered limited healthcare customisation, creating the need for a practical, internally governed digital alternative. This project aimed to establish an EHS-wide Scope 3 baseline through a purpose-built, in-house digital platform that converts financial data into decision-ready greenhouse-gas (GHG) outputs. EHS developed a hospital-specific GHG platform with centralised data-quality review and benchmarking. The platform imports available financial data and automates calculations using both spend-based and activity-based methods. Calculations are aligned with the GHG Protocol Scope 3 framework and cover all 15 Scope 3 categories. The 2025 exercise included procurement, transport, waste, catering, professional services, and other expenditure categories across the EHS portfolio. The programme established the first EHS-wide Scope 3 baseline across all 17 hospitals. Total reported emissions for the 2025 reporting year were 154,297 tCO₂e. Medical devices represented 35% and pharmaceuticals 27% of the total footprint; together, these categories accounted for 62% of reported emissions, identifying procurement as the principal reduction opportunity. Al Qassimi Hospital recorded the highest footprint, at 43,697 tCO₂e or 28% of the EHS total. At this site, medical devices and pharmaceuticals accounted for 73% of reported emissions, guiding a targeted reduction roadmap. All 17 EHS hospitals were onboarded to the platform. The in-house model also indicated estimated annual savings exceeding USD 150,000 compared with equivalent commercial GHG software. The EHS platform demonstrates that an automated, governed Scope 3 accounting process can provide healthcare organisations with portfolio visibility and site-level priorities for action. The programme supports green procurement, supplier engagement, hospital reduction roadmaps, ESG reporting readiness, and alignment with UAE Net Zero 2050. Further work will finalise data-quality review and increase activity-based data for priority categories.</p>
<p class="paper_abstract"></p>

